/* Generated by build_book.py from the textbook sources: chapters, numbered equations (MathML, pre-rendered with KaTeX), glossary and exercises. Do not edit by hand. */
window.NG_BOOK={"parts":{"I":"The single neuron","II":"Dynamics and resonance","III":"Synapses, networks and populations","IV":"Learning and computation","V":"Models meet data"},"chapters":[{"n":1,"part":"I","pathway":0,"title":"Membranes, channels and the Hodgkin–Huxley model","intro":["Every model in this book, from a single dendrite to a hippocampal network, rests on one statement: the voltage across a membrane changes when the currents crossing it do not balance. This chapter builds that statement from physical parts (a capacitor, conductances and batteries) and then adds the voltage-dependent gates that let a membrane generate an action potential.","We work in the units used throughout the guide: capacitance in pF, conductance in nS, current in pA, voltage in mV and time in ms. These are consistent: pA/nS gives mV and pF/nS gives ms, so most calculations need no conversion factors."],"obj":["Write the current-balance equation for a patch of membrane and solve it for a current step","Compute reversal potentials with the Nernst and GHK equations and use driving force to predict current direction","Explain the Hodgkin–Huxley gates as first-order kinetics with voltage-dependent targets and time constants","Convert between specific (per-area) and total quantities when moving from squid axon to a CA1 compartment"],"prereq":["Exponential functions and first-order linear ODEs","Ohm’s law, V = IR"],"sections":[{"route":"/topics/channels/membrane-circuit/","num":"1.1","obj":["Write the current balance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\,dV/dt = I_\\text{in} - g_L(V-E_L)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and say what each term means","Solve it for a current step and read the time constant and steady state off the solution","Explain why the membrane time constant does not depend on cell size"],"body":"<p>A lipid bilayer is about 4 nm thick and is an excellent insulator. Ions on either side are separated by it, so the membrane behaves as a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"capacitance\" tabindex=\"0\">capacitor</dfn>: the charge <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> held on it is proportional to the voltage across it, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q = CV</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Biological membranes have a remarkably constant <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"specific-capacitance\" tabindex=\"0\">specific capacitance</dfn> of about <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi><msup><mtext>F/cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m \\approx 1\\ \\mu\\text{F/cm}^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so total capacitance is simply membrane area times <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><p>Ion channels pierce the bilayer and let charge leak across. At rest, the many always-open channels behave together like a resistor in series with a battery: a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"leak-conductance\" tabindex=\"0\">leak conductance</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L</annotation></semantics></math></span> that pulls the voltage toward the leak <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"reversal-potential\" tabindex=\"0\">reversal potential</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_L</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Charge conservation says that any current injected into the cell must either charge the capacitor or flow out through the conductance:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-1-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mtext>  </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mtext>  </mtext><mo>−</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\,\\frac{dV}{dt} \\;=\\; I_\\text{in} \\;-\\; g_L\\,(V - E_L)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.1.1)</span></div><p>The term <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L(V-E_L)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the ionic current, written <b>outward-positive</b>: when <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V &gt; E_L</annotation></semantics></math></span> it is positive and carries charge out, lowering <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This sign convention is used everywhere in the guide. Dividing Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-1-1-1\">(1.1.1)</a> by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L</annotation></semantics></math></span> shows that only two combinations of parameters matter, the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"time-constant\" tabindex=\"0\">membrane time constant</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = C/g_L</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"input-resistance\" tabindex=\"0\">input resistance</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in} = 1/g_L</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-1-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\,\\frac{dV}{dt} = -(V - E_L) + R_\\text{in}\\,I_\\text{in}, \\qquad \\tau = \\frac{C}{g_L},\\quad R_\\text{in} = \\frac{1}{g_L}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.1.2)</span></div><p>For a current step of size <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> switched on at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> with the cell at rest, the solution is a saturating exponential:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-1-3\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(t) = E_L + R_\\text{in} I\\left(1 - e^{-t/\\tau}\\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.1.3)</span></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-1-1-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/step-response.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/step-response.svg\" alt=\"Figure 1.1.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 1.1.1.</b> Passive charging, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-1-1-3\">(1.1.3)</a>. <b>A</b> 25 and 50 pA steps into a cell with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS: the final deflection scales with current, and the dotted lines mark 63% at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>. <b>B</b> The normalized response for three time constants: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> sets only the speed.</figcaption></figure><p>The steady-state deflection <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta V_\\infty = R_\\text{in} I</annotation></semantics></math></span> is set by the conductance alone; the capacitance only decides how long the cell takes to get there. After one time constant the voltage has covered <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>63</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-e^{-1} \\approx 63\\%</annotation></semantics></math></span> of the way, after two about 86% and after three about 95%.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-1-1-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 1.1.1</p><h4>A passive soma</h4><p>Take <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS and a 50 pA step. Then <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 100/5 = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mtext> nS</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in} = 1/(5\\text{ nS}) = 200</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta V_\\infty = 50/5 = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV. The voltage reaches 90% of its final value when <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-t/\\tau} = 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>46</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = \\tau\\ln 10 \\approx 46</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms.</p><p>How much charge moved? <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext> pF</mtext><mo>×</mo><mn>10</mn><mtext> mV</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q = C\\,\\Delta V = 100\\text{ pF} \\times 10\\text{ mV} = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> pC, about <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6\\times10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math></span> monovalent ions. That sounds like a lot, but a cell body holds roughly <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{11}</annotation></semantics></math></span>–<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>12</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{12}</annotation></semantics></math></span> K⁺ ions, so charging the membrane changes intracellular concentrations by a negligible fraction. This is why concentrations, and hence reversal potentials, can be treated as constant during electrical signalling.</p></div><p><b>Why the time constant is size-independent.</b> If a cell has area <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = c_m A</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L = g_m A</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> the leak conductance per unit area. The area cancels in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = c_m/g_m</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A big neuron and a small one with the same membrane properties charge with the same time course, but the big one needs more current for the same deflection because its <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is smaller (<a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"/topics/morphology/area-and-density/\">§2.3</a>).</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 1.1</span> Simulate a passive membrane (exponential Euler)</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">C, gL, EL = 100e-12, 5e-9, -65e-3      # F, S, V\ndt, T      = 0.1e-3, 0.2                # s\nV = EL\nfor t in range(0, T, dt):\n    I     = I_step(t)                      # injected current (A)\n    V_inf = EL + I / gL                    # where V is heading\n    tau   = C / gL\n    V     = V_inf + (V - V_inf) * exp(-dt / tau)   # exact for constant I\n    record(t, V)</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Time constant ≠ “how fast the cell is”</h4><p><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> describes the passive response to a small input around one operating point. Once voltage-gated channels open, the effective conductance changes with voltage, and so does the effective time constant. A CA1 pyramidal cell measured at −80 mV and at −60 mV can have different apparent <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> values largely because <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and other currents are more or less active.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-note tb-biology\"><p class=\"tb-label\">In real CA1 cells</p><h4>Measured values</h4><p>For CA1 pyramidal neurons in slices, whole-cell input resistances of roughly 50–200 MΩ and membrane time constants of roughly 15–40 ms are typical; the spread reflects temperature, age, recording method (sharp versus whole-cell) and how much <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> is active at the holding potential. Treat any single number as a property of the protocol, not of “the” CA1 cell.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Lipid bilayer (capacitor) and always-open K⁺ leak channels</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Every neuron; CA1 somatic τ ≈ 15–40 ms</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Whole-cell current clamp, small hyperpolarizing steps</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>First-order linear ODE, exponential relaxation</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Exponential-Euler update; Neuron workbench, LIF preset</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["The membrane is a capacitor in parallel with conductances; current balance gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>ion</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\,dV/dt = I_\\text{in} - \\sum I_\\text{ion}</annotation></semantics></math></span> with outward-positive ionic currents.","A passive patch is described by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = C/g_L</annotation></semantics></math></span> (how fast) and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in} = 1/g_L</annotation></semantics></math></span> (how far).","The response to a current step is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta V(t) = R_\\text{in}I(1-e^{-t/\\tau})</annotation></semantics></math></span>: 63% after <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>, 95% after <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Area cancels in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> but not in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."],"terms":["capacitance","specific-capacitance","leak-conductance","reversal-potential","time-constant","input-resistance"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"A cell has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 150</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS. Find <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the steady deflection for a 30 pA step.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 150/10 = 15</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mtext> nS</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in} = 1/(10\\text{ nS}) = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta V_\\infty = 30/10 = 3</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Estimate the capacitance of a spherical soma 20 µm in diameter, assuming <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m = 1\\ \\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>F/cm².","a":"Area <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> cm</mtext><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.26</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 4\\pi r^2 = 4\\pi(10^{-3}\\text{ cm})^2 \\approx 1.26\\times10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math></span> cm². <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi><msup><mtext>F/cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>1.26</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mtext> cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>12.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 1\\ \\mu\\text{F/cm}^2 \\times 1.26\\times10^{-5}\\text{ cm}^2 \\approx 12.6</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF. A whole CA1 pyramidal cell measures roughly ten times more because most of its membrane is in the dendrites."},{"kind":"concept","q":"You double a model cell’s area while keeping <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> fixed, and inject the same 50 pA. What happens to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the steady deflection?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> is unchanged (area cancels). <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span> halves because total leak doubles, so the steady deflection halves (10 → 5 mV in the example above). Same time course, smaller amplitude."},{"kind":"derive","q":"Show by substitution that Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-1-1-3\">(1.1.3)</a> solves Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-1-1-1\">(1.1.1)</a> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(0)=E_L</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dV/dt = (R_\\text{in}I/\\tau)e^{-t/\\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\,dV/dt = (C/g_L\\tau)\\,I e^{-t/\\tau} = I e^{-t/\\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The right side is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I - g_L R_\\text{in} I (1-e^{-t/\\tau}) = I - I + Ie^{-t/\\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Both sides agree, and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(0)=E_L</annotation></semantics></math></span>."}],"eqs":[{"num":"1.1.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mtext>  </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mtext>  </mtext><mo>−</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\,\\frac{dV}{dt} \\;=\\; I_\\text{in} \\;-\\; g_L\\,(V - E_L)</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"1.1.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\,\\frac{dV}{dt} = -(V - E_L) + R_\\text{in}\\,I_\\text{in}, \\qquad \\tau = \\frac{C}{g_L},\\quad R_\\text{in} = \\frac{1}{g_L}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"1.1.3","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(t) = E_L + R_\\text{in} I\\left(1 - e^{-t/\\tau}\\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/channels/driving-force/","num":"1.2","obj":["Compute a reversal potential with the Nernst equation at body temperature","Use the sign of the driving force <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V-E</annotation></semantics></math></span> to predict which way current flows","Explain why the resting potential lies between <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Na}</annotation></semantics></math></span> (GHK equation)"],"body":"<p>Each ion species is pushed by two forces: its concentration gradient and the electric field. The voltage at which these exactly cancel, so that no net current flows through a channel selective for that ion, is the ion’s <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"reversal-potential\" tabindex=\"0\">reversal (equilibrium) potential</dfn>, given by the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"nernst\" tabindex=\"0\">Nernst equation</dfn>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-2-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_X = \\frac{RT}{zF}\\,\\ln\\frac{[X]_\\text{out}}{[X]_\\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.2.1)</span></div><p>At 37 °C, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>26.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">RT/F \\approx 26.7</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV (61.5 mV per tenfold concentration ratio); <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the ion’s valence. With typical mammalian concentrations:</p><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Ion</th><th>Outside (mM)</th><th>Inside (mM)</th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span> at 37 °C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>K⁺</td><td>4</td><td>140</td><td>+1</td><td>≈ −95 mV</td></tr><tr><td>Na⁺</td><td>145</td><td>12</td><td>+1</td><td>≈ +67 mV</td></tr><tr><td>Ca²⁺</td><td>2</td><td>0.0001</td><td>+2</td><td>≈ +132 mV</td></tr><tr><td>Cl⁻</td><td>120</td><td>7</td><td>−1</td><td>≈ −76 mV</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For an ohmic channel the current is conductance times <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"driving-force\" tabindex=\"0\">driving force</dfn>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-2-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_X = g_X\\,(V - E_X)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.2.2)</span></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-1-2-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/reversal-ladder.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/reversal-ladder.svg\" alt=\"Figure 1.2.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 1.2.1.</b> <b>A</b> Nernst potentials for the concentrations in the table, and the GHK resting potential. <b>B</b> Ohmic I–V lines, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-1-2-2\">(1.2.2)</a>: each crosses zero at its reversal potential, so at −65 mV the K⁺ current is outward and the Na⁺ current inward.</figcaption></figure><p>Conductance is never negative, so the **sign of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V - E_X</annotation></semantics></math></span> alone decides the direction of current**. With our outward-positive convention, a positive current is net positive charge leaving the cell. At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=-65</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, K⁺ current is outward (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V-E_K = +30</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV) and Na⁺ current inward (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>131</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V - E_\\text{Na} \\approx -131</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV), even though both conductances are positive numbers.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-1-2-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 1.2.1</p><h4>Which way does chloride go?</h4><p>A GABA<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>A</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_\\text{A}</annotation></semantics></math></span> synapse opens a Cl⁻ conductance of 3 nS in a cell sitting at −60 mV, with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Cl</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>76</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Cl} = -76</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV. Then <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>76</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 3\\,(-60 + 76) = +48</annotation></semantics></math></span> pA: an outward current that hyperpolarizes the cell. In terms of ions, outward <i>current</i> of a negative ion means Cl⁻ flowing <i>in</i>. Keep the two words apart: the sign convention is about charge, not about which ion moves.</p><p>In immature neurons with high intracellular Cl⁻, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Cl</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Cl}</annotation></semantics></math></span> can sit above rest; the same conductance then produces inward current and depolarization. The sign of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V-E</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the only thing that changed.</p></div><h3>Where does the resting potential come from?</h3><p>If a membrane were permeable only to K⁺, it would rest at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Real membranes are mostly, but not only, permeable to K⁺. The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"ghk\" tabindex=\"0\">Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz (GHK) voltage equation</dfn> gives the voltage at which the <i>total</i> current through several permeabilities is zero:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-2-3\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rest</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></mfrac><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Na</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Cl</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Cl</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Na</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Cl</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Cl</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\text{rest} = \\frac{RT}{F}\\ln\\frac{P_K[K]_o + P_\\text{Na}[\\text{Na}]_o + P_\\text{Cl}[\\text{Cl}]_i}{P_K[K]_i + P_\\text{Na}[\\text{Na}]_i + P_\\text{Cl}[\\text{Cl}]_o}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.2.3)</span></div><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Cl</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>0.45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_K : P_\\text{Na} : P_\\text{Cl} = 1 : 0.05 : 0.45</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the concentrations above, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-1-2-3\">(1.2.3)</a> gives about −70 mV: close to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K</annotation></semantics></math></span> because K⁺ dominates, pulled up by a small Na⁺ permeability. During an action potential <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\text{Na}</annotation></semantics></math></span> transiently exceeds <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_K</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the voltage swings toward <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Na}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Note that Cl⁻ appears with inside and outside swapped because it is negatively charged.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 1.2</span> Reversal and resting potentials</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">RT_F = 8.314 * (273.15 + temp_C) / 96485 * 1000   # mV\ndef nernst(c_out, c_in, z):\n    return RT_F / z * log(c_out / c_in)\nE_K  = nernst(4, 140, +1);  E_Na = nernst(145, 12, +1)\nV_rest = RT_F * log((pK*K_o + pNa*Na_o + pCl*Cl_i) /\n                    (pK*K_i + pNa*Na_i + pCl*Cl_o))      # GHK\nI_K = g_K * (V - E_K)                    # outward-positive: sign of (V - E) gives direction</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Ohmic is an approximation</h4><p>Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-1-2-2\">(1.2.2)</a> assumes a straight-line current–voltage relation. When concentrations are very asymmetric (Ca²⁺ is the extreme case), the true single-channel current is better described by the GHK <i>current</i> equation, which rectifies. Models of Ca²⁺ currents in dendrites often use GHK for this reason. The reversal potential is still where the current changes sign.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Ion gradients maintained by the Na⁺/K⁺ pump and transporters (KCC2 sets low Cl⁻)</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Mature CA1 pyramidal cells: E_Cl below rest; immature neurons: above</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Voltage clamp I–V curves; reversal from the zero crossing</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Nernst and GHK equations, ohmic I = g(V − E)</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Neuron workbench HH voltage clamp, I–V tab</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Reversal potentials follow from concentration ratios (Nernst); at 37 °C, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K\\approx-95</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Na}\\approx+67</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Cl</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>76</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Cl}\\approx-76</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV for typical values.","Ohmic current is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = g(V-E)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the sign of the driving force sets the direction of current.","Outward-positive current is a statement about charge: outward Cl⁻ current means Cl⁻ ions entering.","The resting potential is a permeability-weighted compromise between reversal potentials (GHK)."],"terms":["nernst","driving-force","ghk","reversal-potential"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"Extracellular K⁺ rises from 4 to 10 mM (as can happen during intense activity or ischaemia). Recompute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K</annotation></semantics></math></span> at 37 °C with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>140</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[K]_i = 140</annotation></semantics></math></span> mM.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>26.7</mn><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>140</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>26.7</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2.64</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>70.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K = 26.7\\ln(10/140) \\approx 26.7\\times(-2.64) \\approx -70.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, about 25 mV more depolarized than −95 mV. Because the resting potential tracks <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K</annotation></semantics></math></span>, cells depolarize and can become hyperexcitable or, if depolarized enough, inactivated."},{"kind":"calc","q":"A 0.5 nS Na⁺ conductance is open at −65 mV with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>66</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Na} = +66</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV. What is the current and its direction?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>66</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 0.5\\,(-65 - 66) = -65.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> pA: about 66 pA inward, depolarizing."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why does the Cl⁻ term in Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-1-2-3\">(1.2.3)</a> have <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Cl</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\text{Cl}]_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> in the numerator?","a":"For an anion (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=-1</annotation></semantics></math></span>) the Nernst ratio is inverted: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Cl</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Cl</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Cl</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Cl</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Cl</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Cl} = -(RT/F)\\ln([\\text{Cl}]_o/[\\text{Cl}]_i) = (RT/F)\\ln([\\text{Cl}]_i/[\\text{Cl}]_o)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. GHK keeps the same orientation for every permeant ion, so the anion’s inside concentration moves to the top."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Using GHK with only K⁺ and Na⁺ (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Cl</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\text{Cl}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>), what resting potential do you get for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\text{Na}/P_K = 0.05</annotation></semantics></math></span>? And for 0.5?","a":"0.05: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>26.7</mn><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>7.25</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>140</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>67.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">26.7\\ln\\frac{4+7.25}{140+0.6} \\approx -67.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV. 0.5: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>26.7</mn><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>72.5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>140</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">26.7\\ln\\frac{4+72.5}{140+6} \\approx -17</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV. A tenfold rise in relative Na⁺ permeability pulls the membrane far toward <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Na}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."}],"eqs":[{"num":"1.2.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_X = \\frac{RT}{zF}\\,\\ln\\frac{[X]_\\text{out}}{[X]_\\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"1.2.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_X = g_X\\,(V - E_X)</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"1.2.3","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rest</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></mfrac><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Na</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Cl</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Cl</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>K</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Na</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>Cl</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>Cl</mtext><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\text{rest} = \\frac{RT}{F}\\ln\\frac{P_K[K]_o + P_\\text{Na}[\\text{Na}]_o + P_\\text{Cl}[\\text{Cl}]_i}{P_K[K]_i + P_\\text{Na}[\\text{Na}]_i + P_\\text{Cl}[\\text{Cl}]_o}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/channels/hh-gates/","num":"1.3","obj":["Write a gate equation in both <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha/\\beta</annotation></semantics></math></span> form and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty/\\tau_x</annotation></semantics></math></span> form and convert between them","State the HH currents <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g_\\text{Na} m^3 h(V-E_\\text{Na})</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g_K n^4(V-E_K)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the role of each gate","Explain the spike sequence (activation, inactivation, delayed rectification) from gate time constants"],"body":"<p>Hodgkin and Huxley (1952) described the squid giant axon with three voltage-dependent <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"gating-variable\" tabindex=\"0\">gating variables</dfn>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span> (Na⁺ activation), <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span> (Na⁺ inactivation) and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> (K⁺ activation). Each is the fraction of gates in the permissive state, a number between 0 and 1, and obeys first-order kinetics with voltage-dependent opening rate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha(V)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and closing rate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta(V)</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-3-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>x</mi><mtext>  </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\alpha_x(V)\\,(1-x) - \\beta_x(V)\\,x \\;=\\; \\frac{x_\\infty(V) - x}{\\tau_x(V)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.3.1)</span></div><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-3-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty = \\frac{\\alpha_x}{\\alpha_x + \\beta_x}, \\qquad \\tau_x = \\frac{1}{\\alpha_x + \\beta_x}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.3.2)</span></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-1-3-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/hh-gates.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/hh-gates.svg\" alt=\"Figure 1.3.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 1.3.1.</b> The Hodgkin–Huxley gates (squid parameters, rest −65 mV, 6.3 °C). <b>A</b> Steady states <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty(V)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. <b>B</b> Time constants <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_x(V)</annotation></semantics></math></span> on a log scale: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span> is about ten times faster than <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</figcaption></figure><p>The second form is easier to read: at any fixed voltage, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span> relaxes exponentially toward its <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"steady-state-activation\" tabindex=\"0\">steady-state value</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty(V)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with time constant <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_x(V)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Both depend on voltage, so when <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> moves, the target and the speed of every gate move with it.</p><p>The ionic currents are products of gates:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-3-3\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>h</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{Na} = \\bar g_\\text{Na}\\,m^3 h\\,(V - E_\\text{Na}), \\qquad I_K = \\bar g_K\\,n^4\\,(V - E_K)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.3.3)</span></div><p>and the membrane equation is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\,dV/dt = I_\\text{in} - I_\\text{Na} - I_K - I_L</annotation></semantics></math></span>, a four-dimensional system in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(V, m, h, n)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The powers (3 and 4) were chosen to fit the sigmoidal delay of the conductance after a voltage step; they are often interpreted as independent subunits, but this is a fit, not a molecular measurement.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-1-3-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 1.3.1</p><h4>The gates at rest and at −20 mV</h4><p>With the standard squid parameters (rest −65 mV, 6.3 °C), the steady states and time constants are:</p><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty(-65)</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_x(-65)</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty(-20)</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_x(-20)</annotation></semantics></math></span></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>0.053</td><td>0.24 ms</td><td>0.88</td><td>0.38 ms</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>0.60</td><td>8.5 ms</td><td>0.009</td><td>1.2 ms</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>0.32</td><td>5.5 ms</td><td>0.84</td><td>2.3 ms</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Read the spike off this table. After a jump to −20 mV, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span> races toward 0.88 within a fraction of a millisecond (0.5 ms after the step it is already ≈ 0.66), so Na⁺ current switches on and drives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> up. Over the next millisecond or two, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span> falls toward zero (inactivation) and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> rises (delayed rectifier), together terminating the spike. Because <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> recover with time constants of several ms at rest, a second spike cannot be triggered right away: this is the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"refractory-period\" tabindex=\"0\">refractory period</dfn>.</p><p>At rest, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>0.318</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.010</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^4 = 0.318^4 \\approx 0.010</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so only about 1% of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g_K = 36</annotation></semantics></math></span> mS/cm² is open (≈ 0.37 mS/cm²), comparable to the leak. Most of the K⁺ machinery is held in reserve for the spike.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-1-3-2\"><a href=\"img/tb/hh-spike.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/hh-spike.svg\" alt=\"Figure 1.3.2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 1.3.2.</b> One spike computed from Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-1-3-3\">(1.3.3)</a> with exponential-Euler gate updates. <b>A</b> Voltage. <b>B</b> Gates: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span> jumps first, then <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span> falls and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> rises, ending the spike; both recover over several ms (refractoriness).</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 1.3</span> One Hodgkin–Huxley time step</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">for each gate x in (m, h, n):\n    a, b   = alpha_x(V), beta_x(V)\n    x_inf  = a / (a + b);  tau_x = 1 / (a + b)\n    x      = x_inf + (x - x_inf) * exp(-dt / tau_x)     # gates first, V frozen\ng_Na = gNa_bar * m**3 * h;   g_K = gK_bar * n**4\ng_tot = g_Na + g_K + gL\nV_inf = (g_Na*E_Na + g_K*E_K + gL*EL + I_ext) / g_tot\nV     = V_inf + (V - V_inf) * exp(-dt * g_tot / Cm)    # then V, gates frozen</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-aside\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Aside</p><h4>Temperature</h4><p>Gate rates are steeply temperature dependent. HH scaled them by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6.3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi = 3^{(T-6.3)/10}</annotation></semantics></math></span> (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{10}=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>). At 36 °C that is about 26 times faster than at 6.3 °C. Mammalian channel models carry their own temperature and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{10}</annotation></semantics></math></span>; mixing kinetics measured at room temperature with a simulation at 35 °C without correction is a common, silent error.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>h means two different things</h4><p>In HH, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span> is Na⁺ inactivation. In CA1 models, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the hyperpolarization-activated HCN current. They are unrelated; the guide writes the HCN current as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and its gate with a different letter wherever both appear.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Voltage sensors (S4 segments) and the Na⁺ inactivation gate</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Squid giant axon (original); same logic in every spiking cell</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Voltage-clamp steps, ion substitution, pharmacology (TTX, TEA)</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>First-order kinetics, products of gates, 4-D nonlinear ODE</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>HH model lab; Neuron workbench, HH squid preset</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Each gate obeys <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dx/dt = (x_\\infty(V)-x)/\\tau_x(V)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, equivalently <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha(1-x)-\\beta x</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{Na} = \\bar g_\\text{Na}m^3h(V-E_\\text{Na})</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_K = \\bar g_K n^4(V-E_K)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; HH is a 4-D system.","Fast <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span> starts the spike; slower <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span> (inactivation) and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> (delayed rectification) end it and cause refractoriness.","Rates scale with temperature (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{10}\\approx3</annotation></semantics></math></span> in HH)."],"terms":["gating-variable","steady-state-activation","refractory-period","inactivation","q10"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"At some voltage a gate has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms⁻¹ and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta = 1.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms⁻¹. Find <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Starting from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, what is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span> after 1 ms?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty = 0.5/2 = 0.25</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_x = 1/2 = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.216</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(1) = 0.25(1 - e^{-2}) \\approx 0.216</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Using the table in Worked example 1.3.1, estimate the open fraction of the Na⁺ conductance, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m^3h</annotation></semantics></math></span>, at rest.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0.053</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>0.60</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>8.9</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.053^3 \\times 0.60 \\approx 8.9\\times10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, i.e. about 0.01 mS/cm² of the 120 mS/cm² maximum: essentially closed."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why would blocking K⁺ channels (e.g. with TEA) not prevent the spike from ending, but make it much broader?","a":"Na⁺ inactivation (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\to0</annotation></semantics></math></span>) still shuts off the inward current, so the voltage eventually returns; but without <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span>-gated K⁺ current, repolarization relies on the small leak and is slow, so the spike becomes a long plateau."},{"kind":"calc","q":"By what factor are HH gate rates multiplied at 22 °C relative to 6.3 °C, with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{10} = 3</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>6.3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>1.57</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>5.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi = 3^{(22-6.3)/10} = 3^{1.57} \\approx 5.6</annotation></semantics></math></span>."}],"eqs":[{"num":"1.3.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>x</mi><mtext>  </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\alpha_x(V)\\,(1-x) - \\beta_x(V)\\,x \\;=\\; \\frac{x_\\infty(V) - x}{\\tau_x(V)}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"1.3.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty = \\frac{\\alpha_x}{\\alpha_x + \\beta_x}, \\qquad \\tau_x = \\frac{1}{\\alpha_x + \\beta_x}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"1.3.3","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>h</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{Na} = \\bar g_\\text{Na}\\,m^3 h\\,(V - E_\\text{Na}), \\qquad I_K = \\bar g_K\\,n^4\\,(V - E_K)</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/","num":"1.4","obj":["Write the compartment current balance with an axial term","Convert channel densities (mS/cm², µF/cm²) to totals (nS, pF) for a compartment of known area","List the main CA1 conductances beyond HH and where they sit on the cell"],"body":"<p>A CA1 pyramidal neuron is not a squid axon. It has a soma, a long apical dendrite running through stratum radiatum into stratum lacunosum-moleculare, and a basal tree in stratum oriens. Modelling it means two changes to the HH recipe: more kinds of channels, and spatial structure. In a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"compartment\" tabindex=\"0\">compartmental model</dfn> the cell is split into pieces, each obeying</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-4-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mtext>ion</mtext></munder><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mtext>ion</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo>∼</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_k\\,\\frac{dV_k}{dt} = I_{\\text{in},k} - \\sum_\\text{ion} I_{\\text{ion},k} + \\sum_{j\\,\\sim\\,k} g_{jk}\\,(V_j - V_k)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.4.1)</span></div><p>The last term is the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"axial-current\" tabindex=\"0\">axial current</dfn> arriving from neighbouring compartments <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math></span> through coupling conductances <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{jk}</annotation></semantics></math></span> (developed in <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"/topics/morphology/coupled-compartments/\">§2.2</a>). Each axial current appears with opposite sign in the two compartments it connects, so charge is conserved.</p><h3>Specific versus total quantities</h3><p>Channel models are published as <b>densities</b>: conductance per area (mS/cm² or S/cm²) and capacitance per area (µF/cm²). A compartment needs <b>totals</b>. The conversion is multiplication by area, and the most useful identity is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mtext>cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\ \\mu\\text{m}^2 = 10^{-8}\\ \\text{cm}^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-1-4-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>nS</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mtext>mS/cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>×</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>μ</mi><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>pF</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>μ</mi><msup><mtext>F/cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>×</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>μ</mi><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\,[\\text{nS}] = \\bar g\\,[\\text{mS/cm}^2]\\times A\\,[\\mu\\text{m}^2]\\times 10^{-2}, \\qquad C\\,[\\text{pF}] = c_m\\,[\\mu\\text{F/cm}^2]\\times A\\,[\\mu\\text{m}^2]\\times10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(1.4.2)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-1-4-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 1.4.1</p><h4>Densities to totals</h4><p>A dendritic compartment of 1000 µm² with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g_h = 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span> mS/cm² carries <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1 \\times 1000\\times10^{-2} = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF. A 20 µm spherical soma (≈ 1257 µm²) with the squid value <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g_K = 36</annotation></semantics></math></span> mS/cm² would carry about 450 nS of K⁺ conductance. Writing totals next to densities in your parameter table catches factor-of-100 errors before they become “biology”.</p></div><h3>The CA1 conductance set</h3><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Current</th><th>Channel family</th><th>Activates</th><th>Notable in CA1</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{Na}</annotation></semantics></math></span> (transient)</td><td>Nav1.x</td><td>depolarization, fast</td><td>spikes; backpropagation into dendrites</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>Kdr</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{Kdr}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>Kv2 (mainly Kv2.1)</td><td>depolarization, delayed</td><td>repolarization</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_A</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>Kv4.2</td><td>depolarization, fast; inactivates</td><td>density rises with distance along the apical dendrite (Hoffman et al. 1997)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>HCN1 (with HCN2)</td><td>hyperpolarization, slow</td><td>density rises several-fold toward distal dendrites (Magee 1998)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>Kv7/KCNQ</td><td>depolarization (≈ −60 mV and up), slow</td><td>spike-frequency adaptation; theta resonance near threshold (Hu et al. 2002)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>NaP</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{NaP}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>Nav (persistent)</td><td>depolarization, non-inactivating</td><td>amplifies subthreshold responses and resonance</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>Ca</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{Ca}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>Cav1, Cav2, Cav3</td><td>depolarization</td><td>dendritic Ca²⁺ spikes, plasticity</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>SK</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{SK}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>BK</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{BK}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>K<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>Ca</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_\\text{Ca}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>intracellular Ca²⁺</td><td>afterhyperpolarizations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Two of these gradients matter throughout the book. Distal <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> shortens dendritic EPSPs so that temporal summation at the soma becomes nearly independent of where inputs arrive (Magee 1999), and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> together with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives CA1 cells their theta-range resonance (Chapter 5).</p><h3>A real CA1 pyramidal cell, measured</h3><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-1-4-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/morph-ca1-layers.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/morph-ca1-layers.svg\" alt=\"Figure 1.4.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 1.4.1.</b> A real CA1 pyramidal cell (mpg141209_A; Migliore et al. 2018, ModelDB 244688). <b>A</b> The full reconstruction drawn in its own traced layer boundaries; apical dendrites pink, basal blue, axon grey. <b>B</b> The soma and proximal tree drawn at true diameter: the apical trunk is several times thicker than the obliques that leave it.</figcaption></figure><p>The reconstruction in <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#fig-1-4-1\">Figure 1.4.1</a> (cell mpg141209_A from Migliore et al. 2018, traced in Neurolucida with its layer boundaries) makes the abstract compartment concrete. Summing the frustum area of every traced segment gives 8.9 mm of dendrite (4.1 mm basal, 4.9 mm apical) with 22,214 µm² of membrane, 78 branch points and 84 tips. The soma contributes only about 462 µm², roughly 2% of the total. At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m = 1\\ \\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>F/cm² the traced membrane alone holds about 227 pF; spines, which are not traced, would add substantially more (Section 2.3).</p><p>The cell spans every layer of CA1. Along its own traced boundaries, stratum oriens is about 147 µm thick, the pyramidal layer about 60 µm, radiatum about 227 µm and lacunosum-moleculare about 204 µm, and the longest apical path reaches 787 µm from the soma. Each layer receives a different input (oriens and radiatum: CA3 Schaffer collaterals; lacunosum-moleculare: entorhinal cortex layer III), so a compartmental model of this cell inherits a map of where each pathway arrives.</p><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-1-4-2\"><a href=\"img/tb/ca1-gradients.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/ca1-gradients.svg\" alt=\"Figure 1.4.2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 1.4.2.</b> A CA1 pyramidal cell and two documented density gradients along its apical dendrite. The straight lines show only the direction and approximate size of the change (Iₕ ≈ 6-fold over ≈ 350 µm, Magee 1998; A-type K⁺ rising with distance, Hoffman et al. 1997), not fitted profiles.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 1.4</span> Multi-compartment update</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">for k in compartments:                       # densities -&gt; totals once, at setup\n    g[k][ion] = gbar[k][ion] * area[k]         # mS/cm2 * um2 * 1e-2 = nS\nfor each step:\n    for k in compartments:\n        I_ion = sum(g[k][ion] * gates(k, ion) * (V[k] - E[ion]) for ion in channels)\n        I_ax  = sum(g_c[k][j] * (V[j] - V[k]) for j in neighbours(k))\n        dV[k] = (I_in[k] - I_ion + I_ax) / C[k]\n    V += dt * dV                                # or an implicit / staggered scheme\n    assert abs(sum(all axial currents)) &lt; tol   # charge conservation check</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Same name, different density convention</h4><p>Some simulators (NEURON) express <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g</annotation></semantics></math></span> in S/cm² and current densities in mA/cm²; others use totals. Brian2 accepts any units but will not guess which you meant. When reproducing a paper, write down for every parameter whether it is specific or total, and the temperature it was measured at.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Dendritic HCN1, Kv4.2, Kv7, Nav and Cav channels with location-dependent density</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1: stratum oriens, pyramidale, radiatum, lacunosum-moleculare</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Dendritic patch clamp, cell-attached channel counts</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Coupled compartmental ODEs with axial terms</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>NEURON or Brian2 multicompartment models; Morphology workbench</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Compartments obey current balance plus axial currents <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum g_{jk}(V_j - V_k)</annotation></semantics></math></span> from neighbours.","Published densities become totals by multiplying by area: mS/cm² × µm² × 10⁻² = nS.","CA1 cells add <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_A</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>NaP</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{NaP}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, Ca²⁺ and Ca²⁺-activated K⁺ currents to HH, several with location-dependent densities."],"terms":["compartment","axial-current","hcn","m-current"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"A cylindrical dendritic segment is 2 µm in diameter and 100 µm long. What are its membrane area, capacitance (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m = 1\\ \\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>F/cm²) and leak conductance for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20,000</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mtext>cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m = 20{,}000\\ \\Omega\\,\\text{cm}^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"Area <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>628</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= \\pi d L = \\pi\\times2\\times100 \\approx 628</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm². <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>6.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C \\approx 6.3</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m = 1/R_m = 50\\ \\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>S/cm² <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 0.05</annotation></semantics></math></span> mS/cm², so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>628</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L = 0.05\\times628\\times10^{-2} \\approx 0.31</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS. Check: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = R_m c_m = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.31</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6.3/0.31 \\approx 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms. ✓"},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why is it essential that the axial term in Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-1-4-1\">(1.4.1)</a> appears with opposite signs in the two compartments it couples?","a":"Current leaving one compartment must enter the other; otherwise the model creates or destroys charge, and the summed membrane currents would no longer balance the injected current. It is a useful numerical test: sum all axial currents in the model and check that they add to zero."},{"kind":"concept","q":"A paper gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g_h = 0.0001</annotation></semantics></math></span> S/cm² at the soma. Another gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_h = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS for a 1000 µm² soma. Are these the same?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.0001</annotation></semantics></math></span> S/cm² <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span> mS/cm²; × 1000 µm² × 10⁻² = 1 nS. Yes, identical, once units and area are made explicit."}],"eqs":[{"num":"1.4.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mtext>in</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mtext>ion</mtext></munder><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mtext>ion</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo>∼</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_k\\,\\frac{dV_k}{dt} = I_{\\text{in},k} - \\sum_\\text{ion} I_{\\text{ion},k} + \\sum_{j\\,\\sim\\,k} g_{jk}\\,(V_j - V_k)</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"1.4.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>nS</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mtext>mS/cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>×</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>μ</mi><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>pF</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>μ</mi><msup><mtext>F/cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>×</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>μ</mi><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\,[\\text{nS}] = \\bar g\\,[\\text{mS/cm}^2]\\times A\\,[\\mu\\text{m}^2]\\times 10^{-2}, \\qquad C\\,[\\text{pF}] = c_m\\,[\\mu\\text{F/cm}^2]\\times A\\,[\\mu\\text{m}^2]\\times10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]}]},{"n":2,"part":"I","pathway":6,"title":"Dendrites and cables","intro":["A CA1 pyramidal neuron receives tens of thousands of synapses spread over several hundred micrometres of dendrite. Whether an input far out in stratum lacunosum-moleculare matters at the soma depends on how voltage spreads through thin, leaky tubes. Cable theory answers that question for passive dendrites, and compartmental models extend it to any shape and any set of channels.","This chapter moves from a continuous cable to two coupled compartments, then asks how area and channel density scale a response, and finally separates the local response at an input site from what arrives at the soma."],"obj":["Derive the length constant and use it to estimate steady-state attenuation","Write and solve the passive two-compartment model","Predict how changing area at fixed density changes <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and response size","Distinguish input impedance, transfer impedance and voltage attenuation"],"prereq":["Chapter 1 (current balance, time constant)","Exponentials and hyperbolic functions"],"sections":[{"route":"/topics/morphology/electrotonic-distance/","num":"2.1","obj":["State the passive cable equation and the meaning of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>","Compute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and diameter, and the steady attenuation at a given distance","Explain why fast signals attenuate more than slow ones"],"body":"<p>Treat a dendrite as a uniform cylinder of diameter <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math></span> with specific membrane resistance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> (Ω·cm²), specific capacitance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"axial-resistivity\" tabindex=\"0\">axial (intracellular) resistivity</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> (Ω·cm). Current balance on a thin slice, in the limit of zero thickness, gives the passive <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"cable-equation\" tabindex=\"0\">cable equation</dfn> for the deflection <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></span> from rest:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-2-1-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mtext>  </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>τ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda^2\\,\\frac{\\partial^2 V}{\\partial x^2} \\;=\\; \\tau\\,\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial t} + V, \\qquad \\lambda = \\sqrt{\\frac{R_m\\,d}{4R_i}}, \\quad \\tau = R_m c_m</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(2.1.1)</span></div><p>The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"length-constant\" tabindex=\"0\">length constant</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> compares how easily current leaves through the membrane with how easily it flows along the core. It grows with the <b>square root</b> of diameter: a fourfold thicker dendrite has twice the length constant. Distances divided by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> are called <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"electrotonic-distance\" tabindex=\"0\">electrotonic distances</dfn>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X = x/\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><p>For a steady input at one end of a semi-infinite cable the solution is a pure exponential:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-2-1-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x) = V_0\\,e^{-x/\\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(2.1.2)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-2-1-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 2.1.1</p><h4>How far does a dendrite reach?</h4><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20,000</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mtext>cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m = 20{,}000\\ \\Omega\\,\\text{cm}^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i = 150\\ \\Omega\\,\\text{cm}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math></span> cm): <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>20000</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>150</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>0.00667</mn></msqrt><mtext> cm</mtext><mo>≈</mo><mn>816</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = \\sqrt{20000\\times2\\times10^{-4}/(4\\times150)} = \\sqrt{0.00667}\\ \\text{cm} \\approx 816</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm. A steady deflection therefore falls to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>400</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>816</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.61</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-400/816}\\approx 0.61</annotation></semantics></math></span> of its value 400 µm away. Halving the diameter to 0.5 µm gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>408</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\approx 408</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm; fine oblique branches are electrically much “longer” than the main apical trunk.</p></div><h3>Finite branches and sealed ends</h3><p>Real branches end. If a cable of electrotonic length <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L = \\ell/\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> has a sealed end (no current leaves the tip), the steady profile is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(X) = V_0\\cosh(L - X)/\\cosh L</annotation></semantics></math></span>. At the tip (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=L=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>) this gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>cosh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\cosh 1 \\approx 0.65</annotation></semantics></math></span> rather than <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.37</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-1}\\approx0.37</annotation></semantics></math></span>: current that cannot escape through the end piles up and raises the voltage. Boundary conditions matter as much as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><h3>Frequency matters</h3><p>For a sinusoidal input of angular frequency <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the membrane capacitance offers an extra path to ground and the effective length constant shrinks:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-2-1-3\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>AC</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msqrt><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_\\text{AC} = \\lambda\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+\\sqrt{1+(\\omega\\tau)^2}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(2.1.3)</span></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-2-1-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/cable-profiles.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/cable-profiles.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2.1.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 2.1.1.</b> <b>A</b> Steady profiles <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-x/\\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math></span> for three diameters (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20,000</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m = 20{,}000\\ \\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>·cm², <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i = 150\\ \\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>·cm): <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\propto\\sqrt d</annotation></semantics></math></span>. <b>B</b> Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-2-1-3\">(2.1.3)</a>: the effective length constant shrinks with frequency, much more in the gamma than the theta band.</figcaption></figure><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>AC</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_\\text{AC}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is 88% of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> at 10 Hz but only 38% at 100 Hz. Dendrites are low-pass filters: the fast rising phase of a distal EPSP is attenuated far more than its slow tail, which is why distal EPSPs look smaller and slower at the soma.</p><h3>Electrotonic distance on a real tree</h3><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-2-1-2\"><a href=\"img/tb/morph-electrotonic.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/morph-electrotonic.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2.1.2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 2.1.2.</b> <b>A</b> mpg141209_A coloured by electrotonic distance from the soma, accumulated segment by segment with each segment’s own diameter (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20,000</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m = 20{,}000\\ \\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>·cm², <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i = 150\\ \\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>·cm). <b>B</b> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span> against path distance for every segment, with uniform 2 µm and 0.6 µm cables for comparison: thin basal and oblique branches gain electrotonic distance fastest.</figcaption></figure><p>In a real dendrite the diameter changes from point to point, so the electrotonic distance is accumulated segment by segment, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X = \\int dx/\\lambda(d(x))</annotation></semantics></math></span>. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20,000</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m = 20{,}000\\ \\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>·cm² and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i = 150\\ \\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>·cm, the tip of the apical tuft of mpg141209_A lies at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.26</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X \\approx 1.26</annotation></semantics></math></span> (787 µm of path), while the farthest basal tips reach only <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.43</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X \\approx 0.43</annotation></semantics></math></span> (<a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#fig-2-1-2\">Figure 2.1.2</a>). Taken naively, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-X} \\approx 0.28</annotation></semantics></math></span> would be the steady attenuation from tuft to soma; the true value is smaller, because every branch point along the way adds load, and larger in an active dendrite with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> open, which lowers <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><p>Rall (1959) showed that a branched tree behaves like a single equivalent cylinder if, at every branch point, the parent diameter raised to the 3/2 power equals the sum of the children’s, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_p^{3/2} = \\sum d_c^{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math></span> (and all tips end at the same electrotonic distance). Measured on the traced branch points of three CA1 cells, the ratio <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum d_c^{3/2}/d_p^{3/2}</annotation></semantics></math></span> has medians of 1.10, 1.03 and 1.27 (<a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#fig-2-1-3\">Figure 2.1.3</a>): close to 1 on average, but individual branch points scatter from below 0.5 to above 2. The equivalent cylinder is a useful idealization, not a description of these cells. In the n5038804 reconstruction most dendritic diameters were recorded at a single value (1.36 µm), which pushes its median to 1.66: a reminder that diameters are the least reliable part of a tracing and the part that matters most for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-2-1-3\"><a href=\"img/tb/morph-rall.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/morph-rall.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2.1.3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 2.1.3.</b> <b>A</b> Rall’s 3/2-power ratio at every traced dendritic branch point of three CA1 cells; 1 is the equivalent-cylinder condition. <b>B</b> Segment diameter against path distance in mpg141209_A (log scale).</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 2.1</span> Length constant and attenuation from morphology</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">def length_constant(Rm, Ri, d):              # ohm*cm2, ohm*cm, cm\n    return sqrt(Rm * d / (4 * Ri))            # cm\nlam = length_constant(20000, 150, 2e-4)\nX   = distance / lam\nV_semi_infinite = V0 * exp(-X)\nV_sealed_end    = V0 * cosh(L - X) / cosh(L)  # finite branch, L = length/lam\nlam_ac = lam * sqrt(2 / (1 + sqrt(1 + (2*pi*f*tau)**2)))</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>λ is not a fixed property of a dendrite</h4><p><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> is not constant in CA1: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and other open channels lower the effective <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m</annotation></semantics></math></span>, especially distally where HCN density is high. In an active dendrite, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> depends on voltage and on synaptic activity (synaptic conductance also lowers <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m</annotation></semantics></math></span>). Use Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-2-1-1\">(2.1.1)</a> as a reference point, then measure in the model.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Thin, leaky dendrites; cytoplasm resistivity</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1 apical trunk versus thin oblique and tuft branches</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Dual soma–dendrite recordings, voltage imaging</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Cable PDE, exponential and hyperbolic solutions</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>NEURON d_lambda rule; Branching morphology lab</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["The passive cable equation is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda^2 V_{xx} = \\tau V_t + V</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = \\sqrt{R_m d/4R_i}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = R_mc_m</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Steady attenuation in a semi-infinite cable is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-x/\\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math></span>; sealed ends reduce attenuation.","<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda \\propto \\sqrt d</annotation></semantics></math></span>; higher frequencies see a shorter <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>AC</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_\\text{AC}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so dendrites low-pass filter."],"terms":["cable-equation","length-constant","electrotonic-distance","axial-resistivity"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"Compute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>30,000</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mtext>cm</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m = 30{,}000\\ \\Omega\\,\\text{cm}^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i = 200\\ \\Omega\\,\\text{cm}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>30000</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>200</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mtext> cm</mtext><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>0.00375</mn></msqrt><mtext> cm</mtext><mo>≈</mo><mn>612</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = \\sqrt{30000\\times10^{-4}/(4\\times200)}\\ \\text{cm} = \\sqrt{0.00375}\\ \\text{cm}\\approx 612</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm."},{"kind":"calc","q":"For the 816 µm cable in the worked example, how far from the input must you go before a steady deflection has fallen to half?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>566</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-x/\\lambda} = 0.5 \\Rightarrow x = \\lambda\\ln 2 \\approx 566</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Two dendrites have the same physical length but diameters of 1 and 4 µm. Which is electrotonically longer, and by what factor?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\propto\\sqrt d</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so the 4 µm dendrite has twice the length constant. Electrotonic length <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L = \\ell/\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> is therefore twice as large for the 1 µm dendrite."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Using Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-2-1-3\">(2.1.3)</a> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, by what fraction is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> reduced at 8 Hz (theta)?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.02</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\tau = 2\\pi\\times8\\times0.02 \\approx 1.005</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1.01</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.418</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{1+1.01}\\approx1.418</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>AC</mtext></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2.418</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.91</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_\\text{AC}/\\lambda = \\sqrt{2/2.418}\\approx0.91</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Theta-band signals see about 91% of the DC length constant."}],"eqs":[{"num":"2.1.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mtext>  </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>τ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda^2\\,\\frac{\\partial^2 V}{\\partial x^2} \\;=\\; \\tau\\,\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial t} + V, \\qquad \\lambda = \\sqrt{\\frac{R_m\\,d}{4R_i}}, \\quad \\tau = R_m c_m</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"2.1.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(x) = V_0\\,e^{-x/\\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"2.1.3","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>AC</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msqrt><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_\\text{AC} = \\lambda\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1+\\sqrt{1+(\\omega\\tau)^2}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/morphology/coupled-compartments/","num":"2.2","obj":["Write the passive two-compartment equations and the coupling conductance from geometry","Solve for the steady soma and dendrite voltages and check charge conservation","Identify the two time constants of the coupled system"],"body":"<p>Discretize the cable into pieces short compared with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> and each becomes a single-voltage compartment. The simplest useful case is two: a soma (s) and a dendrite (d), coupled by an axial conductance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with a current <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_d</annotation></semantics></math></span> injected into the dendrite.</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-2-2-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mtable rowspacing=\"0.25em\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned} C\\,\\dot V_s &amp;= -g_s V_s + g_c\\,(V_d - V_s)\\\\ C\\,\\dot V_d &amp;= -g_d V_d + g_c\\,(V_s - V_d) + I_d \\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(2.2.1)</span></div><p>Voltages are deflections from rest. The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"coupling-conductance\" tabindex=\"0\">coupling conductance</dfn> follows from the cylinder that joins the compartment centres: its resistance is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i\\ell/(\\pi d^2/4)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-2-2-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c = \\frac{\\pi d^2}{4R_i\\,\\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(2.2.2)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-2-2-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 2.2.1</p><h4>Soma and one dendrite</h4><p>For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i = 150\\ \\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>·cm, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>150</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> S</mtext><mo>≈</mo><mn>20.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c = \\pi(10^{-4})^2/(150\\times10^{-2})\\ \\text{S} \\approx 20.9</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS. Take <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_s = g_d = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_d = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> pA.</p><p>Setting the derivatives to zero and solving the 2×2 linear system:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-2-2-3\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s = \\frac{g_c\\,I_d}{g_sg_d + g_c(g_s+g_d)},\\qquad V_d = \\frac{(g_s+g_c)\\,I_d}{g_sg_d + g_c(g_s+g_d)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(2.2.3)</span></div><p>The denominator is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>20.9</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>234</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">25 + 20.9\\times10 \\approx 234</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS², giving <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>8.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s\\approx 8.9</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>11.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_d\\approx 11.1</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV. Check conservation: the dendrite loses <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>55</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_dV_d \\approx 55</annotation></semantics></math></span> pA through its leak and sends <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>45</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c(V_d-V_s)\\approx 45</annotation></semantics></math></span> pA to the soma, which leaks exactly those 45 pA (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_sV_s</annotation></semantics></math></span>). Total 100 pA. ✓</p><p>The coupled system has two time constants, the eigenvalues of its 2×2 matrix: 20 ms (both compartments charging together) and about 2.1 ms (charge redistributing between them). Fast equalization, slow charging: the same separation that appears in a full cable.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-2-2-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/two-comp-trace.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/two-comp-trace.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2.2.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 2.2.1.</b> The worked example simulated from Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-2-2-1\">(2.2.1)</a>. <b>A</b> A 100 pA step into the dendrite: both compartments charge with the slow 20 ms mode, settling at 11.1 and 8.9 mV. <b>B</b> Their difference rises within a few ms, the fast equalizing mode.</figcaption></figure><p>Two limits are worth memorising. As <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c\\to\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span> the compartments merge and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s=V_d=I_d/(g_s+g_d) = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV. As <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c\\to0</annotation></semantics></math></span> the dendrite is isolated, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_d = I_d/g_d = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV and the soma never moves.</p><h3>How many compartments does a real cell need?</h3><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-2-2-2\"><a href=\"img/tb/morph-dlambda.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/morph-dlambda.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2.2.2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 2.2.2.</b> <b>A</b> Segments per section for mpg141209_A under NEURON’s d_lambda rule (d_lambda = 0.1 at 100 Hz). <b>B</b> Total dendritic compartments for four CA1 reconstructions at three d_lambda values; the black bars mark one compartment per section.</figcaption></figure><p>NEURON’s d_lambda rule chooses the number of segments in each section so that none is longer than a fraction d_lambda of the AC length constant at 100 Hz, using an odd count so that the section centre is a node. Applied to the 162 dendritic sections of mpg141209_A (with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i = 150\\ \\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>·cm, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m = 1\\ \\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>F/cm²), d_lambda = 0.3 gives 282 compartments, the common default of 0.1 gives 574, and 0.05 gives 1058 (<a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#fig-2-2-2\">Figure 2.2.2</a>). Long thin obliques and tuft branches need the most segments; short thick trunk pieces need one. The two-compartment model of this section is the far end of the same spectrum: useful for intuition and for fitting, but the d_lambda count is what a quantitative model of this cell should start from.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 2.2</span> Two-compartment steady state and modes</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">gc = pi * d**2 / (4 * Ri * ell)               # coupling from geometry\nG  = [[gs + gc, -gc],\n      [-gc,     gd + gc]]                     # conductance matrix\nV_ss = solve(G, [0, I_d])                    # steady soma and dendrite voltages\ntaus = -1 / eigenvalues(-G / C)              # slow (charging) and fast (equalizing) modes\ncheck: gs*V_ss[0] + gd*V_ss[1] == I_d        # all injected current leaks somewhere</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>How many compartments?</h4><p>A compartment must be short enough that voltage is nearly uniform within it. A common rule is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>AC</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell &lt; 0.1\\,\\lambda_\\text{AC}</annotation></semantics></math></span> at the highest frequency of interest (NEURON’s “d_lambda” rule uses 100 Hz). Too few compartments shift both amplitudes and delays; the morphology pages show this in <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"/topics/morphology/local-versus-soma/\">§2.4</a> and the discretization lab.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Soma and dendrite joined by a cytoplasmic core</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Two-compartment CA1 models (soma + apical dendrite)</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Somatic and dendritic responses to the same injection</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>2×2 linear system, eigenvalues as charging modes</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Neuron workbench, CA1 two-compartment preset</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Two compartments: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\dot V_s = -g_sV_s + g_c(V_d-V_s)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\dot V_d = -g_dV_d + g_c(V_s - V_d) + I_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c = \\pi d^2/(4R_i\\ell)</annotation></semantics></math></span> from the joining cylinder.","Steady state: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s = g_cI_d/D</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_d = (g_s+g_c)I_d/D</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D = g_sg_d + g_c(g_s+g_d)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Coupled compartments have a slow charging mode and a fast equalizing mode."],"terms":["coupling-conductance","compartment","axial-current"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_s=g_d=5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, inject 100 pA into the dendrite. Find <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_d</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the attenuation <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s/V_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>125</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D = 25 + 100 = 125</annotation></semantics></math></span>. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>125</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s = 1000/125 = 8</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1500</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>125</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_d = 1500/125 = 12</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s/V_d = 2/3</annotation></semantics></math></span>. In general <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s/V_d = g_c/(g_s + g_c)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Find <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c</annotation></semantics></math></span> for a 1 µm diameter, 50 µm long connection with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i = 150\\ \\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>·cm.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>150</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> S</mtext><mo>≈</mo><mn>10.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c = \\pi(0.5\\times10^{-4})^2/(150\\times50\\times10^{-4})\\ \\text{S} \\approx 10.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS. Half the diameter quarters the area; half the length doubles <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c</annotation></semantics></math></span>; net factor ½ relative to the worked example."},{"kind":"derive","q":"Derive Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-2-2-3\">(2.2.3)</a> from Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-2-2-1\">(2.2.1)</a>.","a":"At steady state: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(g_s+g_c)V_s = g_cV_d</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(g_d+g_c)V_d - g_cV_s = I_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Substitute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s = g_cV_d/(g_s+g_c)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_d[(g_d+g_c)(g_s+g_c) - g_c^2] = (g_s+g_c)I_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The bracket is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_sg_d + g_c(g_s+g_d)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Then <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s</annotation></semantics></math></span> follows."}],"eqs":[{"num":"2.2.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mtable rowspacing=\"0.25em\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned} C\\,\\dot V_s &amp;= -g_s V_s + g_c\\,(V_d - V_s)\\\\ C\\,\\dot V_d &amp;= -g_d V_d + g_c\\,(V_s - V_d) + I_d \\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"2.2.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c = \\frac{\\pi d^2}{4R_i\\,\\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"2.2.3","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s = \\frac{g_c\\,I_d}{g_sg_d + g_c(g_s+g_d)},\\qquad V_d = \\frac{(g_s+g_c)\\,I_d}{g_sg_d + g_c(g_s+g_d)}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/morphology/area-and-density/","num":"2.3","obj":["Predict the effect of area at fixed densities on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span>","Distinguish experiments that fix total current from those that fix current density","Explain how dendritic spines are included in reduced models"],"body":"<p>Most channel and membrane parameters are measured or reported as densities. A model cell’s totals are densities times area. Scaling area by a factor <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math></span> at fixed densities scales both <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span> and every conductance by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-2-3-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>C</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>g</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></mfrac><mtext> unchanged</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>→</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C \\to aC,\\quad g \\to a g \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tau = \\frac{C}{g}\\ \\text{unchanged},\\qquad R_\\text{in}\\to\\frac{R_\\text{in}}{a}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(2.3.1)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-2-3-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 2.3.1</p><h4>Same cell, bigger membrane</h4><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Area factor</th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span> (pF)</th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L</annotation></semantics></math></span> (nS)</th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> (ms)</th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span> (MΩ)</th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta V</annotation></semantics></math></span> for 50 pA</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>100</td><td>5</td><td>20</td><td>200</td><td>10 mV</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>200</td><td>10</td><td>20</td><td>100</td><td>5 mV</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>400</td><td>20</td><td>20</td><td>50</td><td>2.5 mV</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The time course is identical; only the amplitude for a fixed <i>total</i> current falls. If instead you inject a fixed current <i>density</i> (current per area), the deflection is the same at every size. Which comparison is right depends on the biology: a synapse delivers a fixed conductance, not a fixed density.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-2-3-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/area-scaling.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/area-scaling.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2.3.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 2.3.1.</b> Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-2-3-1\">(2.3.1)</a> at fixed densities and a fixed 50 pA current: identical time course, amplitude inversely proportional to area.</figcaption></figure><p>This is the basis of the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"size-principle\" tabindex=\"0\">size principle</dfn> for motor neurons (Henneman): small motor neurons have high input resistance, reach threshold with less synaptic current and are recruited first.</p><h3>Spines</h3><p>Dendritic spines can roughly double the membrane area of spiny CA1 dendrites. Rather than model every spine, reduced models multiply <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> and all membrane conductances on spiny compartments by a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"spine-factor\" tabindex=\"0\">spine factor</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span> (often 1.5–2), leaving axial properties unchanged. The time constant is preserved but <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> falls by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>F</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt F</annotation></semantics></math></span>, because more membrane leaks per unit length.</p><h3>Real cells: size, shape and membrane</h3><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-2-3-2\"><a href=\"img/tb/morph-cell-types.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/morph-cell-types.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2.3.2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 2.3.2.</b> Principal cells and interneurons of the hippocampal circuit at one scale, from the reconstructions used in the guide (granule cell: Claiborne archive via Beining et al. 2017; CA3b: Hemond et al. 2008; CA1: Jarsky et al. 2005; O-LM: Sekulic et al. 2015; PV basket: Tukker et al. 2013 via Tzilivaki et al. 2019). Interneuron axons (grey) are drawn thin; principal-cell axons are omitted.</figcaption></figure><p>The same measurements on cells from each stage of the hippocampal circuit (<a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#fig-2-3-2\">Figure 2.3.2</a>) show how differently they are built. The table lists traced dendrites only (axons excluded), with capacitance at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m = 1\\ \\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>F/cm² before any spine correction:</p><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cell</th><th>Region</th><th>Dendrite (mm)</th><th>Area (µm²)</th><th>C (pF)</th><th>Branch points</th><th>Max path (µm)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dentate granule cell</td><td>DG</td><td>4.7</td><td>25,211</td><td>252</td><td>18</td><td>506</td></tr><tr><td>CA3b pyramidal</td><td>CA3</td><td>12.2</td><td>29,961</td><td>300</td><td>63</td><td>710</td></tr><tr><td>CA1 pyramidal mpg141209_A</td><td>CA1</td><td>8.9</td><td>22,214</td><td>222</td><td>78</td><td>787</td></tr><tr><td>CA1 pyramidal mpg141208_B</td><td>CA1</td><td>7.3</td><td>19,109</td><td>191</td><td>68</td><td>739</td></tr><tr><td>CA1 pyramidal (Jarsky 2005)</td><td>CA1</td><td>9.7</td><td>20,223</td><td>202</td><td>74</td><td>837</td></tr><tr><td>O-LM interneuron</td><td>CA1</td><td>1.3</td><td>6,397</td><td>64</td><td>19</td><td>165</td></tr><tr><td>PV basket cell</td><td>CA3</td><td>9.9</td><td>24,862</td><td>249</td><td>105</td><td>798</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Two lessons follow. First, totals differ several-fold between cells of the same type, so a single “CA1 cell” value of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span> hides real variation. Second, most membrane is far from the soma: in mpg141209_A, 32% of the traced dendritic area lies more than 300 µm along the path (<a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#fig-2-3-3\">Figure 2.3.3</a>B). Capacitance, leak and channels are therefore mostly distal, which is why distal <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> densities shape the somatic response so strongly. Diameter tracing limits these numbers: the n5038804 cell, with its single recorded dendritic diameter, has more than twice the area of the others.</p><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-2-3-3\"><a href=\"img/tb/morph-sholl.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/morph-sholl.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2.3.3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 2.3.3.</b> <b>A</b> Sholl analysis: dendritic crossings of spheres centred on the soma (10 µm steps), basal to the left, apical to the right, for three CA1 cells. <b>B</b> Fraction of the traced dendritic membrane lying beyond each path distance.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 2.3</span> Scale a morphology consistently</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">for compartment in model:\n    area = compartment.area * (spine_factor if compartment.spiny else 1)\n    C    = cm * area\n    for ion in channels: g[ion] = gbar[ion] * area\ndecide and record ONE convention for inputs:\n    fixed total current   -&gt; response shrinks as 1 / area\n    fixed current density -&gt; I = J * area, response unchanged\n    synaptic conductance  -&gt; biology: fixed g per synapse</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Scaling one thing at a time</h4><p>Changing diameter is not the same as changing area: diameter also changes <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>. When comparing morphologies, decide explicitly what is held fixed (densities, totals, injected current or conductance) and report it.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Cell size, spines, channel density</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Spiny CA1 dendrites; motor neuron pools (size principle)</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Capacitance from transients; reconstructions with spine counts</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Scaling of totals with area; invariance of τ</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Spine factor on c_m and ḡ in reconstructed models</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["At fixed densities, area scales <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span> together: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> is unchanged, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>∝</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}\\propto 1/A</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Fixed total current gives smaller responses in bigger cells; fixed current density does not.","Spines are folded in with a spine factor on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> and membrane conductances, which shortens <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>F</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt F</annotation></semantics></math></span>."],"terms":["size-principle","spine-factor","input-resistance"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"A model has 20,000 µm² of membrane, half of it spiny, with spine factor 2 on the spiny half. What is total capacitance at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m = 1\\ \\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>F/cm²?","a":"Effective area <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10,000</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>10,000</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>30,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 10{,}000 + 2\\times10{,}000 = 30{,}000</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm², so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 300</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF. (Without spines, 200 pF.)"},{"kind":"concept","q":"A colleague reports that a larger cell type “responds more slowly” to current steps. Give two explanations, only one of which is about size.","a":"Size alone does not change <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> at fixed densities, so either (1) the membrane properties differ (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> lower, e.g. less <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span>), or (2) the larger cell has more extended dendrites, adding slow charge-redistribution through the cable, which a single-compartment <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> hides."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Spine factor <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is applied to a dendrite with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>800</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = 800</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm. What is its new length constant?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∝</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda \\propto 1/\\sqrt{g_m}</annotation></semantics></math></span> at fixed <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math></span>; doubling <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>800</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mn>566</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">800/\\sqrt2\\approx566</annotation></semantics></math></span> µm."}],"eqs":[{"num":"2.3.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>C</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>g</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>g</mi></mfrac><mtext> unchanged</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>→</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C \\to aC,\\quad g \\to a g \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\tau = \\frac{C}{g}\\ \\text{unchanged},\\qquad R_\\text{in}\\to\\frac{R_\\text{in}}{a}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/morphology/local-versus-soma/","num":"2.4","obj":["Define input impedance and transfer impedance","Show that transfer impedance is symmetric while voltage attenuation is not","Explain how CA1 compensates for distance (synaptic scaling and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span>)"],"body":"<p>A dendritic input produces two voltages that are easy to confuse: the <b>local</b> response at the input site and the <b>transferred</b> response at the soma. Linear passive systems describe both with impedances. The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"input-impedance\" tabindex=\"0\">input impedance</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{dd}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the local voltage per unit current injected at the same site; the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"transfer-impedance\" tabindex=\"0\">transfer impedance</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{sd}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the somatic voltage per unit current injected in the dendrite. At steady state these are resistances; for sinusoids they are complex.</p><p>From Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"/topics/morphology/coupled-compartments/\">(2.2.3)</a>, with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D = g_sg_d + g_c(g_s+g_d)</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-2-4-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mi>D</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mi>D</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mi>D</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{dd} = \\frac{g_s+g_c}{D},\\qquad Z_{ss} = \\frac{g_d+g_c}{D},\\qquad Z_{sd} = Z_{ds} = \\frac{g_c}{D}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(2.4.1)</span></div><p>The transfer impedance is the same in both directions. This is the <b>reciprocity theorem</b> for linear passive networks: current into the dendrite produces the same somatic voltage as the same current into the soma produces in the dendrite. Voltage <b>attenuation</b>, however, is the ratio of transfer to input impedance, and input impedances differ:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-2-4-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mfrac><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">∣</mo><mrow><mtext>input at </mtext><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mfrac><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">∣</mo><mrow><mtext>input at </mtext><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{V_s}{V_d}\\Big|_{\\text{input at }d} = \\frac{g_c}{g_s+g_c},\\qquad \\frac{V_d}{V_s}\\Big|_{\\text{input at }s} = \\frac{g_c}{g_d+g_c}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(2.4.2)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-2-4-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 2.4.1</p><h4>A thin dendrite on a leaky soma</h4><p>Take <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_s = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_d = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS. Then <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D = 80</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS², <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>187.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{dd} = 187.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{ss} = 150</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>125</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{sd} = 125</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ. A dendritic input is attenuated to 2/3 at the soma, while a somatic input reaches the dendrite at 5/6. Signals travel outward (soma → dendrite, as for a backpropagating action potential) with less attenuation than inward, because the soma is the bigger sink.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-2-4-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/local-transfer.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/local-transfer.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2.4.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 2.4.1.</b> Two compartments with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_s = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_d = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS as coupling varies. <b>A</b> Voltage attenuation differs by direction (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-2-4-2\">(2.4.2)</a>). <b>B</b> Input impedances differ, but the transfer impedance is the same both ways (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-2-4-1\">(2.4.1)</a>).</figcaption></figure><h3>Why distal synapses still matter in CA1</h3><p>If distal inputs were simply attenuated, their somatic effect would be tiny. Two compensations have been measured in CA1. Synaptic AMPA conductance increases with distance along the apical dendrite so that somatic EPSP amplitude becomes roughly independent of synapse location (Magee &amp; Cook 2000). Distal <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> shortens local EPSPs, equalizing their temporal summation at the soma (Magee 1999). In models, both show up as location-dependent parameters, not as properties of the cable.</p><h3>Signals travelling outward: the back-propagating spike</h3><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-2-4-2\"><a href=\"img/tb/morph-bap.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/morph-bap.svg\" alt=\"Figure 2.4.2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 2.4.2.</b> NEURON simulations from the guide’s active-dendrites pipeline (Migliore, Ferrante &amp; Ascoli 2005 channels; 2.5 nA, 1.5 ms somatic step). <b>A</b> Peak bAP amplitude on each section of mpg141209_A; grey sections were not recorded. <b>B</b> Apical bAP against distance for three A-type K⁺ densities. <b>C</b> The distal (350–450 µm) bAP as A-type density varies, for four CA1 reconstructions.</figcaption></figure><p>Reciprocity concerns passive transfer. Active channels make outward signalling more interesting. In NEURON simulations with the Migliore, Ferrante &amp; Ascoli (2005) channel set on the real mpg141209_A tree (<a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#fig-2-4-2\">Figure 2.4.2</a>), a somatic spike invades the apical dendrite with a peak of about 96 mV at the soma, 64 mV at 200 µm and 52 mV at 300 µm, then fails abruptly: 10 mV at 400 µm. Halving the A-type K⁺ density keeps it above 63 mV out to 450 µm; raising it by half makes it fail by 300 µm (14 mV).</p><p>The same channel densities on four different CA1 reconstructions fail at different A-type densities ({<a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#fig-2-4-2\">Figure 2.4.2</a>}C). Branching pattern and diameters, not only channels, decide where a spike stops, which is the morphology-versus-channels question this chapter has been building toward. Golding, Kath &amp; Spruston (2001) observed exactly this dichotomy in CA1 recordings: some cells show strong, others weak backpropagation into the distal dendrites.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 2.4</span> Input and transfer impedance matrix</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">G = conductance_matrix(model)               # leak + coupling, at the operating point\nZ = inverse(G)                              # steady state; use (G + i*w*C) for sinusoids\nZ_local    = Z[d, d]                         # dendritic input, dendritic voltage\nZ_transfer = Z[s, d]                         # dendritic input, somatic voltage\nassert isclose(Z[s, d], Z[d, s])            # reciprocity\nattenuation_d_to_s = Z[s, d] / Z[d, d]</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Somatic change, ambiguous cause</h4><p>If a manipulation changes the somatic EPSP, it could have changed the local response (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{dd}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, synaptic conductance) or the transfer (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c</annotation></semantics></math></span>, intervening membrane). Recording or simulating both sites is the only way to tell them apart.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Synapses far from the soma; distance-dependent AMPA scaling</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Schaffer collateral (radiatum) vs perforant path (lacunosum-moleculare) inputs</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Dendritic vs somatic EPSP recordings, uncaging</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Impedance matrix, reciprocity theorem</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Morphology workbench: local and transfer impedance</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Input impedance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{dd}</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives the local response; transfer impedance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{sd}</annotation></semantics></math></span> the response elsewhere.","Transfer impedance is symmetric (reciprocity); voltage attenuation is not, because input impedances differ.","CA1 offsets dendritic attenuation with distance-dependent synaptic conductance and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span>."],"terms":["input-impedance","transfer-impedance","reciprocity"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_s = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_d = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c = 20.9</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, compute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{dd}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{sd}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the dendrite → soma attenuation.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>234.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\approx 234.4</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>25.9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>234.4</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mtext>nS</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>110.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{dd} = 25.9/234.4\\ \\text{nS}^{-1} \\approx 110.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20.9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>234.4</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>89.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{sd} = 20.9/234.4 \\approx 89.3</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ; attenuation <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>20.9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>25.9</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.81</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 20.9/25.9 \\approx 0.81</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Use reciprocity to argue that a 100 pA current injected at the soma produces the same dendritic voltage as 100 pA injected in the dendrite produces at the soma.","a":"Both voltages equal <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{sd}\\times 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> pA, and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{sd}=Z_{ds}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The local voltages (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{ss}</annotation></semantics></math></span> versus <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{dd}</annotation></semantics></math></span>) differ, which is why the attenuation ratios differ."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Blocking <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> makes a distal EPSP larger and slower at the soma. Is this a local or a transfer effect?","a":"Both: removing <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> raises local <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_m</annotation></semantics></math></span> (bigger, slower local EPSP) and the membrane along the path (larger <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>, less attenuation). Separating them needs local and somatic recordings."}],"eqs":[{"num":"2.4.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mi>D</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mi>D</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mi>D</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{dd} = \\frac{g_s+g_c}{D},\\qquad Z_{ss} = \\frac{g_d+g_c}{D},\\qquad Z_{sd} = Z_{ds} = \\frac{g_c}{D}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"2.4.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mfrac><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">∣</mo><mrow><mtext>input at </mtext><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mfrac><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">∣</mo><mrow><mtext>input at </mtext><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{V_s}{V_d}\\Big|_{\\text{input at }d} = \\frac{g_c}{g_s+g_c},\\qquad \\frac{V_d}{V_s}\\Big|_{\\text{input at }s} = \\frac{g_c}{g_d+g_c}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]}]},{"n":3,"part":"II","pathway":1,"title":"Dynamics and resonance","intro":["A neuron model is a set of coupled differential equations: a dynamical system. Instead of solving it for one input at a time, dynamical-systems thinking asks about the shape of all possible solutions at once. Where does the state settle? Which disturbances grow? Which change in a parameter turns rest into repetitive firing?","We use the two-variable FitzHugh–Nagumo (FHN) model, a caricature of HH with one fast voltage-like variable and one slow recovery variable, to introduce state space, nullclines, fixed points, linear stability and bifurcations. The chapter ends with resonance, the frequency preference that is the subject of Chapter 5."],"obj":["Represent a model as a vector field and read trajectories in its phase plane","Find fixed points from nullcline intersections","Classify stability from the Jacobian’s trace and determinant","Distinguish resonance (a filter property) from oscillation (a dynamical state)"],"prereq":["Chapter 1","Derivatives, 2×2 matrices and eigenvalues"],"sections":[{"route":"/topics/dynamics/states-trajectories/","num":"3.1","obj":["Define state, state space and vector field","Write the FitzHugh–Nagumo model and evaluate its vector field at a point","Explain why trajectories of an autonomous system never cross"],"body":"<p>Patch a CA1 pyramidal cell and inject a brief current pulse. Whether the cell fires depends not only on the pulse but on what its channels were doing at that moment: how many Na⁺ channels are inactivated from the last spike, how many K⁺ channels are still open, how far <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> has relaxed. The voltage you record is one visible number; the channel populations behind it are hidden but just as real, and together they decide what happens next.</p><p>Dynamical systems make that idea precise. Every quantity that carries memory of the past (voltage on the capacitor, the open fraction of each channel population, intracellular Ca²⁺) is a coordinate of the cell’s <b>state</b>.</p><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>In the cell</th><th>In the model</th><th>Changes over</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charge on the membrane</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>≈ 10–40 ms (membrane time constant)</td></tr><tr><td>Na⁺ channels opened by depolarization</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>&lt; 1 ms</td></tr><tr><td>Na⁺ channels inactivated</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>several ms</td></tr><tr><td>Delayed-rectifier K⁺ channels open</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>several ms</td></tr><tr><td>HCN channels open (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span>)</td><td>an <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> gate</td><td>tens–hundreds of ms</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"state\" tabindex=\"0\">state</dfn> of a model is the smallest set of numbers that, together with the equations and inputs, determines its future. For a passive membrane the state is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> alone. For HH it is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(V, m, h, n)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The equations assign to every point of <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"state-space\" tabindex=\"0\">state space</dfn> a velocity, the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"vector-field\" tabindex=\"0\">vector field</dfn>; a solution is a curve that follows these arrows, a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"trajectory\" tabindex=\"0\">trajectory</dfn>.</p><p>FitzHugh (1961) and Nagumo et al. (1962) reduced HH to two variables by lumping the fast variables (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span>) into a voltage-like <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the slow ones (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span>) into a recovery variable <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-3-1-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>τ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dv}{dt} = v - \\frac{v^3}{3} - w + I, \\qquad \\tau\\,\\frac{dw}{dt} = v + a - b\\,w</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(3.1.1)</span></div><p>The lab uses <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a = 0.7</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0.8</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 12.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>; everything is dimensionless. The cubic term gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span> a region of positive feedback (self-excitation, like Na⁺ activation) flanked by negative feedback; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span> slowly opposes <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>, like K⁺ activation and Na⁺ inactivation together.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-3-1-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 3.1.1</p><h4>Reading the arrows</h4><p>At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(v,w) = (0,0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot v = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.056</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot w = 0.7/12.5 = 0.056</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The state moves straight up in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>. At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,-0.5)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.33</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot v = -1 + 1/3 + 0.5 + 0.5 = 0.33</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.008</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot w = (-1+0.7+0.4)/12.5 = 0.008</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Voltage changes about 40 times faster than recovery here, which is why trajectories run nearly horizontally until they reach the region where <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot v \\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-3-1-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/fhn-trajectory.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/fhn-trajectory.svg\" alt=\"Figure 3.1.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 3.1.1.</b> FitzHugh–Nagumo, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-3-1-1\">(3.1.1)</a>, with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>. <b>A</b> The vector field (grey arrows), nullclines and one trajectory from rest; it settles onto a closed orbit. <b>B</b> The same solution as time series: fast jumps in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>, slow drift in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</figcaption></figure><p>Because the right-hand side depends only on the current state (the system is <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"autonomous\" tabindex=\"0\">autonomous</dfn> when <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> is constant), two trajectories cannot pass through the same point heading different ways. In two dimensions this is a strong constraint: a closed orbit fences off its interior, so a trajectory starting inside must stay inside. That is the geometric root of the Poincaré–Bendixson theorem, which guarantees that bounded 2-D trajectories approach either a fixed point or a closed orbit.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 3.1</span> Integrate and plot a 2-D trajectory</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">state = (v0, w0)\ntrajectory = [state]\nfor step in range(n_steps):\n    v, w = state\n    dv = v - v**3/3 - w + I\n    dw = (v + a - b*w) / tau\n    state = (v + dt*dv, w + dt*dw)           # forward Euler; check dt by halving\n    trajectory.append(state)\nplot(trajectory.v, trajectory.w)             # phase plane\nquiver(grid_v, grid_w, dv(grid), dw(grid))   # vector field behind it</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>A loop in a plot is not a limit cycle</h4><p>If you plot <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> against a variable that is not part of the state (say, a filtered version of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span>), or project a 4-D trajectory onto two axes, curves can cross and loops can appear without any limit cycle. Phase-plane arguments apply to the variables that actually form the state.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Channel populations with memory of past voltage</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Any excitable cell; reduced models of CA1 spiking</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Spike trains, phase plots of V against dV/dt</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Vector fields, existence and uniqueness, Poincaré–Bendixson</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Phase-plane workbench, FitzHugh–Nagumo</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["The state determines the future; equations define a vector field on state space; solutions are trajectories.","FHN: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot v = v - v^3/3 - w + I</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\dot w = v + a - bw</annotation></semantics></math></span> captures fast excitation and slow recovery.","Trajectories of an autonomous system never cross; in 2-D this forces them toward fixed points or closed orbits."],"terms":["state","state-space","vector-field","trajectory","autonomous"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"Evaluate the FHN vector field at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(v,w) = (1, 0)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Which way does the state move?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.667</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot v = 1 - 1/3 - 0 = 0.667</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.136</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot w = (1 + 0.7)/12.5 = 0.136</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Up and to the right, mostly right: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span> keeps increasing until the cubic term turns it around."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why can the 1-D passive membrane <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\dot V = -V + RI</annotation></semantics></math></span> never oscillate, whatever <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> is (constant)?","a":"In one dimension a trajectory moves along a line and can only approach or leave fixed points monotonically; turning around would require <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V</annotation></semantics></math></span> to change sign at a point the trajectory passes twice, which uniqueness forbids. Oscillation needs at least two dimensions."},{"kind":"concept","q":"How many state variables does a two-compartment HH model have?","a":"Each compartment has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span>: 8 state variables (more if synaptic or Ca²⁺ variables are added)."}],"eqs":[{"num":"3.1.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>τ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dv}{dt} = v - \\frac{v^3}{3} - w + I, \\qquad \\tau\\,\\frac{dw}{dt} = v + a - b\\,w</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/nullclines/","num":"3.2","obj":["Derive the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>- and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullclines of FHN","Locate fixed points as nullcline intersections, graphically and numerically","Predict how input <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> moves the fixed point"],"body":"<p>In the FHN caricature, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span> stands for the membrane voltage together with the fast Na⁺ activation that follows it almost instantly, and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span> for everything slow that fights back: Na⁺ inactivation and K⁺ activation. A nullcline is therefore a biological balance. On the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline the inward Na⁺-like current exactly cancels the outward currents, so the voltage momentarily stops moving; on the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline the recovery processes have caught up with the current voltage.</p><p>The N-shape of the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline is the signature of Na⁺ channels. Near rest, depolarizing the cell increases outward leak and K⁺ current faster than inward current, so the net current restores rest. In the middle range, each millivolt opens enough Na⁺ channels that inward current grows faster than outward: positive feedback, the regenerative upstroke. At high voltages the driving force on Na⁺ shrinks toward <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Na}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and outward current wins again. Three regimes, three branches.</p><p>Formally, a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"nullcline\" tabindex=\"0\">nullcline</dfn> is the set of states where one variable stops changing. For FHN, setting each derivative to zero:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-3-2-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot v = 0:\\ \\ w = v - \\frac{v^3}{3} + I, \\qquad\\qquad \\dot w = 0:\\ \\ w = \\frac{v + a}{b}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(3.2.1)</span></div><p>The <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline is a cubic (an N-shape) and the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline a straight line. On the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline trajectories move vertically; on the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline horizontally. Wherever they cross, both derivatives vanish: a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"fixed-point\" tabindex=\"0\">fixed point</dfn> (equilibrium), <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(v^*, w^*)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-3-2-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 3.2.1</p><h4>The resting state of FHN</h4><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, substitute the line into the cubic: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v - v^3/3 = (v+0.7)/0.8</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This cubic in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span> has one real root, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1.199</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^*\\approx -1.199</annotation></semantics></math></span>, giving <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.8</mn><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.624</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w^* = (v^*+0.7)/0.8 \\approx -0.624</annotation></semantics></math></span>. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> the cubic shifts up by 0.5 and the intersection moves to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.805</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^*\\approx-0.805</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.131</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w^*\\approx-0.131</annotation></semantics></math></span>: onto the middle, rising branch of the N.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-3-2-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/fhn-nullclines.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/fhn-nullclines.svg\" alt=\"Figure 3.2.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 3.2.1.</b> Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-3-2-1\">(3.2.1)</a> for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Input raises the cubic; the intersection (fixed point) moves from the left branch to the middle branch.</figcaption></figure><p>Input <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> simply lifts the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline. The fixed point slides along the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline toward the cubic’s local minimum (the knee) and then up its middle branch. Whether a fixed point on the middle branch is stable is a separate question, answered by linearization in <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/\">§3.3</a>.</p><p>Nullclines also give a qualitative picture of the spike. Start just right of the knee: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot v</annotation></semantics></math></span> is large and positive, so the state shoots right to the far branch of the cubic (the upstroke); it then creeps up that branch as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span> grows (the plateau), falls off the top knee, shoots left (downstroke) and creeps down the left branch back to rest (recovery). Fast jumps between slow branches are the signature of a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"relaxation-oscillation\" tabindex=\"0\">relaxation</dfn> system.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 3.2</span> Fixed points from nullclines</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">v_grid = linspace(-3, 3, 2000)\nv_null = v_grid - v_grid**3/3 + I            # where dv/dt = 0\nw_null = (v_grid + a) / b                    # where dw/dt = 0\ng = v_null - w_null                          # zero where the curves cross\nfixed_points = [root(g, bracket) for bracket in sign_changes(g)]\nfor v_star in fixed_points: w_star = (v_star + a) / b</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-aside\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Aside</p><h4>Nullclines in real models</h4><p>For HH, set the fast gate to its steady state (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=m_\\infty(V)</annotation></semantics></math></span>) and combine <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> into one slow variable; the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline of the reduced model is again N-shaped. The Morris–Lecar model (Ch. 4 and the phase-plane workbench) is a biophysical 2-D model whose nullclines can be drawn exactly.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Balance points of inward Na⁺ and outward currents</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Morris–Lecar (barnacle muscle), reduced HH</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Steady-state I–V curve (its zero crossings are fixed points)</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Implicit curves, root finding</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Phase-plane workbench, Morris–Lecar preset</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Nullclines are curves where one derivative vanishes; fixed points lie at their intersections.","FHN has a cubic <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline and a linear <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline; input <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> shifts the cubic vertically.","Spikes in relaxation systems are fast jumps between the outer branches of the cubic."],"terms":["nullcline","fixed-point","relaxation-oscillation"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For FHN with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, verify that <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1.199</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^* = -1.199</annotation></semantics></math></span> is (approximately) a fixed point.","a":"Cubic side: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.199</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>1.199</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1.199</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5746</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.624</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1.199 + 1.199^3/3 = -1.199 + 0.5746 = -0.624</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Line: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1.199</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.8</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.624</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1.199+0.7)/0.8 = -0.624</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Equal. ✓"},{"kind":"concept","q":"Where on the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline do trajectories move straight up, and where straight down?","a":"On the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot v = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so motion is vertical, with sign of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot w = (v+a-bw)/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>: upward where the nullcline lies below the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>-line (to the right of the intersection), downward where it lies above."},{"kind":"concept","q":"With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.8</annotation></semantics></math></span>, can the line and the cubic intersect three times for some <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span>? What would that imply?","a":"Only if the line’s slope <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/b = 1.25</annotation></semantics></math></span> were less than the cubic’s maximum slope (1, at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>). It is not, so there is always exactly one fixed point for these parameters. With a shallower line (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math></span>), three fixed points are possible: bistability between rest and a depolarized state."}],"eqs":[{"num":"3.2.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot v = 0:\\ \\ w = v - \\frac{v^3}{3} + I, \\qquad\\qquad \\dot w = 0:\\ \\ w = \\frac{v + a}{b}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/","num":"3.3","obj":["Linearize a 2-D system at a fixed point and compute the Jacobian","Classify the fixed point from trace and determinant","Find the Hopf point of FHN and contrast Hopf and saddle-node onsets of firing"],"body":"<p>A resting CA1 cell is constantly nudged: a single EPSP, channel noise, a passing inhibitory input. After each nudge it returns to rest. That return is what “stable” means. Sometimes it returns smoothly; sometimes it overshoots and rings for a cycle or two, a damped subthreshold oscillation you can see when you inject a small step into a cell with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><p>Now raise the steady current through the pipette, step by step. At some value the cell no longer settles: it starts firing repetitively. Nothing about the channels changed; only the operating point did. That switch is a <b>bifurcation</b>, and the way a cell crosses it (does it start at 1 Hz or jump to 40 Hz? does it ring first?) is a measurable fingerprint of which channels dominate near threshold. The rest of this section shows how to read that fingerprint from the equations.</p><p>Near a fixed point, write the state as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf x = \\mathbf x^* + \\boldsymbol\\delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Keeping first-order terms gives a linear system governed by the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"jacobian\" tabindex=\"0\">Jacobian</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the matrix of partial derivatives at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf x^*</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-3-3-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>J</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\delta} = J\\,\\boldsymbol\\delta,\\qquad J = \\begin{pmatrix}\\partial\\dot v/\\partial v &amp; \\partial\\dot v/\\partial w\\\\ \\partial\\dot w/\\partial v &amp; \\partial\\dot w/\\partial w\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}1 - v^{*2} &amp; -1\\\\ 1/\\tau &amp; -b/\\tau\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(3.3.1)</span></div><p>Solutions are combinations of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{\\lambda t}</annotation></semantics></math></span> where <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> are the eigenvalues of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For a 2×2 matrix they follow from the trace <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span> and determinant <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-3-3-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>J</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{1,2} = \\frac{T \\pm \\sqrt{T^2 - 4\\Delta}}{2},\\qquad T = \\operatorname{tr}J,\\quad \\Delta = \\det J</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(3.3.2)</span></div><ul class=\"tb-list\"><li><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta &lt; 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: a <b>saddle</b> (one growing, one shrinking direction); always unstable.</li><li><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta &gt; 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <b>stable</b>; a node if <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2&gt;4\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, a spiral (focus) if <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2&lt;4\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</li><li><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta &gt; 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <b>unstable</b> node or spiral.</li><li><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: eigenvalues <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi><msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm i\\sqrt\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the boundary where a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"hopf\" tabindex=\"0\">Hopf bifurcation</dfn> can occur.</li></ul><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-3-3-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 3.3.1</p><h4>Rest and its loss of stability</h4><p>At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1.199</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^*=-1.199</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1.439</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.439</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J_{11} = 1 - 1.439 = -0.439</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.439</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.064</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.503</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = -0.439 - 0.064 = -0.503</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.439</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.064</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>0.08</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.108</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = (-0.439)(-0.064) + 0.08 = 0.108</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Since <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.43</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2 = 0.25 &lt; 4\\Delta = 0.43</annotation></semantics></math></span>, rest is a <b>stable spiral</b> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.21</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = -0.25 \\pm 0.21i</annotation></semantics></math></span>: perturbations decay with damped ringing.</p><p>Increasing <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> moves <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^*</annotation></semantics></math></span> toward the knee. Stability is lost when <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.064</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 - v^{*2} = b/\\tau = 0.064</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>0.936</mn></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.967</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^* = -\\sqrt{0.936} \\approx -0.967</annotation></semantics></math></span>. On the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.334</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w^* \\approx -0.334</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline then requires <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.331</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = w^* - v^* + v^{*3}/3 \\approx 0.331</annotation></semantics></math></span>. At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>0.29</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = +0.29</annotation></semantics></math></span>: rest is an unstable spiral and the model fires repetitively.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-3-3-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/fhn-hopf.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/fhn-hopf.svg\" alt=\"Figure 3.3.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 3.3.1.</b> <b>A</b> Eigenvalues of the FHN fixed point as input increases: the real part crosses zero at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.331</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\approx0.331</annotation></semantics></math></span> while the imaginary part is non-zero (Hopf). <b>B</b> Just below (0.25) the voltage rings and settles; above (0.5) it fires repetitively.</figcaption></figure><h3>Two ways to start firing</h3><p>A <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"bifurcation\" tabindex=\"0\">bifurcation</dfn> is a qualitative change in the dynamics as a parameter crosses a critical value. Neurons begin firing in two classic ways (Hodgkin’s excitability classes; Rinzel &amp; Ermentrout 1989):</p><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Onset</th><th>What happens</th><th>Firing at onset</th><th>Excitability</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Saddle-node on invariant circle (SNIC)</td><td>rest collides with a saddle and both vanish; the orbit passes through the ghost</td><td>arbitrarily low rate, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\propto\\sqrt{I-I_c}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>class I</td></tr><tr><td>Hopf</td><td>a complex pair crosses the imaginary axis; rest becomes unstable</td><td>non-zero rate; subthreshold ringing beforehand</td><td>class II</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>FHN and HH (with standard parameters) lose stability through a Hopf bifurcation and are class II; many cortical pyramidal cells are closer to class I. The excitability class is a property of the model’s equations at a particular operating point, which is why the phase-plane and neuron workbenches compute it rather than assume it.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 3.3</span> Continuation: follow a fixed point as a parameter changes</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">for I in linspace(I_min, I_max, n):\n    v_star = solve(f(v; I) == 0, start=v_star_previous)   # reuse last solution\n    J = jacobian(f, v_star; I)\n    lam = eigenvalues(J)\n    record(I, v_star, max(real(lam)), imag(lam))\nHopf:        max(real(lam)) changes sign while imag(lam) != 0\nsaddle-node: a real eigenvalue reaches 0 and the fixed point disappears</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Linear stability is local</h4><p>Eigenvalues describe small perturbations only. A stable fixed point can coexist with a stable limit cycle (bistability), so a large enough kick can start firing even though rest is linearly stable. This is what happens near a subcritical Hopf bifurcation (Ch. 4).</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Onset of repetitive firing as drive increases</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Class I cortical/hippocampal pyramidal cells; class II squid axon</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>f–I curves, subthreshold ringing</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Jacobians, eigenvalues, Hopf and saddle-node bifurcations</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Phase-plane workbench, bifurcation tab; XPPAUT/MatCont</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Linearize at a fixed point: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>J</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\delta} = J\\boldsymbol\\delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>; eigenvalues from trace and determinant decide stability.","<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> saddle; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T&lt;0&lt;\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span> stable; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T&gt;0&lt;\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span> unstable; complex eigenvalues mean spiralling.","FHN loses stability at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.331</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\approx0.331</annotation></semantics></math></span> (Hopf). SNIC onset gives class I, Hopf onset class II excitability."],"terms":["jacobian","eigenvalue","bifurcation","hopf","saddle-node","excitability-class"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"Classify the fixed point with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J = \\begin{pmatrix}-1 &amp; -2\\\\ 1 &amp; -1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = -2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = 1 + 2 = 3</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2 - 4\\Delta = -8 &lt; 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Stable spiral, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = -1 \\pm i\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Classify <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J = \\begin{pmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\ 0 &amp; -2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = -2 &lt; 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: a saddle (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = 1, -2</annotation></semantics></math></span>)."},{"kind":"calc","q":"For FHN at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> the fixed point is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.805</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v^*\\approx-0.805</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Compute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span> and classify.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.648</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.352</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J_{11} = 1 - 0.648 = 0.352</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.352</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.064</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.288</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 0.352 - 0.064 = 0.288</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.352</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.064</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>0.08</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0575</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = 0.352\\times(-0.064) + 0.08 = 0.0575</annotation></semantics></math></span>. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.083</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2 = 0.083 &lt; 4\\Delta = 0.23</annotation></semantics></math></span>: unstable spiral, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.144</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.19</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\approx 0.144\\pm0.19i</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"A model fires at 2 Hz just above threshold. Which onset bifurcation is more likely, and what experiment on the model would confirm it?","a":"Very low onset rates suggest a SNIC (class I). Confirm by continuing the fixed points in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span>: at a SNIC two fixed points (node and saddle) merge and disappear; at a Hopf the fixed point persists but its eigenvalues cross the imaginary axis with non-zero imaginary part."}],"eqs":[{"num":"3.3.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>J</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>v</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>w</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot{\\boldsymbol\\delta} = J\\,\\boldsymbol\\delta,\\qquad J = \\begin{pmatrix}\\partial\\dot v/\\partial v &amp; \\partial\\dot v/\\partial w\\\\ \\partial\\dot w/\\partial v &amp; \\partial\\dot w/\\partial w\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}1 - v^{*2} &amp; -1\\\\ 1/\\tau &amp; -b/\\tau\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"3.3.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>J</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{1,2} = \\frac{T \\pm \\sqrt{T^2 - 4\\Delta}}{2},\\qquad T = \\operatorname{tr}J,\\quad \\Delta = \\det J</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/resonance-measurement/","num":"3.4","obj":["Define impedance for sinusoidal input and compute it for a passive membrane","Explain how a slow restoring current turns a low-pass filter into a band-pass filter","Measure resonance frequency <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span> and strength <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> from an impedance curve"],"body":"<p>For small sinusoidal currents a stable model responds with a sinusoid at the same frequency, scaled and shifted. The complex <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"impedance\" tabindex=\"0\">impedance</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z(f)</annotation></semantics></math></span> records both: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the voltage amplitude per unit current amplitude and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg Z</annotation></semantics></math></span> the phase (Ch. 5). Its reciprocal is the admittance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y = 1/Z</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which simply adds over parallel membrane pathways.</p><p>A passive membrane has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y = g_L + i\\omega C</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-3-4-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>RC</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z_\\text{RC}(f)| = \\frac{R_\\text{in}}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega\\tau)^2}},\\qquad \\omega = 2\\pi f</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(3.4.1)</span></div><p>This is a low-pass filter: largest at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and down to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math></span> at the corner frequency <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_c = 1/(2\\pi\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, about 8 Hz for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms.</p><p>Now add a slow current that opposes voltage changes, with effective conductance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S</annotation></semantics></math></span> and time constant <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_S</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Linearized (Ch. 5), it contributes an admittance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S/(1+i\\omega\\tau_S)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: large at low frequency, where it has time to respond, and negligible at high frequency, where it cannot keep up:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-3-4-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(f) = g_L + i\\omega C + \\frac{g_S}{1+i\\omega\\tau_S}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(3.4.2)</span></div><p>At low frequencies the slow current shunts the response; at high frequencies the capacitor does. In between there is a window where neither dominates and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span> peaks: <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"resonance\" tabindex=\"0\">resonance</dfn>. The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"resonance-frequency\" tabindex=\"0\">resonance frequency</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the location of the peak, and its strength is usually quantified by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q = |Z(f_R)|/|Z(f_\\text{low})|</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\text{low}</annotation></semantics></math></span> a low reference frequency such as 0.5 Hz (Hu et al. 2002).</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-3-4-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 3.4.1</p><h4>A theta resonator</h4><p>Take <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_S = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms. At DC the admittance is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>66.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(0)|\\approx 66.7</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ. Numerically, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span> peaks at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>5.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R\\approx 5.9</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>172</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|\\approx172</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ, and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mtext> Hz</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>69.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(0.5\\text{ Hz})|\\approx 69.8</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\approx 2.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Without the slow current the same cell is a low-pass filter with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(0.5)|\\approx200</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-3-4-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/resonance-curve.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/resonance-curve.svg\" alt=\"Figure 3.4.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 3.4.1.</b> Impedance magnitude from Eqs. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-3-4-1\">(3.4.1)</a> and <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-3-4-2\">(3.4.2)</a>. The passive cell (grey) is a low-pass filter; adding a slow restoring current (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_S = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms) carves out a band-pass peak in the theta range (shaded).</figcaption></figure><p>Resonance is a <b>filter property</b>: it describes how a stable system responds to input. It does not mean the cell oscillates on its own. Damped subthreshold ringing after a step (complex eigenvalues of the linearization) and an impedance peak are related but not identical: a resonator can have an impedance peak with no visible ringing, and a model can ring without a prominent peak.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 3.4</span> Impedance curve of a linearized model</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">for f in logspace(-1, 2, 200):\n    w = 2 * pi * f / 1000                     # rad/ms, because C/g is in ms\n    Y = gL + 1j*w*C + gS / (1 + 1j*w*tauS)   # admittances of parallel paths add\n    Z = 1 / Y                                 # 1/nS = GOhm -&gt; *1000 for MOhm\n    record(f, abs(Z), angle(Z))\nf_R = f[argmax(abs(Z))]\nQ   = abs(Z(f_R)) / abs(Z(0.5))              # state the reference frequency</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Q depends on the reference frequency</h4><p>Some papers use <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(f_R)|/|Z(0)|</annotation></semantics></math></span>, others the value at 0.5 or 1 Hz. With a strong slow current <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span> changes steeply at low frequency, so the choice changes <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Always report the definition next to the number.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Slow restoring currents (Iₕ, I_M)</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1 pyramidal cells, entorhinal stellate cells, thalamic neurons</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Chirp/ZAP current clamp</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Complex admittance, linear filters</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Neuron workbench, impedance tab</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Impedance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z(f) = V/I</annotation></semantics></math></span> for sinusoids; admittances of parallel pathways add.","A passive membrane is low-pass with corner <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_c = 1/(2\\pi\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","A slow restoring current adds <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S/(1+i\\omega\\tau_S)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and creates a band-pass peak at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> measures its strength.","Resonance (filtering) is not the same as oscillation (a dynamical state)."],"terms":["impedance","admittance","resonance","resonance-frequency","q-factor"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For a passive cell with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in} = 200</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, compute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span> at 8 Hz.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.02</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\tau = 2\\pi\\times8\\times0.02 \\approx 1.005</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1.01</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mn>141</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z| = 200/\\sqrt{1+1.01}\\approx 141</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ (≈ 71% of DC)."},{"kind":"calc","q":"A measured impedance curve has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mtext> Hz</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(0.5\\text{ Hz})| = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ and a peak of 120 MΩ at 4 Hz. Report <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R = 4</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q = 120/100 = 1.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> (relative to 0.5 Hz)."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why must the slow current be <i>restoring</i> (opposing voltage changes) to produce resonance?","a":"Only a restoring current reduces the low-frequency response; an amplifying slow current (negative effective <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S</annotation></semantics></math></span>) would increase it, enhancing the low-pass character instead of carving out a peak."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Increasing <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_S</annotation></semantics></math></span> in Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-3-4-2\">(3.4.2)</a> (slower restoring current), with everything else fixed. Which way would you expect <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span> to move?","a":"Lower. The slow current stops tracking the input above roughly <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(2\\pi\\tau_S)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so the window between its shunting and the capacitor’s shunting shifts to lower frequencies. Check it in the Neuron workbench’s impedance tab."}],"eqs":[{"num":"3.4.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>RC</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z_\\text{RC}(f)| = \\frac{R_\\text{in}}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega\\tau)^2}},\\qquad \\omega = 2\\pi f</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"3.4.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(f) = g_L + i\\omega C + \\frac{g_S}{1+i\\omega\\tau_S}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]}]},{"n":4,"part":"II","pathway":8,"title":"A dynamical-systems toolkit","intro":["Chapter 3 introduced the language of dynamics on one model. This chapter collects the tools as a kit you can apply to any model: classifying linear phase portraits, the two normal forms that describe how neurons start firing, and the numerical methods that turn equations into simulations without changing their behaviour.","Normal forms are the simplest equations that show a given bifurcation. They are not neuron models, but near the bifurcation any model that undergoes it behaves like its normal form, which is why they predict measurable things such as how firing rate grows with input."],"obj":["Classify linear 2-D systems on the trace–determinant plane","Use the saddle-node normal form to derive the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt{\\mu}</annotation></semantics></math></span> bottleneck time and class I firing","Use the Hopf normal form to predict oscillation amplitude near onset; distinguish super- and subcritical cases","Choose a time step and integration method, and test convergence"],"prereq":["Chapter 3"],"sections":[{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/phase-portraits/","num":"4.1","obj":["Solve <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot{\\mathbf x} = A\\mathbf x</annotation></semantics></math></span> in terms of eigenvalues and eigenvectors","Place any 2×2 system on the trace–determinant plane","Read period and decay rate from complex eigenvalues"],"body":"<p>Inject a small current step into a CA1 cell held near −75 mV and the voltage rises, overshoots, sags back and settles: the classic <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> sag. Two things are happening at once: the membrane charges (fast), and HCN channels slowly close as the cell depolarizes, pulling the voltage back. Plot voltage against the fraction of open HCN channels instead of against time and the response becomes a curve spiralling into a point. That plot is a <b>phase portrait</b>, and the spiral is the shape of a cell with a slow restoring current.</p><p>Close to rest, small responses like this are linear: double the step, double the deflection. Linear 2-D systems have only a handful of possible portraits, so classifying them gives a vocabulary for every small-signal experiment in the guide.</p><p>Every linearization in this book reduces to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot{\\mathbf x} = A\\mathbf x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. If <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span> has eigenvalues <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_k</annotation></semantics></math></span> with eigenvectors <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf u_k</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the general solution is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf x(t) = c_1 e^{\\lambda_1 t}\\mathbf u_1 + c_2 e^{\\lambda_2 t}\\mathbf u_2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Real eigenvalues give straight-line motion along eigenvectors; complex ones, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = \\sigma \\pm i\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>, give rotation at angular speed <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> with radial growth or decay <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{\\sigma t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><p>The lab’s rotating system makes this explicit:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-4-1-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>ω</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>x</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>y</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}\\dot x\\\\ \\dot y\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}-a &amp; -\\omega\\\\ \\omega &amp; -a\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\ y\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\lambda = -a \\pm i\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(4.1.1)</span></div><p>In polar coordinates it separates exactly: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot r = -ar</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\theta = \\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The period is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi/\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> and each turn shrinks the radius by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2\\pi a/\\omega}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> every circle is an orbit, a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"center\" tabindex=\"0\">centre</dfn>: a structurally fragile case that any small damping destroys.</p><h3>The trace–determinant plane</h3><p>Because the eigenvalues depend only on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = \\operatorname{tr}A</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = \\det A</annotation></semantics></math></span> (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/\">(3.3.2)</a>), every 2-D linear system is a point in the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(T,\\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math></span> plane. The parabola <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = T^2/4</annotation></semantics></math></span> separates nodes (below) from spirals (above); the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>-axis separates stable (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>) from unstable; everything below <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a saddle. As a parameter changes, the fixed point traces a path through this plane, and crossing a boundary is a change in the phase portrait.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-4-1-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 4.1.1</p><h4>Three matrices</h4><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th>Eigenvalues</th><th>Portrait</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\-4&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>−1</td><td>4</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>1.94</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.5\\pm1.94i</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>stable spiral</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>2</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;2\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>3, 1</td><td>unstable node</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}-3&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>−4</td><td>3</td><td>−3, −1</td><td>stable node</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The first matrix is a damped harmonic oscillator <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot x + \\dot x + 4x = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> written as a 2-D system: natural angular frequency 2, damping that makes it ring at 1.94.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-4-1-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/trace-det.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/trace-det.svg\" alt=\"Figure 4.1.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 4.1.1.</b> <b>A</b> The trace–determinant plane with its boundaries (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = T^2/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>) and the three matrices of the worked example. <b>B</b> A trajectory of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-4-1-1\">(4.1.1)</a> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega = 2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 4.1</span> Classify a 2-D linear system</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">T, D = trace(A), det(A)\nif D &lt; 0:                 kind = &#x27;saddle&#x27;\nelif T == 0:              kind = &#x27;centre&#x27;\nelif T*T &lt; 4*D:           kind = &#x27;stable spiral&#x27; if T &lt; 0 else &#x27;unstable spiral&#x27;\nelse:                     kind = &#x27;stable node&#x27;   if T &lt; 0 else &#x27;unstable node&#x27;\nperiod = 2*pi / sqrt(D - T*T/4)  if spiral or centre</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Linear portraits are local pictures</h4><p>A nonlinear model is only locally like its linearization. A stable spiral of the linearization can sit inside an unstable limit cycle, and the global picture (where trajectories go from far away) needs nullclines and simulation.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Small-signal responses around rest</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Any cell near its operating point</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Responses to small steps and sinusoids</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Linear ODEs, trace–determinant classification</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Phase-plane workbench, linear system</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Solutions of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot{\\mathbf x}=A\\mathbf x</annotation></semantics></math></span> combine <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{\\lambda t}</annotation></semantics></math></span> along eigenvectors; complex <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = \\sigma\\pm i\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> means rotation with growth <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{\\sigma t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Every 2-D linear system is a point <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(T,\\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the axes and the parabola <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = T^2/4</annotation></semantics></math></span> separate saddles, nodes, spirals and centres."],"terms":["eigenvalue","center","trace-determinant"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"In Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-4-1-1\">(4.1.1)</a> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega = 2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> (time in seconds), find the period and the fraction of radius left after one turn.","a":"Period <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 2\\pi/\\omega = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> s. Radius factor <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.82</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= e^{-0.2\\times1} \\approx 0.82</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Write <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>¨</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot x + 2\\zeta\\omega_0\\dot x + \\omega_0^2x = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a first-order system and give <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y = \\dot x</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A = \\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1\\\\-\\omega_0^2&amp;-2\\zeta\\omega_0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ζ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = -2\\zeta\\omega_0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = \\omega_0^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Spiral iff <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2&lt;4\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, i.e. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\zeta&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></span> (underdamped)."},{"kind":"concept","q":"A fixed point’s <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(T,\\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math></span> moves from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-0.5, 0.1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.3, 0.1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> as input increases. What bifurcation did it cross, and what do you expect to see?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span> changed sign with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2&lt;4\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span> near the crossing: complex eigenvalues crossing the imaginary axis, a Hopf bifurcation. Expect the onset (or disappearance) of an oscillation with angular frequency near <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\Delta}\\approx0.32</annotation></semantics></math></span>."}],"eqs":[{"num":"4.1.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>ω</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>x</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>y</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}\\dot x\\\\ \\dot y\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}-a &amp; -\\omega\\\\ \\omega &amp; -a\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\ y\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\lambda = -a \\pm i\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/saddle-node/","num":"4.2","obj":["Find and classify the fixed points of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x = \\mu - x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>","Derive the bottleneck passage time <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = \\pi/\\sqrt{|\\mu|}</annotation></semantics></math></span>","Connect a saddle-node on an invariant circle (SNIC) to class I firing"],"body":"<p>Many cortical and hippocampal pyramidal cells can fire very slowly just above threshold: inject a current a hair above rheobase and the cell waits hundreds of milliseconds, creeping toward threshold, before it fires. Physically, the cell sits in a near-balance between inward Na⁺ (and persistent Na⁺) current and outward K⁺ and leak current. Below rheobase there is a voltage where the two balance (rest) and a slightly higher one where they balance precariously (threshold). Raising the current pushes these two balance points together until they merge and vanish.</p><p>After they vanish, the net current is still almost zero near where they used to be, so the voltage drifts there very slowly: that drift is the long wait before each spike. The saddle-node normal form below is the simplest equation with exactly this geometry.</p><p>The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"saddle-node\" tabindex=\"0\">saddle-node</dfn> normal form is the simplest way for equilibria to appear or vanish:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-4-2-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x = \\mu - x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(4.2.1)</span></div><p>For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> there are two fixed points, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^* = \\pm\\sqrt\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The slope of the right-hand side, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2x^*</annotation></semantics></math></span>, is negative at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+\\sqrt\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span> (stable) and positive at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\sqrt\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span> (unstable). As <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu\\to0</annotation></semantics></math></span> they approach each other, merge at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> there are none: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> everywhere and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span> runs off to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><h3>The ghost and the bottleneck</h3><p>Just past the bifurcation (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span> slightly negative) the flow is still very slow near <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, where the fixed points used to be. Time spent passing this “ghost” dominates. Integrating Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-4-2-1\">(4.2.1)</a> from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span> (written with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\to-x</annotation></semantics></math></span> so the motion is to the right):</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-4-2-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\frac{dx}{|\\mu| + x^2} = \\frac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{|\\mu|}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(4.2.2)</span></div><p>When the line is closed into a circle, so that a trajectory leaving on one side returns from the other, the passage repeats periodically: this is a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"snic\" tabindex=\"0\">saddle-node on an invariant circle</dfn>. The period diverges as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>μ</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mu|^{-1/2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so the frequency rises from zero as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\propto\\sqrt{I-I_c}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. That square-root f–I curve is the hallmark of class I excitability, and the theta neuron (Ermentrout &amp; Kopell 1986) is exactly this normal form on a circle.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-4-2-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 4.2.1</p><h4>How slow is slow?</h4><p>For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu = -0.01</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.1</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>31.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = \\pi/0.1 \\approx 31.4</annotation></semantics></math></span> time units; for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0025</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu = -0.0025</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>62.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\approx 62.8</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Quartering the distance to the bifurcation doubles the period. Near threshold, small changes in drive produce large changes in interspike interval: one reason class I neurons can encode input strength in firing rate over a wide range.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-4-2-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/saddle-node.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/saddle-node.svg\" alt=\"Figure 4.2.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 4.2.1.</b> Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-4-2-1\">(4.2.1)</a>. <b>A</b> The flow for three values of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>: two crossings, a touch, none. <b>B</b> Fixed points <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm\\sqrt\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span> (solid stable, dashed unstable). <b>C</b> Passage rate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/T = \\sqrt{|\\mu|}/\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span> past the bifurcation (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-4-2-2\">(4.2.2)</a>): it starts at zero, as in class I firing.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 4.2</span> Measure an f–I curve near onset</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">for I in currents:\n    spikes = simulate(model, I, duration=2.0, discard_first=0.2)\n    rate[I] = len(spikes) / 1.8\nI_c = bisect(lambda I: rate(I) &gt; 0, I_lo, I_hi)    # resolution-independent onset\nfit rate ~ c * (I - I_c)**0.5 near onset         # good fit -&gt; class I (SNIC-like)\nonset rate clearly &gt; 0                           # -&gt; class II (Hopf-like)</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Same bifurcation, different consequences</h4><p>A saddle-node of equilibria can also occur <i>off</i> an invariant circle; then the neuron jumps to a pre-existing limit cycle with non-zero frequency (bistability nearby). Only on an invariant circle does onset frequency start at zero. The Golomb (2006) CA1 bursting model uses a fold of resting states as the burst initiator, which is the same normal form in a fast–slow setting.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Slow firing near threshold, long first-spike latency</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Regular-spiking pyramidal cells; CA1 bursting onset (Golomb 2006)</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>f–I curve near rheobase, latency vs current</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Normal form μ − x², bottleneck integral</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Phase-plane workbench, saddle-node and SNIC presets</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x = \\mu - x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>: two fixed points <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm\\sqrt\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span> (stable and unstable) merge and vanish at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Past the bifurcation a ghost slows the flow; passage time <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = \\pi/\\sqrt{|\\mu|}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","On an invariant circle (SNIC) this gives periodic firing with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\propto\\sqrt{I-I_c}</annotation></semantics></math></span>: class I excitability."],"terms":["saddle-node","snic","excitability-class"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu = 0.25</annotation></semantics></math></span>, find the fixed points of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-4-2-1\">(4.2.1)</a> and their linear growth rates.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^* = \\pm0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Growth rate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= -2x^*</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> (stable), <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1</annotation></semantics></math></span> at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> (unstable)."},{"kind":"calc","q":"A class I model fires at 10 Hz when <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = I_c + 0.04</annotation></semantics></math></span> (arbitrary units). Using the normal-form scaling, predict the rate at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_c + 0.01</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\propto\\sqrt{I-I_c}</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.04</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f = 10\\sqrt{0.01/0.04} = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz. (The scaling holds only close to onset.)"},{"kind":"derive","q":"Show that <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty dx/(c^2+x^2) = \\pi/c</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"Antiderivative <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1c\\arctan(x/c)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; evaluated from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac1c(\\frac\\pi2 + \\frac\\pi2) = \\pi/c</annotation></semantics></math></span>. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c = \\sqrt{|\\mu|}</annotation></semantics></math></span> this is Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-4-2-2\">(4.2.2)</a>."}],"eqs":[{"num":"4.2.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x = \\mu - x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"4.2.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\frac{dx}{|\\mu| + x^2} = \\frac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{|\\mu|}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/hopf-normal-form/","num":"4.3","obj":["Analyse <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot r = \\mu r - r^3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\theta = \\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>","Predict amplitude <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span> and relaxation rate near a supercritical Hopf","Explain the bistability of a subcritical Hopf and its relevance to neurons"],"body":"<p>Squid axon, and some interneurons, behave differently from class I cells: just below threshold a small current step produces damped ringing, and just above threshold the cell does not fire slowly but jumps straight to tens of Hz. The ringing comes from a slow restoring current (K⁺ activation and Na⁺ inactivation in HH) acting with a delay: depolarization recruits it, it overshoots, the voltage swings back. Increase the drive and the damping weakens until the ringing no longer dies out.</p><p>That moment, when a damped oscillation becomes self-sustaining, is a Hopf bifurcation. The normal form separates the two things you can measure: how big the oscillation is (amplitude <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span>) and where it is in its cycle (phase <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>).</p><p>The supercritical <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"hopf\" tabindex=\"0\">Hopf</dfn> normal form separates the amplitude <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span> and phase <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> of an oscillation:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-4-3-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot r = \\mu r - r^3,\\qquad \\dot\\theta = \\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(4.3.1)</span></div><p>For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> the origin is a stable spiral (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = \\mu\\pm i\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>). At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> the eigenvalues cross the imaginary axis, and for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> the origin is unstable while a stable <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"limit-cycle\" tabindex=\"0\">limit cycle</dfn> appears at</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-4-3-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^* = \\sqrt{\\mu}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(4.3.2)</span></div><p>Linearizing the radial equation at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^*</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives growth rate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu - 3r^{*2} = -2\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the cycle attracts with time constant <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(2\\mu)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Two predictions follow and are testable in any model near a supercritical Hopf: amplitude grows as the square root of the distance past onset, while frequency starts at a non-zero value <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx\\omega/2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and recovery from perturbations becomes slow near onset (<dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"critical-slowing\" tabindex=\"0\">critical slowing down</dfn>).</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-4-3-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 4.3.1</p><h4>Amplitude and recovery</h4><p>At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu = 0.04</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^* = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and perturbations of the amplitude decay with time constant <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.08</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>12.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/0.08 = 12.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>. At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.09</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu = 0.09</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^* = 0.3</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Below onset, at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu = -0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, a perturbation of the rest state decays with time constant 10 while rotating at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>: damped ringing, the subthreshold sign of an approaching Hopf.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-4-3-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/hopf.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/hopf.svg\" alt=\"Figure 4.3.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 4.3.1.</b> Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-4-3-1\">(4.3.1)</a>. <b>A</b> Bifurcation diagram: rest is stable for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>; for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> it is unstable and a cycle of radius <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span> appears. <b>B</b> At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu = 0.04</annotation></semantics></math></span> a trajectory spirals out to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^* = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> (dotted).</figcaption></figure><h3>Subcritical Hopf</h3><p>If the cubic term has the opposite sign, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot r = \\mu r + r^3 - r^5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the small cycle born at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=0</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <i>unstable</i> and a large stable cycle already exists. Over a range of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> rest and repetitive firing are both stable: a well-timed pulse can switch the cell on and another switch it off. The HH squid model has this structure; it is why its f–I curve jumps from zero to tens of Hz and shows hysteresis.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 4.3</span> Distinguish super- and subcritical onset</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">sweep I upward:   record oscillation amplitude A_up(I)\nsweep I downward: start ON the oscillation, record A_down(I)\nif A grows smoothly from 0 and A_up == A_down:  supercritical (A ~ sqrt(I - I_H))\nif A jumps to a finite value and A_down persists below the jump:\n    subcritical, bistable range = hysteresis loop\nconfirm with continuation software (first Lyapunov coefficient)</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Classify from equations, not traces</h4><p>An oscillation that grows smoothly from zero amplitude suggests a supercritical Hopf, but noise, slow variables and finite simulation times can all blur this. Continuation of the fixed point and the first Lyapunov coefficient (computed numerically by tools such as XPPAUT or MatCont) decide super- versus subcritical.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Damped subthreshold oscillations that become sustained</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Squid axon; fast-spiking interneurons; network gamma</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Hysteresis in up/down current ramps</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Polar normal form, limit cycles, Lyapunov coefficient</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Phase-plane workbench, Hopf preset</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Supercritical Hopf: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot r = \\mu r - r^3</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives a stable cycle of radius <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with non-zero onset frequency.","Near onset, recovery slows (time constant <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span>) and subthreshold ringing appears below onset.","Subcritical Hopf creates bistability between rest and firing, with hysteresis in the f–I curve."],"terms":["hopf","limit-cycle","critical-slowing","bistability"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"In Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-4-3-1\">(4.3.1)</a>, what <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives an oscillation radius of 0.5?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu = r^{*2} = 0.25</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Starting at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r = 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu = 0.04</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which way does <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math></span> move, and at what initial rate?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.04</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.001</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.003</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot r = 0.04\\times0.1 - 0.001 = 0.003 &gt; 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: outward toward <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^* = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why is a class II neuron unable to fire at arbitrarily low rates?","a":"At a Hopf onset the oscillation is born with the frequency of the eigenvalues’ imaginary part, which is non-zero; the period does not diverge as it does at a SNIC."}],"eqs":[{"num":"4.3.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>θ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot r = \\mu r - r^3,\\qquad \\dot\\theta = \\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"4.3.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^* = \\sqrt{\\mu}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/numerical-time-step/","num":"4.4","obj":["Derive the forward-Euler update and its stability limit <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t&lt;2\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>","Compare forward Euler, backward Euler and exponential Euler","Run a convergence test by halving <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span>"],"body":"<p>A real membrane integrates current continuously: charge flows onto the capacitor every microsecond. A simulator cannot; it updates the state at discrete steps <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span> and assumes nothing important happens in between. For a CA1 cell that assumption fails exactly where biology is fastest: the Na⁺ activation gate changes on a time scale of about 0.1–0.5 ms, while the membrane and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> change over tens of milliseconds.</p><p>If the step is too long compared with the fastest channel, the simulated gate overshoots its target, then overshoots back, and the model fires spikes the real cell never would. The numerical method has to respect the biology’s fastest time scale even when the question is about the slow ones.</p><p>A simulator replaces <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V = f(V)</annotation></semantics></math></span> by a rule that advances the state by a step <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The simplest is <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"forward-euler\" tabindex=\"0\">forward Euler</dfn>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{n+1} = V_n + \\Delta t\\,f(V_n)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Apply it to the passive decay <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V = -V/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-4-4-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{n+1} = \\left(1 - \\frac{\\Delta t}{\\tau}\\right)V_n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad V_n = \\left(1-\\frac{\\Delta t}{\\tau}\\right)^n V_0</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(4.4.1)</span></div><p>The exact solution decays as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-t/\\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The numerical one decays only if <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|1-\\Delta t/\\tau|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, i.e. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\Delta t&lt;2\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>; for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau&lt;\\Delta t&lt;2\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> it decays while flipping sign each step (spurious oscillation), and beyond <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> it explodes. The equation is stable for every <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"numerical-stability\" tabindex=\"0\">instability</dfn> belongs to the method.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-4-4-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 4.4.1</p><h4>Accuracy and stability</h4><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0 = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, after 20 steps Euler gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>0.95</mn><mn>20</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>3.585</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\times0.95^{20}\\approx3.585</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV against the exact <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>3.679</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10e^{-1}\\approx3.679</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV: 2.6% low. Halving <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span> to 0.5 ms gives 3.632 mV, 1.3% low. The error halves with the step: forward Euler is <b>first-order accurate</b>.</p><p>Now consider a fast gate with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_m = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms in the same simulation at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms: the factor is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 - 5 = -4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and the gate explodes. Systems that mix fast and slow time scales are <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"stiff\" tabindex=\"0\">stiff</dfn>, and the fastest one sets the stable step even if you only care about the slow one.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-4-4-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/euler.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/euler.svg\" alt=\"Figure 4.4.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 4.4.1.</b> Forward Euler on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V = -V/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-4-4-1\">(4.4.1)</a>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms. <b>A</b> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms is accurate-ish, 30 ms rings, 45 ms diverges. <b>B</b> Error against <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span> on log axes: slope 1, so halving the step halves the error.</figcaption></figure><h3>Better methods for neurons</h3><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Update for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V = -V/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th>Stable for</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Forward Euler</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-\\Delta t/\\tau)V_n</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t&lt;2\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>simple; first order</td></tr><tr><td>Backward Euler</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_n/(1+\\Delta t/\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>all <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>implicit; damps too much at large steps; NEURON’s default</td></tr><tr><td>Exponential Euler</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-\\Delta t/\\tau}V_n</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>all <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>exact for linear equations; standard for HH gates</td></tr><tr><td>Crank–Nicolson / RK4</td><td>—</td><td>method dependent</td><td>second / fourth order</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Gate equations are linear in the gate variable at fixed <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so exponential Euler, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1} = x_\\infty + (x_n - x_\\infty)e^{-\\Delta t/\\tau_x}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, is exact for them over a step with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> frozen. The guide’s Neuron workbench uses this scheme with voltage and gates updated on staggered half-steps, as NEURON does.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 4.4</span> Convergence test</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">dt = 0.1\nresult = simulate(model, dt)\nwhile True:\n    finer = simulate(model, dt / 2)\n    if relative_change(result, finer) &lt; tolerance: break\n    dt, result = dt / 2, finer\nreport(result, dt, method)                    # never a result without its dt</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Always run a convergence test</h4><p>Before interpreting any surprising result (a new oscillation, a change in spike count), halve <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span> and rerun. If the result changes, it is at least partly numerical. In the workbench, HH with forward Euler at 0.1 ms fires 34 spikes where the converged answer is 11.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Fast Na⁺ gating (sub-ms) next to slow Iₕ (100s of ms)</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Every simulated model</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>—: this is about the simulator, not the cell</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Stability regions, order of accuracy, stiffness</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Neuron workbench Numerics menu (Euler vs exponential Euler)</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Forward Euler on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V = -V/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> multiplies by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\Delta t/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> each step: stable only for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t&lt;2\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>, first-order accurate.","Stiff systems (fast gates plus slow dynamics) need implicit or exponential methods.","Exponential Euler is exact for linear gate equations; halving <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the basic convergence test."],"terms":["forward-euler","numerical-stability","stiff","convergence-test"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, what does forward Euler do at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = 30</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms and at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = 50</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms?","a":"Factors <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 - 1.5 = -0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> (decays, flipping sign each step) and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 - 2.5 = -1.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> (grows, flipping sign: unstable)."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Compute one step of backward Euler and exponential Euler for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = 50</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0 = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV.","a":"Backward: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>2.86</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10/(1+2.5) \\approx 2.86</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV. Exponential: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.82</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10e^{-2.5}\\approx0.82</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV (exact). Backward Euler is stable but inaccurate at this step."},{"kind":"concept","q":"You see 34 spikes at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms and 11 at 0.025 ms. What should you report?","a":"That the 0.1 ms result is not converged. Keep halving until the count stops changing (or switch to a stable method) and report the converged value together with the step and method."}],"eqs":[{"num":"4.4.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_{n+1} = \\left(1 - \\frac{\\Delta t}{\\tau}\\right)V_n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad V_n = \\left(1-\\frac{\\Delta t}{\\tau}\\right)^n V_0</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]}]},{"n":5,"part":"II","pathway":7,"title":"CA1 resonance: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span> and impedance","intro":["CA1 pyramidal neurons prefer inputs in the theta band. Hu, Vervaeke and Storm (2002) showed that this preference has two voltage-dependent sources: the hyperpolarization-activated current <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> near rest and below, and the M-current <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span> near threshold, amplified by the persistent Na⁺ current. This chapter explains the mechanism with one idea, linearization of a gated current, and then turns to measurement: phase, chirp stimuli, units and the quantities <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","The chapter is the measurement backbone for the research thread on interleaved resonance (Ceballos et al. 2025): every number you will compare between a model and a paper comes from the procedures defined here."],"obj":["Linearize a voltage-gated current and decide whether it is restoring or amplifying","Explain the sign and meaning of impedance phase, and why <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> creates a phase lead","Design a chirp protocol and estimate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z(f)</annotation></semantics></math></span> from it, accounting for transients","Report impedance with correct units and an explicit definition of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>"],"prereq":["Chapter 1 (gates)","Chapter 3, Section 3.4 (impedance)"],"sections":[{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/ih-and-m-current/","num":"5.1","obj":["Describe the voltage dependence, reversal potential and kinetics of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span>","Derive the linearized admittance of a gated current","Use the sign rule to classify <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>NaP</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{NaP}</annotation></semantics></math></span> as restoring or amplifying"],"body":"<p>Two slow currents dominate CA1 subthreshold dynamics. <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"hcn\" tabindex=\"0\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span></dfn> flows through HCN channels, which open with <i>hyperpolarization</i>, pass both Na⁺ and K⁺ (reversal near −30 mV) and gate slowly (tens to hundreds of ms). It produces the voltage <b>sag</b> during a hyperpolarizing step and the rebound after it. The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"m-current\" tabindex=\"0\">M-current</dfn> flows through Kv7/KCNQ channels, which open with <i>depolarization</i> from about −60 mV upward, reverse at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K</annotation></semantics></math></span> and gate on a similar slow time scale. It is called “M” because muscarinic receptor activation suppresses it.</p><h3>Linearizing a gated current</h3><p>Consider a current <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_x = \\bar g\\,x\\,(V - E)</annotation></semantics></math></span> whose gate obeys <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_x\\dot x = x_\\infty(V) - x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Perturb around a holding voltage <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0</annotation></semantics></math></span> by a small sinusoid. Two things change: the driving force (instantly) and the gate (with delay). To first order the current change is</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-5-1-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>inst</mtext></msub></munder><mtext> </mtext><mi>δ</mi><mi>V</mi><mtext>  </mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext>  </mtext><munder><munder><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></munder><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta I = \\underbrace{\\bar g\\,x_\\infty(V_0)}_{g_\\text{inst}}\\,\\delta V \\;+\\; \\underbrace{\\bar g\\,(V_0 - E)\\,x_\\infty&#x27;(V_0)}_{g_S}\\,\\frac{\\delta V}{1+i\\omega\\tau_x}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(5.1.1)</span></div><p>So a gated current contributes an instantaneous conductance plus exactly the slow admittance term <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S/(1+i\\omega\\tau_x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"/topics/dynamics/resonance-measurement/\">(3.4.2)</a>. The sign of the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"effective-conductance\" tabindex=\"0\">effective slow conductance</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S</annotation></semantics></math></span> decides everything. With outward-positive currents:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-5-1-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>:</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">{</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.36em\" columnalign=\"left left\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>restoring (resonance)</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>amplifying</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S = \\bar g\\,(V_0 - E)\\,\\frac{dx_\\infty}{dV}:\\qquad \\begin{cases} g_S &gt; 0 &amp; \\text{restoring (resonance)}\\\\ g_S &lt; 0 &amp; \\text{amplifying}\\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(5.1.2)</span></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-5-1-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/ih-im.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/ih-im.svg\" alt=\"Figure 5.1.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 5.1.1.</b> <b>A</b> Illustrative activation curves of opposite direction. <b>B</b> The effective slow conductance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S</annotation></semantics></math></span> of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-5-1-2\">(5.1.2)</a> against holding voltage: each current is most resonance-producing where its activation is steep, Iₕ near −80 mV and I_M near −40 mV. Parameters are illustrative, not fitted.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Current</th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0 - E</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dx_\\infty/dV</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S</annotation></semantics></math></span></th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> at −70 mV (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_h\\approx-30</annotation></semantics></math></span>)</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-40</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> (opens with hyperpolarization)</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>restoring</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span> at −55 mV (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K\\approx-90</annotation></semantics></math></span>)</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>35</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+35</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>restoring</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>NaP</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{NaP}</annotation></semantics></math></span> at −60 mV (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>Na</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>55</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{Na}\\approx+55</annotation></semantics></math></span>)</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>115</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-115</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV</td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>amplifying</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Both <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span> are restoring, by different routes: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> is an inward current that <i>shuts off</i> when the cell depolarizes; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span> is an outward current that <i>turns on</i> when it depolarizes. Either way a depolarization is opposed after a delay <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_x</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This is why Hu et al. found <b>H-resonance</b> at hyperpolarized potentials, where HCN channels are partly open and steeply voltage dependent, and <b>M-resonance</b> near threshold, where Kv7 channels are. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>NaP</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{NaP}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is amplifying: it raises <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span> at the frequencies where M-resonance sits, sharpening the peak.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-5-1-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 5.1.1</p><h4>How big is the slow conductance?</h4><p>Give an H-like gate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>82</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty(V) = 1/(1+e^{(V+82)/8})</annotation></semantics></math></span> (illustrative midpoint −82 mV, slope 8 mV) and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, held at −70 mV. Then <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty = 0.18</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0186</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dx_\\infty/dV \\approx -0.0186</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV⁻¹ and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>40</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0186</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S = 20\\times(-40)\\times(-0.0186)\\approx 15</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS: larger than a typical somatic leak. A modest channel density can therefore dominate the low-frequency impedance.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-5-1-2\"><a href=\"img/tb/ih-sag.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/ih-sag.svg\" alt=\"Figure 5.1.2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 5.1.2.</b> A single-compartment model with an Iₕ-like current (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g = 8</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_h = -30</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 120</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms) and a −150 pA step. Slow HCN opening produces the sag; slow closing after the step produces the rebound. Blocking it (grey) leaves a passive response.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 5.1</span> Linearize a gated current at a holding voltage</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">x_inf, tau_x = gate_curves(V0)\ndxdV  = (x_inf(V0 + h) - x_inf(V0 - h)) / (2*h)\ng_inst = gbar * x_inf(V0)                    # instantaneous part\ng_S    = gbar * (V0 - E_rev) * dxdV          # slow part (sign rule)\nY_current(f) = g_inst + g_S / (1 + 1j * 2*pi*f * tau_x)\nkind = &#x27;restoring&#x27; if g_S &gt; 0 else &#x27;amplifying&#x27;</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Activation curves do not predict resonance on their own</h4><p>Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-5-1-2\">(5.1.2)</a> needs the slope of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the driving force <i>and</i> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_x</annotation></semantics></math></span> at the operating voltage, plus the passive membrane. The same channel can produce strong, weak or no resonance depending on holding potential. Always state <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0</annotation></semantics></math></span> when reporting resonance.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>HCN1/2 and Kv7.2/7.3 channels; muscarinic suppression of I_M</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1 pyramidal cells (H- and M-resonance, Hu et al. 2002)</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Sag/rebound protocols; ZD7288 (Iₕ block), XE991 (I_M block)</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Linearized gated currents, sign rule</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Neuron workbench, CA1 resonance preset</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span>: HCN, opens with hyperpolarization, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_h\\approx-30</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, slow; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span>: Kv7, opens with depolarization above ≈ −60 mV, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K</annotation></semantics></math></span>, slow.","A gated current linearizes to an instantaneous conductance plus <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S/(1+i\\omega\\tau_x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S = \\bar g(V_0-E)x_\\infty&#x27;</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> is restoring and creates resonance (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span>); <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> is amplifying (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>NaP</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{NaP}</annotation></semantics></math></span>)."],"terms":["hcn","m-current","effective-conductance","sag","persistent-sodium"],"problems":[{"kind":"concept","q":"Apply the sign rule to an A-type K⁺ current’s <i>activation</i> at −60 mV. Restoring or amplifying?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_0 - E_K &gt; 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and activation increases with depolarization, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: restoring. (Its inactivation gate, which closes with depolarization, contributes with the opposite sign; the net effect depends on their kinetics.)"},{"kind":"calc","q":"An M-like gate has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dx_\\infty/dV = 0.01</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV⁻¹ at −55 mV and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K = -90</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV. Compute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S = 10\\times35\\times0.01 = 3.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, restoring."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Muscarinic agonists suppress <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span>. What would you predict for resonance measured near −55 mV?","a":"Weaker or absent M-resonance at depolarized potentials (lower <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>, peak shifts or disappears), with little effect on H-resonance measured at −80 mV."}],"eqs":[{"num":"5.1.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>inst</mtext></msub></munder><mtext> </mtext><mi>δ</mi><mi>V</mi><mtext>  </mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext>  </mtext><munder><munder><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></munder><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta I = \\underbrace{\\bar g\\,x_\\infty(V_0)}_{g_\\text{inst}}\\,\\delta V \\;+\\; \\underbrace{\\bar g\\,(V_0 - E)\\,x_\\infty&#x27;(V_0)}_{g_S}\\,\\frac{\\delta V}{1+i\\omega\\tau_x}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"5.1.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>:</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">{</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.36em\" columnalign=\"left left\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>restoring (resonance)</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>amplifying</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S = \\bar g\\,(V_0 - E)\\,\\frac{dx_\\infty}{dV}:\\qquad \\begin{cases} g_S &gt; 0 &amp; \\text{restoring (resonance)}\\\\ g_S &lt; 0 &amp; \\text{amplifying}\\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/impedance-phase/","num":"5.2","obj":["Define impedance phase with an explicit sign convention","Compute the phase of a passive membrane and explain the lag","Explain the inductive phase lead produced by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the zero-phase frequency"],"body":"<p>For an input <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(t) = I_0\\cos\\omega t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, a linear system responds with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(t) = |Z|I_0\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, where <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi = \\arg Z</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"impedance-phase\" tabindex=\"0\">impedance phase</dfn>. In the guide’s convention **positive <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi</annotation></semantics></math></span> means the voltage leads the current**; negative means it lags. Papers differ, so state the convention whenever you plot phase.</p><p>For a passive membrane, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z = R_\\text{in}/(1+i\\omega\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-5-2-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext>RC</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_\\text{RC} = -\\arctan(\\omega\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(5.2.1)</span></div><p>The voltage always lags: the capacitor must charge before the voltage can follow the current. At the corner frequency <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_c = 1/(2\\pi\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></span> the lag is exactly 45°.</p><p>A restoring slow current behaves electrically like an inductor (a “phenomenological inductance”): at low frequencies it opposes slow voltage changes and lets the voltage peak <i>before</i> the current does. The phase becomes <b>positive</b> at low frequencies, crosses zero at the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"zero-phase-frequency\" tabindex=\"0\">zero-phase frequency</dfn> and turns negative at higher frequencies. In CA1 this inductive phase lead is produced by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and grows along the apical dendrite with HCN density (Narayanan &amp; Johnston 2008).</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-5-2-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 5.2.1</p><h4>Phase in the theta resonator</h4><p>In the model of Section 3.4 (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=100</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_S = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms) the phase is about +18° at 1 Hz, crosses zero near 4.8 Hz and is about −12° at the impedance peak, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>5.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R\\approx5.9</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz. The zero-phase frequency and the magnitude peak are close but <b>not the same</b>. For the passive cell alone, the phase at 8 Hz would be −45°.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-5-2-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/phase-curve.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/phase-curve.svg\" alt=\"Figure 5.2.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 5.2.1.</b> Impedance phase for the passive and resonant models of §3.4. The resonant cell leads at low frequencies (inductive behaviour) and crosses zero near 4.8 Hz, below its magnitude peak at 5.9 Hz.</figcaption></figure><p>Phase matters for timing. In a network oscillating at theta, a cell with a phase lead at theta responds earlier in the cycle than a passive one; a gradient of phase leads along the dendrite partly compensates for the delay that the cable introduces.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 5.2</span> Phase from recordings</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">I_hat = fft(I_trace);  V_hat = fft(V_trace)\nZ_hat = V_hat / I_hat                         # complex, at stimulated frequencies\nphase_deg = degrees(angle(Z_hat))            # + means voltage leads (guide convention)\nf_zero = frequency where phase_deg crosses 0\nlag_ms = -phase_deg / 360 * 1000 / f         # same information as a time lag</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Phase of what, relative to what?</h4><p>Phase can be reported as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg Z</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg Y</annotation></semantics></math></span> (opposite sign), in degrees or radians, or as a time lag (phase / <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi f</annotation></semantics></math></span>). The phase of a <i>transfer</i> impedance (dendrite to soma) also includes cable delay. Write down which one you computed.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Inductive phase lead from Iₕ</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1 apical dendrites, lead increasing with distance</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Phase of V relative to injected sinusoids</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Argument of complex numbers</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Neuron workbench, impedance tab (phase)</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V = |Z|I_0\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi = \\arg Z</annotation></semantics></math></span>; positive means voltage leads (guide convention).","Passive membranes lag: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi = -\\arctan\\omega\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>, −45° at the corner frequency.","Restoring slow currents act like inductors: phase lead at low frequency, zero crossing near (but not at) <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span>."],"terms":["impedance-phase","zero-phase-frequency","inductance"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"Compute the phase of a passive membrane with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms at 2 Hz and at 8 Hz.","a":"2 Hz: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.251</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\tau = 0.251</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>14.1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi = -14.1°</annotation></semantics></math></span>. 8 Hz: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\tau = 1.005</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>45.2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi\\approx-45.2°</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"calc","q":"A 6 Hz response has a phase of −30°. Convert to a time lag.","a":"Lag <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>360</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mtext> s</mtext><mo>≈</mo><mn>13.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 30/360 \\times 1/6\\ \\text{s} \\approx 13.9</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Blocking <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> in a distal dendrite: predict the effect on phase at 2 Hz.","a":"The inductive lead disappears; the phase becomes negative (a lag), as for a passive membrane, and the zero-phase frequency vanishes."}],"eqs":[{"num":"5.2.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext>RC</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_\\text{RC} = -\\arctan(\\omega\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/chirp-input/","num":"5.3","obj":["Write a linear chirp and its instantaneous frequency","Estimate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z(f)</annotation></semantics></math></span> as a ratio of Fourier transforms and state its frequency resolution","Identify and remove onset transients; choose sweep duration and amplitude"],"body":"<p>Measuring impedance one sinusoid at a time is slow. A <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"chirp\" tabindex=\"0\">chirp</dfn> (or ZAP) current sweeps frequency continuously:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-5-3-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>inst</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(t) = I_0\\sin\\!\\Big(2\\pi\\big(f_0 t + \\tfrac{f_1 - f_0}{2T}\\,t^2\\big)\\Big),\\qquad f_\\text{inst}(t) = f_0 + \\frac{f_1 - f_0}{T}\\,t</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(5.3.1)</span></div><p>The impedance is estimated as the ratio of the Fourier transforms of voltage and current, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat Z(f) = \\mathcal F[V](f)/\\mathcal F[I](f)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, evaluated where the stimulus has power (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0\\le f\\le f_1</annotation></semantics></math></span>). Puil, Gimbarzevsky and Miura (1986) introduced this approach for neurons; it is the standard protocol for CA1 resonance.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-5-3-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 5.3.1</p><h4>Designing a sweep</h4><p>The lab uses <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0 = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_1 = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 4</annotation></semantics></math></span> s. At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> s the instantaneous frequency is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>19.5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5 + 19.5\\times2/4 = 10.25</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz. The sweep spends only <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>19.5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.82</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4/(19.5/4)\\approx0.82</annotation></semantics></math></span> s between 4 and 8 Hz; a 20 s sweep would spend 4.1 s there.</p><p>The FFT of a 4 s record has frequency resolution <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/T = 0.25</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span> cannot be resolved better than about ±0.1 Hz without longer records or curve fitting (the guide’s analysis fits a parabola through the peak and its neighbours on a log-frequency axis).</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-5-3-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/chirp.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/chirp.svg\" alt=\"Figure 5.3.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 5.3.1.</b> A chirp experiment on the linear resonator of §3.4. <b>A</b> The voltage envelope peaks when the instantaneous frequency passes <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span>. <b>B</b> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\hat Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span> from the ratio of Fourier transforms (dots) against the exact curve: close, with scatter at the ends of the band and from the onset transient.</figcaption></figure><h3>Transients and nonstationarity</h3><p>When the chirp starts, the cell has not reached its periodic response: an onset <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"transient\" tabindex=\"0\">transient</dfn> decays with the cell’s slowest time constant. After <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> it is down to 5%, after <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> to under 1%; for a cell whose slow current has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_S = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, that is 0.3–0.5 s, a large fraction of a 4 s sweep. Remedies are to precede the chirp with a sinusoid at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, to discard the first part of the record, or to start from the model’s steady state.</p><p>A fast sweep also violates the assumption that the response at each instant is the steady-state response at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>inst</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\text{inst}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Slow sweeps, or several sweep durations compared with each other, test whether the estimate has converged.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 5.3</span> Chirp protocol and impedance estimate</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">t = arange(0, T, dt)\nI = I0 * sin(2*pi*(f0*t + (f1 - f0) / (2*T) * t**2))\nI = concatenate([I0*sin(2*pi*f0*t_pre), I])    # lead-in sinusoid absorbs the transient\nV = record_or_simulate(I) - V_hold\nkeep = t &gt; 5 * tau_slow                        # drop what remains of the onset\nZ = fft(V[keep]) / fft(I[keep])\nuse only f in [f0, f1]; df = 1 / duration(keep)\nrepeat with I0/2: estimate must not change     # linearity check</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Stay in the linear range</h4><p><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat Z</annotation></semantics></math></span> assumes linearity. If <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_0</annotation></semantics></math></span> is large enough to recruit channels nonlinearly or to evoke spikes, the ratio is no longer an impedance. Check by halving <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: a linear estimate does not change.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Cells respond to theta-modulated input in vivo</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1 slices; in vivo whole-cell in head-fixed mice</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>ZAP protocol (Puil et al. 1986)</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Fourier transforms, frequency resolution</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Research code: impedance analysis (ir_ladder)</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["A linear chirp has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>inst</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\text{inst} = f_0 + (f_1-f_0)t/T</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat Z(f) = \\mathcal F[V]/\\mathcal F[I]</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Frequency resolution is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>record</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/T_\\text{record}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Remove onset transients, check sweep-duration convergence, and keep amplitudes in the linear range."],"terms":["chirp","transient","frequency-resolution"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0 = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_1 = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> s, when does the sweep pass 8 Hz?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>19.5</mn><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>10</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>3.85</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8 = 0.5 + 19.5t/10 \\Rightarrow t\\approx3.85</annotation></semantics></math></span> s."},{"kind":"calc","q":"What frequency resolution does a 10 s record give, and how many FFT bins fall between 4 and 8 Hz?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f = 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz; about 40 bins (41 including both ends)."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Doubling <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_0</annotation></semantics></math></span> changes your estimated <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span> from 5.0 to 4.4 Hz. What does that tell you?","a":"The response is not linear at these amplitudes; channels are being recruited differently. Reduce <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_0</annotation></semantics></math></span> until <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat Z</annotation></semantics></math></span> stops changing, and report the amplitude used."}],"eqs":[{"num":"5.3.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>inst</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(t) = I_0\\sin\\!\\Big(2\\pi\\big(f_0 t + \\tfrac{f_1 - f_0}{2T}\\,t^2\\big)\\Big),\\qquad f_\\text{inst}(t) = f_0 + \\frac{f_1 - f_0}{T}\\,t</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/impedance-units/","num":"5.4","obj":["Convert voltage/current ratios to MΩ correctly","Use consistent amplitude conventions (peak, peak-to-peak, RMS)","Report <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the protocol so the numbers can be compared"],"body":"<p>Impedance is voltage per current. The unit bookkeeping is simple but easy to get wrong by factors of 1000:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-5-4-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> mV</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> pA</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> G</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn><mtext> M</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> mV</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> nA</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> M</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{1\\ \\text{mV}}{1\\ \\text{pA}} = 1\\ \\text{G}\\Omega = 1000\\ \\text{M}\\Omega,\\qquad \\frac{1\\ \\text{mV}}{1\\ \\text{nA}} = 1\\ \\text{M}\\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(5.4.1)</span></div><p>Frequency in Hz and angular frequency in rad/s differ by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi</annotation></semantics></math></span>; formulas such as Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"/topics/dynamics/resonance-measurement/\">(3.4.1)</a> use <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega = 2\\pi f</annotation></semantics></math></span>. If time is in ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span> must be in rad/ms.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-5-4-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 5.4.1</p><h4>Amplitude conventions</h4><p>A 30 pA (peak) sinusoid produces a 3 mV (peak) voltage oscillation: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>30</mn><mtext> G</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z| = 3/30\\ \\text{G}\\Omega = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ. If you mistakenly divide the peak-to-peak voltage (6 mV) by the peak current, you report 200 MΩ, a factor-of-two error that looks entirely plausible. Peak, peak-to-peak and RMS are all fine <i>if both signals use the same one</i>.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-5-4-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/amplitudes.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/amplitudes.svg\" alt=\"Figure 5.4.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 5.4.1.</b> Peak, peak-to-peak and RMS of one sinusoid. Any can be used for impedance as long as the current is measured the same way.</figcaption></figure><h3>A reporting checklist</h3><ul class=\"tb-list\"><li>Holding potential (and how it was set: current injection or not).</li><li>Recording site (soma or dendritic distance) and whether <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math></span> is input or transfer.</li><li>Stimulus: type, frequency range, duration, amplitude, and how transients were handled.</li><li>Temperature.</li><li>Definitions: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span> = frequency of max <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> = <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(f_R)|/|Z(f_\\text{ref})|</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_\\text{ref}</annotation></semantics></math></span> stated; phase convention.</li></ul><p>With these, a model’s resonance can be compared with an experiment, and two models with each other. Without them, a difference of 1 Hz in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span> or 0.3 in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> may be entirely methodological.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 5.4</span> Units-safe impedance report</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">amp_V = peak(V_ss)            # mV, steady-state cycles only\namp_I = peak(I)               # pA, SAME convention as amp_V\nZ_MOhm = amp_V / amp_I * 1000                  # mV/pA = GOhm\nreport = {&#x27;f_R_Hz&#x27;: f_R, &#x27;Q&#x27;: Q, &#x27;Q_ref_Hz&#x27;: 0.5, &#x27;Z_peak_MOhm&#x27;: Z_max,\n          &#x27;V_hold_mV&#x27;: V_hold, &#x27;site&#x27;: &#x27;soma&#x27;, &#x27;temp_C&#x27;: 34,\n          &#x27;stimulus&#x27;: &#x27;chirp 0.5-20 Hz, 20 s, 20 pA&#x27;, &#x27;phase_sign&#x27;: &#x27;+ = V leads&#x27;}</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Normalized plots hide amplitude</h4><p>Plotting <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|/|Z|_\\text{max}</annotation></semantics></math></span> makes curves easy to compare in shape but removes input resistance, which is often the bigger effect of a manipulation. Show absolute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span> in MΩ at least once.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>—</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Any impedance comparison across papers</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Peak, peak-to-peak, RMS conventions</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Unit algebra</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Store units in variable names or with a units library (Brian2 units)</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["mV/pA = GΩ; mV/nA = MΩ; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega = 2\\pi f</annotation></semantics></math></span> in consistent time units.","Use the same amplitude convention for voltage and current.","Report holding potential, site, stimulus, temperature and the definitions of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> and phase."],"terms":["impedance","q-factor","resonance-frequency"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"A 0.05 nA peak current gives a 2 mV peak response. What is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.05</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/0.05 = 40</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ."},{"kind":"calc","q":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>150</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(f_R)| = 150</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mtext> Hz</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(0.5\\text{ Hz})| = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(0)| = 90</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ. Give <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> under both common definitions.","a":"Relative to 0.5 Hz: 1.5. Relative to DC: 1.67. Report which one you used."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why might a model with <i>higher</i> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> than an experiment still have the <i>same</i> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(f_R)|</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a ratio. The model could have a lower low-frequency impedance (more slow restoring conductance) with the same peak. Compare absolute curves, not only <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>."}],"eqs":[{"num":"5.4.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> mV</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> pA</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> G</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn><mtext> M</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> mV</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> nA</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext> M</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{1\\ \\text{mV}}{1\\ \\text{pA}} = 1\\ \\text{G}\\Omega = 1000\\ \\text{M}\\Omega,\\qquad \\frac{1\\ \\text{mV}}{1\\ \\text{nA}} = 1\\ \\text{M}\\Omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]}]},{"n":6,"part":"III","pathway":2,"title":"Synapses and excitation–inhibition","intro":["Neurons communicate mainly through chemical synapses, where a presynaptic spike releases transmitter that opens postsynaptic channels, and to a lesser extent through electrical synapses (gap junctions). In models, both become currents in the postsynaptic current balance. The key modelling decision is to treat a synapse as a <b>conductance with a reversal potential</b>, not as a fixed current.","This chapter quantifies release, explains why the same conductance can excite, inhibit or merely shunt, models synaptic time courses, and introduces the feedforward and feedback inhibitory motifs that shape CA1 activity."],"obj":["Describe release with the binomial quantal model and gap junctions with a coupling coefficient","Compute steady-state voltage under combined excitatory, inhibitory and leak conductances","Model synaptic time courses and compute peak time and summation","Explain feedforward and feedback inhibition and the linear E–I loop"],"prereq":["Chapter 1 (driving force)","Chapter 2 helps for location effects"],"sections":[{"route":"/topics/synapses/chemical-electrical/","num":"6.1","obj":["Explain the binomial model of release: mean <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Npq</annotation></semantics></math></span> and failure probability","Write the gap-junction current and the steady coupling coefficient","Contrast chemical and electrical synapses in sign, delay, direction and plasticity"],"body":"<p>At a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"chemical-synapse\" tabindex=\"0\">chemical synapse</dfn> an action potential opens presynaptic Ca²⁺ channels; Ca²⁺ triggers fusion of vesicles, each releasing a roughly fixed amount of transmitter (a <b>quantum</b>). Del Castillo and Katz (1954) described release at the neuromuscular junction as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> independent release sites, each releasing with probability <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span> and producing a response of size <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"quantal-release\" tabindex=\"0\">binomial quantal model</dfn> predicts</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-6-1-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mtext>EPSC</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>failure</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\text{EPSC}\\rangle = Npq,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var} = Np(1-p)\\,q^2,\\qquad P(\\text{failure}) = (1-p)^N</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(6.1.1)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-6-1-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 6.1.1</p><h4>A CA3→CA1 connection</h4><p>Take <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=5</annotation></semantics></math></span> sites, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=10</annotation></semantics></math></span> pA. The mean EPSC is 15 pA, failures occur on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.7</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>17</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.7)^5\\approx17\\%</annotation></semantics></math></span> of trials and the coefficient of variation is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mi>q</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.68</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{Np(1-p)}\\,q/(Npq)\\approx0.68</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Single Schaffer-collateral connections in CA1 are indeed unreliable; reliability at the circuit level comes from many convergent inputs and from short-term plasticity.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-6-1-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/quantal.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/quantal.svg\" alt=\"Figure 6.1.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 6.1.1.</b> <b>A</b> The binomial amplitude distribution of the worked example, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-1-1\">(6.1.1)</a>: failures on 17% of trials. <b>B</b> The steady gap-junction coupling coefficient, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-1-2\">(6.1.2)</a>, for two cell leak conductances.</figcaption></figure><p>An <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"electrical-synapse\" tabindex=\"0\">electrical synapse</dfn> is a cluster of connexin channels (gap junctions) joining two cytoplasms. It passes current in proportion to the voltage difference, with no delay:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-6-1-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mtext>into </mtext><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mtext>CC</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"2.4em\" maxsize=\"2.4em\">∣</mo><mtext>steady</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\text{into }1} = g_\\text{gap}\\,(V_2 - V_1),\\qquad \\text{CC} = \\frac{\\Delta V_1}{\\Delta V_2}\\bigg|_\\text{steady} = \\frac{g_\\text{gap}}{g_\\text{gap} + g_{L,1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(6.1.2)</span></div><p>The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"coupling-coefficient\" tabindex=\"0\">coupling coefficient</dfn> CC is the fraction of a steady voltage change in cell 2 seen in cell 1. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_\\text{gap} = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_{L,1} = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, CC ≈ 0.17. For fast signals CC is smaller, because cell 1’s capacitance shunts them: gap junctions are low-pass filters. They are common between interneurons (e.g. parvalbumin basket cells) and support synchrony.</p><div class=\"table-scroll tb-table\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Chemical</th><th>Electrical</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sign</td><td>excitatory or inhibitory, set by receptor and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{syn}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td><td>follows the voltage difference</td></tr><tr><td>Delay</td><td>≈ 0.5–1 ms (release, diffusion, binding)</td><td>essentially none</td></tr><tr><td>Direction</td><td>one-way</td><td>usually two-way</td></tr><tr><td>Gain</td><td>can amplify (one spike → many channels)</td><td>always attenuates</td></tr><tr><td>Plasticity</td><td>short- and long-term</td><td>modulated, less so</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 6.1</span> Quantal analysis from repeated trials</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">amps = [peak_EPSC(trial) for trial in trials]\nfailures = fraction(amps &lt; noise_threshold)\nmean, var = mean(amps), variance(amps)\n# binomial: mean = N p q,  var = N p (1-p) q**2,  failures = (1-p)**N\n# vary release probability (e.g. extracellular Ca2+) and fit var-mean parabola:\nfit var = q*mean - mean**2 / N   -&gt;  q, N   -&gt;  p = mean / (N q)</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Sign convention change</h4><p>Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-1-2\">(6.1.2)</a> is written as current <i>into</i> cell 1, the opposite of the outward-positive membrane convention, because it is an axial current between cells. When you add it to cell 1’s current balance it enters with a plus sign, like the axial term in Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/\">(1.4.1)</a>.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Vesicle release, connexin gap junctions</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA3→CA1 Schaffer synapses; PV basket-cell gap-junction networks</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Paired recordings, failure analysis, variance–mean analysis</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Binomial statistics</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Synapse workbench, AMPA preset</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Binomial release: mean <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Npq</annotation></semantics></math></span>, variance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Np(1-p)q^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, failures <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-p)^N</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Gap junctions pass <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_\\text{gap}(V_2 - V_1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; steady coupling coefficient <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_\\text{gap}/(g_\\text{gap}+g_L)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, lower for fast signals.","Chemical synapses are delayed, directional and plastic; electrical ones fast, bidirectional and attenuating."],"terms":["chemical-synapse","quantal-release","electrical-synapse","coupling-coefficient"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> pA, what are the mean EPSC and the failure probability?","a":"Mean <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 25</annotation></semantics></math></span> pA. Failures <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>0.5</mn><mn>10</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 0.5^{10}\\approx0.001</annotation></semantics></math></span>: essentially never."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Two cells are coupled by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_\\text{gap} = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS; cell 1 has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS. Cell 2 is held 10 mV above rest. How much does cell 1 depolarize at steady state?","a":"CC <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 0.5/2.5 = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so 2 mV."},{"kind":"concept","q":"In a train of presynaptic spikes, which binomial parameter does short-term facilitation most plausibly change?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span>: residual Ca²⁺ from earlier spikes raises release probability. Depression can reflect lower available <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> (vesicle depletion)."}],"eqs":[{"num":"6.1.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mtext>EPSC</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>failure</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\text{EPSC}\\rangle = Npq,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var} = Np(1-p)\\,q^2,\\qquad P(\\text{failure}) = (1-p)^N</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"6.1.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mtext>into </mtext><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mtext>CC</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"2.4em\" maxsize=\"2.4em\">∣</mo><mtext>steady</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\text{into }1} = g_\\text{gap}\\,(V_2 - V_1),\\qquad \\text{CC} = \\frac{\\Delta V_1}{\\Delta V_2}\\bigg|_\\text{steady} = \\frac{g_\\text{gap}}{g_\\text{gap} + g_{L,1}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/synapses/synaptic-conductance/","num":"6.2","obj":["Write the synaptic current as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_\\text{syn}(V - E_\\text{syn})</annotation></semantics></math></span> and give typical reversal potentials","Compute steady voltage under several conductances and explain shunting inhibition","Include voltage-dependent Mg²⁺ block for NMDA receptors"],"body":"<p>A synaptic current is ohmic in the open conductance, like any channel: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{syn} = g_\\text{syn}(t)\\,(V - E_\\text{syn})</annotation></semantics></math></span>. AMPA and NMDA receptors pass Na⁺ and K⁺ and reverse near <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_e\\approx0</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV; GABA<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>A</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_\\text{A}</annotation></semantics></math></span> receptors pass mainly Cl⁻ (and some HCO₃⁻) and reverse near <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_i\\approx-70</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV in mature CA1 pyramidal cells; GABA<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>B</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_\\text{B}</annotation></semantics></math></span> receptors open K⁺ channels and reverse near <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_K</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><p>With steady conductances the membrane settles where total current vanishes, a conductance-weighted average of reversal potentials:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-6-2-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\infty = \\frac{g_L E_L + g_e E_e + g_i E_i}{g_L + g_e + g_i},\\qquad R_\\text{in} = \\frac{1}{g_L + g_e + g_i}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(6.2.1)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-6-2-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 6.2.1</p><h4>Shunting inhibition</h4><p>Let <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_L = -65</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV and an excitatory <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_e = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_e = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>). Then <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>325</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>7</mn><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>46.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\infty = -325/7\\approx-46.4</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, an 18.6 mV depolarization. Add <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_i = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_i = -65</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, <i>equal to rest</i>. Alone, this inhibition would not move the voltage at all. Together, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>650</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>12</mn><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>54.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\infty = -650/12\\approx-54.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV: the depolarization drops to 10.8 mV and input resistance from 143 to 83 MΩ.</p><p>This is <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"shunting-inhibition\" tabindex=\"0\">shunting inhibition</dfn>: inhibition acting by conductance rather than by hyperpolarization. It divides the excitatory effect rather than subtracting a fixed amount, and it is invisible if you only look at inhibition alone.</p></div><h3>NMDA receptors and the magnesium block</h3><p>NMDA receptors are blocked by extracellular Mg²⁺ at negative voltages. Jahr and Stevens (1990) fitted the unblocked fraction as</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-6-2-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mtext>Mg</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>3.57</mn><mtext> mM</mtext></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.062</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(V) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\dfrac{[\\text{Mg}^{2+}]_o}{3.57\\ \\text{mM}}\\,e^{-0.062\\,V}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(6.2.2)</span></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-6-2-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/shunt-mg.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/shunt-mg.svg\" alt=\"Figure 6.2.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 6.2.1.</b> <b>A</b> Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-2-1\">(6.2.1)</a> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_e = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS: inhibition reversing at rest (shunting) reduces the depolarization divisively without hyperpolarizing; inhibition at −75 mV can push below rest. <b>B</b> NMDA Mg²⁺ block, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-2-2\">(6.2.2)</a>.</figcaption></figure><p>with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> in mV. At 1 mM Mg²⁺, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.06</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\approx0.06</annotation></semantics></math></span> at −65 mV, 0.23 at −40 mV and 0.51 at −20 mV. NMDA current therefore requires both transmitter (presynaptic activity) and depolarization (postsynaptic activity), which makes the receptor a coincidence detector and a natural substrate for Hebbian plasticity (Ch. 9).</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 6.2</span> Conductance-based synapses with NMDA block</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">def I_syn(V, t):\n    I = g_ampa(t) * (V - 0)\n    I += g_nmda(t) * B(V) * (V - 0)          # B(V) = 1/(1 + Mg/3.57 * exp(-0.062 V))\n    I += g_gabaA(t) * (V - E_Cl)\n    return I                                  # outward-positive, enters C dV/dt = -I_syn ...\nV_inf = (gL*EL + ge*Ee + gi*Ei) / (gL + ge + gi)   # steady-state check</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Current-based synapses</h4><p>Many network models use current-based synapses, adding a fixed <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{syn}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span> regardless of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span>. This is cheaper and sometimes adequate, but it cannot express shunting, cannot saturate at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\text{syn}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and makes inhibition always hyperpolarizing. Check whether your conclusion depends on that choice.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>AMPA, NMDA, GABA_A, GABA_B receptors</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1 dendrites: NMDA spikes, somatic shunting by basket cells</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Reversal potential of PSCs; dynamic clamp</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Conductance-weighted averages, Mg²⁺ block function</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Synapse workbench, shunting preset</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{syn} = g_\\text{syn}(V-E_\\text{syn})</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_e\\approx0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mtext>GABA</mtext><mtext>A</mtext></msub></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\text{GABA}_\\text{A}}\\approx-70</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mtext>GABA</mtext><mtext>B</mtext></msub></msub><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\text{GABA}_\\text{B}}\\approx E_K</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Steady voltage is a conductance-weighted average of reversal potentials (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-2-1\">(6.2.1)</a>).","Inhibition with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_i\\approx V</annotation></semantics></math></span> shunts: it reduces excitation divisively without hyperpolarizing.","NMDA current is gated by Mg²⁺ block, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(V)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, making it a coincidence detector."],"terms":["shunting-inhibition","nmda","mg-block","reversal-potential"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_L = -65</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, what does a <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_i = 4</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS inhibitory conductance with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_i = -75</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV do on its own?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>×</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>75</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>9</mn><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>69.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\infty = (5\\times-65 + 4\\times-75)/9 \\approx -69.4</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV: a 4.4 mV hyperpolarization, and input resistance falls from 200 to 111 MΩ."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Using Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-2-2\">(6.2.2)</a>, by what factor does NMDA conductance increase when a spine depolarizes from −65 to −20 mV?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.51</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.060</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>8.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(-20)/B(-65)\\approx0.51/0.060\\approx8.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why does shunting inhibition reduce an EPSP more when the inhibitory synapse is near the excitatory one than when it is far away on another branch?","a":"The shunt acts on the local input resistance. Nearby, it directly lowers <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z_{dd}</annotation></semantics></math></span> where the excitatory current enters; far away on another branch, it mainly changes the shared path, so the effect is diluted (location dependence, Ch. 2)."}],"eqs":[{"num":"6.2.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\infty = \\frac{g_L E_L + g_e E_e + g_i E_i}{g_L + g_e + g_i},\\qquad R_\\text{in} = \\frac{1}{g_L + g_e + g_i}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"6.2.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mtext>Mg</mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>3.57</mn><mtext> mM</mtext></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.062</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B(V) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\dfrac{[\\text{Mg}^{2+}]_o}{3.57\\ \\text{mM}}\\,e^{-0.062\\,V}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/synapses/timing-location/","num":"6.3","obj":["Model synaptic conductance with exponential, alpha and difference-of-exponentials kernels","Compute the peak time of a dual-exponential conductance","Explain temporal summation and the CA1 feedforward-inhibition window"],"body":"<p>After a presynaptic spike at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the postsynaptic conductance rises and decays. Three <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"synaptic-kernel\" tabindex=\"0\">kernels</dfn> are standard:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-6-3-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(t) = \\bar g\\,e^{-t/\\tau},\\qquad g(t) = \\bar g\\,\\frac{t}{\\tau}e^{1-t/\\tau},\\qquad g(t) = \\bar g\\,\\eta\\left(e^{-t/\\tau_d} - e^{-t/\\tau_r}\\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(6.3.1)</span></div><p>(single exponential, alpha function, difference of exponentials), each for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\ge0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The last separates rise <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_r</annotation></semantics></math></span> from decay <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta</annotation></semantics></math></span> normalizes the peak to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Its peak time is</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-6-3-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_\\text{peak} = \\frac{\\tau_r\\tau_d}{\\tau_d - \\tau_r}\\ln\\frac{\\tau_d}{\\tau_r}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(6.3.2)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-6-3-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 6.3.1</p><h4>AMPA and NMDA time courses</h4><p>AMPA-like kinetics (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_r = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_d = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms) peak at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2.5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_\\text{peak} = (2.5/4.5)\\ln10\\approx1.28</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.44</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta\\approx1.44</annotation></semantics></math></span>. NMDA-like kinetics (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_r = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_d = 50</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms) peak at about 6.7 ms and last ten times longer, which is why NMDA receptors dominate temporal summation over tens of milliseconds.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-6-3-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/kernels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/kernels.svg\" alt=\"Figure 6.3.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 6.3.1.</b> <b>A</b> The kernels of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-3-1\">(6.3.1)</a>, normalized to their peaks. <b>B</b> Two AMPA-like inputs summate strongly 2 ms apart, partly at 10 ms and not at 25 ms.</figcaption></figure><p>Successive inputs add if they arrive before earlier conductances have decayed: <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"temporal-summation\" tabindex=\"0\">temporal summation</dfn>. Two inputs 2 ms apart with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms overlap strongly (the first is still at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>67</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2/5}\\approx67\\%</annotation></semantics></math></span>); 20 ms apart, hardly at all. Dendritic location adds to the timing problem: distal EPSPs reach the soma attenuated, delayed and broadened (Ch. 2), unless <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and synaptic scaling compensate.</p><h3>The feedforward inhibition window</h3><p>Schaffer collaterals excite CA1 pyramidal cells and, in parallel, interneurons that inhibit the same pyramidal cells about 2 ms later. Pouille and Scanziani (2001) showed that this <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"feedforward-inhibition\" tabindex=\"0\">feedforward inhibition</dfn> narrows the time window during which excitatory inputs can summate to reach threshold: only inputs arriving within a few milliseconds of each other succeed. The circuit turns a cell that integrates over tens of milliseconds into a coincidence detector.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 6.3</span> Event-driven synaptic conductance</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\"># difference of exponentials as two decaying traces (no kernel convolution needed)\non presynaptic spike: a += w * eta;  b += w * eta\nevery step:\n    a *= exp(-dt / tau_d)\n    b *= exp(-dt / tau_r)\n    g  = a - b                                # rises with tau_r, decays with tau_d\neta = 1 / (exp(-t_peak/tau_d) - exp(-t_peak/tau_r))   # peak normalization</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Normalize consistently</h4><p>Comparing kernels by peak conductance or by total charge (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>g</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int g\\,dt</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which equals <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the exponential) gives different conclusions. State whether two synapse models were matched on peak or on charge.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Receptor kinetics, dendritic filtering, feedforward interneurons</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1 feedforward inhibition window (Pouille &amp; Scanziani 2001)</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Paired-pulse and timed stimulation</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Convolution kernels, peak-time formula</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Synapse workbench, feedforward-inhibition preset</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Synaptic conductance kernels: exponential, alpha, difference of exponentials (rise <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_r</annotation></semantics></math></span>, decay <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_d</annotation></semantics></math></span>).","Dual-exponential peak at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_\\text{peak} = \\frac{\\tau_r\\tau_d}{\\tau_d-\\tau_r}\\ln(\\tau_d/\\tau_r)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; AMPA ≈ 1 ms, NMDA several ms.","Temporal summation depends on decay time; feedforward inhibition in CA1 shortens the summation window to a few ms."],"terms":["synaptic-kernel","temporal-summation","feedforward-inhibition"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_r = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_d = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, compute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_\\text{peak}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>2.56</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(10/9)\\ln10\\approx2.56</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms."},{"kind":"calc","q":"What is the total charge-equivalent <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msubsup><mi>g</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_0^\\infty g\\,dt</annotation></semantics></math></span> of an exponential conductance with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g\\tau = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS·ms. (Multiplying by a driving force in mV gives charge in fC: e.g. at 65 mV, 325 fC.)"},{"kind":"concept","q":"Feedforward inhibition is blocked with a GABA<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>A</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_\\text{A}</annotation></semantics></math></span> antagonist. Predict the effect on the range of input timing that can trigger a spike.","a":"The window widens: inputs separated by tens of ms can summate, so spikes become less precisely timed relative to the input."}],"eqs":[{"num":"6.3.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(t) = \\bar g\\,e^{-t/\\tau},\\qquad g(t) = \\bar g\\,\\frac{t}{\\tau}e^{1-t/\\tau},\\qquad g(t) = \\bar g\\,\\eta\\left(e^{-t/\\tau_d} - e^{-t/\\tau_r}\\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"6.3.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_\\text{peak} = \\frac{\\tau_r\\tau_d}{\\tau_d - \\tau_r}\\ln\\frac{\\tau_d}{\\tau_r}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/synapses/ei-feedback/","num":"6.4","obj":["Distinguish feedforward, feedback and lateral inhibition","Linearize an E–I rate loop and find when it oscillates","Explain what E/I balance does and does not mean"],"body":"<p>Inhibitory interneurons enter circuits in a few recurring motifs. In <b>feedforward</b> inhibition an input drives both a principal cell and an interneuron that inhibits it. In <b>feedback</b> (recurrent) inhibition principal cells drive interneurons that inhibit them back. <b>Lateral</b> inhibition spreads that feedback to neighbours. In CA1, basket cells mediate both feedforward (from CA3) and feedback (from CA1 pyramidal cells) inhibition; O-LM cells provide feedback inhibition to distal dendrites.</p><p>Model an excitatory and an inhibitory population as rates (developed in Ch. 7). Linearized around an operating point with gains absorbed into the weights:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-6-4-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>e</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>e</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>i</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\begin{pmatrix}\\dot e\\\\ \\dot i\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}-1 + w_{EE} &amp; -w_{EI}\\\\ w_{IE} &amp; -1 - w_{II}\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}e\\\\ i\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(6.4.1)</span></div><p>The trace is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE} - w_{II} - 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the determinant <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1 - w_{EE})(1 + w_{II}) + w_{EI}w_{IE}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The loop is stable when the trace is negative and the determinant positive; strong E→I→E feedback makes the determinant large and the eigenvalues complex, so the loop <b>rings</b>. When recurrent excitation pushes the trace through zero, the loop oscillates (a Hopf bifurcation, Ch. 4).</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-6-4-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 6.4.1</p><h4>Ringing in an E–I loop</h4><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE} = 1.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EI} = w_{IE} = 3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{II} = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = -0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>8.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = -0.5 + 9 = 8.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, eigenvalues <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>2.90</mn><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-0.25\\pm2.90i)/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A perturbation decays slowly while oscillating with period <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>τ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2.9</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>2.2</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi\\tau/2.9\\approx2.2\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\approx5</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms that is ≈ 11 ms, a gamma-range period (≈ 90 Hz). Raising <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE}</annotation></semantics></math></span> to 2 brings <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span> to 0: sustained oscillation.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-6-4-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/ei-loop.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/ei-loop.svg\" alt=\"Figure 6.4.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 6.4.1.</b> The linearized E–I loop of the worked example (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-4-1\">(6.4.1)</a>). <b>A</b> A perturbation of E rings and decays, with I lagging E. <b>B</b> The same solution in the (E, I) plane: a stable spiral.</figcaption></figure><p>This E–I mechanism, with the period set largely by inhibitory synaptic decay, is the basis of the PING (pyramidal–interneuron network gamma) model of hippocampal gamma. In the alternative ING mechanism, mutually connected interneurons alone generate the rhythm.</p><h3>What balance means</h3><p><dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"ei-balance\" tabindex=\"0\">E/I balance</dfn> usually means that excitatory and inhibitory <i>currents</i> onto a cell are large but nearly cancel, so that the mean input is near threshold and spikes are driven by fluctuations. It does not mean equal numbers of E and I cells (in CA1, interneurons are roughly 10% of neurons), nor equal synaptic weights. Balance can be loose (on average over seconds) or tight (tracking within milliseconds).</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 6.4</span> Is an E–I loop going to oscillate?</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">J = [[-1 + wEE, -wEI],\n     [ wIE,     -1 - wII]] / tau            # gains folded into weights\nT, D = trace(J), det(J)\nif T &lt; 0 and D &gt; 0:\n    ringing = (T*T &lt; 4*D);  f_ring = sqrt(D - T*T/4) / (2*pi)\nelse: unstable -&gt; simulate the nonlinear model to find the limit cycle</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Rate loops lose spike timing</h4><p>Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-4-1\">(6.4.1)</a> predicts when a population rhythm appears and roughly its frequency, but not which cells fire on which cycle. Whether the rhythm is carried by sparse, irregular single-cell firing or by every cell firing every cycle needs a spiking network (Ch. 7).</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Basket, O-LM and bistratified interneurons</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1 and CA3 gamma (PING/ING)</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>LFP gamma, interneuron–pyramid spike timing</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Linear stability of a 2-D loop</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Wilson–Cowan lab; Synapse workbench</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Inhibition enters as feedforward, feedback and lateral motifs; CA1 uses all three.","A linearized E–I loop has trace <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE}-w_{II}-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and determinant <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-w_{EE})(1+w_{II})+w_{EI}w_{IE}</annotation></semantics></math></span>; strong E–I coupling makes it ring, and loss of stability gives oscillation.","E/I balance means near-cancelling currents, not equal cell numbers."],"terms":["feedforward-inhibition","feedback-inhibition","ei-balance","ping"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE}=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EI}=w_{IE}=2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{II}=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, compute trace, determinant and eigenvalues of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-6-4-1\">(6.4.1)</a>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = -1 + 4 = 3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>i</mi><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda = \\pm i\\sqrt3</annotation></semantics></math></span> (in units of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>): exactly at the oscillation boundary."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why does increasing inhibitory self-coupling <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{II}</annotation></semantics></math></span> tend to stabilize the loop?","a":"It makes the trace more negative (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-w_{II}</annotation></semantics></math></span>) and also increases the determinant term <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1 - w_{EE})(1+w_{II})</annotation></semantics></math></span> when <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></span>; inhibition of inhibition damps the I population’s response."},{"kind":"concept","q":"An experiment finds excitatory and inhibitory conductances onto CA1 cells that scale together across input strengths. Is the circuit “balanced” in the sense above?","a":"It shows proportional E and I, which is consistent with balance, but balance in the current sense also needs the <i>currents</i> (conductance × driving force) to largely cancel at the operating voltage. Report both."}],"eqs":[{"num":"6.4.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>e</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>i</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>e</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>i</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\begin{pmatrix}\\dot e\\\\ \\dot i\\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix}-1 + w_{EE} &amp; -w_{EI}\\\\ w_{IE} &amp; -1 - w_{II}\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}e\\\\ i\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]}]},{"n":7,"part":"III","pathway":3,"title":"Network models","intro":["To study circuits we must simplify cells. This chapter introduces the leaky integrate-and-fire neuron, the workhorse of network modelling; describes networks as weighted directed graphs with delays; derives rate models of the Wilson–Cowan type; and defines the measurements (rate, irregularity, synchrony) that classify collective states such as those in Brunel’s (2000) network.","The guiding question is the one in the guide’s first lesson on networks: which details does the question need? A detailed CA1 cell is the right tool for dendritic integration; a thousand LIF neurons are the right tool for asking when a recurrent circuit fires irregularly or synchronously."],"obj":["Derive the LIF firing rate for constant input, including rheobase and refractoriness","Describe connectivity with a weight matrix and compute degree statistics of random graphs","Write the Wilson–Cowan equations and find their fixed points with nullclines","Classify network states with rate, CV of interspike intervals and synchrony"],"prereq":["Chapters 1, 3 and 6"],"sections":[{"route":"/topics/networks/spiking-neurons/","num":"7.1","obj":["Write the LIF model with threshold, reset and refractory period","Derive the interspike interval and f–I curve for constant input","State what the LIF model omits relative to HH"],"body":"<p>The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"lif\" tabindex=\"0\">leaky integrate-and-fire</dfn> (LIF) neuron keeps the passive membrane of Chapter 1 and replaces the spike mechanism by a rule:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-7-1-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rest</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>V</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo>⇒</mo><mtext> spike</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>V</mi><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>reset</mtext></msub><mtext> for </mtext><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_m\\frac{dV}{dt} = -(V - V_\\text{rest}) + RI;\\qquad V \\ge V_\\text{th}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\text{spike},\\ V\\to V_\\text{reset}\\ \\text{for}\\ t_\\text{ref}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(7.1.1)</span></div><p>Below threshold the voltage charges toward <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rest</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\text{rest} + RI</annotation></semantics></math></span>. If that target lies below <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\text{th}</annotation></semantics></math></span> the neuron never fires; the smallest current that fires it is the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"rheobase\" tabindex=\"0\">rheobase</dfn>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rh</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rest</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{rh} = (V_\\text{th} - V_\\text{rest})/R</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Above rheobase, with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>reset</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rest</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\text{reset} = V_\\text{rest}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rest</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = V_\\text{th} - V_\\text{rest}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, solving Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-7-1-1\">(7.1.1)</a> from reset to threshold gives the interspike interval</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-7-1-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>ISI</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>ISI</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\text{ISI} = t_\\text{ref} + \\tau_m\\ln\\frac{RI}{RI - \\Delta},\\qquad f = \\frac{1}{T_\\text{ISI}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(7.1.2)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-7-1-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 7.1.1</p><h4>An f–I curve by hand</h4><p>Take <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_m = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> MΩ (so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">RI</annotation></semantics></math></span> in mV is 100 × <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> in nA), <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rest</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>reset</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\text{rest} = V_\\text{reset} = -65</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_\\text{th} = -50</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = 15</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV and rheobase is 0.15 nA. Without refractoriness: at 0.2 nA, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>27.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 20\\ln(20/5) = 27.7</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms (36 Hz); at 0.3 nA, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>13.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 20\\ln 2 = 13.9</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms (72 Hz); at 1 nA, about 308 Hz. A 2 ms refractory period lowers the 0.3 nA rate to 63 Hz and caps the rate at 500 Hz.</p><p>Near rheobase the LIF rate rises steeply but not as a square root: it rises as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\ln</annotation></semantics></math></span>, a logarithmic singularity. The LIF is a caricature of class I firing, not a normal form for it (compare the quadratic integrate-and-fire neuron, which is).</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-7-1-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/lif.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/lif.svg\" alt=\"Figure 7.1.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 7.1.1.</b> <b>A</b> An LIF neuron (τ = 20 ms, R = 100 MΩ, threshold −50 mV, reset −65 mV, 2 ms refractory) driven by 0.3 nA. Vertical lines mark spikes. <b>B</b> The f–I curve of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-7-1-2\">(7.1.2)</a>, with and without refractoriness.</figcaption></figure><p>What is lost? The spike shape (irrelevant for most network questions), the voltage dependence of channels (no resonance unless an adaptation variable is added), and the dendrites. Extensions add these back selectively: adaptive exponential IF (AdEx; Brette &amp; Gerstner 2005) adds a spike-initiation nonlinearity and a slow adaptation current, enough to fit many cortical firing patterns and, with the right parameters, subthreshold resonance.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 7.1</span> LIF network step (clock-driven)</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">V += dt / tau_m * (-(V - V_rest) + R * I_syn + R * I_ext)\nV[refractory &gt; 0] = V_reset;  refractory -= dt\nfired = V &gt;= V_th\nV[fired] = V_reset;  refractory[fired] = t_ref\nrecord spikes(t, fired)\ndeliver to targets after delay: I_syn[targets] += W[targets, fired]</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Hard thresholds and time steps</h4><p>In a clock-driven simulation the crossing time is only known to within <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which can synchronize neurons artificially. Interpolating the crossing time, or using small steps, avoids spurious synchrony in large networks.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Integration below threshold, stereotyped spike</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Point-neuron models of any area</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>f–I curves, rheobase</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Threshold-and-reset hybrid ODE</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Brian2 NeuronGroup; Brunel lab</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["LIF: passive membrane plus threshold, reset and refractory period.","Rheobase <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>rh</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{rh} = \\Delta/R</annotation></semantics></math></span>; ISI <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= t_\\text{ref} + \\tau_m\\ln[RI/(RI-\\Delta)]</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","LIF omits spike shape, channel dynamics and dendrites; AdEx and related models add some back."],"terms":["lif","rheobase","fi-curve","refractory-period"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For the LIF in the example, what current gives exactly 50 Hz with no refractory period?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>⇒</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>23.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Rightarrow \\ln[RI/(RI-15)] = 1 \\Rightarrow RI = 15e/(e-1) \\approx 23.7</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, i.e. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.237</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\approx0.237</annotation></semantics></math></span> nA."},{"kind":"derive","q":"Derive Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-7-1-2\">(7.1.2)</a> from Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-7-1-1\">(7.1.1)</a>.","a":"Between spikes <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rest</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(t) - V_\\text{rest} = RI(1 - e^{-t/\\tau_m})</annotation></semantics></math></span> starting from reset at rest. Setting this equal to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-t/\\tau_m} = 1 - \\Delta/RI</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = \\tau_m\\ln[RI/(RI-\\Delta)]</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Add <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_\\text{ref}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why can a plain LIF neuron not show subthreshold resonance?","a":"Below threshold it is a 1-D linear system, a low-pass filter. Resonance needs a second, slow restoring variable (Ch. 3), as in AdEx with positive subthreshold adaptation <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math></span>."}],"eqs":[{"num":"7.1.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>rest</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>V</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo>⇒</mo><mtext> spike</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>V</mi><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>reset</mtext></msub><mtext> for </mtext><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_m\\frac{dV}{dt} = -(V - V_\\text{rest}) + RI;\\qquad V \\ge V_\\text{th}\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\text{spike},\\ V\\to V_\\text{reset}\\ \\text{for}\\ t_\\text{ref}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"7.1.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>ISI</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>ISI</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_\\text{ISI} = t_\\text{ref} + \\tau_m\\ln\\frac{RI}{RI - \\Delta},\\qquad f = \\frac{1}{T_\\text{ISI}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/networks/wiring-delays/","num":"7.2","obj":["Represent a network with a weight matrix <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span> (from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","Compute mean and variance of in-degree in an Erdős–Rényi graph","Estimate conduction and synaptic delays and explain their role in rhythms"],"body":"<p>A CA3 pyramidal cell sends its axon (the Schaffer collateral) across stratum radiatum, where it makes excitatory synapses on the apical dendrites of thousands of CA1 pyramidal cells; its other branches recurrently contact other CA3 cells. Each contact has a strength (how many AMPA receptors, how reliable release is) and a delay: the spike must travel along a thin, mostly unmyelinated axon and then cross the synapse. CA1 pyramidal cells, by contrast, make few connections with each other; most of their recurrent influence goes through interneurons.</p><p>A network model compresses this anatomy into three objects: who connects to whom, how strongly, and with what delay. The weight matrix holds the first two; a delay table the third.</p><p>A network is a directed graph. Its <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"weight-matrix\" tabindex=\"0\">weight matrix</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W</annotation></semantics></math></span> has entry <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the connection **from neuron <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math></span> to neuron <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span>**, so that the input to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_j W_{ij}s_j</annotation></semantics></math></span> for presynaptic activities <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_j</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Zero means no connection; diagonal entries (self-connections, autapses) are usually set to zero.</p><p><dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"dales-principle\" tabindex=\"0\">Dale’s principle</dfn> says a neuron releases the same transmitter at all its terminals, so each <b>column</b> of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W</annotation></semantics></math></span> is all non-negative (excitatory neuron) or all non-positive (inhibitory). Random matrices that ignore this behave differently, especially in their eigenvalue spectra.</p><p>In the simplest random (<dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"erdos-renyi\" tabindex=\"0\">Erdős–Rényi</dfn>) graph each possible connection exists independently with probability <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The in-degree <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K</annotation></semantics></math></span> of a neuron is then binomial:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-7-2-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle K\\rangle = Np,\\qquad \\operatorname{SD}(K) = \\sqrt{Np(1-p)}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(7.2.1)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-7-2-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 7.2.1</p><h4>A sparse random network</h4><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N = 1000</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p = 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, each neuron receives on average 100 inputs, with SD ≈ 9.5. Brunel (2000) used a fixed in-degree instead (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_E = 4C_I</annotation></semantics></math></span> inputs per cell) to remove this variability; the choice matters when comparing to theory.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-7-2-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/weights.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/weights.svg\" alt=\"Figure 7.2.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 7.2.1.</b> <b>A</b> A random 40-neuron weight matrix obeying Dale’s principle: excitatory columns (pink) and inhibitory columns (blue, four times stronger). <b>B</b> In-degrees of 5,000 neurons with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N = 1000</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p = 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, against the binomial prediction of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-7-2-1\">(7.2.1)</a>.</figcaption></figure><p>Every connection also has a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"synaptic-delay\" tabindex=\"0\">delay</dfn>: axonal conduction (distance / velocity) plus synaptic delay. A 2 mm path at 0.5 m/s adds 4 ms. Delays turn instantaneous feedback into delayed feedback, which can destabilize steady activity and create oscillations whose period scales with the loop delay; in Brunel’s network fast synchronous oscillations have periods of a few times the delay.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 7.2</span> Build a random Dale network</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">N_E, N_I = 800, 200\nW = zeros(N, N)\nfor i in range(N):                       # target\n    for j in range(N):                   # source\n        if i != j and random() &lt; p:\n            W[i, j] = J_E if j &lt; N_E else -g * J_E\ndelay[i, j] = d_syn + distance(i, j) / velocity\ncheck: sign(W[:, j]) constant for every column j     # Dale</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Indexing convention</h4><p>Some libraries store <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span> as from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math></span> (rows = sources). Brian2’s `Synapses` uses source <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span>, target <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Transposing a weight matrix silently swaps in- and out-degree; check with a two-neuron example before running a large network.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Axons, synapses, conduction velocity</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA3 recurrents vs sparse CA1–CA1 connectivity</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Paired recordings, connectomics, tracing</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Random graphs, degree distributions</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Brian2 Synapses (connect p=...), connectome graph page</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span>: weight from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span>; Dale’s principle fixes the sign of each column.","Erdős–Rényi in-degree: mean <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Np</annotation></semantics></math></span>, SD <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{Np(1-p)}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Delays (conduction + synaptic) shape network rhythms and stability."],"terms":["weight-matrix","dales-principle","erdos-renyi","synaptic-delay"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N = 200</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p = 0.05</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Mean and SD of in-degree?","a":"Mean 10, SD <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>200</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mn>3.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{200\\times0.05\\times0.95}\\approx3.1</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"In a 3-neuron network neuron 1 excites 2 with weight 0.5, 2 inhibits 3 with weight 1. Write <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W</annotation></semantics></math></span> (from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span>).","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{21} = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>32</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{32} = -1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, all else 0: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0.5</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W = \\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0&amp;0\\\\0.5&amp;0&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"calc","q":"CA3→CA1 Schaffer collaterals are unmyelinated. At 0.3 m/s, how long does a 1.5 mm path take?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\times10^{-3}/0.3</annotation></semantics></math></span> s <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms (plus about 1 ms synaptic delay). The velocity is illustrative; measured values vary with axon and temperature."}],"eqs":[{"num":"7.2.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle K\\rangle = Np,\\qquad \\operatorname{SD}(K) = \\sqrt{Np(1-p)}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/networks/rates-wilson-cowan/","num":"7.3","obj":["Explain the assumptions behind a population rate equation","Write the Wilson–Cowan equations and their nullclines","Describe how increasing drive produces a limit cycle"],"body":"<p>Record from a hundred CA1 pyramidal cells at once (with a silicon probe, or with calcium imaging) and the individual spike trains look irregular. Average them in short windows and a smooth curve appears: the population’s firing rate, rising and falling with the theta rhythm. In the same tissue, fast-spiking interneurons follow the pyramidal cells with a short lag and inhibit them back.</p><p>Wilson and Cowan asked whether those smooth averages obey their own equations, without tracking every spike. Their answer treats each population (pyramidal cells, interneurons) as a single unit whose activity relaxes toward a saturating function of its input, much as a single cell’s rate saturates in its f–I curve.</p><p>If a population is large and its neurons fire asynchronously, its activity can be described by a smooth <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"firing-rate\" tabindex=\"0\">rate</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span> instead of spike times. Wilson and Cowan (1972) wrote coupled equations for an excitatory and an inhibitory population:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-7-3-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mtable rowspacing=\"0.25em\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>E</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>I</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\\tau_E\\dot E &amp;= -E + S_E(w_{EE}E - w_{EI}I + P)\\\\ \\tau_I\\dot I &amp;= -I + S_I(w_{IE}E - w_{II}I + Q)\\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(7.3.1)</span></div><p>Each population relaxes toward a sigmoidal function <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span> of its net input. The weights are positive magnitudes; the minus signs make inhibition inhibitory. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span> are external drives. The equations assume that the population’s response to input is fast compared with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> and well summarized by a static nonlinearity: assumptions examined in Chapter 8.</p><p>Fixed points lie at the intersection of the nullclines <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E = S_E(w_{EE}E - w_{EI}I + P)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = S_I(w_{IE}E - w_{II}I + Q)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Because <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_E</annotation></semantics></math></span> is sigmoidal and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is large, the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline has an N-shape, like FHN’s cubic, and the same geometry follows: up to three fixed points, and a limit cycle when the fixed point sits on the middle branch and is unstable.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-7-3-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 7.3.1</p><h4>The classic limit cycle</h4><p>The guide’s lab uses Wilson &amp; Cowan’s Fig. 11a parameters (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE} = 16</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EI} = 12</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{IE} = 15</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{II} = 3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_E = 1.3</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_E = 4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_I = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_I = 3.7</annotation></semantics></math></span>). As <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P</annotation></semantics></math></span> increases from 0 the single fixed point moves onto the middle branch of the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span>-nullcline, its eigenvalues cross the imaginary axis (Hopf) and E and I oscillate, I lagging E. The lab omits the paper’s refractory factor <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1 - rE)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, as most textbook versions do, so its exact bifurcation values differ from the paper while the geometry is the same.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-7-3-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/wilson-cowan.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/wilson-cowan.svg\" alt=\"Figure 7.3.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 7.3.1.</b> Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-7-3-1\">(7.3.1)</a> with the Fig. 11a parameters of Wilson &amp; Cowan (1972), logistic <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P = 1.25</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, no refractory factor. <b>A</b> E and I oscillate, I lagging E. <b>B</b> The cycle in the phase plane with both nullclines.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 7.3</span> Simulate a Wilson–Cowan model</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">S = lambda x, a, th: 1 / (1 + exp(-a * (x - th)))\nE, I = 0.1, 0.1\nfor step in range(n_steps):\n    dE = (-E + S(wEE*E - wEI*I + P, aE, thE)) / tauE\n    dI = (-I + S(wIE*E - wII*I + Q, aI, thI)) / tauI\n    E, I = E + dt*dE, I + dt*dI\nsweep P; record min and max of E after transients -&gt; bifurcation diagram</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Rates are not single-cell firing rates</h4><p><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a population average, often normalized to [0, 1]. A population oscillation in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span> does not imply that each neuron fires on every cycle; it can arise from sparse, irregular firing with a periodic population rate.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Pyramidal and interneuron populations</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Cortical columns, hippocampal E–I rhythms</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Multi-unit activity, LFP</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Coupled nonlinear rate ODEs</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Wilson–Cowan lab, phase-plane workbench</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Rate models replace spikes with population activity relaxing toward <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>net input</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(\\text{net input})</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Wilson–Cowan: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>E</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_E\\dot E = -E + S_E(w_{EE}E - w_{EI}I + P)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and its I counterpart.","N-shaped E-nullclines give multiple fixed points and, through a Hopf bifurcation, E–I limit cycles."],"terms":["firing-rate","wilson-cowan","sigmoid"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(x) = 1/(1+e^{-(x-4)})</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE}=16</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EI}=12</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, compute <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>E</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot E</annotation></semantics></math></span> at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(E, I) = (0.5, 0.5)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_E = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"Net input <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= 8 - 6 + 0 = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S = 1/(1+e^{2})\\approx0.12</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>E</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.12</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.38</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot E = -0.5 + 0.12 = -0.38</annotation></semantics></math></span>: E decreases."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why does strong recurrent excitation (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE}</annotation></semantics></math></span> large) give the E-nullcline its N-shape?","a":"For fixed I, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span> appears on both sides: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>…</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E = S(w_{EE}E + \\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. When <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>S</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{EE}S&#x27;</annotation></semantics></math></span> exceeds 1 somewhere, the self-consistency curve folds back on itself, producing three branches."},{"kind":"concept","q":"What would you change in Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-7-3-1\">(7.3.1)</a> to model GABA<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>B</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">_\\text{B}</annotation></semantics></math></span> inhibition, which is slow?","a":"Make <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_I</annotation></semantics></math></span> larger, or add a separate slow synaptic variable between <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> and its effect on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Slower inhibition generally lowers oscillation frequency."}],"eqs":[{"num":"7.3.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mtable rowspacing=\"0.25em\" columnalign=\"right left\" columnspacing=\"0em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>E</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>I</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\\tau_E\\dot E &amp;= -E + S_E(w_{EE}E - w_{EI}I + P)\\\\ \\tau_I\\dot I &amp;= -I + S_I(w_{IE}E - w_{II}I + Q)\\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/networks/collective-states/","num":"7.4","obj":["Compute mean rate and coefficient of variation of interspike intervals","Describe Brunel’s synchronous-regular and asynchronous-irregular states","Explain why the mean rate alone cannot distinguish them"],"body":"<p>Hippocampal recordings in a behaving animal show the circuit switching between very different collective modes. During exploration, pyramidal cells fire sparsely and irregularly, organized by theta and gamma rhythms. During quiet rest and slow-wave sleep, the network produces sharp-wave ripples: brief, highly synchronous bursts in which many CA1 cells fire within tens of milliseconds, riding a ≈ 150–200 Hz oscillation. In epilepsy, synchrony becomes pathological.</p><p>The same cells and synapses produce all of these, so the difference lies in how activity is coordinated. To compare a model with these states you need numbers that capture coordination, not only how much the cells fire.</p><p>Three measurements classify most network states. The <b>mean rate</b> is spikes per neuron per second. The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"cv-isi\" tabindex=\"0\">coefficient of variation</dfn> of interspike intervals, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CV</mtext><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>ISI</mtext></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>ISI</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{CV} = \\sigma_\\text{ISI}/\\mu_\\text{ISI}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, measures irregularity: 0 for a clock, 1 for a Poisson process. <b>Synchrony</b> compares the variance of the population-averaged signal with the average single-neuron variance; it is near 1 if neurons fluctuate together and near <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/N</annotation></semantics></math></span> if independently.</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-7-4-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>spikes</mtext></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cells</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mtext>CV</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>ISI</mtext></msub><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>ISI</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar\\nu = \\frac{N_\\text{spikes}}{N_\\text{cells}\\,T},\\qquad \\text{CV} = \\frac{\\sigma_\\text{ISI}}{\\mu_\\text{ISI}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(7.4.1)</span></div><p>Brunel (2000) mapped the states of a sparse network of excitatory and inhibitory LIF neurons as a function of relative inhibition <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span> and external drive. When excitation dominates (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span> small), neurons fire regularly and in synchrony at high rates (<b>SR</b>). When inhibition dominates (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span> above about 4, the balance point given four times more E inputs), firing is <b>asynchronous and irregular</b> (AI) at low rates, the state that most resembles cortex in vivo, with <b>synchronous irregular</b> states (fast or slow population oscillations with irregular single cells) at the boundaries.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-7-4-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 7.4.1</p><h4>Reading a raster</h4><p>12,000 spikes from 1,000 cells over 2 s give <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar\\nu = 6</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz. One neuron’s ISIs of 20, 80, 35, 60, 15 and 90 ms have mean 50 ms and SD 28.7 ms: CV ≈ 0.57, more regular than Poisson but far from a clock. A synchronous-regular network and an asynchronous-irregular one can both produce 6 Hz; only CV and synchrony tell them apart.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-7-4-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/raster-states.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/raster-states.svg\" alt=\"Figure 7.4.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 7.4.1.</b> Synthetic rasters illustrating two of Brunel’s states (not simulations of his network). <b>A</b> Synchronous regular: every cell fires every 25 ms, CV ≈ 0. <b>B</b> Asynchronous irregular: independent Poisson firing at 8 Hz, CV ≈ 1.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 7.4</span> Classify a network state</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">rate = n_spikes / (N * T)\ncv = mean(std(isi_i) / mean(isi_i) for each neuron with &gt;= 5 spikes)\nA = population_activity(spikes, bin=2ms)\nchi = sqrt(var(A_smoothed) / mean(var(v_i)))         # synchrony index\nif chi high and cv low:  &#x27;synchronous regular&#x27;\nif chi low  and cv ~ 1:  &#x27;asynchronous irregular&#x27;\ncompare with a shuffled (Poisson) control of the same rates</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Finite-size and bin-size effects</h4><p>Synchrony measures depend on the bin size used to compute population activity and on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span>: a small network looks synchronous by chance. Report both, and compare against a shuffled or Poisson control.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Theta, gamma, sharp-wave ripples, seizures</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1/CA3 in exploration, rest and sleep</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Silicon probes, rasters, ISI statistics</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>CV, synchrony indices, Brunel phase diagram</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Brunel lab; Brian2 SpikeMonitor</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Mean rate, CV of ISIs and a synchrony index classify network states.","Brunel’s network shows SR when excitation dominates and AI when inhibition dominates, with SI states between.","Equal mean rates can hide very different states."],"terms":["cv-isi","asynchronous-irregular","synchrony"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"A neuron fires every 25 ms exactly. Rate and CV?","a":"40 Hz, CV = 0."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why does a Poisson process have CV = 1?","a":"Its ISIs are exponentially distributed, and the exponential distribution’s SD equals its mean."},{"kind":"concept","q":"In Brunel’s network, why does <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\approx4</annotation></semantics></math></span> mark the balance point?","a":"Each neuron receives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_E = 4C_I</annotation></semantics></math></span> excitatory inputs. With inhibitory weights <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span> times the excitatory ones, total inhibition equals total excitation when <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\,C_I = C_E</annotation></semantics></math></span>, i.e. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g = 4</annotation></semantics></math></span> (for equal presynaptic rates)."}],"eqs":[{"num":"7.4.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>ν</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>spikes</mtext></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cells</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mtext>CV</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>ISI</mtext></msub><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>ISI</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar\\nu = \\frac{N_\\text{spikes}}{N_\\text{cells}\\,T},\\qquad \\text{CV} = \\frac{\\sigma_\\text{ISI}}{\\mu_\\text{ISI}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]}]},{"n":8,"part":"III","pathway":4,"title":"Mean-field theory and diversity","intro":["Mean-field theory replaces a network of many interacting neurons with an equation for their average activity. The price is a set of assumptions: many inputs per cell, weak correlations, a known input–output function. The reward is understanding: fixed points, stability and bistability of a whole network can be read off a one-line equation.","This chapter builds the closure step by step: estimating population activity from spikes, characterizing the gain function, solving the self-consistency equation, and finally asking what heterogeneity among cells does to all of it."],"obj":["Estimate population activity from spikes and quantify its sampling noise","Describe and parameterize gain functions; distinguish additive and multiplicative modulation","Solve <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r = F(Jr + I)</annotation></semantics></math></span> graphically and determine stability","Compute the population response of heterogeneous cells and explain why it differs from the average cell"],"prereq":["Chapter 7"],"sections":[{"route":"/topics/mean-field/population-activity/","num":"8.1","obj":["Define population activity <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and estimate it in time bins","Compute its sampling uncertainty under a Poisson assumption","Explain the trade-off between bin width and population size"],"body":"<p>A CA1 pyramidal cell in a moving rat fires a few spikes per second on average. A single downstream neuron cannot learn much from one such cell in a 10 ms window, because there is usually no spike to read. But each subiculum or entorhinal neuron receives input from many CA1 cells at once, and the local field potential reflects the summed synaptic currents of thousands. What the circuit “sees” is the population.</p><p>Population activity is the quantity that captures this: the fraction of the population that fires per unit time. It is also what a silicon probe or calcium imaging session gives you after pooling cells.</p><p><dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"population-activity\" tabindex=\"0\">Population activity</dfn> is the fraction of a population that fires per unit time. Counting spikes in a bin of width <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span> across <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> neurons:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-8-1-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>spikes</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(t) = \\frac{n_\\text{spikes}(t,\\,t+\\Delta t)}{N\\,\\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(8.1.1)</span></div><p>in Hz per neuron. Unlike a single neuron’s rate, which needs a long window to estimate, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span> can follow fast changes if <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> is large: averaging over neurons replaces averaging over time (Gerstner et al. 2014, Ch. 12).</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-8-1-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 8.1.1</p><h4>How noisy is the estimate?</h4><p>If neurons fire independently as Poisson processes at rate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the count in a bin is Poisson with mean <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\nu\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span> and SD <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{N\\nu\\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N = 1000</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms the mean count is 50, SD ≈ 7.1, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span> = 5 ± 0.71 Hz. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> the SD rises to ≈ 2.2 Hz: a population of 100 cannot resolve a 1 Hz modulation in 10 ms bins.</p></div><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-8-1-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{SD}(A) = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\nu}{N\\Delta t}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(8.1.2)</span></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-8-1-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/population.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/population.svg\" alt=\"Figure 8.1.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 8.1.1.</b> 200 independent Poisson neurons whose rate is modulated at theta frequency. Population activity (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-8-1-1\">(8.1.1)</a>) in 5 ms bins is noisy; 25 ms bins track the true rate more smoothly but blur fast changes (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-8-1-2\">(8.1.2)</a>).</figcaption></figure><p>The SD falls as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt{N\\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>: doubling either the population or the bin halves the variance. Correlated firing breaks this scaling, because correlated noise does not average out. This is one reason the weak-correlation assumption of mean-field theory matters.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 8.1</span> Population activity with an error bar</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">counts = histogram(all_spike_times, bins=arange(0, T, dt_bin))\nA = counts / (N * dt_bin)                          # Hz per neuron\nA_sd_poisson = sqrt(A / (N * dt_bin))              # if neurons were independent\ncompare var(A) with A_sd_poisson**2:  excess -&gt; correlated firing</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Smoothing changes the answer</h4><p>Gaussian kernels, causal exponentials and rectangular bins of different widths all give different-looking <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. When comparing a mean-field prediction with a spiking simulation, filter both identically.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Many weakly coupled cells read out together</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1 ensembles projecting to subiculum and entorhinal cortex</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Population rasters, calcium imaging</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Poisson counting statistics</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Brian2 PopulationRateMonitor</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(t) = n/(N\\Delta t)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the per-neuron population rate.","Under independent Poisson firing, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{SD}(A) = \\sqrt{\\nu/(N\\Delta t)}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Large populations allow fast tracking; correlations break the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt N</annotation></semantics></math></span> averaging."],"terms":["population-activity","poisson-process"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"30 spikes from 200 neurons in a 5 ms bin. What is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.005</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30/(200\\times0.005) = 30</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz."},{"kind":"calc","q":"How many neurons are needed for SD(A) ≤ 0.5 Hz at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> Hz with 10 ms bins?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{5/(N\\times0.01)}\\le0.5 \\Rightarrow N\\ge5/(0.25\\times0.01) = 2000</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"If all neurons fired in perfect synchrony, how would SD(A) scale with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"It would not shrink at all: the population behaves like one neuron, so the relative fluctuations stay those of a single cell."}],"eqs":[{"num":"8.1.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>spikes</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(t) = \\frac{n_\\text{spikes}(t,\\,t+\\Delta t)}{N\\,\\Delta t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"8.1.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{SD}(A) = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\nu}{N\\Delta t}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/gain-function/","num":"8.2","obj":["Define a gain (transfer) function and its slope (gain)","Parameterize a sigmoid by threshold and slope","Distinguish subtractive from divisive modulation and give a biophysical example"],"body":"<p>Inject a series of current steps into a CA1 pyramidal cell and count spikes: no firing below rheobase, then a rate that climbs with current and eventually levels off as Na⁺ inactivation and afterhyperpolarizations limit how fast the cell can recover. This f–I curve is the cell’s input–output rule. In vivo, the cell is bombarded by synaptic noise, so it fires occasionally even below rheobase, and the sharp corner becomes a smooth S-shaped curve.</p><p>A population’s gain function is the same idea one level up: how the average rate of many such cells depends on their shared input.</p><p>A <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"gain-function\" tabindex=\"0\">gain function</dfn> <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span> maps a neuron’s or population’s input to its output rate. For single neurons it is the f–I curve measured with steps; for populations with noisy input it is smoother. A convenient form is the logistic sigmoid with threshold <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> and slope parameter <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-8-2-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x) = \\frac{1}{1+e^{-s(x-\\theta)}},\\qquad F&#x27;(\\theta) = \\frac{s}{4}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(8.2.1)</span></div><p>The local slope <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F&#x27;(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"gain\" tabindex=\"0\">gain</dfn>: how much output changes per unit input at the operating point. Gain, not the full curve, enters every linear stability calculation (Ch. 6–8).</p><h3>Noise smooths thresholds</h3><p>A deterministic LIF has a sharp onset at rheobase. Adding input noise lets the neuron fire occasionally below rheobase and makes <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span> a smooth sigmoid; the classical result for the LIF with white-noise input (the Siegert formula) gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span> as an integral involving the mean and SD of the input. More noise means a lower effective threshold and a shallower curve.</p><h3>Modulating gain</h3><p>A change in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> shifts the curve (additive or subtractive modulation); a change in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math></span> or in the maximum rate scales it (multiplicative or divisive modulation). Shunting inhibition alone acts mostly subtractively on firing rate, but Chance, Abbott and Reyes (2002) showed that increasing balanced background excitation <i>and</i> inhibition together changes the gain: the extra noise and conductance combine into divisive modulation.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-8-2-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 8.2.1</p><h4>Gain at and away from threshold</h4><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the gain at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x = \\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> per unit input and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.88</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(\\theta+1) = 1/(1+e^{-2})\\approx0.88</annotation></semantics></math></span>. One unit above threshold the gain has already fallen to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2F(1-F)\\approx0.21</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The same input perturbation has a 2.4× smaller effect there.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-8-2-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/gain.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/gain.svg\" alt=\"Figure 8.2.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 8.2.1.</b> <b>A</b> Logistic gain functions, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-8-2-1\">(8.2.1)</a>, with three slopes. <b>B</b> Simulated LIF populations: without noise the rate jumps at rheobase (15 mV of drive); with 3 mV input noise the curve is smooth and starts below rheobase.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 8.2</span> Measure a population gain function</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">for mu in drives:\n    rates = []\n    for trial in range(n_trials):\n        spikes = simulate(neurons, I_mean=mu, noise_sd=sigma)\n        rates.append(n_spikes / (N * T))\n    F[mu] = mean(rates)\nfit logistic: F(x) = r_max / (1 + exp(-s (x - theta)))\ngain at operating point = dF/dx (numerical)</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Static gain functions ignore dynamics</h4><p><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span> describes the steady-state rate for constant input. For inputs that change faster than the neurons’ time constants (including theta and gamma rhythms), the population responds with a frequency-dependent gain and phase, much like the impedance of Chapter 3. Static mean-field theory is a low-frequency approximation.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>f–I curves under synaptic noise</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>In vivo-like conductance states in cortex and hippocampus</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Dynamic clamp with noise injection</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Sigmoids, Siegert formula</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Neuron workbench f–I tab; Fitting studio: f–I curve</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["A gain function maps input to rate; its slope <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F&#x27;</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the gain.","Logistic sigmoid: threshold <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, slope <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math></span>, maximal gain <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Noise smooths the threshold; threshold shifts are subtractive, slope or amplitude changes divisive."],"terms":["gain-function","gain","sigmoid","fi-curve"],"problems":[{"kind":"derive","q":"Show that the logistic sigmoid satisfies <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F&#x27; = sF(1-F)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and hence <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F&#x27;(\\theta) = s/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F = (1+u)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u = e^{-s(x-\\theta)}</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F&#x27; = su/(1+u)^2 = sF\\cdot u/(1+u) = sF(1-F)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F = 1/2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F&#x27; = s/4</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"A drug multiplies all firing rates by 0.5. Is this subtractive or divisive, and how does the gain at threshold change?","a":"Divisive. The gain at every input, including threshold, halves."},{"kind":"calc","q":"For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, at what input is the gain half its maximum?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.854</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(1-F) = 1/8 \\Rightarrow F = (1\\pm1/\\sqrt2)/2 \\Rightarrow F\\approx0.854</annotation></semantics></math></span> or 0.146, i.e. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>0.854</mn><mn>0.146</mn></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1.76</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x = \\pm\\ln\\frac{0.854}{0.146}\\approx\\pm1.76</annotation></semantics></math></span>."}],"eqs":[{"num":"8.2.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x) = \\frac{1}{1+e^{-s(x-\\theta)}},\\qquad F&#x27;(\\theta) = \\frac{s}{4}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/self-consistency/","num":"8.3","obj":["Write the self-consistency condition <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r = F(Jr+I)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for a recurrent population","Find its solutions graphically and numerically","Determine stability from the condition <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JF&#x27;(Jr^*+I) &lt; 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>"],"body":"<p>In CA3, pyramidal cells excite each other through recurrent collaterals; the more cells are active, the more excitatory input each receives, which activates more cells. Interneurons and adaptation hold this loop in check. Experimentally, persistent activity that outlasts a brief stimulus (in prefrontal cortex during working memory, or in attractor models of CA3 recall) is thought to depend on exactly this kind of recurrent excitation.</p><p>The question a mean-field model asks is: which levels of activity can sustain themselves? A level is self-sustaining when the input it generates through recurrent synapses produces exactly that level of activity again.</p><p>In a recurrent population each neuron’s input includes the population’s own output. With coupling <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J</annotation></semantics></math></span> and external input <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the rate dynamics and their equilibria are</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-8-3-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\dot r = -r + F(Jr + I),\\qquad r^* = F(Jr^* + I)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(8.3.1)</span></div><p>The second equation is the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"self-consistency\" tabindex=\"0\">self-consistency condition</dfn>: the rate assumed to compute the input must equal the rate that input produces. Graphically, plot <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y = r</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y = F(Jr+I)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the intersections are the fixed points.</p><p>Linearizing Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-8-3-1\">(8.3.1)</a> at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^*</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives growth rate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1 + JF&#x27;(Jr^*+I))/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A fixed point is stable when</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-8-3-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\,F&#x27;(Jr^* + I) &lt; 1</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(8.3.2)</span></div><p>i.e. when the curve <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(Jr+I)</annotation></semantics></math></span> crosses the diagonal with slope less than 1. Strong recurrent excitation can make the slope exceed 1 at a middle crossing, creating an unstable fixed point flanked by two stable ones: <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"bistability\" tabindex=\"0\">bistability</dfn>, a minimal model of persistent activity and working memory.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-8-3-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 8.3.1</p><h4>One state or two?</h4><p>Use the logistic <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> there is one fixed point, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.844</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^*\\approx0.844</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.26</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JF&#x27;\\approx0.26</annotation></semantics></math></span>: stable. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J = 8</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = -4</annotation></semantics></math></span> there are three: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.021</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^*\\approx0.021</annotation></semantics></math></span> (stable, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.17</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JF&#x27;\\approx0.17</annotation></semantics></math></span>), <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> (unstable, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JF&#x27; = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span>) and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.979</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.979</annotation></semantics></math></span> (stable). A transient input can switch the population between its low and high states, which then persist after the input ends.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-8-3-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/self-consistency.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/self-consistency.svg\" alt=\"Figure 8.3.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 8.3.1.</b> Graphical solution of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-8-3-1\">(8.3.1)</a>: intersections of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y = r</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y = F(Jr + I)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Filled circles are stable (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JF&#x27; &lt; 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>), open circles unstable. <b>A</b> Weak recurrence: one state. <b>B</b> Strong recurrence: a low and a high stable state.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 8.3</span> Solve and classify self-consistent states</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">g = lambda r: F(J*r + I_ext) - r\nroots = [brentq(g, lo, hi) for (lo, hi) in sign_changes(g, linspace(0, r_max, 1000))]\nfor r in roots:\n    slope = J * F_prime(J*r + I_ext)\n    label = &#x27;stable&#x27; if slope &lt; 1 else &#x27;unstable&#x27;\nthen simulate the spiking network started near each stable root</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>A solution is not automatically stable or reachable</h4><p>Mean-field equations can have solutions that the spiking network never visits, because they are unstable, because fluctuations push the network out of their basin, or because the dynamics (delays, adaptation) make them oscillate. Always simulate the spiking model at the predicted state.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Recurrent excitation held in check by inhibition</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA3 autoassociation; prefrontal persistent activity</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Persistent firing after brief stimuli</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Fixed-point equations, graphical stability</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Mean-field bridge page</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Recurrent populations satisfy <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^* = F(Jr^*+I)</annotation></semantics></math></span> at equilibrium.","Fixed points are intersections of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=r</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=F(Jr+I)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; stable iff <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JF&#x27;&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></span> there.","Strong recurrence gives bistability between low and high activity."],"terms":["self-consistency","bistability","mean-field"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J = 4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = -2</annotation></semantics></math></span> (logistic, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=0</annotation></semantics></math></span>), show that <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a fixed point and find <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JF&#x27;</annotation></semantics></math></span> there.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Jr + I = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(0) = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> ✓. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F&#x27;(0) = 0.25</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JF&#x27; = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the borderline case where the two outer fixed points are about to appear (a pitchfork here, by symmetry)."},{"kind":"concept","q":"In the <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J = 8</annotation></semantics></math></span> bistable example, what happens if <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> is raised from −4 to −3?","a":"The curve shifts left: the low state rises to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\approx0.10</annotation></semantics></math></span> and moves toward the middle (unstable) fixed point at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx0.21</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Raising <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> further makes them merge in a saddle-node, leaving only the high state."},{"kind":"derive","q":"Derive the stability condition Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-8-3-2\">(8.3.2)</a>.","a":"Set <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r = r^* + \\delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>δ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\dot\\delta = -\\delta + F&#x27;(Jr^*+I)J\\delta</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Growth rate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(JF&#x27; - 1)/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> is negative iff <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JF&#x27;&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math></span>."}],"eqs":[{"num":"8.3.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>r</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\dot r = -r + F(Jr + I),\\qquad r^* = F(Jr^* + I)</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"8.3.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\,F&#x27;(Jr^* + I) &lt; 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/heterogeneity/","num":"8.4","obj":["Write the population gain function for heterogeneous thresholds","Use Jensen’s inequality to explain why average-cell and average-response differ","Compute the exact result for Gaussian thresholds and a probit gain function"],"body":"<p>No two CA1 pyramidal cells are alike. Their rheobase, input resistance, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> density and dendritic arbors vary from cell to cell, and so do deep and superficial pyramidal cells within the same layer. Interneurons come in more than twenty types. In Alzheimer’s disease models this variability can itself change: some cells become hyperexcitable while others fall silent.</p><p>A mean-field model built on one “typical” cell ignores all of this. The question for this section is when that matters: when does the population respond differently from its average member?</p><p>Real CA1 cells differ: thresholds, channel densities and morphologies vary from cell to cell. If cell parameters <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> are distributed with density <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the population response is the <b>average of the responses</b>, not the response of the average cell:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-8-4-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi><mtext>  </mtext><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>θ</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mtext>in general</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar F(x) = \\int F(x;\\theta)\\,p(\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\;\\neq\\; F(x;\\bar\\theta)\\quad\\text{in general}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(8.4.1)</span></div><p><dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"jensen\" tabindex=\"0\">Jensen’s inequality</dfn> makes this precise: averaging and a nonlinear function do not commute. Where <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span> is convex (below threshold) heterogeneity raises the average response; where it is concave (near saturation) it lowers it.</p><h3>An exact example</h3><p>Let each cell’s gain function be a cumulative Gaussian (probit) of width <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>s</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(x;\\theta) = \\Phi\\big((x-\\theta)/s\\big)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and let thresholds be Gaussian with mean <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and SD <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Then the average is again a probit, with a wider width:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-8-4-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar F(x) = \\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac{x - \\theta_0}{\\sqrt{s^2+\\sigma^2}}\\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(8.4.2)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-8-4-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 8.4.1</p><h4>Heterogeneity linearizes the population</h4><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and input two units above <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_0</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the average cell responds with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.977</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi(2) \\approx 0.977</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the population with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.814</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi(2/\\sqrt5)\\approx0.814</annotation></semantics></math></span> (a Monte Carlo average over 400,000 cells gives 0.814). The population’s maximal gain falls from 0.40 to 0.18, but its response is graded over a range <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mn>2.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt5\\approx2.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> times wider.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-8-4-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/heterogeneity.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/heterogeneity.svg\" alt=\"Figure 8.4.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 8.4.1.</b> <b>A</b> Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-8-4-2\">(8.4.2)</a>: wider threshold distributions give shallower, wider population curves. <b>B</b> Twelve cells with different thresholds (tan), their average (pink) and the response of the average cell (dashed).</figcaption></figure><p>Heterogeneity therefore trades peak gain for dynamic range, and it changes the stability of the recurrent fixed points of the previous section, since <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JF&#x27;</annotation></semantics></math></span> changes. Mean-field models of the CA1 parameter variants in the research thread should average responses over measured variability rather than use one representative cell.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 8.4</span> Average over a heterogeneous population</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">thetas = sample(theta_distribution, n=10000)     # or measured cell parameters\nF_pop(x) = mean([F(x; th) for th in thetas])\nF_avg_cell(x) = F(x; mean(thetas))\nplot both; their difference is the cost of the &#x27;typical cell&#x27; assumption\nuse F_pop (not F_avg_cell) inside r = F_pop(J r + I)</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Limits of the scalar closure</h4><p>Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-8-4-1\">(8.4.1)</a> still assumes cells differ only in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> and respond to a shared input. Dendritic location of inputs, resonance and correlated variability across parameters are not captured by a scalar rate. Test a reduced description against the detailed network before relying on it.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Cell-to-cell variability; deep vs superficial CA1 cells</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA1 populations; AD-related hyper/hypoactivity</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Population surveys of rheobase, R_in, sag</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Jensen’s inequality, convolution of distributions</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Monte Carlo over parameter samples</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Population gain is the average of cell gains over the parameter distribution.","By Jensen’s inequality the average response differs from the average cell’s response.","Gaussian thresholds with probit gains give a probit of width <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{s^2+\\sigma^2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>: lower peak gain, wider dynamic range."],"terms":["jensen","heterogeneity","gain-function"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, what is the population response 2 units above <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_0</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.414</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.921</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi(2/\\sqrt2) = \\Phi(1.414)\\approx0.921</annotation></semantics></math></span>, versus 0.977 for the average cell."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Below threshold (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&lt;\\theta_0</annotation></semantics></math></span>), does heterogeneity increase or decrease the population response, and why?","a":"Increase. There <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span> is convex: low-threshold cells fire disproportionately, so the average exceeds the average cell’s (near-zero) output."},{"kind":"calc","q":"How does the maximal population gain scale with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span> for large <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"Max gain <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.40</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= \\phi(0)/\\sqrt{s^2+\\sigma^2}\\approx 0.40/\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma\\gg s</annotation></semantics></math></span>: inversely with the spread."}],"eqs":[{"num":"8.4.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi><mtext>  </mtext><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>θ</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mtext>in general</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar F(x) = \\int F(x;\\theta)\\,p(\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\;\\neq\\; F(x;\\bar\\theta)\\quad\\text{in general}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"8.4.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>F</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar F(x) = \\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac{x - \\theta_0}{\\sqrt{s^2+\\sigma^2}}\\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]}]},{"n":9,"part":"IV","pathway":5,"title":"Learning and computation","intro":["So far parameters have been fixed. This chapter lets them change. We separate three things that are easy to conflate: <b>dynamics</b> (activity changing under fixed parameters), <b>learning</b> (parameters changing with experience) and <b>computation</b> (a useful mapping from inputs to outputs, judged against a task). Recurrent networks can compute without learning; learning rules can change weights without improving any task.","We cover readouts and decision rules, attractor networks as models of memory, Hebbian and spike-timing-dependent plasticity, and the methods used to evaluate whether a model or a decoder actually performs a task."],"obj":["Define a task and a readout, and separate information from mechanism","Analyse attractors in a scalar model and estimate Hopfield capacity","Write Hebbian, Oja and STDP rules and explain why Hebbian learning needs stabilization","Evaluate a decoder with held-out data, chance level and uncertainty"],"prereq":["Chapters 3 and 8"],"sections":[{"route":"/topics/learning/computation-task/","num":"9.1","obj":["State what makes an input–output mapping a computation","Write a linear threshold readout and its decision boundary","Explain why decodability shows information but not mechanism"],"body":"<p>A <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"computation\" tabindex=\"0\">computation</dfn> is a mapping from inputs to outputs that is useful for a specified task: deciding whether a stimulus is present, estimating position, predicting reward. Without a task, “the network computes” is not a testable statement.</p><p>The simplest output stage is a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"readout\" tabindex=\"0\">linear threshold readout</dfn> of a response feature vector <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf x</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-9-1-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">{</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.36em\" columnalign=\"left left\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y = H(\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x + b - \\theta) = \\begin{cases}1 &amp; \\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x + b &gt; \\theta\\\\ 0 &amp; \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(9.1.1)</span></div><p>The set <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x + b = \\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a hyperplane, the <b>decision boundary</b>; inputs on one side are labelled 1. A single readout can only separate classes that are linearly separable in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which is why rich, nonlinear recurrent responses help: they can make separable what was not separable in the raw input (the logic of reservoir computing).</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-9-1-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 9.1.1</p><h4>A one-feature decision</h4><p>With feature <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x = 0.6</annotation></semantics></math></span>, bias <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and threshold <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.7 &gt; 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Lowering <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math></span> to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> flips the decision. Bias and threshold are interchangeable; only <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b - \\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> matters.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-9-1-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/readout.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/readout.svg\" alt=\"Figure 9.1.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 9.1.1.</b> Synthetic data. <b>A</b> A linear readout, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-9-1-1\">(9.1.1)</a>, separates two classes with a line. <b>B</b> A middle class cannot be separated by one threshold on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, but a threshold on the nonlinear feature <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-0.5)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> separates it.</figcaption></figure><p>If a decoder can predict, say, animal position from CA1 activity, the activity <b>contains</b> information about position in a form that decoder can use. It does not show that downstream neurons use that information, or use it that way. Information, readout and biological mechanism are three separate claims.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 9.1</span> Train and apply a linear readout</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">X_train, y_train, X_test, y_test = split(responses, labels, by=&#x27;session&#x27;)\nw, b = fit_logistic_regression(X_train, y_train, regularization=lam)\ny_hat = (X_test @ w + b &gt; 0)\naccuracy = mean(y_hat == y_test)\nreport alongside chance level and a shuffled-label control</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Training on the test set</h4><p>Adjusting <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> after looking at test-set errors turns the test set into training data; the reported accuracy is then optimistic. The evaluation section returns to this.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Downstream neurons reading CA1 output</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Hippocampal position decoding</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Population recordings during behaviour</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Hyperplanes, linear separability</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>scikit-learn LogisticRegression; RNN dynamics page</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Computation is a task-defined input–output mapping.","A linear threshold readout <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x + b - \\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span> draws a hyperplane decision boundary.","Decodability shows available information, not the brain’s mechanism."],"terms":["computation","readout","decision-boundary","reservoir-computing"],"problems":[{"kind":"concept","q":"Two classes are <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\in\\{0.1, 0.9\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span> for class A and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> for class B. Can one threshold on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span> separate them? What feature would help?","a":"No: B lies between the A values. A nonlinear feature such as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x - 0.5)^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> makes them separable with one threshold. Recurrent dynamics can supply such features."},{"kind":"calc","q":"With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf w = (1, -1)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, classify <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf x = (0.3, 0.5)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.8, 0.2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.2\\to0</annotation></semantics></math></span>; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6</mn><mo>→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6\\to1</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The boundary is the line <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1 = x_2</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"A decoder predicts theta phase from a CA1 cell’s spike times with 90% accuracy. List two things this does <i>not</i> show.","a":"That downstream cells read phase this way, and that phase coding is why the animal behaves as it does. It also does not show that the information is unique to this cell or this code."}],"eqs":[{"num":"9.1.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">{</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.36em\" columnalign=\"left left\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y = H(\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x + b - \\theta) = \\begin{cases}1 &amp; \\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x + b &gt; \\theta\\\\ 0 &amp; \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/learning/recurrence-attractors/","num":"9.2","obj":["Find attractors of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x = x - x^3 + b</annotation></semantics></math></span> and their basins","Relate attractors to an energy (Lyapunov) function","State the Hopfield storage rule and approximate capacity"],"body":"<p>An <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"attractor\" tabindex=\"0\">attractor</dfn> is a state (or set of states) that nearby trajectories approach and remain near. Recurrent networks can hold several attractors at once, each a candidate memory; the input selects which basin the state falls into, and the dynamics complete the pattern.</p><p>The guide’s scalar example is</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-9-2-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>U</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x = x - x^3 + b = -\\frac{dU}{dx},\\qquad U(x) = -\\frac{x^2}{2} + \\frac{x^4}{4} - bx</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(9.2.1)</span></div><p>For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, fixed points are <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1, 0, +1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with slopes <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 - 3x^2 = -2, 1, -2</annotation></semantics></math></span>: two stable states separated by an unstable one. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a double-well <b>energy</b>: the state always rolls downhill, so it never oscillates and ends in a minimum. Its <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"basin\" tabindex=\"0\">basins</dfn> are <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-9-2-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 9.2.1</p><h4>Bias tilts the landscape</h4><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> the fixed points move to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.879</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.879</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.209</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.209</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.088</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.088</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the right well deepens and its basin grows. At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.385</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_c = 2/(3\\sqrt3)\\approx0.385</annotation></semantics></math></span> the left well and the barrier merge in a saddle-node, and for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> only one fixed point, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.19</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.19</annotation></semantics></math></span>, remains. A memory can be erased by a bias that removes its basin.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-9-2-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/double-well.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/double-well.svg\" alt=\"Figure 9.2.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 9.2.1.</b> The energy of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-9-2-1\">(9.2.1)</a> for three biases. At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> two equal wells (memories); <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0.2</annotation></semantics></math></span> deepens the right well; at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> the left well has vanished.</figcaption></figure><h3>Hopfield networks</h3><p>Hopfield (1982) stored binary patterns <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">ξ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol\\xi^\\mu\\in\\{-1,1\\}^N</annotation></semantics></math></span> in symmetric weights by a Hebbian rule, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>μ</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{ij} = \\frac1N\\sum_\\mu\\xi^\\mu_i\\xi^\\mu_j</annotation></semantics></math></span> (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i\\ne j</annotation></semantics></math></span>). With symmetric weights there is an energy function and the patterns are (approximately) its minima. For random patterns, recall fails above about <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.138</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.138N</annotation></semantics></math></span> patterns (Amit, Gutfreund &amp; Sompolinsky 1985): a 1,000-neuron network stores about 138. The CA3 recurrent network is the classic hippocampal candidate for such autoassociative memory.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 9.2</span> Store and recall patterns in a Hopfield network</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">W = (1/N) * sum(outer(xi, xi) for xi in patterns);  fill_diagonal(W, 0)\ns = corrupt(patterns[0], flip_fraction=0.2)        # cue\nrepeat until no change:\n    for i in random_order(N):\n        s[i] = sign(W[i] @ s)                      # asynchronous update\noverlap = s @ patterns[0] / N                      # 1.0 = perfect recall</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Attractors need not be fixed points</h4><p>Limit cycles (rhythms) and continuous families of states (line or ring attractors, proposed for head direction and grid cells) are also attractors. And a network can produce a long transient that looks persistent without being an attractor at all; check by perturbing the state.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Recurrent collaterals, pattern completion</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA3 (autoassociative memory); head-direction ring attractor</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Remapping, pattern completion experiments</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Energy functions, basins of attraction</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Hippocampal memory page (Hopfield recall)</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Attractors are states that trajectories approach; basins are the sets that reach them.","<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x = x - x^3 + b</annotation></semantics></math></span> has a double-well energy; bias tilts it and can remove a well (saddle-node at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.385</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_c\\approx0.385</annotation></semantics></math></span>).","Hopfield networks store patterns with Hebbian weights; capacity ≈ <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.138</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.138N</annotation></semantics></math></span> random patterns."],"terms":["attractor","basin","lyapunov-function","hopfield"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, a perturbation of size 0.01 from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^* = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span> decays with what time constant?","a":"Slope <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 - 3 = -2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta\\propto e^{-2t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>: time constant 0.5."},{"kind":"derive","q":"Show that <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>U</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot U \\le 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> along trajectories of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-9-2-1\">(9.2.1)</a>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>U</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot U = U&#x27;(x)\\dot x = -\\dot x^2 \\le 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, zero only at fixed points."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Approximately how many random patterns can a 5,000-neuron Hopfield network store?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.138</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5000</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>690</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.138\\times5000\\approx690</annotation></semantics></math></span>."}],"eqs":[{"num":"9.2.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>x</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>U</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x = x - x^3 + b = -\\frac{dU}{dx},\\qquad U(x) = -\\frac{x^2}{2} + \\frac{x^4}{4} - bx</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/learning/plasticity-rules/","num":"9.3","obj":["Write the basic Hebbian rule and show why it is unstable","Explain Oja normalization and BCM’s sliding threshold","Write the pair-based STDP window and compute weight changes"],"body":"<p><dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"hebbian\" tabindex=\"0\">Hebbian learning</dfn> strengthens a connection when pre- and postsynaptic activity coincide:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-9-3-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>pre</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta w = \\eta\\,a_\\text{pre}\\,a_\\text{post}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(9.3.1)</span></div><p>With non-negative activities this only ever increases <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>; if <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_\\text{post}</annotation></semantics></math></span> grows with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the feedback is positive and weights run away. Every usable rule adds stabilization: hard bounds (as in the lab’s clip to [0, 1]), normalization, or a threshold that adapts.</p><ul class=\"tb-list\"><li><b>Oja’s rule</b>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\mathbf w = \\eta\\,y(\\mathbf x - y\\mathbf w)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y = \\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, keeps <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf w|\\to1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and makes <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf w</annotation></semantics></math></span> converge to the input’s first principal component (Oja 1982).</li><li><b>BCM</b>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta w = \\eta\\,x\\,y(y - \\theta_M)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_M</annotation></semantics></math></span> tracking the recent average of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, gives LTD at low and LTP at high postsynaptic activity and stabilizes by sliding the threshold (Bienenstock, Cooper &amp; Munro 1982).</li></ul><h3>Spike-timing-dependent plasticity</h3><p>In hippocampal neurons, Bi and Poo (1998) found that a presynaptic spike a few ms <i>before</i> a postsynaptic spike potentiates the synapse and one <i>after</i> depresses it, with effects fading over tens of ms. The pair-based <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"stdp\" tabindex=\"0\">STDP</dfn> window, with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>pre</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = t_\\text{post} - t_\\text{pre}</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-9-3-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">{</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.36em\" columnalign=\"left left\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta w = \\begin{cases} A_+\\,e^{-\\Delta t/\\tau_+} &amp; \\Delta t &gt; 0\\\\ -A_-\\,e^{\\Delta t/\\tau_-} &amp; \\Delta t&lt;0\\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(9.3.2)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-9-3-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 9.3.1</p><h4>Pairs of spikes</h4><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_+ = 0.01</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0105</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_- = 0.0105</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_\\pm = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms (illustrative values): a pairing at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t = +10</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.0061</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+0.0061</annotation></semantics></math></span>; at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-10</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0064</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.0064</annotation></semantics></math></span>; at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+40</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms only <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.0014</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+0.0014</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Making <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_-\\tau_- </annotation></semantics></math></span> slightly larger than <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_+\\tau_+</annotation></semantics></math></span> makes uncorrelated firing depress on average, which stabilizes weights.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-9-3-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/stdp.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/stdp.svg\" alt=\"Figure 9.3.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 9.3.1.</b> <b>A</b> The pair STDP window, Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-9-3-2\">(9.3.2)</a>, with the example values. <b>B</b> Weight norm under plain Hebbian learning (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-9-3-1\">(9.3.1)</a>) and Oja’s rule on correlated inputs: Hebb grows without bound, Oja settles at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">w</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf w| = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</figcaption></figure><p>Pair-based STDP misses frequency effects measured in cortex and hippocampus; triplet rules (Pfister &amp; Gerstner 2006) and calcium-based rules (Graupner &amp; Brunel 2012) capture them, and both are in the Synapse workbench. In CA1 the dominant plasticity during place-field formation may be behavioural-timescale plasticity (BTSP), which operates over seconds rather than milliseconds (Bittner et al. 2017).</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 9.3</span> Pair-based STDP with traces</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">on pre spike:   x_pre += 1;   w -= A_minus * y_post      # depression\non post spike:  y_post += 1;  w += A_plus * x_pre      # potentiation\nevery step:     x_pre  *= exp(-dt / tau_plus)\n                y_post *= exp(-dt / tau_minus)\nw = clip(w, 0, w_max)                                 # bound (or soft bounds)</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Rule parameters are preparation-specific</h4><p>Window widths, amplitudes and even the sign of STDP depend on synapse type, dendritic location, developmental stage, temperature and neuromodulation. Treat fitted values as belonging to their preparation.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>NMDA-dependent LTP/LTD, plateau-driven BTSP</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>CA3→CA1 synapses; CA1 place-field formation</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Pairing protocols, field EPSP slope</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Hebbian, Oja, BCM, STDP rules</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Synapse workbench, STDP tab; STDP lab</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Hebb’s rule <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>pre</mtext></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta w = \\eta a_\\text{pre}a_\\text{post}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is unstable without bounds, normalization or sliding thresholds.","Oja’s rule extracts the first principal component; BCM switches LTD/LTP with a sliding threshold.","Pair STDP: potentiation for pre-before-post, depression for post-before-pre, decaying over tens of ms."],"terms":["hebbian","stdp","ltp","ltd","btsp"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"Starting from <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w = 0.5</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta = 0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>pre</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_\\text{pre} = 0.8</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_\\text{post} = 0.9</annotation></semantics></math></span>, apply three Hebbian updates with a clip to [0, 1].","a":"Each update adds 0.072: 0.572, 0.644, 0.716."},{"kind":"calc","q":"Integrate the STDP window over all <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the example values. What does the sign mean?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.0105</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_+\\tau_+ - A_-\\tau_- = 0.01\\times20 - 0.0105\\times20 = -0.01</annotation></semantics></math></span> (ms units). Negative: random, uncorrelated spike pairs produce net depression."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why does the NMDA receptor (Ch. 6) make a natural Hebbian coincidence detector?","a":"It needs glutamate (presynaptic activity) and depolarization to relieve Mg²⁺ block (postsynaptic activity); the resulting Ca²⁺ influx triggers LTP signalling."}],"eqs":[{"num":"9.3.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>pre</mtext></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta w = \\eta\\,a_\\text{pre}\\,a_\\text{post}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"9.3.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">{</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.36em\" columnalign=\"left left\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta w = \\begin{cases} A_+\\,e^{-\\Delta t/\\tau_+} &amp; \\Delta t &gt; 0\\\\ -A_-\\,e^{\\Delta t/\\tau_-} &amp; \\Delta t&lt;0\\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/learning/readouts-evaluation/","num":"9.4","obj":["Split data into training, validation and test sets and explain why","Compute accuracy with a binomial standard error and compare with chance","Recognize leakage and overfitting"],"body":"<p>A decoder is only as convincing as its evaluation. Data are split into a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"train-test\" tabindex=\"0\">training set</dfn> (fit parameters), a validation set (choose hyperparameters such as regularization) and a <b>test set</b> used once, at the end. Cross-validation rotates the roles to use small datasets efficiently.</p><p>For classification, accuracy is the fraction correct. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> independent test trials and true accuracy <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the estimate has a binomial standard error</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-9-4-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{SE}(\\hat p) = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}{n}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(9.4.1)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-9-4-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 9.4.1</p><h4>Is 80% good?</h4><p>80% correct on 50 test trials has SE ≈ 0.057, so a rough 95% interval is 0.69–0.91. On 200 trials the SE halves to 0.028. Against chance (50% for two balanced classes), 32/50 correct has a one-sided binomial <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx0.03</annotation></semantics></math></span>; 30/50 has <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx0.10</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Small test sets give wide intervals.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-9-4-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/evaluation.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/evaluation.svg\" alt=\"Figure 9.4.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 9.4.1.</b> <b>A</b> The 95% band for an 80% decoder (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-9-4-1\">(9.4.1)</a>) shrinks as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt n</annotation></semantics></math></span>. <b>B</b> Synthetic regression: training error keeps falling with model flexibility, held-out error rises once the model fits noise.</figcaption></figure><p>Two failures are common. <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"overfitting\" tabindex=\"0\">Overfitting</dfn>: a flexible model fits noise in the training set and generalizes poorly; the gap between training and test accuracy reveals it. <b>Leakage</b>: information from the test set reaches training, through preprocessing fitted on all data, through trials that are not independent (neighbouring time bins of the same run), or by tuning on test results. In neural data, splitting by recording session or by run, not by time bin, is often essential.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 9.4</span> Honest evaluation</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">folds = group_k_fold(trials, groups=session_id, k=5)\nfor train, test in folds:\n    scaler = fit_scaler(X[train])                    # fit preprocessing on train only\n    model  = fit(scaler(X[train]), y[train])\n    score.append(evaluate(model, scaler(X[test]), y[test]))\nreport mean(score), sd(score), chance, n_test\ntouch the final test set once</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Accuracy is not always the right metric</h4><p>With unbalanced classes, a decoder that always guesses the majority class can score high. Use balanced accuracy, ROC-AUC, or a task-specific error (e.g. mean position error in cm) and always report chance level.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>—: about the analysis</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Any decoding study</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Held-out sessions or laps</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Binomial confidence intervals, bias–variance</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>scikit-learn GroupKFold, Pipeline</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Train, validate and test on separate data; the test set is used once.","Accuracy SE <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= \\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)/n}</annotation></semantics></math></span>; compare with chance and report intervals.","Watch for overfitting (train/test gap) and leakage (non-independent splits, preprocessing on all data)."],"terms":["train-test","overfitting","cross-validation","leakage"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"A decoder gets 90% on 100 test trials. Give the SE and an approximate 95% interval.","a":"SE <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>100</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= \\sqrt{0.9\\times0.1/100} = 0.03</annotation></semantics></math></span>; interval ≈ 0.84–0.96."},{"kind":"concept","q":"You z-score each neuron using the mean and SD of the whole dataset, then split into train and test. What is wrong?","a":"Test-set statistics leak into training through the normalization. Fit the mean and SD on the training set only and apply them to the test set."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Neighbouring 100 ms bins from the same lap go into training and test. Why is the test accuracy optimistic?","a":"Adjacent bins are highly correlated, so test bins are near-duplicates of training bins. Split by lap or session."}],"eqs":[{"num":"9.4.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{SE}(\\hat p) = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}{n}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]}]},{"n":10,"part":"V","pathway":9,"title":"Fitting, sensitivity and validation","intro":["A model becomes scientific evidence only when it is confronted with data under stated rules. This chapter covers the core steps on the simplest possible case, a passive charging transient, so that every idea can be checked by hand: fitting by least squares, local sensitivity, identifiability, and propagating parameter uncertainty into predictions.","The same steps apply unchanged to fitting an f–I curve, an impedance profile or a place field in the Fitting studio; only the computational cost changes."],"obj":["Fit a model by minimizing a stated loss and diagnose the fit","Compute analytic and finite-difference sensitivities and normalize them","Recognize structural non-identifiability and design observations that remove it","Propagate parameter uncertainty into predictions by the delta method and by sampling"],"prereq":["Chapter 1 (passive response)","Basic statistics (mean, variance)"],"sections":[{"route":"/topics/validation/fit-a-transient/","num":"10.1","obj":["Define a least-squares loss for a transient","Estimate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> by linearization and by numerical minimization","Explain why a good fit to one protocol is not validation"],"body":"<p>The first thing done after breaking into a cell in whole-cell mode is usually a small hyperpolarizing current step. The voltage falls, curves and settles. From that curve the experimenter reads input resistance (from the steady deflection) and the membrane time constant (from the curvature). These numbers are the cell’s passive identity card, and every model of that cell should reproduce them.</p><p>In a real CA1 cell the curve is never a perfect single exponential. Charge first spreads from the soma into the dendrites (a fast component), and with larger steps <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> activates and produces sag. That is why protocols use small steps, and why fitting is done with an explicit model whose assumptions you can state.</p><p>Fitting chooses parameters <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span> that make model predictions <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(t_k;\\boldsymbol\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span> close to observations <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_k</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Closeness is defined by a <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"loss-function\" tabindex=\"0\">loss</dfn>, most often the mean squared error:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-10-1-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\boldsymbol\\theta) = \\frac1n\\sum_{k=1}^n\\big(V(t_k;\\boldsymbol\\theta) - y_k\\big)^2,\\qquad V(t; A,\\tau) = A\\big(1-e^{-t/\\tau}\\big)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(10.1.1)</span></div><p>Minimizing MSE is maximum likelihood if the errors are independent Gaussians with equal variance. That assumption is worth checking: residuals that are correlated in time, or larger at some times than others, call for a different loss or weighting.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-10-1-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 10.1.1</p><h4>Two ways to find τ</h4><p><b>Linearize.</b> If <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span> is known, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(1 - V/A) = -t/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a straight line through the origin with slope <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mtext> ms</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3.93</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(10\\text{ ms}) = 3.93</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0.393</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = -10/\\ln(1-0.393) = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms. Linearization is quick but weights errors unevenly: near saturation <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 - V/A</annotation></semantics></math></span> is small and noise is magnified by the log.</p><p><b>Minimize.</b> On the lab’s 21 samples from 0 to 100 ms (true <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms), trying <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 15</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms gives MSE ≈ 0.34 mV² and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 25</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms gives ≈ 0.26 mV². The loss is asymmetric around the minimum (zero at 20 ms), so a symmetric error bar on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> would be misleading.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-10-1-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/fit-transient.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/fit-transient.svg\" alt=\"Figure 10.1.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 10.1.1.</b> <b>A</b> A synthetic noisy transient (true τ = 20 ms) and its least-squares fit. <b>B</b> The loss of Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-10-1-1\">(10.1.1)</a> as a function of τ: one minimum, steeper on the short side.</figcaption></figure><p>A small loss means the model <i>can</i> reproduce these data. It does not mean the parameters are unique (next sections), nor that the model predicts other protocols. <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"validation\" tabindex=\"0\">Validation</dfn> means predicting data that played no part in fitting: a different current amplitude, a different stimulus shape, a held-out cell.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 10.1</span> Fit a passive transient</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">t, y = load_hyperpolarizing_step()             # small step, Ih minimal\nmodel = lambda t, A, tau: A * (1 - exp(-t / tau))\nbest = minimize(lambda p: mean((model(t, *p) - y)**2), p0=[guess_A, guess_tau])\nresiduals = y - model(t, *best)\ncheck residuals for structure (two time constants? sag?)\nvalidate: predict a different step size without refitting</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Many features for one fit</h4><p>Fitting a single feature (say <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>) can be achieved by many mechanisms. When the guide tried to fit the CA1 preset’s input resistance and time constant alone, the fit broke its sag and afterhyperpolarization. Fit several features jointly, or check the unfitted ones afterwards.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Passive membrane plus dendritic charging and sag</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Whole-cell CA1 recordings</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Hyperpolarizing step protocol</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Least squares, maximum likelihood</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>scipy.optimize.curve_fit; Fitting studio</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["A fit minimizes an explicit loss; MSE corresponds to independent equal-variance Gaussian errors.","Linearization (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(1-V/A)</annotation></semantics></math></span> vs <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math></span>) is quick but distorts noise; numerical minimization is general.","A good fit is not validation; validation predicts data not used in fitting."],"terms":["loss-function","least-squares","residual","validation"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"A transient with known <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A = 8</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV reaches 6.0 mV at 30 ms. Estimate <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>30</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>21.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = -30/\\ln(1 - 6/8) = -30/\\ln0.25\\approx21.6</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Residuals from your fit are positive for the first 10 ms and negative after. What does that suggest?","a":"A systematic misfit: the model shape is wrong (e.g. the real response has two time constants from a dendrite, or an onset delay), not just noise."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Name a validation test for a passive model fitted to a 50 pA step.","a":"Predict the response to a 100 pA or −50 pA step, or to a sinusoid, without refitting; compare with data."}],"eqs":[{"num":"10.1.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo separator=\"true\">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\boldsymbol\\theta) = \\frac1n\\sum_{k=1}^n\\big(V(t_k;\\boldsymbol\\theta) - y_k\\big)^2,\\qquad V(t; A,\\tau) = A\\big(1-e^{-t/\\tau}\\big)</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/validation/parameter-sensitivity/","num":"10.2","obj":["Derive <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial V/\\partial g</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the passive step response","Compute a central finite difference and its error order","Use normalized sensitivities to compare parameters with different units"],"body":"<p>Wash a drug onto a slice that blocks part of the leak or <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> conductance and the recorded responses change. Some change a lot, some barely. A sensitivity analysis is the model version of that pharmacology experiment: perturb one parameter slightly, record how much a measured quantity moves, and repeat for each parameter.</p><p>The answer depends on which measurement you look at, just as in the slice: blocking <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> changes the sag and the resonance frequency much more than it changes the peak of an action potential.</p><p>The <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"sensitivity\" tabindex=\"0\">sensitivity</dfn> of an observable to a parameter is its derivative. For the passive step response with leak <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>, capacitance <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span> and current <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(t) = (I/g)(1 - e^{-gt/C})</annotation></semantics></math></span>, differentiation gives</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-10-2-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>I</mi><mi>g</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial g} = -\\frac{I}{g^2}\\left(1 - e^{-gt/C}\\right) + \\frac{I}{g}\\,\\frac{t}{C}\\,e^{-gt/C}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(10.2.1)</span></div><p>The first term says a larger leak lowers the steady state; the second, that it speeds the approach. Because parameters have different units, compare <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"normalized-sensitivity\" tabindex=\"0\">normalized (relative) sensitivities</dfn>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S = (g/V)\\,\\partial V/\\partial g</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the percentage change in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> per percent change in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-10-2-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 10.2.1</p><h4>Sensitivity depends on when you look</h4><p>With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> pA and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12.64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V = 12.64</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1.057</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial V/\\partial g = -1.057</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV/nS and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S = -0.42</annotation></semantics></math></span>. At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\approx-0.05</annotation></semantics></math></span> (early charging depends on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span>, not <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>); at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 200</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1.00</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\approx-1.00</annotation></semantics></math></span> (the steady state is <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/g</annotation></semantics></math></span>).</p><p>A central finite difference with a 5% step, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.05</mn><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.95</mn><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[V(1.05g) - V(0.95g)]/(0.1g)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, gives −1.0572 against the analytic −1.0570. Central differences have error <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; forward differences only <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p></div><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-10-2-2\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial g}\\approx\\frac{V(g+h) - V(g-h)}{2h}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(10.2.2)</span></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-10-2-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/sensitivity.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/sensitivity.svg\" alt=\"Figure 10.2.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 10.2.1.</b> Normalized sensitivities of the passive step response to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span> (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-10-2-1\">(10.2.1)</a>), against measurement time: early samples report <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span>, late samples report <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 10.2</span> Local sensitivity of every parameter</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">base = observable(params)\nfor name in params:\n    h = 0.01 * params[name]\n    up, down = observable(params | {name: +h}), observable(params | {name: -h})\n    S[name] = (up - down) / (2*h) * params[name] / base     # normalized\nrank parameters by |S| for EACH observable separately</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Local sensitivity is local</h4><p>Derivatives describe small changes around one operating point. A parameter can be irrelevant for one protocol and dominant for another (compare <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 200</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms above), or matter only in combination with others. Global methods (sampling the whole plausible range, variance-based indices) answer different questions.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Partial channel block</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Any model of a recorded cell</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Pharmacology (ZD7288, TTX, TEA)</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Partial derivatives, finite differences</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Finite differences or automatic differentiation (JAX)</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Sensitivity <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>observable</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>parameter</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">= \\partial(\\text{observable})/\\partial(\\text{parameter})</annotation></semantics></math></span>; normalized <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S = (\\theta/V)\\,\\partial V/\\partial\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","For the passive step, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span> for leak goes from ≈ 0 early to −1 at steady state.","Central finite differences are <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> accurate; check against analytic derivatives when possible."],"terms":["sensitivity","normalized-sensitivity","finite-difference"],"problems":[{"kind":"derive","q":"Show that <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\to-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\to\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"For large <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V\\to I/g</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>→</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial V/\\partial g\\to -I/g^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S = (g/V)(-I/g^2) = (g^2/I)(-I/g^2) = -1</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Which parameter would you expect to dominate the normalized sensitivity at very early times, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\ll\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V\\approx It/C</annotation></semantics></math></span>, independent of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_C\\approx-1</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_g\\approx0</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why is a central difference more accurate than a forward one for the same <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"In the Taylor expansions of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(g\\pm h)</annotation></semantics></math></span> the second-order terms cancel in the difference, leaving an <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> error; the forward difference keeps an <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h)</annotation></semantics></math></span> term."}],"eqs":[{"num":"10.2.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>I</mi><mi>g</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial g} = -\\frac{I}{g^2}\\left(1 - e^{-gt/C}\\right) + \\frac{I}{g}\\,\\frac{t}{C}\\,e^{-gt/C}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"},{"num":"10.2.2","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial g}\\approx\\frac{V(g+h) - V(g-h)}{2h}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/validation/identifiability/","num":"10.3","obj":["Distinguish structural and practical identifiability","Show that a normalized transient identifies only <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = C/g</annotation></semantics></math></span>","Propose an additional observation that separates <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>"],"body":"<p>Two CA1 cells can produce indistinguishable voltage traces with different underlying channels. One might have more <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> and more leak K⁺ conductance; the other less of both. Studies of small circuits found that very different combinations of channel densities can generate the same activity (Prinz, Bucher &amp; Marder 2004), and biological variability between animals is consistent with this.</p><p>For a modeler this means a recording may not determine every parameter. Before trusting a fitted value, ask whether some other combination would have fit just as well.</p><p>A parameter is <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"identifiability\" tabindex=\"0\">identifiable</dfn> if the observations determine it uniquely. <b>Structural</b> non-identifiability is a property of the model and the observable even with perfect data; <b>practical</b> non-identifiability arises because noisy, finite data constrain a parameter only weakly.</p><p>Normalize the passive step response by its steady state:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-10-3-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{V(t)}{V_\\infty} = 1 - e^{-t\\,g/C}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(10.3.1)</span></div><p>Only the ratio <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C/g = \\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> appears. The pairs <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext> pF</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mtext> nS</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(C, g) = (100\\text{ pF}, 5\\text{ nS})</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>200</mn><mtext> pF</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>10</mn><mtext> nS</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(200\\text{ pF}, 10\\text{ nS})</annotation></semantics></math></span> produce identical normalized curves: any point on the line <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 20\\,g</annotation></semantics></math></span> fits perfectly. The loss landscape has a flat valley rather than a single minimum.</p><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-10-3-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 10.3.1</p><h4>Breaking the tie</h4><p>Keep the absolute voltage for a <i>known</i> current. With 100 pA, the steady states are <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100/5 = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>10</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100/10 = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, which distinguishes the two pairs: amplitude identifies <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and then <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span>. If the injected current were also unknown (a synaptic input of unknown size), the ambiguity would return.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-10-3-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/identifiability.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/identifiability.svg\" alt=\"Figure 10.3.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 10.3.1.</b> Loss over (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span>) for data generated with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> nS, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 100</annotation></semantics></math></span> pF (dot). <b>A</b> Fitting the normalized trace (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-10-3-1\">(10.3.1)</a>): a flat valley along <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C = 20g</annotation></semantics></math></span> (dashed). <b>B</b> Fitting absolute voltage for a known 100 pA: a single minimum.</figcaption></figure><p>In larger models such ridges are everywhere: different combinations of channel densities can produce the same voltage trace (Prinz, Bucher &amp; Marder 2004 found many such solutions in a small circuit model). Profile likelihoods, which re-optimize all other parameters while scanning one, reveal them. The remedy is always more informative data: different protocols, different recording sites, pharmacology.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 10.3</span> Profile likelihood</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">for value in grid(theta_1):\n    fit all other parameters with theta_1 fixed at value\n    profile[value] = best_loss\nflat profile             -&gt; theta_1 not identifiable from these data\nsharp minimum            -&gt; identifiable\nalso: multi-start fits; do the solutions agree?</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Fitting algorithms “succeed” on unidentifiable models</h4><p>An optimizer will return <i>some</i> point on a flat valley, often wherever it started near. Reporting that point with a small standard error, as if it were unique, is a classic mistake. Restart from several initial guesses and compare.</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Degeneracy of channel mixes</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Stomatogastric ganglion (Prinz et al. 2004); CA1 models</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Multiple protocols, dendritic recordings</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Profile likelihood, Fisher information</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>Fitting studio: loss landscapes</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Structural non-identifiability: the observable depends on parameters only through combinations.","A normalized passive transient identifies <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = C/g</annotation></semantics></math></span> only; absolute amplitude to a known current adds <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","Detect ridges with profile likelihoods and multi-start fits; remove them with new protocols."],"terms":["identifiability","profile-likelihood","degeneracy"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"Name two <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(C, g)</annotation></semantics></math></span> pairs other than those above that give the same normalized curve as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>100</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(100, 5)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","a":"Any with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C/g = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, e.g. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>50</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>2.5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(50, 2.5)</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>300</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(300, 15)</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"You fit a two-compartment model to somatic recordings only and find that <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_d</annotation></semantics></math></span> trade off. Suggest an experiment.","a":"Record or stimulate in the dendrite (local and transfer responses separate coupling from dendritic leak), or use a frequency sweep, since <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_d</annotation></semantics></math></span> shape the fast and slow modes differently."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Is “many parameter sets fit equally well” a failure of the model?","a":"Not necessarily. Biological degeneracy means cells may achieve the same function with different channel mixes. It is a failure only if you then claim to have measured a unique parameter value."}],"eqs":[{"num":"10.3.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{V(t)}{V_\\infty} = 1 - e^{-t\\,g/C}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]},{"route":"/topics/validation/uncertainty-in-predictions/","num":"10.4","obj":["Distinguish a scenario envelope from a statistical interval","Propagate uncertainty with the delta method","Check the delta method by Monte Carlo sampling"],"body":"<p>Measured membrane properties come with spread. Repeat the time-constant measurement in one cell and the values scatter; measure it across cells and the spread grows; change temperature or recording method and the mean moves. A model built on one number inherits none of this, yet its predictions (when a dendritic EPSP peaks, how much a theta input is amplified) are only as certain as the numbers it was built from.</p><p>This section propagates that biological and experimental spread through a model to a prediction you can compare with data.</p><p>Parameter uncertainty becomes prediction uncertainty. The simplest analysis is a <b>scenario envelope</b>: compute predictions at chosen low, central and high parameter values. With <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 18, 20, 22</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mtext> ms</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(20\\text{ ms})</annotation></semantics></math></span> is 6.71, 6.32 and 5.97 mV. This is useful but it is not a confidence interval: no probability is attached to the chosen range.</p><p>If a parameter has a distribution, say <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> with SD <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the <dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"delta-method\" tabindex=\"0\">delta method</dfn> linearizes the prediction:</p><div class=\"tb-eq\" id=\"eq-10-4-1\"><div class=\"tb-eq-body\"><span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">∣</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">∣</mo></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{SD}\\big(V(t)\\big) \\approx \\left|\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial\\tau}\\right|\\sigma_\\tau,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial\\tau} = -A\\,\\frac{t}{\\tau^2}\\,e^{-t/\\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div><span class=\"tb-eq-num\">(10.4.1)</span></div><div class=\"tb-example\" id=\"ex-10-4-1\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Worked example 10.4.1</p><h4>Delta method versus sampling</h4><p>At <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20 \\pm 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms: <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>400</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.184</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial V/\\partial\\tau = -10\\times(20/400)e^{-1}\\approx-0.184</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV/ms, so SD(V) ≈ 0.37 mV. Sampling 200,000 values of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> from a Gaussian and computing <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> for each gives SD 0.370 mV and a mean of 6.34 mV, slightly above <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>τ</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>6.32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(\\bar\\tau) = 6.32</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV: the curvature of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></span> shifts the mean, an effect the linear method misses.</p></div><figure class=\"tb-fig\" id=\"fig-10-4-1\"><a href=\"img/tb/uncertainty.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open full size\"><img src=\"img/tb/uncertainty.svg\" alt=\"Figure 10.4.1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><b>Figure 10.4.1.</b> <b>A</b> Monte Carlo prediction band for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau\\sim\\mathcal N(20, 2^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms, and the ±2 ms scenarios (dotted). <b>B</b> The SD of the prediction from sampling and from the delta method (Eq. <a class=\"tb-xref\" href=\"#eq-10-4-1\">(10.4.1)</a>) agree and peak near <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = \\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</figcaption></figure><p><dfn class=\"tb-term\" data-term=\"monte-carlo\" tabindex=\"0\">Monte Carlo</dfn> propagation (sample parameters, simulate, summarize) handles nonlinearity, several correlated parameters and non-Gaussian distributions, at the cost of many simulations. For expensive models, surrogate models or a smaller design of runs are used. Whatever the method, state where the parameter distribution came from (a fit’s posterior, measured cell-to-cell variability, or a plausible guess), because that is what the prediction interval means.</p><figure class=\"tb-algo\"><figcaption><span class=\"tb-label\">Algorithm 10.4</span> Monte Carlo propagation</figcaption><pre class=\"tb-pseudo\">samples = draw(parameter_distribution, n=2000)     # posterior, bootstrap, or measured spread\npredictions = [simulate(model, p) for p in samples]\nband = percentile(predictions, [2.5, 50, 97.5], axis=0)\ndelta_check = abs(dV_dtau) * sd(tau)                # quick linear comparison\nstate where parameter_distribution came from</pre><p class=\"tb-algo-note\">Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.</p></figure><aside class=\"tb-note tb-pitfall\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Common pitfall</p><h4>Uncertainty in the model, not just its parameters</h4><p>Propagating parameter uncertainty assumes the model structure is right. Structural uncertainty (missing currents, wrong morphology) is usually larger and can only be explored by comparing alternative models (Fitting studio: model comparison).</p></aside><aside class=\"tb-scales\"><p class=\"tb-label\">Across scales</p><dl><div><dt>Biology</dt><dd>Measurement spread and cell-to-cell variability</dd></div><div><dt>Brain region</dt><dd>Any model prediction compared with data</dd></div><div><dt>Measurement</dt><dd>Repeated measures, bootstrap</dd></div><div><dt>Math</dt><dd>Delta method, Monte Carlo</dd></div><div><dt>Code</dt><dd>numpy sampling loops; Fitting studio: bootstrap f–I</dd></div></dl></aside>","summary":["Scenario envelopes show ranges without probabilities; statistical intervals need a parameter distribution.","Delta method: SD(prediction) ≈ |derivative| × SD(parameter).","Monte Carlo captures nonlinearity and correlations; structural uncertainty needs model comparison."],"terms":["delta-method","monte-carlo","scenario-envelope"],"problems":[{"kind":"calc","q":"Using the delta method, what is SD(V) at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 40</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms for <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A = 10</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau = 20\\pm2</annotation></semantics></math></span> ms?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>40</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>400</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.135</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial V/\\partial\\tau = -10\\times(40/400)e^{-2}\\approx-0.135</annotation></semantics></math></span> mV/ms; SD ≈ 0.27 mV."},{"kind":"concept","q":"At what time is the prediction <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span> most sensitive to <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>?","a":"Maximize <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(t/\\tau^2)e^{-t/\\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></span>: derivative zero at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = \\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>. So uncertainty in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> matters most around one time constant, and not at all at <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> or <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\to\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span>."},{"kind":"concept","q":"Why is the Monte Carlo mean of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> larger than <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>τ</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(\\bar\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></span> in the example?","a":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(\\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is convex in <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> near 20 ms (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial^2V/\\partial\\tau^2&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></span> there), so by Jensen’s inequality (Ch. 8) the average of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> exceeds <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span> at the average."}],"eqs":[{"num":"10.4.1","html":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">∣</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">∣</mo></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{SD}\\big(V(t)\\big) \\approx \\left|\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial\\tau}\\right|\\sigma_\\tau,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial\\tau} = -A\\,\\frac{t}{\\tau^2}\\,e^{-t/\\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></span>"}]}]}],"glossary":{"admittance":{"term":"Admittance","def":"The reciprocal of impedance, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y = 1/Z</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Admittances of parallel membrane pathways add, which makes them convenient for building <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z(f)</annotation></semantics></math></span> from currents.","sec":"3.4","route":"/topics/dynamics/resonance-measurement/","plain":"Admittance"},"asynchronous-irregular":{"term":"Asynchronous irregular (AI) state","def":"A network state in which neurons fire at low rates, irregularly (CV ≈ 1) and nearly independently of each other.","sec":"7.4","route":"/topics/networks/collective-states/","plain":"Asynchronous irregular (AI) state"},"attractor":{"term":"Attractor","def":"A state or set of states that nearby trajectories approach and stay near: a stable fixed point, a stable limit cycle or a continuous family of stable states.","sec":"9.2","route":"/topics/learning/recurrence-attractors/","plain":"Attractor"},"autonomous":{"term":"Autonomous system","def":"A system whose equations do not depend explicitly on time (constant inputs), so the vector field is fixed and trajectories cannot cross.","sec":"3.1","route":"/topics/dynamics/states-trajectories/","plain":"Autonomous system"},"axial-current":{"term":"Axial current","def":"Current flowing along the inside of a neurite between neighbouring compartments, proportional to their voltage difference.","sec":"1.4","route":"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/","plain":"Axial current"},"axial-resistivity":{"term":"Axial resistivity (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_i</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"The resistivity of cytoplasm along a neurite, in Ω·cm (typically 100–300 Ω·cm in models).","sec":"2.1","route":"/topics/morphology/electrotonic-distance/","plain":"Axial resistivity ( R_i )"},"basin":{"term":"Basin of attraction","def":"The set of initial states whose trajectories end on a given attractor.","sec":"9.2","route":"/topics/learning/recurrence-attractors/","plain":"Basin of attraction"},"bifurcation":{"term":"Bifurcation","def":"A qualitative change in a system’s dynamics (number or stability of fixed points or cycles) as a parameter crosses a critical value.","sec":"3.3","route":"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/","plain":"Bifurcation"},"bistability":{"term":"Bistability","def":"Coexistence of two stable states (e.g. rest and repetitive firing, or low and high population activity) for the same parameters.","sec":"4.3","route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/hopf-normal-form/","plain":"Bistability"},"btsp":{"term":"Behavioural-timescale plasticity (BTSP)","def":"Synaptic plasticity in CA1 triggered by dendritic plateau potentials that potentiates inputs active within seconds of the plateau; implicated in rapid place-field formation (Bittner et al. 2017).","sec":"9.3","route":"/topics/learning/plasticity-rules/","plain":"Behavioural-timescale plasticity (BTSP)"},"cable-equation":{"term":"Cable equation","def":"The partial differential equation <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda^2 V_{xx} = \\tau V_t + V</annotation></semantics></math></span> describing passive voltage spread along a uniform cylindrical neurite.","sec":"2.1","route":"/topics/morphology/electrotonic-distance/","plain":"Cable equation"},"capacitance":{"term":"Membrane capacitance (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"The membrane’s ability to store charge, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q = CV</annotation></semantics></math></span>; the lipid bilayer acts as the dielectric. Measured in pF for a cell.","sec":"1.1","route":"/topics/channels/membrane-circuit/","plain":"Membrane capacitance ( C )"},"center":{"term":"Centre","def":"A fixed point surrounded by a continuous family of closed orbits (purely imaginary eigenvalues in a linear system); structurally unstable.","sec":"4.1","route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/phase-portraits/","plain":"Centre"},"chemical-synapse":{"term":"Chemical synapse","def":"A connection at which a presynaptic spike releases neurotransmitter that opens postsynaptic receptor channels.","sec":"6.1","route":"/topics/synapses/chemical-electrical/","plain":"Chemical synapse"},"chirp":{"term":"Chirp (ZAP) stimulus","def":"A sinusoidal current whose frequency sweeps over time, used to estimate impedance across frequencies in one recording.","sec":"5.3","route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/chirp-input/","plain":"Chirp (ZAP) stimulus"},"compartment":{"term":"Compartment","def":"A piece of a neuron treated as having a single voltage; compartmental models couple many compartments by axial conductances.","sec":"1.4","route":"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/","plain":"Compartment"},"computation":{"term":"Computation","def":"An input–output mapping that is useful for a specified task; defined relative to that task.","sec":"9.1","route":"/topics/learning/computation-task/","plain":"Computation"},"convergence-test":{"term":"Convergence test","def":"Re-running a simulation with a smaller time step (or finer spatial discretization) to check that results no longer change.","sec":"4.4","route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/numerical-time-step/","plain":"Convergence test"},"coupling-coefficient":{"term":"Coupling coefficient","def":"The fraction of a voltage change in one cell that appears in a coupled cell; for steady signals across a gap junction <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>gap</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_\\text{gap}/(g_\\text{gap}+g_L)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"6.1","route":"/topics/synapses/chemical-electrical/","plain":"Coupling coefficient"},"coupling-conductance":{"term":"Coupling conductance (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_c</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"The axial conductance joining two compartments, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi d^2/(4R_i\\ell)</annotation></semantics></math></span> for a cylinder of diameter <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math></span> and length <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"2.2","route":"/topics/morphology/coupled-compartments/","plain":"Coupling conductance ( g_c )"},"critical-slowing":{"term":"Critical slowing down","def":"The lengthening of recovery times from perturbations as a system approaches a bifurcation, because an eigenvalue approaches zero real part.","sec":"4.3","route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/hopf-normal-form/","plain":"Critical slowing down"},"cross-validation":{"term":"Cross-validation","def":"Repeatedly splitting data into training and held-out parts and averaging held-out performance, to estimate generalization from limited data.","sec":"9.4","route":"/topics/learning/readouts-evaluation/","plain":"Cross-validation"},"cv-isi":{"term":"Coefficient of variation (CV) of ISIs","def":"SD divided by mean of a neuron’s interspike intervals: 0 for perfectly regular firing, 1 for a Poisson process.","sec":"7.4","route":"/topics/networks/collective-states/","plain":"Coefficient of variation (CV) of ISIs"},"dales-principle":{"term":"Dale’s principle","def":"The observation that a neuron releases the same transmitter(s) at all its synapses, so its outgoing connections share a sign.","sec":"7.2","route":"/topics/networks/wiring-delays/","plain":"Dale’s principle"},"decision-boundary":{"term":"Decision boundary","def":"The set of inputs where a classifier’s output changes; a hyperplane for a linear threshold readout.","sec":"9.1","route":"/topics/learning/computation-task/","plain":"Decision boundary"},"degeneracy":{"term":"Degeneracy","def":"Different parameter combinations (e.g. channel densities) producing the same function or output; in biology, a source of robustness.","sec":"10.3","route":"/topics/validation/identifiability/","plain":"Degeneracy"},"delta-method":{"term":"Delta method","def":"Approximating the variance of a function of uncertain parameters by linearization: Var<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">′</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(f)\\approx f&#x27;^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>Var<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"10.4","route":"/topics/validation/uncertainty-in-predictions/","plain":"Delta method"},"driving-force":{"term":"Driving force","def":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V - E</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the difference between membrane voltage and an ion’s reversal potential. Its sign sets the direction of current.","sec":"1.2","route":"/topics/channels/driving-force/","plain":"Driving force"},"effective-conductance":{"term":"Effective slow conductance (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_S</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"In a linearized gated current, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>g</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar g\\,(V_0-E)\\,dx_\\infty/dV</annotation></semantics></math></span>; positive values are restoring (resonance), negative values amplifying.","sec":"5.1","route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/ih-and-m-current/","plain":"Effective slow conductance ( g_S )"},"ei-balance":{"term":"E/I balance","def":"Near-cancellation of large excitatory and inhibitory currents onto neurons, leaving fluctuations to drive spiking. Not equal cell numbers.","sec":"6.4","route":"/topics/synapses/ei-feedback/","plain":"E/I balance"},"eigenvalue":{"term":"Eigenvalue","def":"A number <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span> with <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\mathbf u = \\lambda\\mathbf u</annotation></semantics></math></span>; for a linearized system, the growth rate (real part) and rotation rate (imaginary part) of a mode.","sec":"3.3","route":"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/","plain":"Eigenvalue"},"electrical-synapse":{"term":"Electrical synapse (gap junction)","def":"A direct cytoplasmic connection through connexin channels that passes current in proportion to the voltage difference.","sec":"6.1","route":"/topics/synapses/chemical-electrical/","plain":"Electrical synapse (gap junction)"},"electrotonic-distance":{"term":"Electrotonic distance","def":"Physical distance divided by the length constant, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X = x/\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>; a measure of how much a neurite attenuates.","sec":"2.1","route":"/topics/morphology/electrotonic-distance/","plain":"Electrotonic distance"},"erdos-renyi":{"term":"Erdős–Rényi graph","def":"A random graph in which every possible edge exists independently with the same probability <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"7.2","route":"/topics/networks/wiring-delays/","plain":"Erdős–Rényi graph"},"excitability-class":{"term":"Excitability class","def":"Hodgkin’s classification of repetitive-firing onset: class I can fire at arbitrarily low rates (SNIC), class II starts at a non-zero rate (often Hopf).","sec":"3.3","route":"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/","plain":"Excitability class"},"feedback-inhibition":{"term":"Feedback inhibition","def":"Inhibition driven by the principal cells it inhibits (recurrent loop through interneurons).","sec":"6.4","route":"/topics/synapses/ei-feedback/","plain":"Feedback inhibition"},"feedforward-inhibition":{"term":"Feedforward inhibition","def":"Inhibition driven by the same input that excites the principal cells, arriving shortly after the excitation.","sec":"6.3","route":"/topics/synapses/timing-location/","plain":"Feedforward inhibition"},"fi-curve":{"term":"f–I curve","def":"Steady firing rate as a function of injected current; its shape near onset reveals the excitability class.","sec":"7.1","route":"/topics/networks/spiking-neurons/","plain":"f–I curve"},"finite-difference":{"term":"Finite difference","def":"Approximating a derivative from function values at nearby points; the central difference <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[f(x+h)-f(x-h)]/2h</annotation></semantics></math></span> has error <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"10.2","route":"/topics/validation/parameter-sensitivity/","plain":"Finite difference"},"firing-rate":{"term":"Firing rate","def":"Spikes per unit time, for a neuron (time average) or a population (average over neurons in a short window).","sec":"7.3","route":"/topics/networks/rates-wilson-cowan/","plain":"Firing rate"},"fixed-point":{"term":"Fixed point (equilibrium)","def":"A state at which all derivatives vanish, so the system stays there if undisturbed.","sec":"3.2","route":"/topics/dynamics/nullclines/","plain":"Fixed point (equilibrium)"},"forward-euler":{"term":"Forward Euler method","def":"The explicit update <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1} = x_n + \\Delta t\\,f(x_n)</annotation></semantics></math></span>; first-order accurate and conditionally stable.","sec":"4.4","route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/numerical-time-step/","plain":"Forward Euler method"},"frequency-resolution":{"term":"Frequency resolution","def":"The spacing between frequencies in a discrete Fourier transform, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/T</annotation></semantics></math></span> for a record of duration <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"5.3","route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/chirp-input/","plain":"Frequency resolution"},"gain":{"term":"Gain","def":"The slope of a gain function at the operating point: output change per unit input change.","sec":"8.2","route":"/topics/mean-field/gain-function/","plain":"Gain"},"gain-function":{"term":"Gain (transfer) function","def":"The mapping from input to output rate of a neuron or population, e.g. an f–I curve or a sigmoid.","sec":"8.2","route":"/topics/mean-field/gain-function/","plain":"Gain (transfer) function"},"gating-variable":{"term":"Gating variable","def":"A number from 0 to 1 giving the fraction of channel gates in the permissive state; obeys first-order voltage-dependent kinetics.","sec":"1.3","route":"/topics/channels/hh-gates/","plain":"Gating variable"},"ghk":{"term":"Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz (GHK) equation","def":"Gives the resting voltage at which total current through several permeant ions is zero, weighting each by its permeability.","sec":"1.2","route":"/topics/channels/driving-force/","plain":"Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz (GHK) equation"},"hcn":{"term":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> (HCN current)","def":"A mixed cation current through HCN channels that activates with hyperpolarization, reverses near −30 mV and gates slowly; produces sag and theta resonance in CA1.","sec":"1.4","route":"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/","plain":"I_h (HCN current)"},"hebbian":{"term":"Hebbian learning","def":"Synaptic change driven by correlated pre- and postsynaptic activity, schematically <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>pre</mtext></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta w\\propto a_\\text{pre}a_\\text{post}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"9.3","route":"/topics/learning/plasticity-rules/","plain":"Hebbian learning"},"heterogeneity":{"term":"Heterogeneity","def":"Cell-to-cell variation in parameters such as thresholds or channel densities within a population.","sec":"8.4","route":"/topics/mean-field/heterogeneity/","plain":"Heterogeneity"},"hopf":{"term":"Hopf bifurcation","def":"Loss of stability of a fixed point as a complex-conjugate pair of eigenvalues crosses the imaginary axis, creating or destroying a limit cycle.","sec":"3.3","route":"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/","plain":"Hopf bifurcation"},"hopfield":{"term":"Hopfield network","def":"A recurrent network with symmetric Hebbian weights whose stored patterns are attractors; capacity ≈ <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.138</mn><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.138N</annotation></semantics></math></span> random patterns.","sec":"9.2","route":"/topics/learning/recurrence-attractors/","plain":"Hopfield network"},"identifiability":{"term":"Identifiability","def":"Whether observations determine a parameter uniquely; structural (model and observable) or practical (noise and finite data).","sec":"10.3","route":"/topics/validation/identifiability/","plain":"Identifiability"},"impedance":{"term":"Impedance (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"Complex ratio of voltage to current for sinusoidal input; <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z|</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the amplitude ratio and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg Z</annotation></semantics></math></span> the phase.","sec":"3.4","route":"/topics/dynamics/resonance-measurement/","plain":"Impedance ( Z )"},"impedance-phase":{"term":"Impedance phase","def":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\arg Z</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the timing of the voltage relative to a sinusoidal current; in the guide, positive means voltage leads.","sec":"5.2","route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/impedance-phase/","plain":"Impedance phase"},"inactivation":{"term":"Inactivation","def":"A gating process that closes a channel during maintained depolarization even though its activation gate is open (e.g. HH <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math></span>).","sec":"1.3","route":"/topics/channels/hh-gates/","plain":"Inactivation"},"inductance":{"term":"Phenomenological inductance","def":"Inductor-like electrical behaviour of a slow restoring current: it opposes slow voltage changes and produces a phase lead.","sec":"5.2","route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/impedance-phase/","plain":"Phenomenological inductance"},"input-impedance":{"term":"Input impedance","def":"Voltage response at a site per unit current injected at the same site.","sec":"2.4","route":"/topics/morphology/local-versus-soma/","plain":"Input impedance"},"input-resistance":{"term":"Input resistance (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\text{in}</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"Steady voltage change per unit injected current, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/g_\\text{total}</annotation></semantics></math></span> for a single compartment; measured in MΩ.","sec":"1.1","route":"/topics/channels/membrane-circuit/","plain":"Input resistance ( R_in )"},"jacobian":{"term":"Jacobian","def":"Matrix of partial derivatives of the right-hand sides with respect to the state variables, evaluated at a fixed point.","sec":"3.3","route":"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/","plain":"Jacobian"},"jensen":{"term":"Jensen’s inequality","def":"For convex <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the average of <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math></span> exceeds <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math></span> of the average (reversed for concave <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math></span>).","sec":"8.4","route":"/topics/mean-field/heterogeneity/","plain":"Jensen’s inequality"},"leak-conductance":{"term":"Leak conductance (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_L</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"The lumped conductance of always-open channels that sets the passive input resistance.","sec":"1.1","route":"/topics/channels/membrane-circuit/","plain":"Leak conductance ( g_L )"},"leakage":{"term":"Data leakage","def":"Information from evaluation data reaching model training, making reported performance optimistic.","sec":"9.4","route":"/topics/learning/readouts-evaluation/","plain":"Data leakage"},"least-squares":{"term":"Least squares","def":"Fitting by minimizing the sum (or mean) of squared residuals.","sec":"10.1","route":"/topics/validation/fit-a-transient/","plain":"Least squares"},"length-constant":{"term":"Length constant (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{R_md/4R_i}</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the distance over which a steady voltage decays by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e</annotation></semantics></math></span> in a semi-infinite passive cable.","sec":"2.1","route":"/topics/morphology/electrotonic-distance/","plain":"Length constant ( lambda )"},"lif":{"term":"Leaky integrate-and-fire (LIF) neuron","def":"A passive membrane with a voltage threshold, reset and refractory period standing in for the spike mechanism.","sec":"7.1","route":"/topics/networks/spiking-neurons/","plain":"Leaky integrate-and-fire (LIF) neuron"},"limit-cycle":{"term":"Limit cycle","def":"An isolated closed trajectory; a stable limit cycle is a self-sustained oscillation such as repetitive firing.","sec":"4.3","route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/hopf-normal-form/","plain":"Limit cycle"},"loss-function":{"term":"Loss function","def":"A number measuring mismatch between model and data that fitting minimizes, e.g. mean squared error.","sec":"10.1","route":"/topics/validation/fit-a-transient/","plain":"Loss function"},"ltd":{"term":"Long-term depression (LTD)","def":"A lasting decrease in synaptic strength.","sec":"9.3","route":"/topics/learning/plasticity-rules/","plain":"Long-term depression (LTD)"},"ltp":{"term":"Long-term potentiation (LTP)","def":"A lasting increase in synaptic strength, first described in the hippocampus.","sec":"9.3","route":"/topics/learning/plasticity-rules/","plain":"Long-term potentiation (LTP)"},"lyapunov-function":{"term":"Energy (Lyapunov) function","def":"A function that never increases along trajectories, so the state flows downhill to its minima.","sec":"9.2","route":"/topics/learning/recurrence-attractors/","plain":"Energy (Lyapunov) function"},"m-current":{"term":"M-current (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_M</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"A slow, non-inactivating K⁺ current through Kv7/KCNQ channels that activates near −60 mV and above; suppressed by muscarinic receptor activation.","sec":"1.4","route":"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/","plain":"M-current ( I_M )"},"mean-field":{"term":"Mean-field approximation","def":"Replacing interactions among many units by their average effect, giving a closed equation for population activity.","sec":"8.3","route":"/topics/mean-field/self-consistency/","plain":"Mean-field approximation"},"mg-block":{"term":"Mg²⁺ block","def":"Voltage-dependent block of NMDA receptor channels by extracellular Mg²⁺, relieved by depolarization.","sec":"6.2","route":"/topics/synapses/synaptic-conductance/","plain":"Mg²⁺ block"},"monte-carlo":{"term":"Monte Carlo propagation","def":"Estimating the distribution of a prediction by sampling parameters, simulating each sample and summarizing the outputs.","sec":"10.4","route":"/topics/validation/uncertainty-in-predictions/","plain":"Monte Carlo propagation"},"nernst":{"term":"Nernst equation","def":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>o</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E = (RT/zF)\\ln([X]_o/[X]_i)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the voltage at which an ion’s electrical and concentration forces balance.","sec":"1.2","route":"/topics/channels/driving-force/","plain":"Nernst equation"},"nmda":{"term":"NMDA receptor","def":"A glutamate receptor permeable to Ca²⁺ whose current needs both glutamate and depolarization (Mg²⁺ block); slow kinetics.","sec":"6.2","route":"/topics/synapses/synaptic-conductance/","plain":"NMDA receptor"},"normalized-sensitivity":{"term":"Normalized sensitivity","def":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\theta/y)\\,\\partial y/\\partial\\theta</annotation></semantics></math></span>: percentage change in an observable per percentage change in a parameter.","sec":"10.2","route":"/topics/validation/parameter-sensitivity/","plain":"Normalized sensitivity"},"nullcline":{"term":"Nullcline","def":"The set of states where one state variable’s derivative is zero.","sec":"3.2","route":"/topics/dynamics/nullclines/","plain":"Nullcline"},"numerical-stability":{"term":"Numerical stability","def":"Whether a numerical method keeps errors bounded; forward Euler on <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot V=-V/\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span> requires <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t&lt;2\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"4.4","route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/numerical-time-step/","plain":"Numerical stability"},"overfitting":{"term":"Overfitting","def":"Fitting noise specific to the training data, revealed by worse performance on held-out data.","sec":"9.4","route":"/topics/learning/readouts-evaluation/","plain":"Overfitting"},"persistent-sodium":{"term":"Persistent Na⁺ current (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>NaP</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_\\text{NaP}</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"A small non-inactivating Na⁺ current active below threshold; amplifies subthreshold responses.","sec":"5.1","route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/ih-and-m-current/","plain":"Persistent Na⁺ current ( I_NaP )"},"ping":{"term":"PING","def":"Pyramidal–interneuron network gamma: a rhythm generated by excitatory cells driving interneurons that inhibit them, with period set largely by inhibitory decay.","sec":"6.4","route":"/topics/synapses/ei-feedback/","plain":"PING"},"poisson-process":{"term":"Poisson process","def":"Random events occurring independently at a constant rate; interspike intervals are exponential with CV = 1.","sec":"8.1","route":"/topics/mean-field/population-activity/","plain":"Poisson process"},"population-activity":{"term":"Population activity (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"Spikes per neuron per unit time across a population in a short window, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n/(N\\Delta t)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"8.1","route":"/topics/mean-field/population-activity/","plain":"Population activity ( A )"},"profile-likelihood":{"term":"Profile likelihood","def":"The best achievable fit as one parameter is scanned while all others are re-optimized; flat profiles reveal non-identifiability.","sec":"10.3","route":"/topics/validation/identifiability/","plain":"Profile likelihood"},"q-factor":{"term":"Resonance strength (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"Ratio of peak impedance to impedance at a stated low reference frequency (often 0.5 Hz or 0 Hz).","sec":"3.4","route":"/topics/dynamics/resonance-measurement/","plain":"Resonance strength ( Q )"},"q10":{"term":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{10}</annotation></semantics></math></span>","def":"The factor by which a rate increases for a 10 °C rise in temperature.","sec":"1.3","route":"/topics/channels/hh-gates/","plain":"Q_10"},"quantal-release":{"term":"Quantal (binomial) release","def":"Model of transmitter release as <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math></span> sites releasing with probability <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span>, each producing a response <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"6.1","route":"/topics/synapses/chemical-electrical/","plain":"Quantal (binomial) release"},"readout":{"term":"Readout","def":"A function, often linear plus threshold, that maps network activity to a task output.","sec":"9.1","route":"/topics/learning/computation-task/","plain":"Readout"},"reciprocity":{"term":"Reciprocity","def":"In a linear passive network, the transfer impedance from site A to B equals that from B to A.","sec":"2.4","route":"/topics/morphology/local-versus-soma/","plain":"Reciprocity"},"refractory-period":{"term":"Refractory period","def":"Time after a spike during which another spike is impossible (absolute) or harder to evoke (relative).","sec":"1.3","route":"/topics/channels/hh-gates/","plain":"Refractory period"},"relaxation-oscillation":{"term":"Relaxation oscillation","def":"An oscillation alternating slow drift along branches of a nullcline with fast jumps between them.","sec":"3.2","route":"/topics/dynamics/nullclines/","plain":"Relaxation oscillation"},"reservoir-computing":{"term":"Reservoir computing","def":"Using a fixed recurrent network’s rich dynamics as features and training only a readout.","sec":"9.1","route":"/topics/learning/computation-task/","plain":"Reservoir computing"},"residual":{"term":"Residual","def":"Difference between an observation and the fitted model’s prediction.","sec":"10.1","route":"/topics/validation/fit-a-transient/","plain":"Residual"},"resonance":{"term":"Resonance","def":"A peak in the impedance at a non-zero frequency: a preference for inputs near that frequency, without requiring spontaneous oscillation.","sec":"3.4","route":"/topics/dynamics/resonance-measurement/","plain":"Resonance"},"resonance-frequency":{"term":"Resonance frequency (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_R</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"The frequency at which <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|Z(f)|</annotation></semantics></math></span> is maximal.","sec":"3.4","route":"/topics/dynamics/resonance-measurement/","plain":"Resonance frequency ( f_R )"},"reversal-potential":{"term":"Reversal potential (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"The voltage at which the net current through a channel changes sign.","sec":"1.1","route":"/topics/channels/membrane-circuit/","plain":"Reversal potential ( E )"},"rheobase":{"term":"Rheobase","def":"The smallest constant current that makes a neuron fire.","sec":"7.1","route":"/topics/networks/spiking-neurons/","plain":"Rheobase"},"saddle-node":{"term":"Saddle-node bifurcation","def":"Two fixed points (one stable, one unstable) collide and disappear as a parameter changes.","sec":"3.3","route":"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/","plain":"Saddle-node bifurcation"},"sag":{"term":"Sag","def":"The slow partial recovery of voltage during a hyperpolarizing step, caused by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_h</annotation></semantics></math></span> activation.","sec":"5.1","route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/ih-and-m-current/","plain":"Sag"},"scenario-envelope":{"term":"Scenario envelope","def":"Predictions at chosen low, central and high parameter values; a range without attached probabilities.","sec":"10.4","route":"/topics/validation/uncertainty-in-predictions/","plain":"Scenario envelope"},"self-consistency":{"term":"Self-consistency condition","def":"In a recurrent population, the requirement that the assumed rate equals the rate produced, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r = F(Jr+I)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"8.3","route":"/topics/mean-field/self-consistency/","plain":"Self-consistency condition"},"sensitivity":{"term":"Sensitivity","def":"The derivative of an observable with respect to a parameter.","sec":"10.2","route":"/topics/validation/parameter-sensitivity/","plain":"Sensitivity"},"shunting-inhibition":{"term":"Shunting inhibition","def":"Inhibition by a conductance whose reversal is near the membrane voltage: it reduces excitatory responses without much hyperpolarization.","sec":"6.2","route":"/topics/synapses/synaptic-conductance/","plain":"Shunting inhibition"},"sigmoid":{"term":"Sigmoid","def":"An S-shaped function, e.g. <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(1+e^{-s(x-\\theta)})</annotation></semantics></math></span>, used for gating curves and gain functions.","sec":"7.3","route":"/topics/networks/rates-wilson-cowan/","plain":"Sigmoid"},"size-principle":{"term":"Size principle","def":"Smaller motor neurons, with higher input resistance, are recruited before larger ones (Henneman).","sec":"2.3","route":"/topics/morphology/area-and-density/","plain":"Size principle"},"snic":{"term":"SNIC","def":"Saddle-node on an invariant circle: a saddle-node bifurcation on a closed orbit, giving firing that starts at zero frequency.","sec":"4.2","route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/saddle-node/","plain":"SNIC"},"specific-capacitance":{"term":"Specific capacitance (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_m</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"Capacitance per membrane area, about 1 µF/cm² for biological membranes.","sec":"1.1","route":"/topics/channels/membrane-circuit/","plain":"Specific capacitance ( c_m )"},"spine-factor":{"term":"Spine factor","def":"A multiplier on membrane capacitance and conductances of spiny compartments that accounts for spine membrane without modelling spines.","sec":"2.3","route":"/topics/morphology/area-and-density/","plain":"Spine factor"},"state":{"term":"State","def":"The smallest set of variables that, with the equations and inputs, determines the future.","sec":"3.1","route":"/topics/dynamics/states-trajectories/","plain":"State"},"state-space":{"term":"State (phase) space","def":"The space of all possible states; for two variables, the phase plane.","sec":"3.1","route":"/topics/dynamics/states-trajectories/","plain":"State (phase) space"},"stdp":{"term":"Spike-timing-dependent plasticity (STDP)","def":"Synaptic change that depends on the relative timing of pre- and postsynaptic spikes.","sec":"9.3","route":"/topics/learning/plasticity-rules/","plain":"Spike-timing-dependent plasticity (STDP)"},"steady-state-activation":{"term":"Steady-state activation (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_\\infty</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"The value a gate relaxes to at a fixed voltage, <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha/(\\alpha+\\beta)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"1.3","route":"/topics/channels/hh-gates/","plain":"Steady-state activation ( x_infty )"},"stiff":{"term":"Stiff system","def":"A system with widely separated time scales, for which explicit methods need steps set by the fastest one.","sec":"4.4","route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/numerical-time-step/","plain":"Stiff system"},"synaptic-delay":{"term":"Delay","def":"Time between a presynaptic spike and its postsynaptic effect: axonal conduction plus synaptic transmission.","sec":"7.2","route":"/topics/networks/wiring-delays/","plain":"Delay"},"synaptic-kernel":{"term":"Synaptic kernel","def":"The conductance time course after one presynaptic spike: exponential, alpha or difference of exponentials.","sec":"6.3","route":"/topics/synapses/timing-location/","plain":"Synaptic kernel"},"synchrony":{"term":"Synchrony","def":"The degree to which neurons’ activity fluctuates together, often measured by population variance relative to single-cell variance.","sec":"7.4","route":"/topics/networks/collective-states/","plain":"Synchrony"},"temporal-summation":{"term":"Temporal summation","def":"Addition of postsynaptic responses that overlap in time.","sec":"6.3","route":"/topics/synapses/timing-location/","plain":"Temporal summation"},"time-constant":{"term":"Membrane time constant (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C/g</annotation></semantics></math></span>: the time for a passive membrane to cover 63% of its response to a step.","sec":"1.1","route":"/topics/channels/membrane-circuit/","plain":"Membrane time constant ( tau )"},"trace-determinant":{"term":"Trace–determinant plane","def":"The classification of 2-D linear systems by <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T = \\operatorname{tr}A</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>det</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta = \\det A</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"4.1","route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/phase-portraits/","plain":"Trace–determinant plane"},"train-test":{"term":"Training / validation / test sets","def":"Disjoint data used to fit parameters, choose settings and estimate final performance, respectively.","sec":"9.4","route":"/topics/learning/readouts-evaluation/","plain":"Training / validation / test sets"},"trajectory":{"term":"Trajectory","def":"The path a state traces through state space over time.","sec":"3.1","route":"/topics/dynamics/states-trajectories/","plain":"Trajectory"},"transfer-impedance":{"term":"Transfer impedance","def":"Voltage response at one site per unit current injected at another.","sec":"2.4","route":"/topics/morphology/local-versus-soma/","plain":"Transfer impedance"},"transient":{"term":"Transient","def":"The part of a response that depends on initial conditions and decays with the system’s time constants.","sec":"5.3","route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/chirp-input/","plain":"Transient"},"validation":{"term":"Validation","def":"Testing a fitted model’s predictions on data or protocols not used to fit it.","sec":"10.1","route":"/topics/validation/fit-a-transient/","plain":"Validation"},"vector-field":{"term":"Vector field","def":"The assignment of a velocity (the right-hand side of the equations) to every point of state space.","sec":"3.1","route":"/topics/dynamics/states-trajectories/","plain":"Vector field"},"weight-matrix":{"term":"Weight matrix (<span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W</annotation></semantics></math></span>)","def":"Matrix of connection strengths; in this guide <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is from neuron <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math></span> to neuron <span class=\"tb-m\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i</annotation></semantics></math></span>.","sec":"7.2","route":"/topics/networks/wiring-delays/","plain":"Weight matrix ( W )"},"wilson-cowan":{"term":"Wilson–Cowan model","def":"Coupled rate equations for excitatory and inhibitory populations with sigmoidal gain functions (1972).","sec":"7.3","route":"/topics/networks/rates-wilson-cowan/","plain":"Wilson–Cowan model"},"zero-phase-frequency":{"term":"Zero-phase frequency","def":"The frequency at which impedance phase crosses zero; near, but not equal to, the resonance frequency.","sec":"5.2","route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/impedance-phase/","plain":"Zero-phase frequency"}},"built":"2026-10-25"};
window.NG_BOOK_CORPUS=[{"route":"/topics/channels/membrane-circuit/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §1.1. A lipid bilayer is about 4 nm thick and is an excellent insulator. Ions on either side are separated by it, so the membrane behaves as a capacitor: the charge Q held on it is proportional to the voltage across it, Q = CV . Biological membranes have a remarkably constant specific capacitance of about c_m approx 1 muF/cm^2 , so total capacitance is simply membrane area times c_m .Ion channels pierce the bilayer and let charge leak across. At rest, the many always-open channels behave together like a resistor in series with a battery: a leak conductance g_L that pulls the voltage toward the leak reversal potential E_L . Charge conservation says that any current injected into the cell must either charge the capacitor or flow out through the conductance: C,fracdVdt ;=; I_in ;-; g_L,(V - E_L) (1.1.1)The term g_L(V-E_L) is the ionic current, written outward-positive: when V > E_L it is positive and carries charge out, lowering V . This sign convention is used everywhere in the guide. Dividing Eq. (1.1.1) by g_L shows that only two combinations of parameters matter, the membrane time constant tau = C/g_L and the input resistance R_in = 1/g_L : tau,fracdVdt = -(V - E_L) + R_in,I_in, qquad tau = fracCg_L,quad R_in = frac1g_L (1.1.2)For a current step of size I switched on at t=0 with the cell at rest, the solution is a saturating exponential: V(t) = E_L + R_in Ileft(1 - e^-t/tauright) (1.1.3)Figure 1.1.1. Passive charging, Eq. (1.1.3). A 25 and 50 pA steps into a cell with C = 100 pF, g_L = 5 nS: the final deflection scales with current, and the dotted lines mark 63% at t=tau . B The normalized response for three time constants: tau sets only the speed.The steady-state deflection Delta V_infty = R_in I is set by the conductance alone; the capacitance only decides h"},{"route":"/topics/channels/membrane-circuit/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 1.1. A cell has C = 150 pF and g_L = 10 nS. Find tau , R_in and the steady deflection for a 30 pA step. Estimate the capacitance of a spherical soma 20 µm in diameter, assuming c_m = 1 mu F/cm². You double a model cell’s area while keeping c_m and g_m fixed, and inject the same 50 pA. What happens to tau , R_in and the steady deflection? Show by substitution that Eq. (1.1.3) solves Eq. (1.1.1) with V(0)=E_L ."},{"route":"/topics/channels/driving-force/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §1.2. Each ion species is pushed by two forces: its concentration gradient and the electric field. The voltage at which these exactly cancel, so that no net current flows through a channel selective for that ion, is the ion’s reversal (equilibrium) potential, given by the Nernst equation: E_X = fracRTzF,lnfrac[X]_out[X]_in (1.2.1)At 37 °C, RT/F approx 26.7 mV (61.5 mV per tenfold concentration ratio); z is the ion’s valence. With typical mammalian concentrations:IonOutside (mM)Inside (mM) z E at 37 °CK⁺4140+1≈ −95 mVNa⁺14512+1≈ +67 mVCa²⁺20.0001+2≈ +132 mVCl⁻1207−1≈ −76 mVFor an ohmic channel the current is conductance times driving force: I_X = g_X,(V - E_X) (1.2.2)Figure 1.2.1. A Nernst potentials for the concentrations in the table, and the GHK resting potential. B Ohmic I–V lines, Eq. (1.2.2): each crosses zero at its reversal potential, so at −65 mV the K⁺ current is outward and the Na⁺ current inward.Conductance is never negative, so the **sign of V - E_X alone decides the direction of current**. With our outward-positive convention, a positive current is net positive charge leaving the cell. At V=-65 mV, K⁺ current is outward ( V-E_K = +30 mV) and Na⁺ current inward ( V - E_Na approx -131 mV), even though both conductances are positive numbers.Worked example 1.2.1Which way does chloride go?A GABA _A synapse opens a Cl⁻ conductance of 3 nS in a cell sitting at −60 mV, with E_Cl = -76 mV. Then I = 3,(-60 + 76) = +48 pA: an outward current that hyperpolarizes the cell. In terms of ions, outward current of a negative ion means Cl⁻ flowing in. Keep the two words apart: the sign convention is about charge, not about which ion moves.In immature neurons with high intracellular Cl⁻, E_Cl can sit above rest; the same conductance then produces inward current and de"},{"route":"/topics/channels/driving-force/","heading":"Where does the resting potential come from?","text":"Textbook §1.2. Where does the resting potential come from?If a membrane were permeable only to K⁺, it would rest at E_K . Real membranes are mostly, but not only, permeable to K⁺. The Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz (GHK) voltage equation gives the voltage at which the total current through several permeabilities is zero: V_rest = fracRTFlnfracP_K[K]_o + P_Na[Na]_o + P_Cl[Cl]_iP_K[K]_i + P_Na[Na]_i + P_Cl[Cl]_o (1.2.3)With P_K : P_Na : P_Cl = 1 : 0.05 : 0.45 and the concentrations above, Eq. (1.2.3) gives about −70 mV: close to E_K because K⁺ dominates, pulled up by a small Na⁺ permeability. During an action potential P_Na transiently exceeds P_K and the voltage swings toward E_Na . Note that Cl⁻ appears with inside and outside swapped because it is negatively charged.Algorithm 1.2 Reversal and resting potentialsRT_F = 8.314 * (273.15 + temp_C) / 96485 * 1000 # mV def nernst(c_out, c_in, z): return RT_F / z * log(c_out / c_in) E_K = nernst(4, 140, +1); E_Na = nernst(145, 12, +1) V_rest = RT_F * log((pK*K_o + pNa*Na_o + pCl*Cl_i) / (pK*K_i + pNa*Na_i + pCl*Cl_o)) # GHK I_K = g_K * (V - E_K) # outward-positive: sign of (V - E) gives directionPseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallOhmic is an approximationEq. (1.2.2) assumes a straight-line current–voltage relation. When concentrations are very asymmetric (Ca²⁺ is the extreme case), the true single-channel current is better described by the GHK current equation, which rectifies. Models of Ca²⁺ currents in dendrites often use GHK for this reason. The reversal potential is still where the current changes sign.Across scalesBiologyIon gradients maintained by the Na⁺/K⁺ pump and transporters (KCC2 sets low Cl⁻)Brain regionMature CA1 pyramidal cells: E_Cl below rest; immature neurons: aboveMeasurementVoltage cla"},{"route":"/topics/channels/driving-force/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 1.2. Extracellular K⁺ rises from 4 to 10 mM (as can happen during intense activity or ischaemia). Recompute E_K at 37 °C with [K]_i = 140 mM. A 0.5 nS Na⁺ conductance is open at −65 mV with E_Na = +66 mV. What is the current and its direction? Why does the Cl⁻ term in Eq. (1.2.3) have [Cl]_i in the numerator? Using GHK with only K⁺ and Na⁺ ( P_Cl=0 ), what resting potential do you get for P_Na/P_K = 0.05 ? And for 0.5?"},{"route":"/topics/channels/hh-gates/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §1.3. Hodgkin and Huxley (1952) described the squid giant axon with three voltage-dependent gating variables: m (Na⁺ activation), h (Na⁺ inactivation) and n (K⁺ activation). Each is the fraction of gates in the permissive state, a number between 0 and 1, and obeys first-order kinetics with voltage-dependent opening rate alpha(V) and closing rate beta(V) : fracdxdt = alpha_x(V),(1-x) - beta_x(V),x ;=; fracx_infty(V) - xtau_x(V) (1.3.1) x_infty = fracalpha_xalpha_x + beta_x, qquad tau_x = frac1alpha_x + beta_x (1.3.2)Figure 1.3.1. The Hodgkin–Huxley gates (squid parameters, rest −65 mV, 6.3 °C). A Steady states x_infty(V) . B Time constants tau_x(V) on a log scale: m is about ten times faster than h and n .The second form is easier to read: at any fixed voltage, x relaxes exponentially toward its steady-state value x_infty(V) with time constant tau_x(V) . Both depend on voltage, so when V moves, the target and the speed of every gate move with it.The ionic currents are products of gates: I_Na = bar g_Na,m^3 h,(V - E_Na), qquad I_K = bar g_K,n^4,(V - E_K) (1.3.3)and the membrane equation is C,dV/dt = I_in - I_Na - I_K - I_L , a four-dimensional system in (V, m, h, n) . The powers (3 and 4) were chosen to fit the sigmoidal delay of the conductance after a voltage step; they are often interpreted as independent subunits, but this is a fit, not a molecular measurement.Worked example 1.3.1The gates at rest and at −20 mVWith the standard squid parameters (rest −65 mV, 6.3 °C), the steady states and time constants are:Gate x_infty(-65) tau_x(-65) x_infty(-20) tau_x(-20) m 0.0530.24 ms0.880.38 ms h 0.608.5 ms0.0091.2 ms n 0.325.5 ms0.842.3 msRead the spike off this table. After a jump to −20 mV, m races toward 0.88 within a fraction of a millisecond (0.5 ms after the ste"},{"route":"/topics/channels/hh-gates/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 1.3. At some voltage a gate has alpha = 0.5 ms⁻¹ and beta = 1.5 ms⁻¹. Find x_infty and tau_x . Starting from x=0 , what is x after 1 ms? Using the table in Worked example 1.3.1, estimate the open fraction of the Na⁺ conductance, m^3h , at rest. Why would blocking K⁺ channels (e.g. with TEA) not prevent the spike from ending, but make it much broader? By what factor are HH gate rates multiplied at 22 °C relative to 6.3 °C, with Q_10 = 3 ?"},{"route":"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §1.4. A CA1 pyramidal neuron is not a squid axon. It has a soma, a long apical dendrite running through stratum radiatum into stratum lacunosum-moleculare, and a basal tree in stratum oriens. Modelling it means two changes to the HH recipe: more kinds of channels, and spatial structure. In a compartmental model the cell is split into pieces, each obeying C_k,fracdV_kdt = I_in,k - sum_ion I_ion,k + sum_j,sim,k g_jk,(V_j - V_k) (1.4.1)The last term is the axial current arriving from neighbouring compartments j through coupling conductances g_jk (developed in §2.2). Each axial current appears with opposite sign in the two compartments it connects, so charge is conserved."},{"route":"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/","heading":"Specific versus total quantities","text":"Textbook §1.4. Specific versus total quantitiesChannel models are published as densities: conductance per area (mS/cm² or S/cm²) and capacitance per area (µF/cm²). A compartment needs totals. The conversion is multiplication by area, and the most useful identity is 1 mum^2 = 10^-8 cm^2 : g,[nS] = bar g,[mS/cm^2]times A,[mum^2]times 10^-2, qquad C,[pF] = c_m,[muF/cm^2]times A,[mum^2]times10^-2 (1.4.2)Worked example 1.4.1Densities to totalsA dendritic compartment of 1000 µm² with bar g_h = 0.1 mS/cm² carries 0.1 times 1000times10^-2 = 1 nS of I_h and has C = 10 pF. A 20 µm spherical soma (≈ 1257 µm²) with the squid value bar g_K = 36 mS/cm² would carry about 450 nS of K⁺ conductance. Writing totals next to densities in your parameter table catches factor-of-100 errors before they become “biology”."},{"route":"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/","heading":"The CA1 conductance set","text":"Textbook §1.4. The CA1 conductance setCurrentChannel familyActivatesNotable in CA1 I_Na (transient)Nav1.xdepolarization, fastspikes; backpropagation into dendrites I_Kdr Kv2 (mainly Kv2.1)depolarization, delayedrepolarization I_A Kv4.2depolarization, fast; inactivatesdensity rises with distance along the apical dendrite (Hoffman et al. 1997) I_h HCN1 (with HCN2)hyperpolarization, slowdensity rises several-fold toward distal dendrites (Magee 1998) I_M Kv7/KCNQdepolarization (≈ −60 mV and up), slowspike-frequency adaptation; theta resonance near threshold (Hu et al. 2002) I_NaP Nav (persistent)depolarization, non-inactivatingamplifies subthreshold responses and resonance I_Ca Cav1, Cav2, Cav3depolarizationdendritic Ca²⁺ spikes, plasticity I_SK , I_BK K _Ca intracellular Ca²⁺afterhyperpolarizationsTwo of these gradients matter throughout the book. Distal I_h shortens dendritic EPSPs so that temporal summation at the soma becomes nearly independent of where inputs arrive (Magee 1999), and I_h together with I_M gives CA1 cells their theta-range resonance (Chapter 5)."},{"route":"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/","heading":"A real CA1 pyramidal cell, measured","text":"Textbook §1.4. A real CA1 pyramidal cell, measuredFigure 1.4.1. A real CA1 pyramidal cell (mpg141209_A; Migliore et al. 2018, ModelDB 244688). A The full reconstruction drawn in its own traced layer boundaries; apical dendrites pink, basal blue, axon grey. B The soma and proximal tree drawn at true diameter: the apical trunk is several times thicker than the obliques that leave it.The reconstruction in Figure 1.4.1 (cell mpg141209_A from Migliore et al. 2018, traced in Neurolucida with its layer boundaries) makes the abstract compartment concrete. Summing the frustum area of every traced segment gives 8.9 mm of dendrite (4.1 mm basal, 4.9 mm apical) with 22,214 µm² of membrane, 78 branch points and 84 tips. The soma contributes only about 462 µm², roughly 2% of the total. At c_m = 1 mu F/cm² the traced membrane alone holds about 227 pF; spines, which are not traced, would add substantially more (Section 2.3).The cell spans every layer of CA1. Along its own traced boundaries, stratum oriens is about 147 µm thick, the pyramidal layer about 60 µm, radiatum about 227 µm and lacunosum-moleculare about 204 µm, and the longest apical path reaches 787 µm from the soma. Each layer receives a different input (oriens and radiatum: CA3 Schaffer collaterals; lacunosum-moleculare: entorhinal cortex layer III), so a compartmental model of this cell inherits a map of where each pathway arrives.Figure 1.4.2. A CA1 pyramidal cell and two documented density gradients along its apical dendrite. The straight lines show only the direction and approximate size of the change (Iₕ ≈ 6-fold over ≈ 350 µm, Magee 1998; A-type K⁺ rising with distance, Hoffman et al. 1997), not fitted profiles.Algorithm 1.4 Multi-compartment updatefor k in compartments: # densities -> totals once, at setup g[k][ion] "},{"route":"/topics/channels/hh-to-ca1/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 1.4. A cylindrical dendritic segment is 2 µm in diameter and 100 µm long. What are its membrane area, capacitance ( c_m = 1 mu F/cm²) and leak conductance for R_m = 20,000 Omega,cm^2 ? Why is it essential that the axial term in Eq. (1.4.1) appears with opposite signs in the two compartments it couples? A paper gives bar g_h = 0.0001 S/cm² at the soma. Another gives g_h = 1 nS for a 1000 µm² soma. Are these the same?"},{"route":"/topics/morphology/electrotonic-distance/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §2.1. Treat a dendrite as a uniform cylinder of diameter d with specific membrane resistance R_m (Ω·cm²), specific capacitance c_m and axial (intracellular) resistivity R_i (Ω·cm). Current balance on a thin slice, in the limit of zero thickness, gives the passive cable equation for the deflection V(x,t) from rest: lambda^2,fracpartial^2 Vpartial x^2 ;=; tau,fracpartial Vpartial t + V, qquad lambda = sqrtfracR_m,d4R_i, quad tau = R_m c_m (2.1.1)The length constant lambda compares how easily current leaves through the membrane with how easily it flows along the core. It grows with the square root of diameter: a fourfold thicker dendrite has twice the length constant. Distances divided by lambda are called electrotonic distances, X = x/lambda .For a steady input at one end of a semi-infinite cable the solution is a pure exponential: V(x) = V_0,e^-x/lambda (2.1.2)Worked example 2.1.1How far does a dendrite reach?With R_m = 20,000 Omega,cm^2 , R_i = 150 Omega,cm and d = 2 µm ( 2times10^-4 cm): lambda = sqrt20000times2times10^-4/(4times150) = sqrt0.00667 cm approx 816 µm. A steady deflection therefore falls to e^-400/816approx 0.61 of its value 400 µm away. Halving the diameter to 0.5 µm gives lambdaapprox 408 µm; fine oblique branches are electrically much “longer” than the main apical trunk."},{"route":"/topics/morphology/electrotonic-distance/","heading":"Finite branches and sealed ends","text":"Textbook §2.1. Finite branches and sealed endsReal branches end. If a cable of electrotonic length L = ell/lambda has a sealed end (no current leaves the tip), the steady profile is V(X) = V_0cosh(L - X)/cosh L . At the tip ( X=L=1 ) this gives 1/cosh 1 approx 0.65 rather than e^-1approx0.37 : current that cannot escape through the end piles up and raises the voltage. Boundary conditions matter as much as lambda ."},{"route":"/topics/morphology/electrotonic-distance/","heading":"Frequency matters","text":"Textbook §2.1. Frequency mattersFor a sinusoidal input of angular frequency omega , the membrane capacitance offers an extra path to ground and the effective length constant shrinks: lambda_AC = lambda,sqrtfrac21+sqrt1+(omegatau)^2 (2.1.3)Figure 2.1.1. A Steady profiles e^-x/lambda for three diameters ( R_m = 20,000 Omega ·cm², R_i = 150 Omega ·cm): lambdaproptosqrt d . B Eq. (2.1.3): the effective length constant shrinks with frequency, much more in the gamma than the theta band.With tau = 20 ms, lambda_AC is 88% of lambda at 10 Hz but only 38% at 100 Hz. Dendrites are low-pass filters: the fast rising phase of a distal EPSP is attenuated far more than its slow tail, which is why distal EPSPs look smaller and slower at the soma."},{"route":"/topics/morphology/electrotonic-distance/","heading":"Electrotonic distance on a real tree","text":"Textbook §2.1. Electrotonic distance on a real treeFigure 2.1.2. A mpg141209_A coloured by electrotonic distance from the soma, accumulated segment by segment with each segment’s own diameter ( R_m = 20,000 Omega ·cm², R_i = 150 Omega ·cm). B X against path distance for every segment, with uniform 2 µm and 0.6 µm cables for comparison: thin basal and oblique branches gain electrotonic distance fastest.In a real dendrite the diameter changes from point to point, so the electrotonic distance is accumulated segment by segment, X = int dx/lambda(d(x)) . With R_m = 20,000 Omega ·cm² and R_i = 150 Omega ·cm, the tip of the apical tuft of mpg141209_A lies at X approx 1.26 (787 µm of path), while the farthest basal tips reach only X approx 0.43 (Figure 2.1.2). Taken naively, e^-X approx 0.28 would be the steady attenuation from tuft to soma; the true value is smaller, because every branch point along the way adds load, and larger in an active dendrite with I_h open, which lowers R_m .Rall (1959) showed that a branched tree behaves like a single equivalent cylinder if, at every branch point, the parent diameter raised to the 3/2 power equals the sum of the children’s, d_p^3/2 = sum d_c^3/2 (and all tips end at the same electrotonic distance). Measured on the traced branch points of three CA1 cells, the ratio sum d_c^3/2/d_p^3/2 has medians of 1.10, 1.03 and 1.27 (Figure 2.1.3): close to 1 on average, but individual branch points scatter from below 0.5 to above 2. The equivalent cylinder is a useful idealization, not a description of these cells. In the n5038804 reconstruction most dendritic diameters were recorded at a single value (1.36 µm), which pushes its median to 1.66: a reminder that diameters are the least reliable part of a tracing and the part that matters most for lam"},{"route":"/topics/morphology/electrotonic-distance/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 2.1. Compute lambda for d = 1 µm, R_m = 30,000 Omega,cm^2 , R_i = 200 Omega,cm . For the 816 µm cable in the worked example, how far from the input must you go before a steady deflection has fallen to half? Two dendrites have the same physical length but diameters of 1 and 4 µm. Which is electrotonically longer, and by what factor? Using Eq. (2.1.3) with tau = 20 ms, by what fraction is lambda reduced at 8 Hz (theta)?"},{"route":"/topics/morphology/coupled-compartments/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §2.2. Discretize the cable into pieces short compared with lambda and each becomes a single-voltage compartment. The simplest useful case is two: a soma (s) and a dendrite (d), coupled by an axial conductance g_c , with a current I_d injected into the dendrite. beginaligned C,dot V_s &= -g_s V_s + g_c,(V_d - V_s) C,dot V_d &= -g_d V_d + g_c,(V_s - V_d) + I_d endaligned (2.2.1)Voltages are deflections from rest. The coupling conductance follows from the cylinder that joins the compartment centres: its resistance is R_iell/(pi d^2/4) , so g_c = fracpi d^24R_i,ell (2.2.2)Worked example 2.2.1Soma and one dendriteFor d = 2 µm, ell = 100 µm and R_i = 150 Omega ·cm, g_c = pi(10^-4)^2/(150times10^-2) S approx 20.9 nS. Take g_s = g_d = 5 nS, C = 100 pF and I_d = 100 pA.Setting the derivatives to zero and solving the 2×2 linear system: V_s = fracg_c,I_dg_sg_d + g_c(g_s+g_d),qquad V_d = frac(g_s+g_c),I_dg_sg_d + g_c(g_s+g_d) (2.2.3)The denominator is 25 + 20.9times10 approx 234 nS², giving V_sapprox 8.9 mV and V_dapprox 11.1 mV. Check conservation: the dendrite loses g_dV_d approx 55 pA through its leak and sends g_c(V_d-V_s)approx 45 pA to the soma, which leaks exactly those 45 pA ( g_sV_s ). Total 100 pA. ✓The coupled system has two time constants, the eigenvalues of its 2×2 matrix: 20 ms (both compartments charging together) and about 2.1 ms (charge redistributing between them). Fast equalization, slow charging: the same separation that appears in a full cable.Figure 2.2.1. The worked example simulated from Eq. (2.2.1). A A 100 pA step into the dendrite: both compartments charge with the slow 20 ms mode, settling at 11.1 and 8.9 mV. B Their difference rises within a few ms, the fast equalizing mode.Two limits are worth memorising. As g_ctoinfty the compartments merge a"},{"route":"/topics/morphology/coupled-compartments/","heading":"How many compartments does a real cell need?","text":"Textbook §2.2. How many compartments does a real cell need?Figure 2.2.2. A Segments per section for mpg141209_A under NEURON’s d_lambda rule (d_lambda = 0.1 at 100 Hz). B Total dendritic compartments for four CA1 reconstructions at three d_lambda values; the black bars mark one compartment per section.NEURON’s d_lambda rule chooses the number of segments in each section so that none is longer than a fraction d_lambda of the AC length constant at 100 Hz, using an odd count so that the section centre is a node. Applied to the 162 dendritic sections of mpg141209_A (with R_i = 150 Omega ·cm, c_m = 1 mu F/cm²), d_lambda = 0.3 gives 282 compartments, the common default of 0.1 gives 574, and 0.05 gives 1058 (Figure 2.2.2). Long thin obliques and tuft branches need the most segments; short thick trunk pieces need one. The two-compartment model of this section is the far end of the same spectrum: useful for intuition and for fitting, but the d_lambda count is what a quantitative model of this cell should start from.Algorithm 2.2 Two-compartment steady state and modesgc = pi * d**2 / (4 * Ri * ell) # coupling from geometry G = [[gs + gc, -gc], [-gc, gd + gc]] # conductance matrix V_ss = solve(G, [0, I_d]) # steady soma and dendrite voltages taus = -1 / eigenvalues(-G / C) # slow (charging) and fast (equalizing) modes check: gs*V_ss[0] + gd*V_ss[1] == I_d # all injected current leaks somewherePseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallHow many compartments?A compartment must be short enough that voltage is nearly uniform within it. A common rule is ell at the highest frequency of interest (NEURON’s “d_lambda” rule uses 100 Hz). Too few compartments shift both amplitudes and delays; the morphology pages show this in §2.4 and the discretization lab.Across sca"},{"route":"/topics/morphology/coupled-compartments/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 2.2. With g_s=g_d=5 nS and g_c = 10 nS, inject 100 pA into the dendrite. Find V_s , V_d and the attenuation V_s/V_d . Find g_c for a 1 µm diameter, 50 µm long connection with R_i = 150 Omega ·cm. Derive Eq. (2.2.3) from Eq. (2.2.1)."},{"route":"/topics/morphology/area-and-density/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §2.3. Most channel and membrane parameters are measured or reported as densities. A model cell’s totals are densities times area. Scaling area by a factor a at fixed densities scales both C and every conductance by a : C to aC,quad g to a g quadLongrightarrowquad tau = fracCg unchanged,qquad R_intofracR_ina (2.3.1)Worked example 2.3.1Same cell, bigger membraneArea factor C (pF) g_L (nS) tau (ms) R_in (MΩ) Delta V for 50 pA110052020010 mV220010201005 mV44002020502.5 mVThe time course is identical; only the amplitude for a fixed total current falls. If instead you inject a fixed current density (current per area), the deflection is the same at every size. Which comparison is right depends on the biology: a synapse delivers a fixed conductance, not a fixed density.Figure 2.3.1. Eq. (2.3.1) at fixed densities and a fixed 50 pA current: identical time course, amplitude inversely proportional to area.This is the basis of the size principle for motor neurons (Henneman): small motor neurons have high input resistance, reach threshold with less synaptic current and are recruited first."},{"route":"/topics/morphology/area-and-density/","heading":"Spines","text":"Textbook §2.3. SpinesDendritic spines can roughly double the membrane area of spiny CA1 dendrites. Rather than model every spine, reduced models multiply c_m and all membrane conductances on spiny compartments by a spine factor F (often 1.5–2), leaving axial properties unchanged. The time constant is preserved but lambda falls by sqrt F , because more membrane leaks per unit length."},{"route":"/topics/morphology/area-and-density/","heading":"Real cells: size, shape and membrane","text":"Textbook §2.3. Real cells: size, shape and membraneFigure 2.3.2. Principal cells and interneurons of the hippocampal circuit at one scale, from the reconstructions used in the guide (granule cell: Claiborne archive via Beining et al. 2017; CA3b: Hemond et al. 2008; CA1: Jarsky et al. 2005; O-LM: Sekulic et al. 2015; PV basket: Tukker et al. 2013 via Tzilivaki et al. 2019). Interneuron axons (grey) are drawn thin; principal-cell axons are omitted.The same measurements on cells from each stage of the hippocampal circuit (Figure 2.3.2) show how differently they are built. The table lists traced dendrites only (axons excluded), with capacitance at c_m = 1 mu F/cm² before any spine correction:CellRegionDendrite (mm)Area (µm²)C (pF)Branch pointsMax path (µm)Dentate granule cellDG4.725,21125218506CA3b pyramidalCA312.229,96130063710CA1 pyramidal mpg141209_ACA18.922,21422278787CA1 pyramidal mpg141208_BCA17.319,10919168739CA1 pyramidal (Jarsky 2005)CA19.720,22320274837O-LM interneuronCA11.36,3976419165PV basket cellCA39.924,862249105798Two lessons follow. First, totals differ several-fold between cells of the same type, so a single “CA1 cell” value of C or R_in hides real variation. Second, most membrane is far from the soma: in mpg141209_A, 32% of the traced dendritic area lies more than 300 µm along the path (Figure 2.3.3B). Capacitance, leak and channels are therefore mostly distal, which is why distal I_h densities shape the somatic response so strongly. Diameter tracing limits these numbers: the n5038804 cell, with its single recorded dendritic diameter, has more than twice the area of the others.Figure 2.3.3. A Sholl analysis: dendritic crossings of spheres centred on the soma (10 µm steps), basal to the left, apical to the right, for three CA1 cells. B Fraction of the trac"},{"route":"/topics/morphology/area-and-density/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 2.3. A model has 20,000 µm² of membrane, half of it spiny, with spine factor 2 on the spiny half. What is total capacitance at c_m = 1 mu F/cm²? A colleague reports that a larger cell type “responds more slowly” to current steps. Give two explanations, only one of which is about size. Spine factor F=2 is applied to a dendrite with lambda = 800 µm. What is its new length constant?"},{"route":"/topics/morphology/local-versus-soma/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §2.4. A dendritic input produces two voltages that are easy to confuse: the local response at the input site and the transferred response at the soma. Linear passive systems describe both with impedances. The input impedance Z_dd is the local voltage per unit current injected at the same site; the transfer impedance Z_sd is the somatic voltage per unit current injected in the dendrite. At steady state these are resistances; for sinusoids they are complex.From Eq. (2.2.3), with D = g_sg_d + g_c(g_s+g_d) : Z_dd = fracg_s+g_cD,qquad Z_ss = fracg_d+g_cD,qquad Z_sd = Z_ds = fracg_cD (2.4.1)The transfer impedance is the same in both directions. This is the reciprocity theorem for linear passive networks: current into the dendrite produces the same somatic voltage as the same current into the soma produces in the dendrite. Voltage attenuation, however, is the ratio of transfer to input impedance, and input impedances differ: fracV_sV_dBig|_input at d = fracg_cg_s+g_c,qquad fracV_dV_sBig|_input at s = fracg_cg_d+g_c (2.4.2)Worked example 2.4.1A thin dendrite on a leaky somaTake g_s = 5 , g_d = 2 and g_c = 10 nS. Then D = 80 nS², Z_dd = 187.5 MΩ, Z_ss = 150 MΩ and Z_sd = 125 MΩ. A dendritic input is attenuated to 2/3 at the soma, while a somatic input reaches the dendrite at 5/6. Signals travel outward (soma → dendrite, as for a backpropagating action potential) with less attenuation than inward, because the soma is the bigger sink.Figure 2.4.1. Two compartments with g_s = 5 , g_d = 2 nS as coupling varies. A Voltage attenuation differs by direction (Eq. (2.4.2)). B Input impedances differ, but the transfer impedance is the same both ways (Eq. (2.4.1))."},{"route":"/topics/morphology/local-versus-soma/","heading":"Why distal synapses still matter in CA1","text":"Textbook §2.4. Why distal synapses still matter in CA1If distal inputs were simply attenuated, their somatic effect would be tiny. Two compensations have been measured in CA1. Synaptic AMPA conductance increases with distance along the apical dendrite so that somatic EPSP amplitude becomes roughly independent of synapse location (Magee & Cook 2000). Distal I_h shortens local EPSPs, equalizing their temporal summation at the soma (Magee 1999). In models, both show up as location-dependent parameters, not as properties of the cable."},{"route":"/topics/morphology/local-versus-soma/","heading":"Signals travelling outward: the back-propagating spike","text":"Textbook §2.4. Signals travelling outward: the back-propagating spikeFigure 2.4.2. NEURON simulations from the guide’s active-dendrites pipeline (Migliore, Ferrante & Ascoli 2005 channels; 2.5 nA, 1.5 ms somatic step). A Peak bAP amplitude on each section of mpg141209_A; grey sections were not recorded. B Apical bAP against distance for three A-type K⁺ densities. C The distal (350–450 µm) bAP as A-type density varies, for four CA1 reconstructions.Reciprocity concerns passive transfer. Active channels make outward signalling more interesting. In NEURON simulations with the Migliore, Ferrante & Ascoli (2005) channel set on the real mpg141209_A tree (Figure 2.4.2), a somatic spike invades the apical dendrite with a peak of about 96 mV at the soma, 64 mV at 200 µm and 52 mV at 300 µm, then fails abruptly: 10 mV at 400 µm. Halving the A-type K⁺ density keeps it above 63 mV out to 450 µm; raising it by half makes it fail by 300 µm (14 mV).The same channel densities on four different CA1 reconstructions fail at different A-type densities ({Figure 2.4.2}C). Branching pattern and diameters, not only channels, decide where a spike stops, which is the morphology-versus-channels question this chapter has been building toward. Golding, Kath & Spruston (2001) observed exactly this dichotomy in CA1 recordings: some cells show strong, others weak backpropagation into the distal dendrites.Algorithm 2.4 Input and transfer impedance matrixG = conductance_matrix(model) # leak + coupling, at the operating point Z = inverse(G) # steady state; use (G + i*w*C) for sinusoids Z_local = Z[d, d] # dendritic input, dendritic voltage Z_transfer = Z[s, d] # dendritic input, somatic voltage assert isclose(Z[s, d], Z[d, s]) # reciprocity attenuation_d_to_s = Z[s, d] / Z[d, d]Pseudocode: Python-like, un"},{"route":"/topics/morphology/local-versus-soma/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 2.4. With g_s = 5 , g_d = 5 , g_c = 20.9 nS, compute Z_dd , Z_sd and the dendrite → soma attenuation. Use reciprocity to argue that a 100 pA current injected at the soma produces the same dendritic voltage as 100 pA injected in the dendrite produces at the soma. Blocking I_h makes a distal EPSP larger and slower at the soma. Is this a local or a transfer effect?"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/states-trajectories/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §3.1. Patch a CA1 pyramidal cell and inject a brief current pulse. Whether the cell fires depends not only on the pulse but on what its channels were doing at that moment: how many Na⁺ channels are inactivated from the last spike, how many K⁺ channels are still open, how far I_h has relaxed. The voltage you record is one visible number; the channel populations behind it are hidden but just as real, and together they decide what happens next.Dynamical systems make that idea precise. Every quantity that carries memory of the past (voltage on the capacitor, the open fraction of each channel population, intracellular Ca²⁺) is a coordinate of the cell’s state.In the cellIn the modelChanges overCharge on the membrane V ≈ 10–40 ms (membrane time constant)Na⁺ channels opened by depolarization m < 1 msNa⁺ channels inactivated h several msDelayed-rectifier K⁺ channels open n several msHCN channels open ( I_h )an I_h gatetens–hundreds of msThe state of a model is the smallest set of numbers that, together with the equations and inputs, determines its future. For a passive membrane the state is V alone. For HH it is (V, m, h, n) . The equations assign to every point of state space a velocity, the vector field; a solution is a curve that follows these arrows, a trajectory.FitzHugh (1961) and Nagumo et al. (1962) reduced HH to two variables by lumping the fast variables ( V , m ) into a voltage-like v and the slow ones ( h , n ) into a recovery variable w : fracdvdt = v - fracv^33 - w + I, qquad tau,fracdwdt = v + a - b,w (3.1.1)The lab uses a = 0.7 , b = 0.8 , tau = 12.5 ; everything is dimensionless. The cubic term gives v a region of positive feedback (self-excitation, like Na⁺ activation) flanked by negative feedback; w slowly opposes v , like K⁺ activation and Na⁺ inact"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/states-trajectories/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 3.1. Evaluate the FHN vector field at (v,w) = (1, 0) with I=0 . Which way does the state move? Why can the 1-D passive membrane taudot V = -V + RI never oscillate, whatever I is (constant)? How many state variables does a two-compartment HH model have?"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/nullclines/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §3.2. In the FHN caricature, v stands for the membrane voltage together with the fast Na⁺ activation that follows it almost instantly, and w for everything slow that fights back: Na⁺ inactivation and K⁺ activation. A nullcline is therefore a biological balance. On the v -nullcline the inward Na⁺-like current exactly cancels the outward currents, so the voltage momentarily stops moving; on the w -nullcline the recovery processes have caught up with the current voltage.The N-shape of the v -nullcline is the signature of Na⁺ channels. Near rest, depolarizing the cell increases outward leak and K⁺ current faster than inward current, so the net current restores rest. In the middle range, each millivolt opens enough Na⁺ channels that inward current grows faster than outward: positive feedback, the regenerative upstroke. At high voltages the driving force on Na⁺ shrinks toward E_Na and outward current wins again. Three regimes, three branches.Formally, a nullcline is the set of states where one variable stops changing. For FHN, setting each derivative to zero: dot v = 0: w = v - fracv^33 + I, qquadqquad dot w = 0: w = fracv + ab (3.2.1)The v -nullcline is a cubic (an N-shape) and the w -nullcline a straight line. On the v -nullcline trajectories move vertically; on the w -nullcline horizontally. Wherever they cross, both derivatives vanish: a fixed point (equilibrium), (v^*, w^*) .Worked example 3.2.1The resting state of FHNWith I=0 , substitute the line into the cubic: v - v^3/3 = (v+0.7)/0.8 . This cubic in v has one real root, v^*approx -1.199 , giving w^* = (v^*+0.7)/0.8 approx -0.624 . With I = 0.5 the cubic shifts up by 0.5 and the intersection moves to v^*approx-0.805 , w^*approx-0.131 : onto the middle, rising branch of the N.Figure 3.2.1. Eq. (3.2.1) for I = "},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/nullclines/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 3.2. For FHN with I=0 , verify that v^* = -1.199 is (approximately) a fixed point. Where on the v -nullcline do trajectories move straight up, and where straight down? With b=0.8 , can the line and the cubic intersect three times for some I ? What would that imply?"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §3.3. A resting CA1 cell is constantly nudged: a single EPSP, channel noise, a passing inhibitory input. After each nudge it returns to rest. That return is what “stable” means. Sometimes it returns smoothly; sometimes it overshoots and rings for a cycle or two, a damped subthreshold oscillation you can see when you inject a small step into a cell with I_h or I_M .Now raise the steady current through the pipette, step by step. At some value the cell no longer settles: it starts firing repetitively. Nothing about the channels changed; only the operating point did. That switch is a bifurcation, and the way a cell crosses it (does it start at 1 Hz or jump to 40 Hz? does it ring first?) is a measurable fingerprint of which channels dominate near threshold. The rest of this section shows how to read that fingerprint from the equations.Near a fixed point, write the state as mathbf x = mathbf x^* + boldsymboldelta . Keeping first-order terms gives a linear system governed by the Jacobian J , the matrix of partial derivatives at mathbf x^* : dotboldsymboldelta = J,boldsymboldelta,qquad J = beginpmatrixpartialdot v/partial v & partialdot v/partial w partialdot w/partial v & partialdot w/partial wendpmatrix = beginpmatrix1 - v^*2 & -1 1/tau & -b/tauendpmatrix (3.3.1)Solutions are combinations of e^lambda t where lambda are the eigenvalues of J . For a 2×2 matrix they follow from the trace T and determinant Delta : lambda_1,2 = fracT pm sqrtT^2 - 4Delta2,qquad T = trJ,quad Delta = det J (3.3.2) Delta : a saddle (one growing, one shrinking direction); always unstable. Delta > 0 , T: stable; a node if T^2>4Delta , a spiral (focus) if T^2. Delta > 0 , T>0 : unstable node or spiral. T = 0 with Delta>0 : eigenvalues pm isqrtDelta ; the boundary where a Hopf bifurcation can occ"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/","heading":"Two ways to start firing","text":"Textbook §3.3. Two ways to start firingA bifurcation is a qualitative change in the dynamics as a parameter crosses a critical value. Neurons begin firing in two classic ways (Hodgkin’s excitability classes; Rinzel & Ermentrout 1989):OnsetWhat happensFiring at onsetExcitabilitySaddle-node on invariant circle (SNIC)rest collides with a saddle and both vanish; the orbit passes through the ghostarbitrarily low rate, fproptosqrtI-I_c class IHopfa complex pair crosses the imaginary axis; rest becomes unstablenon-zero rate; subthreshold ringing beforehandclass IIFHN and HH (with standard parameters) lose stability through a Hopf bifurcation and are class II; many cortical pyramidal cells are closer to class I. The excitability class is a property of the model’s equations at a particular operating point, which is why the phase-plane and neuron workbenches compute it rather than assume it.Algorithm 3.3 Continuation: follow a fixed point as a parameter changesfor I in linspace(I_min, I_max, n): v_star = solve(f(v; I) == 0, start=v_star_previous) # reuse last solution J = jacobian(f, v_star; I) lam = eigenvalues(J) record(I, v_star, max(real(lam)), imag(lam)) Hopf: max(real(lam)) changes sign while imag(lam) != 0 saddle-node: a real eigenvalue reaches 0 and the fixed point disappearsPseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallLinear stability is localEigenvalues describe small perturbations only. A stable fixed point can coexist with a stable limit cycle (bistability), so a large enough kick can start firing even though rest is linearly stable. This is what happens near a subcritical Hopf bifurcation (Ch. 4).Across scalesBiologyOnset of repetitive firing as drive increasesBrain regionClass I cortical/hippocampal pyramidal cells; class II squid axonMeasureme"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/stability-bifurcations/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 3.3. Classify the fixed point with J = beginpmatrix-1 & -2 1 & -1endpmatrix . Classify J = beginpmatrix1 & 0 0 & -2endpmatrix . For FHN at I=0.5 the fixed point is v^*approx-0.805 . Compute T and Delta and classify. A model fires at 2 Hz just above threshold. Which onset bifurcation is more likely, and what experiment on the model would confirm it?"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/resonance-measurement/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §3.4. For small sinusoidal currents a stable model responds with a sinusoid at the same frequency, scaled and shifted. The complex impedance Z(f) records both: |Z| is the voltage amplitude per unit current amplitude and arg Z the phase (Ch. 5). Its reciprocal is the admittance Y = 1/Z , which simply adds over parallel membrane pathways.A passive membrane has Y = g_L + iomega C , so |Z_RC(f)| = fracR_insqrt1+(omegatau)^2,qquad omega = 2pi f (3.4.1)This is a low-pass filter: largest at f=0 and down to 1/sqrt2 at the corner frequency f_c = 1/(2pitau) , about 8 Hz for tau = 20 ms.Now add a slow current that opposes voltage changes, with effective conductance g_S and time constant tau_S . Linearized (Ch. 5), it contributes an admittance g_S/(1+iomegatau_S) : large at low frequency, where it has time to respond, and negligible at high frequency, where it cannot keep up: Y(f) = g_L + iomega C + fracg_S1+iomegatau_S (3.4.2)At low frequencies the slow current shunts the response; at high frequencies the capacitor does. In between there is a window where neither dominates and |Z| peaks: resonance. The resonance frequency f_R is the location of the peak, and its strength is usually quantified by Q = |Z(f_R)|/|Z(f_low)| with f_low a low reference frequency such as 0.5 Hz (Hu et al. 2002).Worked example 3.4.1A theta resonatorTake g_L = 5 nS, C = 100 pF, g_S = 10 nS and tau_S = 100 ms. At DC the admittance is 15 nS, so |Z(0)|approx 66.7 MΩ. Numerically, |Z| peaks at f_Rapprox 5.9 Hz with |Z|approx172 MΩ, and |Z(0.5 Hz)|approx 69.8 MΩ, so Qapprox 2.5 . Without the slow current the same cell is a low-pass filter with |Z(0.5)|approx200 MΩ.Figure 3.4.1. Impedance magnitude from Eqs. (3.4.1) and (3.4.2). The passive cell (grey) is a low-pass filter; adding a slow restoring curren"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics/resonance-measurement/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 3.4. For a passive cell with R_in = 200 MΩ and tau = 20 ms, compute |Z| at 8 Hz. A measured impedance curve has |Z(0.5 Hz)| = 100 MΩ and a peak of 120 MΩ at 4 Hz. Report f_R and Q . Why must the slow current be restoring (opposing voltage changes) to produce resonance? Increasing tau_S in Eq. (3.4.2) (slower restoring current), with everything else fixed. Which way would you expect f_R to move?"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/phase-portraits/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §4.1. Inject a small current step into a CA1 cell held near −75 mV and the voltage rises, overshoots, sags back and settles: the classic I_h sag. Two things are happening at once: the membrane charges (fast), and HCN channels slowly close as the cell depolarizes, pulling the voltage back. Plot voltage against the fraction of open HCN channels instead of against time and the response becomes a curve spiralling into a point. That plot is a phase portrait, and the spiral is the shape of a cell with a slow restoring current.Close to rest, small responses like this are linear: double the step, double the deflection. Linear 2-D systems have only a handful of possible portraits, so classifying them gives a vocabulary for every small-signal experiment in the guide.Every linearization in this book reduces to dotmathbf x = Amathbf x . If A has eigenvalues lambda_k with eigenvectors mathbf u_k , the general solution is mathbf x(t) = c_1 e^lambda_1 tmathbf u_1 + c_2 e^lambda_2 tmathbf u_2 . Real eigenvalues give straight-line motion along eigenvectors; complex ones, lambda = sigma pm iomega , give rotation at angular speed omega with radial growth or decay e^sigma t .The lab’s rotating system makes this explicit: beginpmatrixdot x dot yendpmatrix = beginpmatrix-a & -omega omega & -aendpmatrixbeginpmatrixx yendpmatrix,qquad lambda = -a pm iomega (4.1.1)In polar coordinates it separates exactly: dot r = -ar and dottheta = omega . The period is 2pi/omega and each turn shrinks the radius by e^-2pi a/omega . With a = 0 every circle is an orbit, a centre: a structurally fragile case that any small damping destroys."},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/phase-portraits/","heading":"The trace–determinant plane","text":"Textbook §4.1. The trace–determinant planeBecause the eigenvalues depend only on T = trA and Delta = det A (Eq. (3.3.2)), every 2-D linear system is a point in the (T,Delta) plane. The parabola Delta = T^2/4 separates nodes (below) from spirals (above); the Delta -axis separates stable ( T) from unstable; everything below Delta = 0 is a saddle. As a parameter changes, the fixed point traces a path through this plane, and crossing a boundary is a change in the phase portrait.Worked example 4.1.1Three matrices A T Delta EigenvaluesPortrait beginpmatrix0&1-4&-1endpmatrix −14 -0.5pm1.94i stable spiral beginpmatrix2&11&2endpmatrix 433, 1unstable node beginpmatrix-3&00&-1endpmatrix −43−3, −1stable nodeThe first matrix is a damped harmonic oscillator ddot x + dot x + 4x = 0 written as a 2-D system: natural angular frequency 2, damping that makes it ring at 1.94.Figure 4.1.1. A The trace–determinant plane with its boundaries ( Delta = 0 , T = 0 , Delta = T^2/4 ) and the three matrices of the worked example. B A trajectory of Eq. (4.1.1) with a = 0.2 , omega = 2pi .Algorithm 4.1 Classify a 2-D linear systemT, D = trace(A), det(A) if D < 0: kind = 'saddle' elif T == 0: kind = 'centre' elif T*T < 4*D: kind = 'stable spiral' if T < 0 else 'unstable spiral' else: kind = 'stable node' if T < 0 else 'unstable node' period = 2*pi / sqrt(D - T*T/4) if spiral or centrePseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallLinear portraits are local picturesA nonlinear model is only locally like its linearization. A stable spiral of the linearization can sit inside an unstable limit cycle, and the global picture (where trajectories go from far away) needs nullclines and simulation.Across scalesBiologySmall-signal responses around restBrain regionAny cell near its operating poi"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/phase-portraits/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 4.1. In Eq. (4.1.1) with a = 0.2 and omega = 2pi (time in seconds), find the period and the fraction of radius left after one turn. Write ddot x + 2zetaomega_0dot x + omega_0^2x = 0 as a first-order system and give T and Delta . A fixed point’s (T,Delta) moves from (-0.5, 0.1) to (0.3, 0.1) as input increases. What bifurcation did it cross, and what do you expect to see?"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/saddle-node/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §4.2. Many cortical and hippocampal pyramidal cells can fire very slowly just above threshold: inject a current a hair above rheobase and the cell waits hundreds of milliseconds, creeping toward threshold, before it fires. Physically, the cell sits in a near-balance between inward Na⁺ (and persistent Na⁺) current and outward K⁺ and leak current. Below rheobase there is a voltage where the two balance (rest) and a slightly higher one where they balance precariously (threshold). Raising the current pushes these two balance points together until they merge and vanish.After they vanish, the net current is still almost zero near where they used to be, so the voltage drifts there very slowly: that drift is the long wait before each spike. The saddle-node normal form below is the simplest equation with exactly this geometry.The saddle-node normal form is the simplest way for equilibria to appear or vanish: dot x = mu - x^2 (4.2.1)For mu>0 there are two fixed points, x^* = pmsqrtmu . The slope of the right-hand side, -2x^* , is negative at +sqrtmu (stable) and positive at -sqrtmu (unstable). As muto0 they approach each other, merge at x=0 , and for mu there are none: dot x everywhere and x runs off to -infty ."},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/saddle-node/","heading":"The ghost and the bottleneck","text":"Textbook §4.2. The ghost and the bottleneckJust past the bifurcation ( mu slightly negative) the flow is still very slow near x=0 , where the fixed points used to be. Time spent passing this “ghost” dominates. Integrating Eq. (4.2.1) from -infty to +infty (written with xto-x so the motion is to the right): T = int_-infty^inftyfracdx|mu| + x^2 = fracpisqrt|mu| (4.2.2)When the line is closed into a circle, so that a trajectory leaving on one side returns from the other, the passage repeats periodically: this is a saddle-node on an invariant circle. The period diverges as |mu|^-1/2 , so the frequency rises from zero as fproptosqrtI-I_c . That square-root f–I curve is the hallmark of class I excitability, and the theta neuron (Ermentrout & Kopell 1986) is exactly this normal form on a circle.Worked example 4.2.1How slow is slow?For mu = -0.01 , T = pi/0.1 approx 31.4 time units; for mu = -0.0025 , Tapprox 62.8 . Quartering the distance to the bifurcation doubles the period. Near threshold, small changes in drive produce large changes in interspike interval: one reason class I neurons can encode input strength in firing rate over a wide range.Figure 4.2.1. Eq. (4.2.1). A The flow for three values of mu : two crossings, a touch, none. B Fixed points pmsqrtmu (solid stable, dashed unstable). C Passage rate 1/T = sqrt|mu|/pi past the bifurcation (Eq. (4.2.2)): it starts at zero, as in class I firing.Algorithm 4.2 Measure an f–I curve near onsetfor I in currents: spikes = simulate(model, I, duration=2.0, discard_first=0.2) rate[I] = len(spikes) / 1.8 I_c = bisect(lambda I: rate(I) > 0, I_lo, I_hi) # resolution-independent onset fit rate ~ c * (I - I_c)**0.5 near onset # good fit -> class I (SNIC-like) onset rate clearly > 0 # -> class II (Hopf-like)Pseudocode: Python-like, units"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/saddle-node/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 4.2. For mu = 0.25 , find the fixed points of Eq. (4.2.1) and their linear growth rates. A class I model fires at 10 Hz when I = I_c + 0.04 (arbitrary units). Using the normal-form scaling, predict the rate at I_c + 0.01 . Show that int_-infty^infty dx/(c^2+x^2) = pi/c ."},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/hopf-normal-form/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §4.3. Squid axon, and some interneurons, behave differently from class I cells: just below threshold a small current step produces damped ringing, and just above threshold the cell does not fire slowly but jumps straight to tens of Hz. The ringing comes from a slow restoring current (K⁺ activation and Na⁺ inactivation in HH) acting with a delay: depolarization recruits it, it overshoots, the voltage swings back. Increase the drive and the damping weakens until the ringing no longer dies out.That moment, when a damped oscillation becomes self-sustaining, is a Hopf bifurcation. The normal form separates the two things you can measure: how big the oscillation is (amplitude r ) and where it is in its cycle (phase theta ).The supercritical Hopf normal form separates the amplitude r and phase theta of an oscillation: dot r = mu r - r^3,qquad dottheta = omega (4.3.1)For mu the origin is a stable spiral ( lambda = mupm iomega ). At mu = 0 the eigenvalues cross the imaginary axis, and for mu>0 the origin is unstable while a stable limit cycle appears at r^* = sqrtmu (4.3.2)Linearizing the radial equation at r^* gives growth rate mu - 3r^*2 = -2mu : the cycle attracts with time constant 1/(2mu) . Two predictions follow and are testable in any model near a supercritical Hopf: amplitude grows as the square root of the distance past onset, while frequency starts at a non-zero value approxomega/2pi , and recovery from perturbations becomes slow near onset (critical slowing down).Worked example 4.3.1Amplitude and recoveryAt mu = 0.04 , r^* = 0.2 and perturbations of the amplitude decay with time constant 1/0.08 = 12.5 . At mu = 0.09 , r^* = 0.3 . Below onset, at mu = -0.1 , a perturbation of the rest state decays with time constant 10 while rotating at omega : damped ringing,"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/hopf-normal-form/","heading":"Subcritical Hopf","text":"Textbook §4.3. Subcritical HopfIf the cubic term has the opposite sign, dot r = mu r + r^3 - r^5 , the small cycle born at mu=0 is unstable and a large stable cycle already exists. Over a range of mu rest and repetitive firing are both stable: a well-timed pulse can switch the cell on and another switch it off. The HH squid model has this structure; it is why its f–I curve jumps from zero to tens of Hz and shows hysteresis.Algorithm 4.3 Distinguish super- and subcritical onsetsweep I upward: record oscillation amplitude A_up(I) sweep I downward: start ON the oscillation, record A_down(I) if A grows smoothly from 0 and A_up == A_down: supercritical (A ~ sqrt(I - I_H)) if A jumps to a finite value and A_down persists below the jump: subcritical, bistable range = hysteresis loop confirm with continuation software (first Lyapunov coefficient)Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallClassify from equations, not tracesAn oscillation that grows smoothly from zero amplitude suggests a supercritical Hopf, but noise, slow variables and finite simulation times can all blur this. Continuation of the fixed point and the first Lyapunov coefficient (computed numerically by tools such as XPPAUT or MatCont) decide super- versus subcritical.Across scalesBiologyDamped subthreshold oscillations that become sustainedBrain regionSquid axon; fast-spiking interneurons; network gammaMeasurementHysteresis in up/down current rampsMathPolar normal form, limit cycles, Lyapunov coefficientCodePhase-plane workbench, Hopf preset"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/hopf-normal-form/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 4.3. In Eq. (4.3.1), what mu gives an oscillation radius of 0.5? Starting at r = 0.1 with mu = 0.04 , which way does r move, and at what initial rate? Why is a class II neuron unable to fire at arbitrarily low rates?"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/numerical-time-step/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §4.4. A real membrane integrates current continuously: charge flows onto the capacitor every microsecond. A simulator cannot; it updates the state at discrete steps Delta t and assumes nothing important happens in between. For a CA1 cell that assumption fails exactly where biology is fastest: the Na⁺ activation gate changes on a time scale of about 0.1–0.5 ms, while the membrane and I_h change over tens of milliseconds.If the step is too long compared with the fastest channel, the simulated gate overshoots its target, then overshoots back, and the model fires spikes the real cell never would. The numerical method has to respect the biology’s fastest time scale even when the question is about the slow ones.A simulator replaces dot V = f(V) by a rule that advances the state by a step Delta t . The simplest is forward Euler, V_n+1 = V_n + Delta t,f(V_n) . Apply it to the passive decay dot V = -V/tau : V_n+1 = left(1 - fracDelta ttauright)V_n quadLongrightarrowquad V_n = left(1-fracDelta ttauright)^n V_0 (4.4.1)The exact solution decays as e^-t/tau . The numerical one decays only if |1-Delta t/tau|, i.e. 0; for tau it decays while flipping sign each step (spurious oscillation), and beyond 2tau it explodes. The equation is stable for every Delta t ; the instability belongs to the method.Worked example 4.4.1Accuracy and stabilityWith tau = 20 ms, V_0 = 10 mV and Delta t = 1 ms, after 20 steps Euler gives 10times0.95^20approx3.585 mV against the exact 10e^-1approx3.679 mV: 2.6% low. Halving Delta t to 0.5 ms gives 3.632 mV, 1.3% low. The error halves with the step: forward Euler is first-order accurate.Now consider a fast gate with tau_m = 0.2 ms in the same simulation at Delta t = 1 ms: the factor is 1 - 5 = -4 , and the gate explodes. Systems that mix fast and slow "},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/numerical-time-step/","heading":"Better methods for neurons","text":"Textbook §4.4. Better methods for neuronsMethodUpdate for dot V = -V/tau Stable forNotesForward Euler (1-Delta t/tau)V_n Delta tsimple; first orderBackward Euler V_n/(1+Delta t/tau) all Delta t implicit; damps too much at large steps; NEURON’s defaultExponential Euler e^-Delta t/tauV_n all Delta t exact for linear equations; standard for HH gatesCrank–Nicolson / RK4—method dependentsecond / fourth orderGate equations are linear in the gate variable at fixed V , so exponential Euler, x_n+1 = x_infty + (x_n - x_infty)e^-Delta t/tau_x , is exact for them over a step with V frozen. The guide’s Neuron workbench uses this scheme with voltage and gates updated on staggered half-steps, as NEURON does.Algorithm 4.4 Convergence testdt = 0.1 result = simulate(model, dt) while True: finer = simulate(model, dt / 2) if relative_change(result, finer) < tolerance: break dt, result = dt / 2, finer report(result, dt, method) # never a result without its dtPseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallAlways run a convergence testBefore interpreting any surprising result (a new oscillation, a change in spike count), halve Delta t and rerun. If the result changes, it is at least partly numerical. In the workbench, HH with forward Euler at 0.1 ms fires 34 spikes where the converged answer is 11.Across scalesBiologyFast Na⁺ gating (sub-ms) next to slow Iₕ (100s of ms)Brain regionEvery simulated modelMeasurement—: this is about the simulator, not the cellMathStability regions, order of accuracy, stiffnessCodeNeuron workbench Numerics menu (Euler vs exponential Euler)"},{"route":"/topics/dynamics-toolkit/numerical-time-step/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 4.4. For tau = 20 ms, what does forward Euler do at Delta t = 30 ms and at Delta t = 50 ms? Compute one step of backward Euler and exponential Euler for tau = 20 ms, Delta t = 50 ms, V_0 = 10 mV. You see 34 spikes at Delta t = 0.1 ms and 11 at 0.025 ms. What should you report?"},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/ih-and-m-current/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §5.1. Two slow currents dominate CA1 subthreshold dynamics. I_h flows through HCN channels, which open with hyperpolarization, pass both Na⁺ and K⁺ (reversal near −30 mV) and gate slowly (tens to hundreds of ms). It produces the voltage sag during a hyperpolarizing step and the rebound after it. The M-current flows through Kv7/KCNQ channels, which open with depolarization from about −60 mV upward, reverse at E_K and gate on a similar slow time scale. It is called “M” because muscarinic receptor activation suppresses it."},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/ih-and-m-current/","heading":"Linearizing a gated current","text":"Textbook §5.1. Linearizing a gated currentConsider a current I_x = bar g,x,(V - E) whose gate obeys tau_xdot x = x_infty(V) - x . Perturb around a holding voltage V_0 by a small sinusoid. Two things change: the driving force (instantly) and the gate (with delay). To first order the current change is delta I = underbracebar g,x_infty(V_0)_g_inst,delta V ;+; underbracebar g,(V_0 - E),x_infty'(V_0)_g_S,fracdelta V1+iomegatau_x (5.1.1)So a gated current contributes an instantaneous conductance plus exactly the slow admittance term g_S/(1+iomegatau_x) of Eq. (3.4.2). The sign of the effective slow conductance g_S decides everything. With outward-positive currents: g_S = bar g,(V_0 - E),fracdx_inftydV:qquad begincases g_S > 0 & restoring (resonance) g_S (5.1.2)Figure 5.1.1. A Illustrative activation curves of opposite direction. B The effective slow conductance g_S of Eq. (5.1.2) against holding voltage: each current is most resonance-producing where its activation is steep, Iₕ near −80 mV and I_M near −40 mV. Parameters are illustrative, not fitted.Current V_0 - E dx_infty/dV g_S Effect I_h at −70 mV ( E_happrox-30 ) -40 mV (opens with hyperpolarization) >0 restoring I_M at −55 mV ( E_Kapprox-90 ) +35 mV >0 >0 restoring I_NaP at −60 mV ( E_Naapprox+55 ) -115 mV >0 amplifyingBoth I_h and I_M are restoring, by different routes: I_h is an inward current that shuts off when the cell depolarizes; I_M is an outward current that turns on when it depolarizes. Either way a depolarization is opposed after a delay tau_x . This is why Hu et al. found H-resonance at hyperpolarized potentials, where HCN channels are partly open and steeply voltage dependent, and M-resonance near threshold, where Kv7 channels are. I_NaP is amplifying: it raises |Z| at the frequencies where M-resonance sits"},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/ih-and-m-current/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 5.1. Apply the sign rule to an A-type K⁺ current’s activation at −60 mV. Restoring or amplifying? An M-like gate has dx_infty/dV = 0.01 mV⁻¹ at −55 mV and bar g = 10 nS, E_K = -90 mV. Compute g_S . Muscarinic agonists suppress I_M . What would you predict for resonance measured near −55 mV?"},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/impedance-phase/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §5.2. For an input I(t) = I_0cosomega t , a linear system responds with V(t) = |Z|I_0cos(omega t + phi) , where phi = arg Z is the impedance phase. In the guide’s convention **positive phi means the voltage leads the current**; negative means it lags. Papers differ, so state the convention whenever you plot phase.For a passive membrane, Z = R_in/(1+iomegatau) and phi_RC = -arctan(omegatau) (5.2.1)The voltage always lags: the capacitor must charge before the voltage can follow the current. At the corner frequency f_c = 1/(2pitau) the lag is exactly 45°.A restoring slow current behaves electrically like an inductor (a “phenomenological inductance”): at low frequencies it opposes slow voltage changes and lets the voltage peak before the current does. The phase becomes positive at low frequencies, crosses zero at the zero-phase frequency and turns negative at higher frequencies. In CA1 this inductive phase lead is produced by I_h and grows along the apical dendrite with HCN density (Narayanan & Johnston 2008).Worked example 5.2.1Phase in the theta resonatorIn the model of Section 3.4 ( g_L = 5 , C=100 , g_S = 10 nS, tau_S = 100 ms) the phase is about +18° at 1 Hz, crosses zero near 4.8 Hz and is about −12° at the impedance peak, f_Rapprox5.9 Hz. The zero-phase frequency and the magnitude peak are close but not the same. For the passive cell alone, the phase at 8 Hz would be −45°.Figure 5.2.1. Impedance phase for the passive and resonant models of §3.4. The resonant cell leads at low frequencies (inductive behaviour) and crosses zero near 4.8 Hz, below its magnitude peak at 5.9 Hz.Phase matters for timing. In a network oscillating at theta, a cell with a phase lead at theta responds earlier in the cycle than a passive one; a gradient of phase leads along the dendrit"},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/impedance-phase/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 5.2. Compute the phase of a passive membrane with tau = 20 ms at 2 Hz and at 8 Hz. A 6 Hz response has a phase of −30°. Convert to a time lag. Blocking I_h in a distal dendrite: predict the effect on phase at 2 Hz."},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/chirp-input/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §5.3. Measuring impedance one sinusoid at a time is slow. A chirp (or ZAP) current sweeps frequency continuously: I(t) = I_0sin!Big(2pibig(f_0 t + tfracf_1 - f_02T,t^2big)Big),qquad f_inst(t) = f_0 + fracf_1 - f_0T,t (5.3.1)The impedance is estimated as the ratio of the Fourier transforms of voltage and current, hat Z(f) = mathcal F[V](f)/mathcal F[I](f) , evaluated where the stimulus has power ( f_0le fle f_1 ). Puil, Gimbarzevsky and Miura (1986) introduced this approach for neurons; it is the standard protocol for CA1 resonance.Worked example 5.3.1Designing a sweepThe lab uses f_0 = 0.5 Hz, f_1 = 20 Hz and T = 4 s. At t = 2 s the instantaneous frequency is 0.5 + 19.5times2/4 = 10.25 Hz. The sweep spends only 4/(19.5/4)approx0.82 s between 4 and 8 Hz; a 20 s sweep would spend 4.1 s there.The FFT of a 4 s record has frequency resolution 1/T = 0.25 Hz, so f_R cannot be resolved better than about ±0.1 Hz without longer records or curve fitting (the guide’s analysis fits a parabola through the peak and its neighbours on a log-frequency axis).Figure 5.3.1. A chirp experiment on the linear resonator of §3.4. A The voltage envelope peaks when the instantaneous frequency passes f_R . B |hat Z| from the ratio of Fourier transforms (dots) against the exact curve: close, with scatter at the ends of the band and from the onset transient."},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/chirp-input/","heading":"Transients and nonstationarity","text":"Textbook §5.3. Transients and nonstationarityWhen the chirp starts, the cell has not reached its periodic response: an onset transient decays with the cell’s slowest time constant. After 3tau it is down to 5%, after 5tau to under 1%; for a cell whose slow current has tau_S = 100 ms, that is 0.3–0.5 s, a large fraction of a 4 s sweep. Remedies are to precede the chirp with a sinusoid at f_0 , to discard the first part of the record, or to start from the model’s steady state.A fast sweep also violates the assumption that the response at each instant is the steady-state response at f_inst . Slow sweeps, or several sweep durations compared with each other, test whether the estimate has converged.Algorithm 5.3 Chirp protocol and impedance estimatet = arange(0, T, dt) I = I0 * sin(2*pi*(f0*t + (f1 - f0) / (2*T) * t**2)) I = concatenate([I0*sin(2*pi*f0*t_pre), I]) # lead-in sinusoid absorbs the transient V = record_or_simulate(I) - V_hold keep = t > 5 * tau_slow # drop what remains of the onset Z = fft(V[keep]) / fft(I[keep]) use only f in [f0, f1]; df = 1 / duration(keep) repeat with I0/2: estimate must not change # linearity checkPseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallStay in the linear range hat Z assumes linearity. If I_0 is large enough to recruit channels nonlinearly or to evoke spikes, the ratio is no longer an impedance. Check by halving I_0 : a linear estimate does not change.Across scalesBiologyCells respond to theta-modulated input in vivoBrain regionCA1 slices; in vivo whole-cell in head-fixed miceMeasurementZAP protocol (Puil et al. 1986)MathFourier transforms, frequency resolutionCodeResearch code: impedance analysis (ir_ladder)"},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/chirp-input/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 5.3. For f_0 = 0.5 , f_1 = 20 Hz, T = 10 s, when does the sweep pass 8 Hz? What frequency resolution does a 10 s record give, and how many FFT bins fall between 4 and 8 Hz? Doubling I_0 changes your estimated f_R from 5.0 to 4.4 Hz. What does that tell you?"},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/impedance-units/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §5.4. Impedance is voltage per current. The unit bookkeeping is simple but easy to get wrong by factors of 1000: frac1 mV1 pA = 1 GOmega = 1000 MOmega,qquad frac1 mV1 nA = 1 MOmega (5.4.1)Frequency in Hz and angular frequency in rad/s differ by 2pi ; formulas such as Eq. (3.4.1) use omega = 2pi f . If time is in ms, omega must be in rad/ms.Worked example 5.4.1Amplitude conventionsA 30 pA (peak) sinusoid produces a 3 mV (peak) voltage oscillation: |Z| = 3/30 GOmega = 100 MΩ. If you mistakenly divide the peak-to-peak voltage (6 mV) by the peak current, you report 200 MΩ, a factor-of-two error that looks entirely plausible. Peak, peak-to-peak and RMS are all fine if both signals use the same one.Figure 5.4.1. Peak, peak-to-peak and RMS of one sinusoid. Any can be used for impedance as long as the current is measured the same way."},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/impedance-units/","heading":"A reporting checklist","text":"Textbook §5.4. A reporting checklistHolding potential (and how it was set: current injection or not).Recording site (soma or dendritic distance) and whether Z is input or transfer.Stimulus: type, frequency range, duration, amplitude, and how transients were handled.Temperature.Definitions: f_R = frequency of max |Z| ; Q = |Z(f_R)|/|Z(f_ref)| with f_ref stated; phase convention.With these, a model’s resonance can be compared with an experiment, and two models with each other. Without them, a difference of 1 Hz in f_R or 0.3 in Q may be entirely methodological.Algorithm 5.4 Units-safe impedance reportamp_V = peak(V_ss) # mV, steady-state cycles only amp_I = peak(I) # pA, SAME convention as amp_V Z_MOhm = amp_V / amp_I * 1000 # mV/pA = GOhm report = {'f_R_Hz': f_R, 'Q': Q, 'Q_ref_Hz': 0.5, 'Z_peak_MOhm': Z_max, 'V_hold_mV': V_hold, 'site': 'soma', 'temp_C': 34, 'stimulus': 'chirp 0.5-20 Hz, 20 s, 20 pA', 'phase_sign': '+ = V leads'}Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallNormalized plots hide amplitudePlotting |Z|/|Z|_max makes curves easy to compare in shape but removes input resistance, which is often the bigger effect of a manipulation. Show absolute |Z| in MΩ at least once.Across scalesBiology—Brain regionAny impedance comparison across papersMeasurementPeak, peak-to-peak, RMS conventionsMathUnit algebraCodeStore units in variable names or with a units library (Brian2 units)"},{"route":"/topics/ca1-resonance/impedance-units/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 5.4. A 0.05 nA peak current gives a 2 mV peak response. What is |Z| ? |Z(f_R)| = 150 MΩ, |Z(0.5 Hz)| = 100 MΩ, |Z(0)| = 90 MΩ. Give Q under both common definitions. Why might a model with higher Q than an experiment still have the same |Z(f_R)| ?"},{"route":"/topics/synapses/chemical-electrical/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §6.1. At a chemical synapse an action potential opens presynaptic Ca²⁺ channels; Ca²⁺ triggers fusion of vesicles, each releasing a roughly fixed amount of transmitter (a quantum). Del Castillo and Katz (1954) described release at the neuromuscular junction as N independent release sites, each releasing with probability p and producing a response of size q . This binomial quantal model predicts langleEPSCrangle = Npq,qquad Var = Np(1-p),q^2,qquad P(failure) = (1-p)^N (6.1.1)Worked example 6.1.1A CA3→CA1 connectionTake N=5 sites, p=0.3 , q=10 pA. The mean EPSC is 15 pA, failures occur on (0.7)^5approx17% of trials and the coefficient of variation is sqrtNp(1-p),q/(Npq)approx0.68 . Single Schaffer-collateral connections in CA1 are indeed unreliable; reliability at the circuit level comes from many convergent inputs and from short-term plasticity.Figure 6.1.1. A The binomial amplitude distribution of the worked example, Eq. (6.1.1): failures on 17% of trials. B The steady gap-junction coupling coefficient, Eq. (6.1.2), for two cell leak conductances.An electrical synapse is a cluster of connexin channels (gap junctions) joining two cytoplasms. It passes current in proportion to the voltage difference, with no delay: I_into 1 = g_gap,(V_2 - V_1),qquad CC = fracDelta V_1Delta V_2bigg|_steady = fracg_gapg_gap + g_L,1 (6.1.2)The coupling coefficient CC is the fraction of a steady voltage change in cell 2 seen in cell 1. With g_gap = 1 nS and g_L,1 = 5 nS, CC ≈ 0.17. For fast signals CC is smaller, because cell 1’s capacitance shunts them: gap junctions are low-pass filters. They are common between interneurons (e.g. parvalbumin basket cells) and support synchrony.ChemicalElectricalSignexcitatory or inhibitory, set by receptor and E_syn follows the voltage differenceDe"},{"route":"/topics/synapses/chemical-electrical/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 6.1. For N = 10 , p = 0.5 , q = 5 pA, what are the mean EPSC and the failure probability? Two cells are coupled by g_gap = 0.5 nS; cell 1 has g_L = 2 nS. Cell 2 is held 10 mV above rest. How much does cell 1 depolarize at steady state? In a train of presynaptic spikes, which binomial parameter does short-term facilitation most plausibly change?"},{"route":"/topics/synapses/synaptic-conductance/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §6.2. A synaptic current is ohmic in the open conductance, like any channel: I_syn = g_syn(t),(V - E_syn) . AMPA and NMDA receptors pass Na⁺ and K⁺ and reverse near E_eapprox0 mV; GABA _A receptors pass mainly Cl⁻ (and some HCO₃⁻) and reverse near E_iapprox-70 mV in mature CA1 pyramidal cells; GABA _B receptors open K⁺ channels and reverse near E_K .With steady conductances the membrane settles where total current vanishes, a conductance-weighted average of reversal potentials: V_infty = fracg_L E_L + g_e E_e + g_i E_ig_L + g_e + g_i,qquad R_in = frac1g_L + g_e + g_i (6.2.1)Worked example 6.2.1Shunting inhibitionLet g_L = 5 nS, E_L = -65 mV and an excitatory g_e = 2 nS ( E_e = 0 ). Then V_infty = -325/7approx-46.4 mV, an 18.6 mV depolarization. Add g_i = 5 nS with E_i = -65 mV, equal to rest. Alone, this inhibition would not move the voltage at all. Together, V_infty = -650/12approx-54.2 mV: the depolarization drops to 10.8 mV and input resistance from 143 to 83 MΩ.This is shunting inhibition: inhibition acting by conductance rather than by hyperpolarization. It divides the excitatory effect rather than subtracting a fixed amount, and it is invisible if you only look at inhibition alone."},{"route":"/topics/synapses/synaptic-conductance/","heading":"NMDA receptors and the magnesium block","text":"Textbook §6.2. NMDA receptors and the magnesium blockNMDA receptors are blocked by extracellular Mg²⁺ at negative voltages. Jahr and Stevens (1990) fitted the unblocked fraction as B(V) = frac11 + dfrac[Mg^2+]_o3.57 mM,e^-0.062,V (6.2.2)Figure 6.2.1. A Eq. (6.2.1) with g_e = 2 nS: inhibition reversing at rest (shunting) reduces the depolarization divisively without hyperpolarizing; inhibition at −75 mV can push below rest. B NMDA Mg²⁺ block, Eq. (6.2.2).with V in mV. At 1 mM Mg²⁺, Bapprox0.06 at −65 mV, 0.23 at −40 mV and 0.51 at −20 mV. NMDA current therefore requires both transmitter (presynaptic activity) and depolarization (postsynaptic activity), which makes the receptor a coincidence detector and a natural substrate for Hebbian plasticity (Ch. 9).Algorithm 6.2 Conductance-based synapses with NMDA blockdef I_syn(V, t): I = g_ampa(t) * (V - 0) I += g_nmda(t) * B(V) * (V - 0) # B(V) = 1/(1 + Mg/3.57 * exp(-0.062 V)) I += g_gabaA(t) * (V - E_Cl) return I # outward-positive, enters C dV/dt = -I_syn ... V_inf = (gL*EL + ge*Ee + gi*Ei) / (gL + ge + gi) # steady-state checkPseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallCurrent-based synapsesMany network models use current-based synapses, adding a fixed I_syn(t) regardless of V . This is cheaper and sometimes adequate, but it cannot express shunting, cannot saturate at E_syn , and makes inhibition always hyperpolarizing. Check whether your conclusion depends on that choice.Across scalesBiologyAMPA, NMDA, GABA_A, GABA_B receptorsBrain regionCA1 dendrites: NMDA spikes, somatic shunting by basket cellsMeasurementReversal potential of PSCs; dynamic clampMathConductance-weighted averages, Mg²⁺ block functionCodeSynapse workbench, shunting preset"},{"route":"/topics/synapses/synaptic-conductance/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 6.2. With g_L = 5 nS at E_L = -65 mV, what does a g_i = 4 nS inhibitory conductance with E_i = -75 mV do on its own? Using Eq. (6.2.2), by what factor does NMDA conductance increase when a spine depolarizes from −65 to −20 mV? Why does shunting inhibition reduce an EPSP more when the inhibitory synapse is near the excitatory one than when it is far away on another branch?"},{"route":"/topics/synapses/timing-location/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §6.3. After a presynaptic spike at t = 0 , the postsynaptic conductance rises and decays. Three kernels are standard: g(t) = bar g,e^-t/tau,qquad g(t) = bar g,fracttaue^1-t/tau,qquad g(t) = bar g,etaleft(e^-t/tau_d - e^-t/tau_rright) (6.3.1)(single exponential, alpha function, difference of exponentials), each for tge0 . The last separates rise tau_r from decay tau_d ; eta normalizes the peak to bar g . Its peak time is t_peak = fractau_rtau_dtau_d - tau_rlnfractau_dtau_r (6.3.2)Worked example 6.3.1AMPA and NMDA time coursesAMPA-like kinetics ( tau_r = 0.5 , tau_d = 5 ms) peak at t_peak = (2.5/4.5)ln10approx1.28 ms, with etaapprox1.44 . NMDA-like kinetics ( tau_r = 2 , tau_d = 50 ms) peak at about 6.7 ms and last ten times longer, which is why NMDA receptors dominate temporal summation over tens of milliseconds.Figure 6.3.1. A The kernels of Eq. (6.3.1), normalized to their peaks. B Two AMPA-like inputs summate strongly 2 ms apart, partly at 10 ms and not at 25 ms.Successive inputs add if they arrive before earlier conductances have decayed: temporal summation. Two inputs 2 ms apart with tau = 5 ms overlap strongly (the first is still at e^-2/5approx67% ); 20 ms apart, hardly at all. Dendritic location adds to the timing problem: distal EPSPs reach the soma attenuated, delayed and broadened (Ch. 2), unless I_h and synaptic scaling compensate."},{"route":"/topics/synapses/timing-location/","heading":"The feedforward inhibition window","text":"Textbook §6.3. The feedforward inhibition windowSchaffer collaterals excite CA1 pyramidal cells and, in parallel, interneurons that inhibit the same pyramidal cells about 2 ms later. Pouille and Scanziani (2001) showed that this feedforward inhibition narrows the time window during which excitatory inputs can summate to reach threshold: only inputs arriving within a few milliseconds of each other succeed. The circuit turns a cell that integrates over tens of milliseconds into a coincidence detector.Algorithm 6.3 Event-driven synaptic conductance# difference of exponentials as two decaying traces (no kernel convolution needed) on presynaptic spike: a += w * eta; b += w * eta every step: a *= exp(-dt / tau_d) b *= exp(-dt / tau_r) g = a - b # rises with tau_r, decays with tau_d eta = 1 / (exp(-t_peak/tau_d) - exp(-t_peak/tau_r)) # peak normalizationPseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallNormalize consistentlyComparing kernels by peak conductance or by total charge ( int g,dt , which equals bar gtau for the exponential) gives different conclusions. State whether two synapse models were matched on peak or on charge.Across scalesBiologyReceptor kinetics, dendritic filtering, feedforward interneuronsBrain regionCA1 feedforward inhibition window (Pouille & Scanziani 2001)MeasurementPaired-pulse and timed stimulationMathConvolution kernels, peak-time formulaCodeSynapse workbench, feedforward-inhibition preset"},{"route":"/topics/synapses/timing-location/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 6.3. For tau_r = 1 , tau_d = 10 ms, compute t_peak . What is the total charge-equivalent int_0^infty g,dt of an exponential conductance with bar g = 1 nS and tau = 5 ms? Feedforward inhibition is blocked with a GABA _A antagonist. Predict the effect on the range of input timing that can trigger a spike."},{"route":"/topics/synapses/ei-feedback/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §6.4. Inhibitory interneurons enter circuits in a few recurring motifs. In feedforward inhibition an input drives both a principal cell and an interneuron that inhibits it. In feedback (recurrent) inhibition principal cells drive interneurons that inhibit them back. Lateral inhibition spreads that feedback to neighbours. In CA1, basket cells mediate both feedforward (from CA3) and feedback (from CA1 pyramidal cells) inhibition; O-LM cells provide feedback inhibition to distal dendrites.Model an excitatory and an inhibitory population as rates (developed in Ch. 7). Linearized around an operating point with gains absorbed into the weights: taubeginpmatrixdot e dot iendpmatrix = beginpmatrix-1 + w_EE & -w_EI w_IE & -1 - w_IIendpmatrixbeginpmatrixe iendpmatrix (6.4.1)The trace is w_EE - w_II - 2 and the determinant (1 - w_EE)(1 + w_II) + w_EIw_IE . The loop is stable when the trace is negative and the determinant positive; strong E→I→E feedback makes the determinant large and the eigenvalues complex, so the loop rings. When recurrent excitation pushes the trace through zero, the loop oscillates (a Hopf bifurcation, Ch. 4).Worked example 6.4.1Ringing in an E–I loopWith w_EE = 1.5 , w_EI = w_IE = 3 , w_II = 0 : T = -0.5 , Delta = -0.5 + 9 = 8.5 , eigenvalues (-0.25pm2.90i)/tau . A perturbation decays slowly while oscillating with period 2pitau/2.9approx2.2tau . With tauapprox5 ms that is ≈ 11 ms, a gamma-range period (≈ 90 Hz). Raising w_EE to 2 brings T to 0: sustained oscillation.Figure 6.4.1. The linearized E–I loop of the worked example (Eq. (6.4.1)). A A perturbation of E rings and decays, with I lagging E. B The same solution in the (E, I) plane: a stable spiral.This E–I mechanism, with the period set largely by inhibitory synaptic decay, is the basis of the PI"},{"route":"/topics/synapses/ei-feedback/","heading":"What balance means","text":"Textbook §6.4. What balance meansE/I balance usually means that excitatory and inhibitory currents onto a cell are large but nearly cancel, so that the mean input is near threshold and spikes are driven by fluctuations. It does not mean equal numbers of E and I cells (in CA1, interneurons are roughly 10% of neurons), nor equal synaptic weights. Balance can be loose (on average over seconds) or tight (tracking within milliseconds).Algorithm 6.4 Is an E–I loop going to oscillate?J = [[-1 + wEE, -wEI], [ wIE, -1 - wII]] / tau # gains folded into weights T, D = trace(J), det(J) if T < 0 and D > 0: ringing = (T*T < 4*D); f_ring = sqrt(D - T*T/4) / (2*pi) else: unstable -> simulate the nonlinear model to find the limit cyclePseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallRate loops lose spike timingEq. (6.4.1) predicts when a population rhythm appears and roughly its frequency, but not which cells fire on which cycle. Whether the rhythm is carried by sparse, irregular single-cell firing or by every cell firing every cycle needs a spiking network (Ch. 7).Across scalesBiologyBasket, O-LM and bistratified interneuronsBrain regionCA1 and CA3 gamma (PING/ING)MeasurementLFP gamma, interneuron–pyramid spike timingMathLinear stability of a 2-D loopCodeWilson–Cowan lab; Synapse workbench"},{"route":"/topics/synapses/ei-feedback/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 6.4. For w_EE=2 , w_EI=w_IE=2 , w_II=0 , compute trace, determinant and eigenvalues of Eq. (6.4.1). Why does increasing inhibitory self-coupling w_II tend to stabilize the loop? An experiment finds excitatory and inhibitory conductances onto CA1 cells that scale together across input strengths. Is the circuit “balanced” in the sense above?"},{"route":"/topics/networks/spiking-neurons/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §7.1. The leaky integrate-and-fire (LIF) neuron keeps the passive membrane of Chapter 1 and replaces the spike mechanism by a rule: tau_mfracdVdt = -(V - V_rest) + RI;qquad V ge V_th Rightarrow spike, Vto V_reset for t_ref (7.1.1)Below threshold the voltage charges toward V_rest + RI . If that target lies below V_th the neuron never fires; the smallest current that fires it is the rheobase, I_rh = (V_th - V_rest)/R . Above rheobase, with V_reset = V_rest and Delta = V_th - V_rest , solving Eq. (7.1.1) from reset to threshold gives the interspike interval T_ISI = t_ref + tau_mlnfracRIRI - Delta,qquad f = frac1T_ISI (7.1.2)Worked example 7.1.1An f–I curve by handTake tau_m = 20 ms, R = 100 MΩ (so RI in mV is 100 × I in nA), V_rest = V_reset = -65 mV and V_th = -50 mV, so Delta = 15 mV and rheobase is 0.15 nA. Without refractoriness: at 0.2 nA, T = 20ln(20/5) = 27.7 ms (36 Hz); at 0.3 nA, T = 20ln 2 = 13.9 ms (72 Hz); at 1 nA, about 308 Hz. A 2 ms refractory period lowers the 0.3 nA rate to 63 Hz and caps the rate at 500 Hz.Near rheobase the LIF rate rises steeply but not as a square root: it rises as 1/ln , a logarithmic singularity. The LIF is a caricature of class I firing, not a normal form for it (compare the quadratic integrate-and-fire neuron, which is).Figure 7.1.1. A An LIF neuron (τ = 20 ms, R = 100 MΩ, threshold −50 mV, reset −65 mV, 2 ms refractory) driven by 0.3 nA. Vertical lines mark spikes. B The f–I curve of Eq. (7.1.2), with and without refractoriness.What is lost? The spike shape (irrelevant for most network questions), the voltage dependence of channels (no resonance unless an adaptation variable is added), and the dendrites. Extensions add these back selectively: adaptive exponential IF (AdEx; Brette & Gerstner 2005) adds a spike-initiation no"},{"route":"/topics/networks/spiking-neurons/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 7.1. For the LIF in the example, what current gives exactly 50 Hz with no refractory period? Derive Eq. (7.1.2) from Eq. (7.1.1). Why can a plain LIF neuron not show subthreshold resonance?"},{"route":"/topics/networks/wiring-delays/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §7.2. A CA3 pyramidal cell sends its axon (the Schaffer collateral) across stratum radiatum, where it makes excitatory synapses on the apical dendrites of thousands of CA1 pyramidal cells; its other branches recurrently contact other CA3 cells. Each contact has a strength (how many AMPA receptors, how reliable release is) and a delay: the spike must travel along a thin, mostly unmyelinated axon and then cross the synapse. CA1 pyramidal cells, by contrast, make few connections with each other; most of their recurrent influence goes through interneurons.A network model compresses this anatomy into three objects: who connects to whom, how strongly, and with what delay. The weight matrix holds the first two; a delay table the third.A network is a directed graph. Its weight matrix W has entry W_ij for the connection **from neuron j to neuron i **, so that the input to i is sum_j W_ijs_j for presynaptic activities s_j . Zero means no connection; diagonal entries (self-connections, autapses) are usually set to zero.Dale’s principle says a neuron releases the same transmitter at all its terminals, so each column of W is all non-negative (excitatory neuron) or all non-positive (inhibitory). Random matrices that ignore this behave differently, especially in their eigenvalue spectra.In the simplest random (Erdős–Rényi) graph each possible connection exists independently with probability p . The in-degree K of a neuron is then binomial: langle Krangle = Np,qquad SD(K) = sqrtNp(1-p) (7.2.1)Worked example 7.2.1A sparse random networkWith N = 1000 and p = 0.1 , each neuron receives on average 100 inputs, with SD ≈ 9.5. Brunel (2000) used a fixed in-degree instead ( C_E = 4C_I inputs per cell) to remove this variability; the choice matters when comparing to theory.Figure 7.2.1"},{"route":"/topics/networks/wiring-delays/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 7.2. N = 200 , p = 0.05 . Mean and SD of in-degree? In a 3-neuron network neuron 1 excites 2 with weight 0.5, 2 inhibits 3 with weight 1. Write W (from j to i ). CA3→CA1 Schaffer collaterals are unmyelinated. At 0.3 m/s, how long does a 1.5 mm path take?"},{"route":"/topics/networks/rates-wilson-cowan/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §7.3. Record from a hundred CA1 pyramidal cells at once (with a silicon probe, or with calcium imaging) and the individual spike trains look irregular. Average them in short windows and a smooth curve appears: the population’s firing rate, rising and falling with the theta rhythm. In the same tissue, fast-spiking interneurons follow the pyramidal cells with a short lag and inhibit them back.Wilson and Cowan asked whether those smooth averages obey their own equations, without tracking every spike. Their answer treats each population (pyramidal cells, interneurons) as a single unit whose activity relaxes toward a saturating function of its input, much as a single cell’s rate saturates in its f–I curve.If a population is large and its neurons fire asynchronously, its activity can be described by a smooth rate E(t) instead of spike times. Wilson and Cowan (1972) wrote coupled equations for an excitatory and an inhibitory population: beginalignedtau_Edot E &= -E + S_E(w_EEE - w_EII + P) tau_Idot I &= -I + S_I(w_IEE - w_III + Q)endaligned (7.3.1)Each population relaxes toward a sigmoidal function S of its net input. The weights are positive magnitudes; the minus signs make inhibition inhibitory. P and Q are external drives. The equations assume that the population’s response to input is fast compared with tau and well summarized by a static nonlinearity: assumptions examined in Chapter 8.Fixed points lie at the intersection of the nullclines E = S_E(w_EEE - w_EII + P) and I = S_I(w_IEE - w_III + Q) . Because S_E is sigmoidal and w_EE is large, the E -nullcline has an N-shape, like FHN’s cubic, and the same geometry follows: up to three fixed points, and a limit cycle when the fixed point sits on the middle branch and is unstable.Worked example 7.3.1The classic limit"},{"route":"/topics/networks/rates-wilson-cowan/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 7.3. With S(x) = 1/(1+e^-(x-4)) , w_EE=16 , w_EI=12 and P=0 , compute dot E at (E, I) = (0.5, 0.5) with tau_E = 1 . Why does strong recurrent excitation ( w_EE large) give the E-nullcline its N-shape? What would you change in Eq. (7.3.1) to model GABA _B inhibition, which is slow?"},{"route":"/topics/networks/collective-states/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §7.4. Hippocampal recordings in a behaving animal show the circuit switching between very different collective modes. During exploration, pyramidal cells fire sparsely and irregularly, organized by theta and gamma rhythms. During quiet rest and slow-wave sleep, the network produces sharp-wave ripples: brief, highly synchronous bursts in which many CA1 cells fire within tens of milliseconds, riding a ≈ 150–200 Hz oscillation. In epilepsy, synchrony becomes pathological.The same cells and synapses produce all of these, so the difference lies in how activity is coordinated. To compare a model with these states you need numbers that capture coordination, not only how much the cells fire.Three measurements classify most network states. The mean rate is spikes per neuron per second. The coefficient of variation of interspike intervals, CV = sigma_ISI/mu_ISI , measures irregularity: 0 for a clock, 1 for a Poisson process. Synchrony compares the variance of the population-averaged signal with the average single-neuron variance; it is near 1 if neurons fluctuate together and near 1/N if independently. barnu = fracN_spikesN_cells,T,qquad CV = fracsigma_ISImu_ISI (7.4.1)Brunel (2000) mapped the states of a sparse network of excitatory and inhibitory LIF neurons as a function of relative inhibition g and external drive. When excitation dominates ( g small), neurons fire regularly and in synchrony at high rates (SR). When inhibition dominates ( g above about 4, the balance point given four times more E inputs), firing is asynchronous and irregular (AI) at low rates, the state that most resembles cortex in vivo, with synchronous irregular states (fast or slow population oscillations with irregular single cells) at the boundaries.Worked example 7.4.1Reading a raster12,000 spi"},{"route":"/topics/networks/collective-states/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 7.4. A neuron fires every 25 ms exactly. Rate and CV? Why does a Poisson process have CV = 1? In Brunel’s network, why does gapprox4 mark the balance point?"},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/population-activity/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §8.1. A CA1 pyramidal cell in a moving rat fires a few spikes per second on average. A single downstream neuron cannot learn much from one such cell in a 10 ms window, because there is usually no spike to read. But each subiculum or entorhinal neuron receives input from many CA1 cells at once, and the local field potential reflects the summed synaptic currents of thousands. What the circuit “sees” is the population.Population activity is the quantity that captures this: the fraction of the population that fires per unit time. It is also what a silicon probe or calcium imaging session gives you after pooling cells.Population activity is the fraction of a population that fires per unit time. Counting spikes in a bin of width Delta t across N neurons: A(t) = fracn_spikes(t,,t+Delta t)N,Delta t (8.1.1)in Hz per neuron. Unlike a single neuron’s rate, which needs a long window to estimate, A(t) can follow fast changes if N is large: averaging over neurons replaces averaging over time (Gerstner et al. 2014, Ch. 12).Worked example 8.1.1How noisy is the estimate?If neurons fire independently as Poisson processes at rate nu , the count in a bin is Poisson with mean NnuDelta t and SD sqrtNnuDelta t . For N = 1000 , nu = 5 Hz and Delta t = 10 ms the mean count is 50, SD ≈ 7.1, so A = 5 ± 0.71 Hz. With N = 100 the SD rises to ≈ 2.2 Hz: a population of 100 cannot resolve a 1 Hz modulation in 10 ms bins. SD(A) = sqrtfracnuNDelta t (8.1.2)Figure 8.1.1. 200 independent Poisson neurons whose rate is modulated at theta frequency. Population activity (Eq. (8.1.1)) in 5 ms bins is noisy; 25 ms bins track the true rate more smoothly but blur fast changes (Eq. (8.1.2)).The SD falls as 1/sqrtNDelta t : doubling either the population or the bin halves the variance. Correlated firing br"},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/population-activity/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 8.1. 30 spikes from 200 neurons in a 5 ms bin. What is A ? How many neurons are needed for SD(A) ≤ 0.5 Hz at nu = 5 Hz with 10 ms bins? If all neurons fired in perfect synchrony, how would SD(A) scale with N ?"},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/gain-function/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §8.2. Inject a series of current steps into a CA1 pyramidal cell and count spikes: no firing below rheobase, then a rate that climbs with current and eventually levels off as Na⁺ inactivation and afterhyperpolarizations limit how fast the cell can recover. This f–I curve is the cell’s input–output rule. In vivo, the cell is bombarded by synaptic noise, so it fires occasionally even below rheobase, and the sharp corner becomes a smooth S-shaped curve.A population’s gain function is the same idea one level up: how the average rate of many such cells depends on their shared input.A gain function F maps a neuron’s or population’s input to its output rate. For single neurons it is the f–I curve measured with steps; for populations with noisy input it is smoother. A convenient form is the logistic sigmoid with threshold theta and slope parameter s : F(x) = frac11+e^-s(x-theta),qquad F'(theta) = fracs4 (8.2.1)The local slope F'(x) is the gain: how much output changes per unit input at the operating point. Gain, not the full curve, enters every linear stability calculation (Ch. 6–8)."},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/gain-function/","heading":"Noise smooths thresholds","text":"Textbook §8.2. Noise smooths thresholdsA deterministic LIF has a sharp onset at rheobase. Adding input noise lets the neuron fire occasionally below rheobase and makes F a smooth sigmoid; the classical result for the LIF with white-noise input (the Siegert formula) gives F as an integral involving the mean and SD of the input. More noise means a lower effective threshold and a shallower curve."},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/gain-function/","heading":"Modulating gain","text":"Textbook §8.2. Modulating gainA change in theta shifts the curve (additive or subtractive modulation); a change in s or in the maximum rate scales it (multiplicative or divisive modulation). Shunting inhibition alone acts mostly subtractively on firing rate, but Chance, Abbott and Reyes (2002) showed that increasing balanced background excitation and inhibition together changes the gain: the extra noise and conductance combine into divisive modulation.Worked example 8.2.1Gain at and away from thresholdWith s = 2 , the gain at x = theta is 0.5 per unit input and F(theta+1) = 1/(1+e^-2)approx0.88 . One unit above threshold the gain has already fallen to 2F(1-F)approx0.21 . The same input perturbation has a 2.4× smaller effect there.Figure 8.2.1. A Logistic gain functions, Eq. (8.2.1), with three slopes. B Simulated LIF populations: without noise the rate jumps at rheobase (15 mV of drive); with 3 mV input noise the curve is smooth and starts below rheobase.Algorithm 8.2 Measure a population gain functionfor mu in drives: rates = [] for trial in range(n_trials): spikes = simulate(neurons, I_mean=mu, noise_sd=sigma) rates.append(n_spikes / (N * T)) F[mu] = mean(rates) fit logistic: F(x) = r_max / (1 + exp(-s (x - theta))) gain at operating point = dF/dx (numerical)Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallStatic gain functions ignore dynamics F describes the steady-state rate for constant input. For inputs that change faster than the neurons’ time constants (including theta and gamma rhythms), the population responds with a frequency-dependent gain and phase, much like the impedance of Chapter 3. Static mean-field theory is a low-frequency approximation.Across scalesBiologyf–I curves under synaptic noiseBrain regionIn vivo-like conductance states in"},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/gain-function/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 8.2. Show that the logistic sigmoid satisfies F' = sF(1-F) and hence F'(theta) = s/4 . A drug multiplies all firing rates by 0.5. Is this subtractive or divisive, and how does the gain at threshold change? For s = 1 , theta = 0 , at what input is the gain half its maximum?"},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/self-consistency/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §8.3. In CA3, pyramidal cells excite each other through recurrent collaterals; the more cells are active, the more excitatory input each receives, which activates more cells. Interneurons and adaptation hold this loop in check. Experimentally, persistent activity that outlasts a brief stimulus (in prefrontal cortex during working memory, or in attractor models of CA3 recall) is thought to depend on exactly this kind of recurrent excitation.The question a mean-field model asks is: which levels of activity can sustain themselves? A level is self-sustaining when the input it generates through recurrent synapses produces exactly that level of activity again.In a recurrent population each neuron’s input includes the population’s own output. With coupling J and external input I , the rate dynamics and their equilibria are taudot r = -r + F(Jr + I),qquad r^* = F(Jr^* + I) (8.3.1)The second equation is the self-consistency condition: the rate assumed to compute the input must equal the rate that input produces. Graphically, plot y = r and y = F(Jr+I) ; the intersections are the fixed points.Linearizing Eq. (8.3.1) at r^* gives growth rate (-1 + JF'(Jr^*+I))/tau . A fixed point is stable when J,F'(Jr^* + I) (8.3.2)i.e. when the curve F(Jr+I) crosses the diagonal with slope less than 1. Strong recurrent excitation can make the slope exceed 1 at a middle crossing, creating an unstable fixed point flanked by two stable ones: bistability, a minimal model of persistent activity and working memory.Worked example 8.3.1One state or two?Use the logistic F with s = 1 , theta = 0 . With J = 2 , I = 0 there is one fixed point, r^*approx0.844 , with JF'approx0.26 : stable. With J = 8 , I = -4 there are three: r^*approx0.021 (stable, JF'approx0.17 ), 0.5 (unstable, JF' = 2 ) and 0.97"},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/self-consistency/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 8.3. For J = 4 , I = -2 (logistic, s=1 , theta=0 ), show that r = 0.5 is a fixed point and find JF' there. In the J = 8 bistable example, what happens if I is raised from −4 to −3? Derive the stability condition Eq. (8.3.2)."},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/heterogeneity/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §8.4. No two CA1 pyramidal cells are alike. Their rheobase, input resistance, I_h density and dendritic arbors vary from cell to cell, and so do deep and superficial pyramidal cells within the same layer. Interneurons come in more than twenty types. In Alzheimer’s disease models this variability can itself change: some cells become hyperexcitable while others fall silent.A mean-field model built on one “typical” cell ignores all of this. The question for this section is when that matters: when does the population respond differently from its average member?Real CA1 cells differ: thresholds, channel densities and morphologies vary from cell to cell. If cell parameters theta are distributed with density p(theta) , the population response is the average of the responses, not the response of the average cell: bar F(x) = int F(x;theta),p(theta),dtheta ;neq; F(x;bartheta)quadin general (8.4.1)Jensen’s inequality makes this precise: averaging and a nonlinear function do not commute. Where F is convex (below threshold) heterogeneity raises the average response; where it is concave (near saturation) it lowers it."},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/heterogeneity/","heading":"An exact example","text":"Textbook §8.4. An exact exampleLet each cell’s gain function be a cumulative Gaussian (probit) of width s , F(x;theta) = Phibig((x-theta)/sbig) , and let thresholds be Gaussian with mean theta_0 and SD sigma . Then the average is again a probit, with a wider width: bar F(x) = Phi!left(fracx - theta_0sqrts^2+sigma^2right) (8.4.2)Worked example 8.4.1Heterogeneity linearizes the populationWith s = 1 , sigma = 2 and input two units above theta_0 : the average cell responds with Phi(2) approx 0.977 , the population with Phi(2/sqrt5)approx0.814 (a Monte Carlo average over 400,000 cells gives 0.814). The population’s maximal gain falls from 0.40 to 0.18, but its response is graded over a range sqrt5approx2.2 times wider.Figure 8.4.1. A Eq. (8.4.2): wider threshold distributions give shallower, wider population curves. B Twelve cells with different thresholds (tan), their average (pink) and the response of the average cell (dashed).Heterogeneity therefore trades peak gain for dynamic range, and it changes the stability of the recurrent fixed points of the previous section, since JF' changes. Mean-field models of the CA1 parameter variants in the research thread should average responses over measured variability rather than use one representative cell.Algorithm 8.4 Average over a heterogeneous populationthetas = sample(theta_distribution, n=10000) # or measured cell parameters F_pop(x) = mean([F(x; th) for th in thetas]) F_avg_cell(x) = F(x; mean(thetas)) plot both; their difference is the cost of the 'typical cell' assumption use F_pop (not F_avg_cell) inside r = F_pop(J r + I)Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallLimits of the scalar closureEq. (8.4.1) still assumes cells differ only in theta and respond to a shared input. Dendritic location of inp"},{"route":"/topics/mean-field/heterogeneity/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 8.4. With s = 1 and sigma = 1 , what is the population response 2 units above theta_0 ? Below threshold ( x), does heterogeneity increase or decrease the population response, and why? How does the maximal population gain scale with sigma for large sigma ?"},{"route":"/topics/learning/computation-task/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §9.1. A computation is a mapping from inputs to outputs that is useful for a specified task: deciding whether a stimulus is present, estimating position, predicting reward. Without a task, “the network computes” is not a testable statement.The simplest output stage is a linear threshold readout of a response feature vector mathbf x : hat y = H(mathbf wcdotmathbf x + b - theta) = begincases1 & mathbf wcdotmathbf x + b > theta 0 & otherwiseendcases (9.1.1)The set mathbf wcdotmathbf x + b = theta is a hyperplane, the decision boundary; inputs on one side are labelled 1. A single readout can only separate classes that are linearly separable in mathbf x , which is why rich, nonlinear recurrent responses help: they can make separable what was not separable in the raw input (the logic of reservoir computing).Worked example 9.1.1A one-feature decisionWith feature x = 0.6 , bias b = 0.1 and threshold theta = 0.5 : 0.7 > 0.5 , so hat y = 1 . Lowering b to -0.2 flips the decision. Bias and threshold are interchangeable; only b - theta matters.Figure 9.1.1. Synthetic data. A A linear readout, Eq. (9.1.1), separates two classes with a line. B A middle class cannot be separated by one threshold on x , but a threshold on the nonlinear feature (x-0.5)^2 separates it.If a decoder can predict, say, animal position from CA1 activity, the activity contains information about position in a form that decoder can use. It does not show that downstream neurons use that information, or use it that way. Information, readout and biological mechanism are three separate claims.Algorithm 9.1 Train and apply a linear readoutX_train, y_train, X_test, y_test = split(responses, labels, by='session') w, b = fit_logistic_regression(X_train, y_train, regularization=lam) y_hat = (X_test @ w + b > 0) "},{"route":"/topics/learning/computation-task/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 9.1. Two classes are xin0.1, 0.9 for class A and x = 0.5 for class B. Can one threshold on x separate them? What feature would help? With mathbf w = (1, -1) , b = 0 , theta = 0 , classify mathbf x = (0.3, 0.5) and (0.8, 0.2) . A decoder predicts theta phase from a CA1 cell’s spike times with 90% accuracy. List two things this does not show."},{"route":"/topics/learning/recurrence-attractors/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §9.2. An attractor is a state (or set of states) that nearby trajectories approach and remain near. Recurrent networks can hold several attractors at once, each a candidate memory; the input selects which basin the state falls into, and the dynamics complete the pattern.The guide’s scalar example is dot x = x - x^3 + b = -fracdUdx,qquad U(x) = -fracx^22 + fracx^44 - bx (9.2.1)For b = 0 , fixed points are -1, 0, +1 , with slopes 1 - 3x^2 = -2, 1, -2 : two stable states separated by an unstable one. U is a double-well energy: the state always rolls downhill, so it never oscillates and ends in a minimum. Its basins are x and x>0 .Worked example 9.2.1Bias tilts the landscapeWith b = 0.2 the fixed points move to -0.879 , -0.209 and 1.088 : the right well deepens and its basin grows. At b_c = 2/(3sqrt3)approx0.385 the left well and the barrier merge in a saddle-node, and for b = 0.5 only one fixed point, 1.19 , remains. A memory can be erased by a bias that removes its basin.Figure 9.2.1. The energy of Eq. (9.2.1) for three biases. At b = 0 two equal wells (memories); b = 0.2 deepens the right well; at b = 0.5 the left well has vanished."},{"route":"/topics/learning/recurrence-attractors/","heading":"Hopfield networks","text":"Textbook §9.2. Hopfield networksHopfield (1982) stored binary patterns boldsymbolxi^muin-1,1^N in symmetric weights by a Hebbian rule, W_ij = frac1Nsum_muxi^mu_ixi^mu_j ( ine j ). With symmetric weights there is an energy function and the patterns are (approximately) its minima. For random patterns, recall fails above about 0.138N patterns (Amit, Gutfreund & Sompolinsky 1985): a 1,000-neuron network stores about 138. The CA3 recurrent network is the classic hippocampal candidate for such autoassociative memory.Algorithm 9.2 Store and recall patterns in a Hopfield networkW = (1/N) * sum(outer(xi, xi) for xi in patterns); fill_diagonal(W, 0) s = corrupt(patterns[0], flip_fraction=0.2) # cue repeat until no change: for i in random_order(N): s[i] = sign(W[i] @ s) # asynchronous update overlap = s @ patterns[0] / N # 1.0 = perfect recallPseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallAttractors need not be fixed pointsLimit cycles (rhythms) and continuous families of states (line or ring attractors, proposed for head direction and grid cells) are also attractors. And a network can produce a long transient that looks persistent without being an attractor at all; check by perturbing the state.Across scalesBiologyRecurrent collaterals, pattern completionBrain regionCA3 (autoassociative memory); head-direction ring attractorMeasurementRemapping, pattern completion experimentsMathEnergy functions, basins of attractionCodeHippocampal memory page (Hopfield recall)"},{"route":"/topics/learning/recurrence-attractors/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 9.2. For b = 0 , a perturbation of size 0.01 from x^* = 1 decays with what time constant? Show that dot U le 0 along trajectories of Eq. (9.2.1). Approximately how many random patterns can a 5,000-neuron Hopfield network store?"},{"route":"/topics/learning/plasticity-rules/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §9.3. Hebbian learning strengthens a connection when pre- and postsynaptic activity coincide: Delta w = eta,a_pre,a_post (9.3.1)With non-negative activities this only ever increases w ; if a_post grows with w , the feedback is positive and weights run away. Every usable rule adds stabilization: hard bounds (as in the lab’s clip to [0, 1]), normalization, or a threshold that adapts.Oja’s rule, Deltamathbf w = eta,y(mathbf x - ymathbf w) with y = mathbf wcdotmathbf x , keeps |mathbf w|to1 and makes mathbf w converge to the input’s first principal component (Oja 1982).BCM, Delta w = eta,x,y(y - theta_M) with theta_M tracking the recent average of y^2 , gives LTD at low and LTP at high postsynaptic activity and stabilizes by sliding the threshold (Bienenstock, Cooper & Munro 1982)."},{"route":"/topics/learning/plasticity-rules/","heading":"Spike-timing-dependent plasticity","text":"Textbook §9.3. Spike-timing-dependent plasticityIn hippocampal neurons, Bi and Poo (1998) found that a presynaptic spike a few ms before a postsynaptic spike potentiates the synapse and one after depresses it, with effects fading over tens of ms. The pair-based STDP window, with Delta t = t_post - t_pre : Delta w = begincases A_+,e^-Delta t/tau_+ & Delta t > 0 -A_-,e^Delta t/tau_- & Delta t(9.3.2)Worked example 9.3.1Pairs of spikesWith A_+ = 0.01 , A_- = 0.0105 and tau_pm = 20 ms (illustrative values): a pairing at Delta t = +10 ms gives +0.0061 ; at -10 ms, -0.0064 ; at +40 ms only +0.0014 . Making A_-tau_- slightly larger than A_+tau_+ makes uncorrelated firing depress on average, which stabilizes weights.Figure 9.3.1. A The pair STDP window, Eq. (9.3.2), with the example values. B Weight norm under plain Hebbian learning (Eq. (9.3.1)) and Oja’s rule on correlated inputs: Hebb grows without bound, Oja settles at |mathbf w| = 1 .Pair-based STDP misses frequency effects measured in cortex and hippocampus; triplet rules (Pfister & Gerstner 2006) and calcium-based rules (Graupner & Brunel 2012) capture them, and both are in the Synapse workbench. In CA1 the dominant plasticity during place-field formation may be behavioural-timescale plasticity (BTSP), which operates over seconds rather than milliseconds (Bittner et al. 2017).Algorithm 9.3 Pair-based STDP with traceson pre spike: x_pre += 1; w -= A_minus * y_post # depression on post spike: y_post += 1; w += A_plus * x_pre # potentiation every step: x_pre *= exp(-dt / tau_plus) y_post *= exp(-dt / tau_minus) w = clip(w, 0, w_max) # bound (or soft bounds)Pseudocode: Python-like, units and helpers schematic.Common pitfallRule parameters are preparation-specificWindow widths, amplitudes and even the sign of STDP depend on sy"},{"route":"/topics/learning/plasticity-rules/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 9.3. Starting from w = 0.5 with eta = 0.1 , a_pre = 0.8 , a_post = 0.9 , apply three Hebbian updates with a clip to [0, 1]. Integrate the STDP window over all Delta t for the example values. What does the sign mean? Why does the NMDA receptor (Ch. 6) make a natural Hebbian coincidence detector?"},{"route":"/topics/learning/readouts-evaluation/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §9.4. A decoder is only as convincing as its evaluation. Data are split into a training set (fit parameters), a validation set (choose hyperparameters such as regularization) and a test set used once, at the end. Cross-validation rotates the roles to use small datasets efficiently.For classification, accuracy is the fraction correct. With n independent test trials and true accuracy p , the estimate has a binomial standard error SE(hat p) = sqrtfrachat p(1-hat p)n (9.4.1)Worked example 9.4.1Is 80% good?80% correct on 50 test trials has SE ≈ 0.057, so a rough 95% interval is 0.69–0.91. On 200 trials the SE halves to 0.028. Against chance (50% for two balanced classes), 32/50 correct has a one-sided binomial papprox0.03 ; 30/50 has papprox0.10 . Small test sets give wide intervals.Figure 9.4.1. A The 95% band for an 80% decoder (Eq. (9.4.1)) shrinks as 1/sqrt n . B Synthetic regression: training error keeps falling with model flexibility, held-out error rises once the model fits noise.Two failures are common. Overfitting: a flexible model fits noise in the training set and generalizes poorly; the gap between training and test accuracy reveals it. Leakage: information from the test set reaches training, through preprocessing fitted on all data, through trials that are not independent (neighbouring time bins of the same run), or by tuning on test results. In neural data, splitting by recording session or by run, not by time bin, is often essential.Algorithm 9.4 Honest evaluationfolds = group_k_fold(trials, groups=session_id, k=5) for train, test in folds: scaler = fit_scaler(X[train]) # fit preprocessing on train only model = fit(scaler(X[train]), y[train]) score.append(evaluate(model, scaler(X[test]), y[test])) report mean(score), sd(score), chance, n_test touch th"},{"route":"/topics/learning/readouts-evaluation/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 9.4. A decoder gets 90% on 100 test trials. Give the SE and an approximate 95% interval. You z-score each neuron using the mean and SD of the whole dataset, then split into train and test. What is wrong? Neighbouring 100 ms bins from the same lap go into training and test. Why is the test accuracy optimistic?"},{"route":"/topics/validation/fit-a-transient/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §10.1. The first thing done after breaking into a cell in whole-cell mode is usually a small hyperpolarizing current step. The voltage falls, curves and settles. From that curve the experimenter reads input resistance (from the steady deflection) and the membrane time constant (from the curvature). These numbers are the cell’s passive identity card, and every model of that cell should reproduce them.In a real CA1 cell the curve is never a perfect single exponential. Charge first spreads from the soma into the dendrites (a fast component), and with larger steps I_h activates and produces sag. That is why protocols use small steps, and why fitting is done with an explicit model whose assumptions you can state.Fitting chooses parameters boldsymboltheta that make model predictions V(t_k;boldsymboltheta) close to observations y_k . Closeness is defined by a loss, most often the mean squared error: L(boldsymboltheta) = frac1nsum_k=1^nbig(V(t_k;boldsymboltheta) - y_kbig)^2,qquad V(t; A,tau) = Abig(1-e^-t/taubig) (10.1.1)Minimizing MSE is maximum likelihood if the errors are independent Gaussians with equal variance. That assumption is worth checking: residuals that are correlated in time, or larger at some times than others, call for a different loss or weighting.Worked example 10.1.1Two ways to find τLinearize. If A is known, ln(1 - V/A) = -t/tau is a straight line through the origin with slope -1/tau . With A = 10 mV and V(10 ms) = 3.93 mV, tau = -10/ln(1-0.393) = 20 ms. Linearization is quick but weights errors unevenly: near saturation 1 - V/A is small and noise is magnified by the log.Minimize. On the lab’s 21 samples from 0 to 100 ms (true A = 10 mV, tau = 20 ms), trying tau = 15 ms gives MSE ≈ 0.34 mV² and tau = 25 ms gives ≈ 0.26 mV². The loss is asymmetric ar"},{"route":"/topics/validation/fit-a-transient/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 10.1. A transient with known A = 8 mV reaches 6.0 mV at 30 ms. Estimate tau . Residuals from your fit are positive for the first 10 ms and negative after. What does that suggest? Name a validation test for a passive model fitted to a 50 pA step."},{"route":"/topics/validation/parameter-sensitivity/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §10.2. Wash a drug onto a slice that blocks part of the leak or I_h conductance and the recorded responses change. Some change a lot, some barely. A sensitivity analysis is the model version of that pharmacology experiment: perturb one parameter slightly, record how much a measured quantity moves, and repeat for each parameter.The answer depends on which measurement you look at, just as in the slice: blocking I_h changes the sag and the resonance frequency much more than it changes the peak of an action potential.The sensitivity of an observable to a parameter is its derivative. For the passive step response with leak g , capacitance C and current I , V(t) = (I/g)(1 - e^-gt/C) , differentiation gives fracpartial Vpartial g = -fracIg^2left(1 - e^-gt/Cright) + fracIg,fractC,e^-gt/C (10.2.1)The first term says a larger leak lowers the steady state; the second, that it speeds the approach. Because parameters have different units, compare normalized (relative) sensitivities, S = (g/V),partial V/partial g : the percentage change in V per percent change in g .Worked example 10.2.1Sensitivity depends on when you lookWith C = 100 pF, I = 100 pA and g = 5 nS at t = 20 ms: V = 12.64 mV, partial V/partial g = -1.057 mV/nS and S = -0.42 . At t = 2 ms, Sapprox-0.05 (early charging depends on C , not g ); at t = 200 ms, Sapprox-1.00 (the steady state is I/g ).A central finite difference with a 5% step, [V(1.05g) - V(0.95g)]/(0.1g) , gives −1.0572 against the analytic −1.0570. Central differences have error O(h^2) ; forward differences only O(h) . fracpartial Vpartial gapproxfracV(g+h) - V(g-h)2h (10.2.2)Figure 10.2.1. Normalized sensitivities of the passive step response to g and C (Eq. (10.2.1)), against measurement time: early samples report C , late samples report g .Algor"},{"route":"/topics/validation/parameter-sensitivity/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 10.2. Show that Sto-1 as ttoinfty . Which parameter would you expect to dominate the normalized sensitivity at very early times, C or g ? Why is a central difference more accurate than a forward one for the same h ?"},{"route":"/topics/validation/identifiability/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §10.3. Two CA1 cells can produce indistinguishable voltage traces with different underlying channels. One might have more I_h and more leak K⁺ conductance; the other less of both. Studies of small circuits found that very different combinations of channel densities can generate the same activity (Prinz, Bucher & Marder 2004), and biological variability between animals is consistent with this.For a modeler this means a recording may not determine every parameter. Before trusting a fitted value, ask whether some other combination would have fit just as well.A parameter is identifiable if the observations determine it uniquely. Structural non-identifiability is a property of the model and the observable even with perfect data; practical non-identifiability arises because noisy, finite data constrain a parameter only weakly.Normalize the passive step response by its steady state: fracV(t)V_infty = 1 - e^-t,g/C (10.3.1)Only the ratio C/g = tau appears. The pairs (C, g) = (100 pF, 5 nS) and (200 pF, 10 nS) produce identical normalized curves: any point on the line C = 20,g fits perfectly. The loss landscape has a flat valley rather than a single minimum.Worked example 10.3.1Breaking the tieKeep the absolute voltage for a known current. With 100 pA, the steady states are 100/5 = 20 mV and 100/10 = 10 mV, which distinguishes the two pairs: amplitude identifies g , and then tau gives C . If the injected current were also unknown (a synaptic input of unknown size), the ambiguity would return.Figure 10.3.1. Loss over ( g , C ) for data generated with g = 5 nS, C = 100 pF (dot). A Fitting the normalized trace (Eq. (10.3.1)): a flat valley along C = 20g (dashed). B Fitting absolute voltage for a known 100 pA: a single minimum.In larger models such ridges are everywhere: dif"},{"route":"/topics/validation/identifiability/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 10.3. Name two (C, g) pairs other than those above that give the same normalized curve as (100, 5) . You fit a two-compartment model to somatic recordings only and find that g_c and g_d trade off. Suggest an experiment. Is “many parameter sets fit equally well” a failure of the model?"},{"route":"/topics/validation/uncertainty-in-predictions/","heading":"The full treatment","text":"Textbook §10.4. Measured membrane properties come with spread. Repeat the time-constant measurement in one cell and the values scatter; measure it across cells and the spread grows; change temperature or recording method and the mean moves. A model built on one number inherits none of this, yet its predictions (when a dendritic EPSP peaks, how much a theta input is amplified) are only as certain as the numbers it was built from.This section propagates that biological and experimental spread through a model to a prediction you can compare with data.Parameter uncertainty becomes prediction uncertainty. The simplest analysis is a scenario envelope: compute predictions at chosen low, central and high parameter values. With A = 10 mV and tau = 18, 20, 22 ms, V(20 ms) is 6.71, 6.32 and 5.97 mV. This is useful but it is not a confidence interval: no probability is attached to the chosen range.If a parameter has a distribution, say tau with SD sigma_tau , the delta method linearizes the prediction: SDbig(V(t)big) approx left|fracpartial Vpartialtauright|sigma_tau,qquad fracpartial Vpartialtau = -A,fracttau^2,e^-t/tau (10.4.1)Worked example 10.4.1Delta method versus samplingAt t = 20 ms with tau = 20 pm 2 ms: partial V/partialtau = -10times(20/400)e^-1approx-0.184 mV/ms, so SD(V) ≈ 0.37 mV. Sampling 200,000 values of tau from a Gaussian and computing V for each gives SD 0.370 mV and a mean of 6.34 mV, slightly above V(bartau) = 6.32 mV: the curvature of V(tau) shifts the mean, an effect the linear method misses.Figure 10.4.1. A Monte Carlo prediction band for V(t) with tausimmathcal N(20, 2^2) ms, and the ±2 ms scenarios (dotted). B The SD of the prediction from sampling and from the delta method (Eq. (10.4.1)) agree and peak near t = tau .Monte Carlo propagation (sample paramet"},{"route":"/topics/validation/uncertainty-in-predictions/","heading":"Key terms and exercises","text":"Exercises 10.4. Using the delta method, what is SD(V) at t = 40 ms for A = 10 mV, tau = 20pm2 ms? At what time is the prediction V(t) most sensitive to tau ? Why is the Monte Carlo mean of V larger than V(bartau) in the example?"}];
